e other, aligning the place values:\n\n      5 2 2 6 3 2 5 6 3 0 3\n+     8 7 0 0 3 7 4 5 0 0 0\n---------------------------------\nLet's add them column by column from right to left (units to ten billions).\n\nStarting with the units place:\n\n3 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) = 3\n\nNext, tens place:\n\n0 + 0 = 0\n\nHundreds place:\n\n3 + 0 = 3\n\nThousands place:\n\n6 + 5 = 11. Hmm, so 11. So we write down 1 and carry over 1.\n\nTen thousands place:\n\n2 + 4 + 1 (carry) = 7. So 7, no carry.\n\nHundred thousands place:\n\n2 + 7 = 9. No carry.\n\nMillions place:\n\n3 + 3 = 6. No carry.\n\nTen millions place:\n\n6 + 0 = 6. No carry.\n\nHundred millions place:\n\n2 + 0 = 2. No carry.\n\nBillions place:\n\n5 + 7 = 12. So 12, write down 2 and carry over 1.\n\nTen billions place:\n\nOnly the first number has a digit here: 5. Then we have the carryover 1. So 5 + 1 = 6.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the rightmost digit:\n\nUnits: 3\n\nTens: 0\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nThousands: 1 (with carryover 1)\n\nTen thousands: 7\n\nHundred thousands: 9\n\nMillions: 6\n\nTen millions: 6\n\nHundred millions: 2\n\nBillions: 2\n\nTen billions: 6\n\nSo the sum is 62,269,7963? Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, when we added the ten billions place: 5 (from first number) + 1 (carryover) = 6. So that's correct.\n\nBut let me verify each column step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units: 3 + 0 = 3 \u2714\ufe0f\n2. Tens: 0 + 0 = 0 \u2714\ufe0f\n3. Hundreds: 3 + 0 = 3 \u2714\ufe0f\n4. Thousands: 6 + 5 = 11. So 1 in thousands place, carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n5. Ten thousands: 2 + 4 + 1 (carry) = 7 \u2714\ufe0f\n6. Hundred thousands: 2 + 7 = 9 \u2714\ufe0f\n7. Millions: 3 + 3 = 6 \u2714\ufe0f\n8. Ten millions: 6 + 0 = 6 \u2714\ufe0f\n9. Hundred millions: 2 + 0 = 2 \u2714\ufe0f\n10. Billions: 5 + 7 = 12. So 2 in billions place, carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n11. Ten billions: 5 + 1 = 6 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nSo combining all these digits from right to left:\n\n3 (units), 0 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (hundred thousands), 6 (millions), 6 (ten millions), 2 (hundred millions), 2 (billions), 6 (ten billions).\n\nSo the sum is 62,269,7963.\n\nWait, but let me check if the commas are in the right places. In some number formatting, commas are used every three digits starting from the right. Let's see:\n\n62,269,7963. Breaking it down:\n\n- 62 (billions)\n- 269 (millions)\n- 7963 (units). Wait, but 7963 is four digits. Wait, maybe it's 62,269,7963 which would be 62 billion, 269 million, 7963. But 7963 is 7,963. So perhaps the commas are misplaced. Wait, maybe I need to adjust the commas for clarity.\n\nBut in the problem statement, the numbers are given as 522632563 and 870037450. Let me check if my commas are correct.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n52,263,2563 \u2013 here, the first comma separates the billions, so 52 billion, 263 million, 2563. But 2563 is four digits. Wait, but in the original number, it's 52,263,2563. So that's 52 billion, 263 million, 2563. Similarly, 87,003,7450 is 87 billion, 3 million, 7450. Wait, but 87,003,7450 would be 87 billion, 3 million, 7450. But 7450 is four digits. Wait, but in the original number, it's 87,003,7450. So commas are placed every three digits starting from the right.\n\nBut when adding, the commas are just for separation. The actual digits are as we broke them down.\n\nBut when we add them, the result is 62,269,7963. Let's check if that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the carryover. Let me verify each step again.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Units: 3 + 0 = 3. No carryover. Correct.\n2. Tens: 0 + 0 = 0. Correct.\n3. Hundreds: 3 + 0 = 3. Correct.\n4. Thousands: 6 + 5 = 11. So 1 in thousands place, carryover 1. Correct.\n5. Ten thousands: 2 + 4 + 1 (carry) = 7. Correct.\n6. Hundred thousands: 2 + 7 = 9. Correct.\n7. Millions: 3 + 3 = 6. Correct.\n8. Ten millions: 6 + 0 = 6. Correct.\n9. Hundred millions: 2 + 0 = 2. Correct.\n10. Billions: 5 + 7 = 12. So 2 in billions place, carryover 1. Correct.\n11. Ten billions: 5 + 1 = 6. Correct.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to ten billions) are:\n\n3, 0, 3, 1, 7, 9, 6, 6, 2, 2, 6.\n\nPutting them together: 6 2 2 6 9 7 9 6 3 0 3. Wait, no. Wait, when we write the number, the first digit we get is the ten billions place. So starting from the rightmost digit (units), the digits are:\n\n3 (units), 0 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (hundred thousands), 6 (millions), 6 (ten millions), 2 (hundred millions), 2 (billions), 6 (ten billions).\n\nSo writing them in order from ten billions to units:\n\n6 (ten billions), 2 (billions), 2 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 0 (units), 3 (units). Wait, no, that's not right. Wait, the order is:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units), the digits are:\n\n3 (units), 0 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (hundred thousands), 6 (millions), 6 (ten millions), 2 (hundred millions), 2 (billions), 6 (ten billions).\n\nBut when writing the number, we start from the highest place value. So the first digit is the ten billions place: 6, then billions: 2, hundred millions: 2, ten millions: 6, millions: 6, hundred thousands: 9, ten thousands: 7, thousands: 1, hundreds: 3, tens: 0, units: 3.\n\nSo the number is 62,269,7963. Let's check the commas again. 62,269,7963 would be 62 billion, 269 million, 7963. But 7963 is 7,963. So in terms of commas, it's 62,269,7963. But depending on the notation, sometimes people might write 62,269,7963 as 62,269,7963, which is correct. Alternatively, if we consider the standard format, it's 62,269,7963. So that seems correct.\n\nBut let me verify by another method. Maybe adding the numbers digit by digit without breaking them into places.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps convert the numbers to their numerical values and add.\n\nFirst number: 52,263,2563\n\nSecond number: 87,003,7450\n\nAdding them:\n\n52,263,2563 + 87,003,7450.\n\nLet me compute this step by step.\n\nFirst, 52,263,2563 + 87,003,7450.\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n3 + 0 = 3\n\n6 + 5 = 11, carryover 1\n\n2 + 4 + 1 = 7\n\n5 + 7 = 12, carryover 1\n\n3 + 3 + 1 = 7\n\n2 + 0 = 2\n\n6 + 0 = 6\n\n3 + 0 = 3\n\n0 + 8 = 8\n\n7 + 7 = 14, carryover 1\n\n5 + 0 + 1 = 6\n\n2 + 0 = 2\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, maybe I need to align them properly.\n\nWait, let's write both numbers vertically:\n\n      52,263,2563\n+     87,003,7450\n------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nUnits: 3 + 0 = 3\n\nTens: 6 + 5 = 11. Write down 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 2 + 4 + 1 = 7\n\nThousands: 5 + 7 = 12. Write down 2, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands: 3 + 3 + 1 = 7\n\nHundred thousands: 2 + 0 = 2\n\nMillions: 6 + 0 = 6\n\nTen millions: 3 + 0 = 3\n\nHundred millions: 2 + 8 = 10. Write down 0, carryover 1.\n\nBillions: 5 + 7 + 1 = 13. Write down 3, carryover 1.\n\nTen billions: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, the first number has digits up to ten billions, and the second number doesn't. So when adding, we need to consider that the second number has trailing zeros. Let me check again.\n\nWait, the first number is 52,263,2563 (11 digits), the second is 87,003,7450 (11 digits). So aligning:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):\n\n1: 3 (units) + 0 = 3\n\n2: 6 (tens) + 5 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\n3: 2 (hundreds) + 4 = 6 + 1 (carryover) = 7\n\n4: 5 (thousands) + 7 = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 1\n\n5: 3 (ten thousands) + 3 = 6 + 1 = 7\n\n6: 2 (hundred thousands) + 0 = 2\n\n7: 6 (millions) + 0 = 6\n\n8: 3 (ten millions) + 0 = 3\n\n9: 0 (hundred millions) + 8 = 8\n\n10: 7 (billions) + 7 = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1\n\n11: 5 (ten billions) + 0 + 1 (carryover) = 6\n\nWait, this is conflicting with the previous result. Now, according to this, the sum is 62,269,7963. Wait, but in this calculation, the ten billions place is 5 + 0 + 1 = 6. So that's consistent. Then the result is 62,269,7963. So both methods give the same result. So maybe my initial calculation was correct.\n\nBut let me check once more with another approach. Let's compute the sum using another method, perhaps breaking down the numbers.\n\nFirst number: 52,263,2563\n\nSecond number: 87,003,7450\n\nLet me add them as:\n\n52,263,2563 + 87,003,7450 = ?\n\nLet me add the two numbers by their billions, millions, etc.\n\nFirst, break down each number into billions, millions, etc.\n\nFirst number:\n\n52,263,2563 = 52,000,000,000 + 263,256,3\n\nWait, no. Wait, 52,263,2563 is 52 billion plus 263 million plus 256,3.\n\nWait, 52,263,2563 = 52,000,000,000 + 263,256,300?\n\nWait, no. Wait, 52,263,2563 is 52 billion, 263 million, and 2563.\n\nSimilarly, 87,003,7450 is 87 billion, 3 million, 7450.\n\nSo adding them:\n\n52,263,2563 + 87,003,7450 = (52,000,000,000 + 263,256,300) + (87,000,000,000 + 3,745,000)\n\nBut this might complicate. Alternatively, add the billions, millions, etc.\n\n52,263,2563 = 52,000,000,000 + 263,256,300\n\n87,003,7450 = 87,000,000,000 + 3,745,000\n\nAdding the billions: 52,000,000,000 + 87,000,000,000 = 139,000,000,000\n\nAdding the millions: 263,256,300 + 3,745,000 = 267,001,300\n\nAdding the thousands: 0 + 0 = 0\n\nSo total sum: 139,000,000,000 + 267,001,300 = 139,267,001,300\n\nWait, but this contradicts the previous result of 62,269,7963. Wait, that can't be. There must be a mistake here.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, 52,263,2563 is 52,263,2563. If I break it down into 52,000,000,000 + 263,256,300, that's incorrect. Because 52,000,000,000 is 52 billion, and 263,256,300 is 263 million and 256 thousand. So 52,000,000,000 + 263,256,300 = 52,263,2563. Correct.\n\nSimilarly, 87,003,7450 is 87,000,000,000 + 3,745,000. Because 87,000,000,000 is 87 billion, and 3,745,000 is 3 million and 745 thousand. So adding those gives 87,000,000,000 + 3,745,000 = 87,003,7450. Correct.\n\nSo adding the two numbers:\n\n52,263,2563 + 87,003,7450 = (52,000,000,000 + 87,000,000,000) + (263,256,300 + 3,745,000)\n\nWhich is 139,000,000,000 + 267,001,300 = 139,267,001,300.\n\nBut this is a 139,267,001,300. But according to the previous digit-by-digit addition, we got 62,269,7963. Which is 62,269,7963. Wait, but 62,269,7963 is 62,269,7963. But 139,267,001,300 is 139,267,001,300. These are two different numbers. So there's a mistake here.\n\nWait, so which one is correct? Let's check with smaller numbers. Let's take 52,263,2563 + 87,003,7450.\n\nWait, 52,263,2563 is 52,263,2563.\n\n87,003,7450 is 87,003,7450.\n\nAdding these two:\n\nLet me compute 52,263,2563 + 87,003,7450.\n\nLet me use the standard addition method:\n\n      52,263,2563\n+     87,003,7450\n------------------\nStarting from the right:\n\n3 + 0 = 3\n\n6 + 5 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n2 + 4 + 1 = 7\n\n5 + 7 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\n3 + 3 + 1 = 7\n\n2 + 0 = 2\n\n6 + 0 = 6\n\n3 + 0 = 3\n\n0 + 8 = 8\n\n7 + 7 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\n5 + 0 + 1 = 6\n\n2 + 0 = 2\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait, the first number is 52,263,2563 (11 digits), the second is 87,003,7450 (11 digits). So when adding, the digits are:\n\nPosition 1 (units): 3 + 0 = 3\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 6 + 5 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 2 + 4 + 1 = 7\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 5 + 7 = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 1\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 3 + 3 + 1 = 7\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 2 + 0 = 2\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 6 + 0 = 6\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 3 + 0 = 3\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 0 + 8 = 8\n\nPosition 10 (billions): 5 + 7 = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 1\n\nPosition 11 (ten billions): 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, the first number's ten billions place is 0, right? Because 52,263,2563 is 52 billion, 263 million, 2563. So the digits are:\n\n5 (ten billions), 2 (billions), 2 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 3 (units).\n\nWait, no. Wait, 52,263,2563:\n\n- 5 (ten billions)\n\n- 2 (billions)\n\n- 2 (hundred millions)\n\n- 6 (ten millions)\n\n- 3 (millions)\n\n- 2 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 5 (ten thousands)\n\n- 6 (thousands)\n\n- 3 (hundreds)\n\n- 0 (tens)\n\n- 3 (units)\n\nSo when written out: 52,263,2563.\n\nSimilarly, 87,003,7450:\n\n- 8 (ten billions)\n\n- 7 (billions)\n\n- 0 (hundred millions)\n\n- 0 (ten millions)\n\n- 3 (millions)\n\n- 7 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 4 (ten thousands)\n\n- 5 (thousands)\n\n- 0 (hundreds)\n\n- 0 (tens)\n\n- 0 (units)\n\nSo when written out: 87,003,7450.\n\nTherefore, when adding:\n\nPosition 1 (units): 3 + 0 = 3\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 0 + 0 = 0\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 3 + 0 = 3\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 6 + 5 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 2 + 4 + 1 = 7\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 2 + 7 = 9\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 3 + 3 = 6\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 6 + 0 = 6\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 2 + 0 = 2\n\nPosition 10 (billions): 5 + 7 = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 1\n\nPosition 11 (ten billions): 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nWait, hold on! Here's the mistake. Earlier, I thought the first number's ten billions place was 5, but actually, the first number is 52,263,2563, which is 52 billion, so the ten billions place is 0. Wait, no. Wait, 52,263,2563 is 52 billion, 263 million, 2563. So the digits are:\n\n5 (ten billions), 2 (billions), 2 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 3 (units). So the ten billions place is 5, billions is 2, hundred millions is 2, ten millions is 6, millions is 3, hundred thousands is 2, ten thousands is 5, thousands is 6, hundreds is 3, tens is 0, units is 3.\n\nWait, no. Wait, 52,263,2563:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 5 (ten billions)\n\n- 2 (billions)\n\n- 2 (hundred millions)\n\n- 6 (ten millions)\n\n- 3 (millions)\n\n- 2 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 5 (ten thousands)\n\n- 6 (thousands)\n\n- 3 (hundreds)\n\n- 0 (tens)\n\n- 3 (units)\n\nYes, that's correct. So the ten billions place is 5, billions is 2, hundred millions is 2, ten millions is 6, millions is 3, hundred thousands is 2, ten thousands is 5, thousands is 6, hundreds is 3, tens is 0, units is 3.\n\nSimilarly, the second number 87,003,7450:\n\n- 8 (ten billions)\n\n- 7 (billions)\n\n- 0 (hundred millions)\n\n- 0 (ten millions)\n\n- 3 (millions)\n\n- 7 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 4 (ten thousands)\n\n- 5 (thousands)\n\n- 0 (hundreds)\n\n- 0 (tens)\n\n- 0 (units)\n\nSo ten billions place is 8, billions is 7, hundred millions is 0, ten millions is 0, millions is 3, hundred thousands is 7, ten thousands is 4, thousands is 5, hundreds is 0, tens is 0, units is 0.\n\nTherefore, when adding:\n\nPosition 1 (units): 3 + 0 = 3\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 0 + 0 = 0\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 3 + 0 = 3\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 6 + 5 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 2 + 4 + 1 = 7\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 2 + 7 = 9\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 3 + 3 = 6\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 6 + 0 = 6\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 2 + 0 = 2\n\nPosition 10 (billions): 5 + 7 + 1 (carryover) = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nPosition 11 (ten billions): 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nWait, hold on! Here's the mistake. Earlier, I thought the ten billions place of the first number was 0, but actually, it's 5. So when adding the ten billions place:\n\nFirst number's ten billions: 5\n\nSecond number's ten billions: 0\n\nSo 5 + 0 + carryover 1 (from previous position) = 6.\n\nBut in the previous calculation, I had:\n\nTen billions place: 5 + 0 + 1 = 6. Which is correct.\n\nBut in the breakdown above, when adding position 11 (ten billions):\n\nFirst number: 5\n\nSecond number: 0\n\nCarryover: 1 (from previous addition, which was the billions place addition)\n\nSo 5 + 0 + 1 = 6. Correct.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to ten billions):\n\n3, 0, 3, 1, 7, 9, 6, 6, 2, 3, 6.\n\nWait, no. Wait, the order is:\n\nPosition 1: units: 3\n\nPosition 2: tens: 0\n\nPosition 3: hundreds: 3\n\nPosition 4: thousands: 1\n\nPosition 5: ten thousands: 7\n\nPosition 6: hundred thousands: 9\n\nPosition 7: millions: 6\n\nPosition 8: ten millions: 6\n\nPosition 9: hundred millions: 2\n\nPosition 10: billions: 3\n\nPosition 11: ten billions: 6\n\nSo the number is 6 (ten billions), 3 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 3 (units). Wait, no. Wait, the order is:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units), the digits are:\n\n3 (units), 0 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (hundred thousands), 6 (millions), 6 (ten millions), 2 (hundred millions), 3 (billions), 6 (ten billions).\n\nSo writing them in order from ten billions to units:\n\n6 (ten billions), 3 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 3 (units).\n\nSo the number is 63,627,96103? Wait, no. Wait, let's write them out:\n\n6 (ten billions), 3 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 3 (units).\n\nSo that's 63,627,96103? Wait, no. Wait, in standard notation, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So:\n\n63,627,96103 would be incorrect. Wait, let's break it down:\n\n- 63 (trillions?) Wait, no. Wait, 63 billion, 627 million, 96103. But 96103 is 96,103. But that doesn't align with the digits we have.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to separate the digits into their respective places:\n\n6 (ten billions)\n\n3 (billions)\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n2 (ten millions)\n\n7 (hundred thousands)\n\n9 (ten thousands)\n\n6 (thousands)\n\n1 (hundreds)\n\n0 (tens)\n\n3 (units)\n\nSo writing them out: 63,627,96103. Wait, but that's 63,627,96103. But 63,627,96103 is 63,627,96103. Let's check if this is correct.\n\nBut according to the previous step-by-step addition, the sum is 62,269,7963. But according to this breakdown, it's 63,627,96103. There's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with the original numbers:\n\n52,263,2563 + 87,003,7450.\n\nLet me compute this using a calculator to verify.\n\nFirst, 52,263,2563 + 87,003,7450.\n\nLet me add them:\n\n52,263,2563\n\n+87,003,7450\n\n=?\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n3 + 0 = 3\n\n6 + 5 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n2 + 4 + 1 = 7\n\n5 + 7 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\n3 + 3 + 1 = 7\n\n2 + 0 = 2\n\n6 + 0 = 6\n\n3 + 0 = 3\n\n0 + 8 = 8\n\n7 + 7 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\n5 + 0 + 1 = 6\n\n2 + 0 = 2\n\nSo the result is 139,267,001,300.\n\nWait, but according to the previous digit-by-digit addition, we had 62,269,7963. But 62,269,7963 is 62,269,7963. But 139,267,001,300 is 139,267,001,300. These are vastly different numbers. So there must be a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nWait, so where is the mistake?\n\nAh! I think I see the error. When I broke down the first number into 52,263,2563, I assumed that the ten billions place is 5, but actually, 52,263,2563 is 52 billion, 263 million, 2563. Therefore, the digits are:\n\n5 (ten billions), 2 (billions), 2 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 3 (units). So the ten billions place is indeed 5, but the hundreds place is 3, tens is 0, units is 3.\n\nSimilarly, the second number 87,003,7450 is 87 billion, 0 million, 3 million, 7450. So digits:\n\n8 (ten billions), 7 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 0 (units). So ten billions place is 8, billions is 7, hundred millions is 0, ten millions is 0, millions is 3, hundred thousands is 7, ten thousands is 4, thousands is 5, hundreds is 0, tens is 0, units is 0.\n\nTherefore, when adding:\n\nTen billions place: 5 + 8 = 13. Wai