\nSo when adding, we need to align them properly:\n\n          9 9 9 4 7 4 6 7 7\n        + 5 0 7 1 9 9 0 8 8\n        ------------------------\nSo starting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units: 7 + 8 = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n2. Tens: 7 + 8 = 15 + 1 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n3. Hundreds: 6 + 9 = 15 + 1 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n4. Thousands: 4 + 1 = 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n5. Ten-thousands: 7 + 9 = 16 + 0 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n6. Hundred-thousands: 9 + 7 = 16 + 1 = 17. Write 7, carry 1.\n7. Millions place: 9 + 0 = 9 + 1 = 10. Wait, hold on. Wait, the millions place in the first number is 9, and in the second number, it's 0. So 9 + 0 = 9, plus any carryover? Wait, up to this point, we have already added the hundred millions, ten millions, millions, hundred thousands, ten thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens, units. So the next place is millions. Wait, let me check the alignment again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the places. Let's list each number with their digits aligned:\n\nFirst number: 999,474,677\n\nDigits: 9 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 7 (units).\n\nSecond number: 507,199,088\n\nDigits: 5 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 8 (units).\n\nSo when adding, the rightmost digits (units) are:\n\nFirst number: 7\n\nSecond number: 8\n\nTens place:\n\nFirst number: 7\n\nSecond number: 8\n\nHundreds place:\n\nFirst number: 6\n\nSecond number: 9\n\nThousands place:\n\nFirst number: 4\n\nSecond number: 1\n\nTen-thousands place:\n\nFirst number: 7\n\nSecond number: 9\n\nHundred-thousands place:\n\nFirst number: 9\n\nSecond number: 7\n\nMillions place:\n\nFirst number: 9\n\nSecond number: 0\n\nTen-millions place:\n\nFirst number: 9\n\nSecond number: 0\n\nHundred-millions place:\n\nFirst number: 9\n\nSecond number: 5\n\nWait, so when adding, the places are as fol