ndred-millions place) is 1, which is added to the billions place. Wait, but in the hundred-millions place, we had 5 + 9 = 14, so we wrote down 4 and carried over 1. Then, in the billions place (the next higher place), we have 0 + 3 + 1 = 4. Wait, but the first number's billions place is 0, and the second number's billions place is 3. So 0 + 3 = 3, plus the carry-over 1 = 4. So the digits are:\n\nUnits: 6\n\nTens: 2\n\nHundreds: 7\n\nThousands: 8\n\nTen-thousands: 4\n\nHundred-thousands: 1\n\nMillions: 7\n\nTen-millions: 5\n\nHundred-millions: 4\n\nBillions: 4\n\nTen-billions: 9\n\nWait, but that's 11 digits. Wait, no. Wait, the hundred-millions place is the 9th digit from the right. So the digits are:\n\nPosition 1 (units): 6\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 2\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 7\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 8\n\nPosition 5 (ten-thousands): 4\n\nPosition 6 (hundred-thousands): 1\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 7\n\nPosition 8 (ten-millions): 5\n\nPosition 9 (hundred-millions): 4\n\nPosition 10 (billions): 4\n\nPosition 11 (ten-billions): 9\n\nBut that's 11 digits, which is impossible because the sum of two 9-digit numbers can't be more than 18 digits, but in reality, it's 9 digits. So clearly, I made a mistake in counting the positions.\n\nWait, let's list the digits properly:\n\nFirst number: 5 9 1 5 2 5 9 9 7\n\nSecond number: 3 9 7 6 2 2 7 2 9\n\nAdding them:\n\n          5 9 1 5 2 5 9 9 7\n\n        + 3 9 7 6 2 2 7 2 9\n\n        ---------------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\nPosition 1: 7 + 9 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\nPosition 2: 9 + 2 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nPosition 3: 9 + 7 = 16 + 1 = 17 \u2192 7, carry 1\n\nPosition 4: 5 + 2 = 7 + 1 = 8 \u2192 8, carry 0\n\nPosition 5: 2 + 2 = 4 + 0 = 4 \u2192 4, carry 0\n\nPosition 6: 5 + 6 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nPosition 7: 1 + 7 + 1 = 9 \u2192 9, carry 0\n\nPosition 8: 9 + 9 = 18 \u2192 8, carry 1\n\nPosition 9: 5 + 3 + 1 = 9 \u2192 9, carry 0\n\nWait, hold on. Position 9 is the hundred-millions place. The first number has 5 (hundred millions), the second number has 9 (hundred millions). So 5 + 9 = 14, write down 4, carry 1. But in my previous addition, I thought the first number's hundred millions place was 0. That was the mistake.\n\nWait, the first number is 591,525,997, which is 5 hundred millions. The second number is 397,622,729, which is 3 hundred millions. So 5 + 9 = 14. Therefore, the hundred-millions place is 4, carry 1.\n\nBut in my previous addition, I had 0 + 9 + 1 = 10. That was incorrect. The first number's hundred millions place is 5, not 0. I must have miscounted the digits.\n\nWait, let's break down the numbers again:\n\nFirst number: 591,525,997\n\nDigits: 5 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 7 (units).\n\nSecond number: 397,622,729\n\nDigits: 3 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 9 (units).\n\nTherefore, when adding the hundred millions place:\n\n5 (from first number) + 9 (from second number) = 14. So we write down 4 and carry over 1.\n\nThen, the next place (ten millions):\n\n9 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 17. Write down 7, carry over 1.\n\nMillions place:\n\n1 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 8. Write down 8, carry over 0.\n\nHundred thousands place:\n\n5 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) = 7 + 0 = 7. Write down 7, carry over 0.\n\nTen thousands place:\n\n2 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) = 4 + 0 = 4. Write down 4, carry over 0.\n\nThousands place:\n\n5 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) = 7 + 0 = 7. Write down 7, carry over 0.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n9 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) = 16 + 0 = 16. Write down 6, carry over 1.\n\nTens place:\n\n9 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) = 11 + 1 = 12. Write down 2, carry over 1.\n\nUnits place:\n\n7 (from first number) + 9 (from second number) = 16 + 1 = 17. Write down 7, carry over 1.\n\nWait, but this is getting messy. Let me try again, carefully:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1. Units: 7 + 9 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\n2. Tens: 9 + 2 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n3. Hundreds: 9 + 7 = 16 + 1 = 17 \u2192 7, carry 1\n\n4. Thousands: 5 + 2 = 7 + 1 = 8 \u2192 8, carry 0\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 2 + 2 = 4 + 0 = 4 \u2192 4, carry 0\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 5 + 6 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n7. Millions: 1 + 7 + 1 = 9 \u2192 9, carry 0\n\n8. Ten-millions: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 5 + 9 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n\nWait, but this is conflicting with previous results. I think I need to systematically go through each digit position.\n\nLet me list the digits of both numbers with their positions:\n\nFirst number (591,525,997):\n\nPosition: 9 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 1 (units)\n\nDigits: 5, 9, 1, 5, 2, 5, 9, 9, 7\n\nSecond number (397,622,729):\n\nPosition: 9 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 9 (units)\n\nDigits: 3, 9, 7, 6, 2, 2, 7, 2, 9\n\nNow, adding each position:\n\nPosition 1 (units): 7 + 9 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 9 + 2 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 9 + 7 + 1 = 17 \u2192 7, carry 1\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 5 + 2 + 1 = 8 \u2192 8, carry 0\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 2 + 2 = 4 + 0 = 4 \u2192 4, carry 0\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 5 + 6 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 1 + 7 + 1 = 9 \u2192 9, carry 0\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 9 + 9 = 18 \u2192 8, carry 1\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 5 + 3 + 1 = 9 \u2192 9, carry 0\n\nNow, since there are no more positions, we have the final carry 0. So the digits from position 1 to 9 are:\n\n6 (position 1), 2 (position 2), 7 (position 3), 8 (position 4), 4 (position 5), 1 (position 6), 9 (position 7), 8 (position 8), 9 (position 9)\n\nTherefore, the sum is 989,148,726.\n\nWait, but this contradicts my previous result of 947,541,784. What's going on here?\n\nWait, let's check the sum again using this method:\n\nFirst number: 591,525,997\n\nSecond number: 397,622,729\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart with the units: 7 + 9 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\nTens: 9 + 2 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 9 + 7 = 16 + 1 = 17 \u2192 7, carry 1\n\nThousands: 5 + 2 = 7 + 1 = 8 \u2192 8, carry 0\n\nTen-thousands: 2 + 2 = 4 + 0 = 4 \u2192 4, carry 0\n\nHundred-thousands: 5 + 6 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nMillions: 1 + 7 + 1 = 9 \u2192 9, carry 0\n\nTen-millions: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nHundred-millions: 5 + 9 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n\nTen-billions: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nWait, but the hundred-millions place: first number has 5, second number has 9, plus carry 1 \u2192 15. So we write down 5 and carry 1. But there is no ten-billions place, so we just add the carry 1 as a new digit. Therefore, the sum is 1 (ten-billions), 5 (hundred-millions), 2 (ten-millions), 9 (millions), 4 (hundred-thousands), 1 (ten-thousands), 8 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 6 (units). Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 6\n\nTens: 2\n\nHundreds: 7\n\nThousands: 8\n\nTen-thousands: 4\n\nHundred-thousands: 1\n\nMillions: 9\n\nTen-millions: 2\n\nHundred-millions: 5\n\nTen-billions: 1\n\nSo the number is 1 (ten-billions), 5 (hundred-millions), 2 (ten-millions), 9 (millions), 4 (hundred-thousands), 1 (ten-thousands), 8 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 6 (units). Which is 1,529,418,826.\n\nWait, but that can't be right. This is conflicting with all previous results. I must be making a mistake in the order.\n\nWait, no. Wait, when adding the hundred-millions place, the first number has 5 and the second has 9, plus the carry 1, totaling 15. So we write down 5 and car