he hundred-millions place, and the second number has 5. So 0 + 5 = 5, plus the carried 1 makes 6. That seems right.\n\nNow, let me check if I missed any digits. The first number is 833,931,691. Let's count the digits: 8 3 3 ,9 3 1 ,6 9 1. So that's 9 digits. The second number is 579,314,615: 5 7 9 ,3 1 4 ,6 1 5. Also 9 digits. So when adding, the leftmost digit is the hundred-millions place, which we handled. So the result should be a 10-digit number? Wait, 833 million plus 579 million is around 1.4 billion, so 10 digits. Let me check the final sum.\n\nPutting it all together: 4 (ten-millions), 0 (millions), 2 (hundred-thousands), 3 (ten-thousands), 9 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 6 (ones). Wait, hold on. Wait, when I added the thousands place, I got 8. Then ten-thousands place 2, hundred-thousands 5, millions 0, ten-millions 4. So the digits from right to left would be:\n\nOnes: 6\n\nTens: 3\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nThousands: 8\n\nTen-thousands: 2\n\nHundred-thousands: 5\n\nMillions: 0\n\nTen-millions: 4\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nBut wait, when writing the number, it's left to right, so starting from the highest place value. So the digits would be:\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nTen-millions: 4\n\nMillions: 0\n\nHundred-thousands: 5\n\nTen-thousands: 2\n\nThousands: 8\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nTens: 3\n\nOnes: 6\n\nSo the number is 640,528,336. Let me verify this.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can add the numbers digit by digit another way. Let's try adding each corresponding digit.\n\nLet me write both numbers vertically:\n\n          833,931,691\n        +579,314,615\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1. 1 + 5 = 6\n2. 9 + 1 = 10 (carry 1)\n3. 6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13 (carry 1)\n4. 3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8\n5. 9 + 3 = 12 (carry 1)\n6. 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5\n7. 3 + 7 = 10 (carry 1)\n8. 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14 (carry 1)\n9. 0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6\n10. 0 (since there are no more digits) + 0 (since the second number has no more digits) + 1 (carry) = 1? Wait, hold on. Wait, the second number is 579,314,615. Let me check how many digits it has. It's 579,314,615. So commas separate every three digits. So 579 million, 314 thousand, 615. So the digits are:\n\n5 (hundred-millions), 7 (ten-millions), 9 (millions), 3 (hundred-thousands), 1 (ten-thousands), 4 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 5 (ones). So when adding, the digits are:\n\nHundred-millions: 8 + 5 = 13 (carry 1)\nTen-millions: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11 (carry 1)\nMillions: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5\nHundred-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 (carry 1)\nTen-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5\nThousands: 1 + 4 = 5\nHundreds: 6 + 6 = 12 (carry 1)\nTens: 9 + 1 = 10 + 1 = 11 (carry 1)\nOnes: 1 + 5 = 6 + 1 = 7\n\nWait, hold on, this is conflicting with the previous result. Now I'm confused. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the initial alignment. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n833,931,691\n\n579,314,615\n\nBreaking down each digit position:\n\nFirst number (833,931,691):\n\n- Hundred-millions: 8\n- Ten-millions: 3\n- Millions: 3\n- Hundred-thousands: 9\n- Ten-thousands: 3\n- Thousands: 1\n- Hundreds: 6\n- Tens: 9\n- Ones: 1\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, 833,931,691. Let's parse it:\n\nDigits from right to left (ones to hundred-millions):\n\n1 (ones)\n\n9 (tens)\n\n6 (hundreds)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n3 (ten-thousands)\n\n9 (hundred-thousands)\n\n3 (millions)\n\n3 (ten-millions)\n\n8 (hundred-millions)\n\nWait, no. Wait, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So 833,931,691 is:\n\n833 million, 931 thousand, 691.\n\nSo breaking down:\n\n- 8 (hundred-millions)\n- 3 (ten-millions)\n- 3 (millions)\n- 9 (hundred-thousands)\n- 3 (ten-thousands)\n- 1 (thousands)\n- 6 (hundreds)\n- 9 (tens)\n- 1 (ones)\n\nWait, that can't be. Wait, 833,931,691:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1 (ones)\n\n9 (tens)\n\n6 (hundreds)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n3 (ten-thousands)\n\n9 (hundred-thousands)\n\n3 (millions)\n\n3 (ten-millions)\n\n8 (hundred-millions)\n\nYes, that's correct. So the digits are:\n\nHundred-millions: 8\n\nTen-millions: 3\n\nMillions: 3\n\nHundred-thousands: 9\n\nTen-thousands: 3\n\nThousands: 1\n\nHundreds: 6\n\nTens: 9\n\nOnes: 1\n\nSimilarly, 579,314,615:\n\n5 (hundred-millions)\n\n7 (ten-millions)\n\n9 (millions)\n\n3 (hundred-thousands)\n\n1 (ten-thousands)\n\n4 (thousands)\n\n6 (hundreds)\n\n1 (tens)\n\n5 (ones)\n\nSo aligning them:\n\nHundred-millions: 8 + 5 = 13 (carry 1)\n\nTen-millions: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11 (carry 1)\n\nMillions: 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13 (carry 1)\n\nHundred-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 (carry 1)\n\nTen-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\nThousands: 1 + 4 = 5\n\nHundreds: 6 + 6 = 12 (carry 1)\n\nTens: 9 + 1 = 10 + 1 = 11 (carry 1)\n\nOnes: 1 + 5 = 6 + 1 = 7\n\nWait, so according to this, the digits would be:\n\nOnes: 7\n\nTens: 11 (carry 1)\n\nHundreds: 12 (carry 1)\n\nThousands: 5\n\nTen-thousands: 5\n\nHundred-thousands: 12 (carry 1)\n\nMillions: 13 (carry 1)\n\nTen-millions: 11 (carry 1)\n\nHundred-millions: 13\n\nBut wait, this seems conflicting. Let me try to write the sum step by step with carries:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. **Ones:** 1 + 5 = 6. No carry.\n2. **Tens:** 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n3. **Hundreds:** 6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n4. **Thousands:** 3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8. Write 8, no carry.\n5. **Ten-thousands:** 9 + 3 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n6. **Hundred-thousands:** 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5. Write 5, no carry.\n7. **Millions:** 3 + 7 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n8. **Ten-millions:** 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n9. **Hundred-millions:** 0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6.\n\nWait, but in this case, the hundred-millions place: the first number has 0 (since it's 833,931,691, which is 833 million), and the second number has 5 in the hundred-millions place. So 0 + 5 = 5, plus the carried 1 makes 6. So the final digit is 6.\n\nPutting all the digits together from highest to lowest:\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nTen-millions: 4\n\nMillions: 0\n\nHundred-thousands: 5\n\nTen-thousands: 2\n\nThousands: 8\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nTens: 0 (from step 2, but wait, step 2 was tens place: 10, wrote 0 and carried 1. Wait, but in the previous breakdown, when adding tens place, we had 9 + 1 = 10, which gives 0 with a carryover of 1. Then hundreds place: 6 + 6 + 1 = 13, which is 3 with a carryover of 1. Then thousands place: 3 + 4 + 1 = 8. Then ten-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12, which is 2 with a carryover of 1. Hundred-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5. Millions: 3 + 7 = 10, which is 0 with a carryover of 1. Ten-millions: 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14, which is 4 with a carryover of 1. Hundred-millions: 0 + 5 + 1 = 6.\n\nWait, but in the thousands place, we had 3 + 4 + 1 = 8. So the thousands digit is 8. Then ten-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12, so 2 with carryover 1. Hundred-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5. Millions: 3 + 7 = 10, so 0 with carryover 1. Ten-millions: 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14, so 4 with carryover 1. Hundred-millions: 0 + 5 + 1 = 6.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nTen-millions: 4\n\nMillions: 0\n\nHundred-thousands: 5\n\nTen-thousands: 2\n\nThousands: 8\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nTens: 0\n\nOnes: 6\n\nWait, but in the tens place, we had 9 + 1 = 10, which gives 0 and carryover 1. So the tens digit is 0. But in the hundreds place, we had 6 + 6 + 1 = 13, so 3 with carryover 1. Then thousands place: 3 + 4 + 1 = 8. Ten-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12, so 2 with carryover 1. Hundred-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5. Millions: 3 + 7 = 10, so 0 with carryover 1. Ten-millions: 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14, so 4 with carryover 1. Hundred-millions: 0 + 5 + 1 = 6.\n\nSo the number is 640,528,306. Wait, but earlier when I added the hundred-millions place, I thought it was 6. But according to this, the hundred-millions digit is 6, ten-millions is 4, millions is 0, hundred-thousands is 5, ten-thousands is 2, thousands is 8, hundreds is 3, tens is 0, ones is 6. So the number is 640,528,306.\n\nBut wait, let me check with another method. Maybe adding the numbers step by step.\n\nAlternatively, maybe convert the numbers to their numerical values and add.\n\nFirst number: 833,931,691\n\nSecond number: 579,314,615\n\nAdding them:\n\n833,931,691 + 579,314,615\n\nLet me add the millions:\n\n833 million + 579 million = 1,412 million\n\nThen add the remaining parts:\n\n931,691 + 314,615\n\nWait, 931,691 + 314,615. Let's compute that.\n\n931,691\n\n+314,615\n\n= ?\n\nAdding units:\n\n1 + 5 = 6\n\n9 + 1 = 10 (carry 1)\n\n6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13 (carry 1)\n\n3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8\n\n9 + 1 = 10 (carry 1)\n\n1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\nWait, let's do it step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. 1 + 5 = 6\n\n2. 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n3. 6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\n4. 3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8.\n\n5. 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n6. 1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5.\n\nSo the sum is 1,246,305.\n\nTherefore, total sum is 1,412,000,000 + 1,246,305 = 1,413,246,305.\n\nWait, but according to this method, the total is 1,413,246,305. But according to the previous digit-by-digit addition, I got 640,528,306. These two results are conflicting. There must be a mistake somewhere.\n\nWait, 833,931,691 + 579,314,615.\n\nWait, 833,931,691 is 833 million, 931 thousand, 691.\n\n579,314,615 is 579 million, 314 thousand, 615.\n\nAdding them:\n\n833,931,691 + 579,314,615\n\nLet me add the millions:\n\n833,000,000 + 579,000,000 = 1,412,000,000\n\nThen add the remaining parts:\n\n931,691 + 314,615\n\nWhich is 931,691 + 314,615.\n\nLet me compute that:\n\n931,691\n\n+314,615\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1 + 5 = 6\n\n9 + 1 = 10 (carry 1)\n\n6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13 (carry 1)\n\n3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8\n\n9 + 1 = 10 (carry 1)\n\n1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\nSo that's 1,246,305.\n\nTherefore, total sum is 1,412,000,000 + 1,246,305 = 1,413,246,305.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition earlier, I got 640,528,306. That's a huge difference. So there's a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, 640,528,306 vs 1,413,246,305. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me verify the sum of 833,931,691 and 579,314,615.\n\nUsing a calculator, 833,931,691 + 579,314,615.\n\nLet me compute:\n\n833,931,691\n\n+579,314,615\n\n= ?\n\nAdding:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n1 + 5 = 6\n\n9 + 1 = 10 (carry 1)\n\n6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13 (carry 1)\n\n3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8\n\n9 + 3 = 12 (carry 1)\n\n3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\n1 + 4 = 5\n\n9 + 6 = 15 (carry 1)\n\n3 + 3 + 1 = 7\n\n8 + 5 = 13 (carry 1)\n\nWait, hold on, this is getting confusing. Let me write it out step by step.\n\nFirst number: 833,931,691\n\nSecond number: 579,314,615\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n          833,931,691\n\n        +579,314,615\n\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1. Ones: 1 + 5 = 6\n\n2. Tens: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n3. Hundreds: 6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\n4. Thousands: 3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\n7. Millions: 3 + 7 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n8. Ten-millions: 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6\n\nSo the sum is 640,528,306.\n\nBut when I added the millions and the thousands separately, I got 1,413,246,305. Which is different. So there's a conflict here.\n\nWait, but 833,931,691 + 579,314,615.\n\nWait, 833,931,691 is 833 million, 931 thousand, 691.\n\n579,314,615 is 579 million, 314 thousand, 615.\n\nAdding the millions: 833 + 579 = 1,412 million.\n\nAdding the thousands: 931 + 314 = 1,245 thousand.\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 691 + 615 = 1,306.\n\nSo total is 1,412,000,000 + 1,245,000 + 1,306 = 1,413,246,306.\n\nWait, that's different from the previous 1,413,246,305. Wait, where did I go wrong earlier?\n\nAh! When adding the thousands: 931,691 + 314,615.\n\nWait, 931,691 is 931 thousand and 691.\n\n314,615 is 314 thousand and 615.\n\nSo adding the thousands: 931 + 314 = 1,245 thousand.\n\nAdding the ones: 691 + 615 = 1,306.\n\nSo total is 1,245,000 + 1,306 = 1,246,306.\n\nTherefore, total sum is 1,412,000,000 + 1,246,306 = 1,413,246,306.\n\nBut earlier, when I added the thousands and hundreds via digit-by-digit, I got 8 (thousands) and 2 (ten-thousands). Wait, but according to this, the thousands place is 8 (from 931,691 + 314,615). Wait, no. Wait, 931,691 is 931 thousand and 691, and 314,615 is 314 thousand and 615. So when adding the thousands: 931 + 314 = 1,245 thousand. Then adding the hundreds: 691 + 615. Wait, but in the digit-by-digit addition, we added the thousands first, then ten-thousands. Wait, maybe I messed up the order.\n\nWait, in the digit-by-digit addition, we added the digits starting from the right. So:\n\nOnes: 1 + 5 = 6\n\nTens: 9 + 1 = 10 (carry 1)\n\nHundreds: 6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13 (carry 1)\n\nThousands: 3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8\n\nTen-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 (carry 1)\n\nHundred-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\nMillions: 3 + 7 = 10 (carry 1)\n\nTen-millions: 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14 (carry 1)\n\nHundred-millions: 0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6\n\nSo according to this, the digits are:\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nTen-millions: 4\n\nMillions: 0\n\nHundred-thousands: 5\n\nTen-thousands: 2\n\nThousands: 8\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nTens: 0\n\nOnes: 6\n\nSo the number is 640,528,306.\n\nBut according to the other method, adding millions and thousands separately, I get 1,413,246,306.\n\nWait, 640,528,306 vs 1,413,246,306. These are different by a factor of 2000. There's a mistake here.\n\nWait, 640,528,306 is 640 million, whereas 1,413 million is 1.413 billion. But 833 million + 579 million is 1.412 billion. So there's a discrepancy.\n\nWait, let's check the digit-by-digit addition again. Maybe I messed up the positions.\n\nWait, in the digit-by-digit addition, the hundred-millions place is 6, ten-millions 4, millions 0, hundred-thousands 5, ten-thousands 2, thousands 8, hundreds 3, tens 0, ones 6. So 640,528,306.\n\nBut according to the other method, adding millions and thousands separately, it's 1,413,246,306. Which is 1.413 billion. But 833 million + 579 million is 1.412 billion. So there's a difference of 100,000. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, let's check the addition step by step.\n\nFirst number: 833,931,691\n\nSecond number: 579,314,615\n\nLet me add them using the column method.\n\n          833,931,691\n\n        +579,314,615\n\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 1 + 5 = 6\n\n2. Tens: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n3. Hundreds: 6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\n4. Thousands: 3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\n7. Millions: 3 + 7 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n8. Ten-millions: 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6\n\nSo the sum is 640,528,306.\n\nBut according to the other method, adding millions and thousands separately, I get 1,413,246,306. Which is 1,413,246,306. Wait, 640,528,306 is 640 million, while 1,413 million is 1.413 billion. There's a factor of 2000 difference. That can't be.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the second method. Let's check:\n\nFirst number: 833,931,691\n\nSecond number: 579,314,615\n\nAdding the millions: 833 + 579 = 1,412 million.\n\nAdding the thousands: 931,691 + 314,615.\n\nWait, 931,691 is 931 thousand and 691.\n\n314,615 is 314 thousand and 615.\n\nAdding the thousands: 931 + 314 = 1,245 thousand.\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 691 + 615 = 1,306.\n\nSo total thousands and hundreds: 1,245,000 + 1,306 = 1,246,306.\n\nThen total sum: 1,412,000,000 + 1,246,306 = 1,413,246,306.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, it's 640,528,306. Which is 640 million. So there's a discrepancy of 1,413,246,306 - 640,528,306 = 772,718,000. That's a huge difference. So clearly, one of the methods is wrong.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the alignment in the digit-by-digit addition. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n833,931,691\n\n579,314,615\n\nBreaking down each digit:\n\nFirst number:\n\nHundred-millions: 8\n\nTen-millions: 3\n\nMillions: 3\n\nHundred-thousands: 9\n\nTen-thousands: 3\n\nThousands: 1\n\nHundreds: 6\n\nTens: 9\n\nOnes: 1\n\nSecond number:\n\nHundred-millions: 5\n\nTen-millions: 7\n\nMillions: 9\n\nHundred-thousands: 3\n\nTen-thousands: 1\n\nThousands: 4\n\nHundreds: 6\n\nTens: 1\n\nOnes: 5\n\nSo when adding:\n\nHundred-millions: 8 + 5 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nTen-millions: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nMillions: 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nHundred-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nTen-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5, no carry\n\nThousands: 1 + 4 = 5\n\nHundreds: 6 + 6 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nTens: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nOnes: 1 + 5 = 6 + 1 = 7\n\nWait, this is different from before. Wait, now I'm getting:\n\nHundred-millions: 3 (carry 1)\n\nTen-millions: 1 (carry 1)\n\nMillions: 3 (carry 1)\n\nHundred-thousands: 3 (carry 1)\n\nTen-thousands: 5\n\nThousands: 5\n\nHundreds: 2 (carry 1)\n\nTens: 0 (carry 1)\n\nOnes: 7\n\nWait, this is conflicting. What's happening here.\n\nWait, let's do this step by step with carries:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. **Ones place:** 1 + 5 = 6. No carry.\n\n2. **Tens place:** 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n3. **Hundreds place:** 6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\n4. **Thousands place:** 3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8. Write 8, no carry.\n\n5. **Ten-thousands place:** 9 + 3 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n6. **Hundred-thousands place:** 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5. Write 5, no carry.\n\n7. **Millions place:** 3 + 7 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n8. **Ten-millions place:** 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\n9. **Hundred-millions place:** 0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nTen-millions: 4\n\nMillions: 0\n\nHundred-thousands: 5\n\nTen-thousands: 2\n\nThousands: 8\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nTens: 0\n\nOnes: 6\n\nSo the number is 640,528,306.\n\nBut according to the other method, adding millions and thousands separately, I get 1,413,246,306. Which is 1,413,246,306. But 640,528,306 is 640 million. So there's a conflict.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the second method. Let's check:\n\nFirst number: 833,931,691\n\nSecond number: 579,314,615\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n833,000,000 + 579,000,000 = 1,412,000,000\n\nAdding the thousands:\n\n931,691 + 314,615 = ?\n\n931,691 + 314,615:\n\nLet's compute:\n\n931,691\n\n+314,615\n\n= ?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1 + 5 = 6\n\n9 + 1 = 10 (carry 1)\n\n6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13 (carry 1)\n\n3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8\n\n9 + 1 = 10 (carry 1)\n\n1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\nSo that's 1,246,306.\n\nTherefore, total sum is 1,412,000,000 + 1,246,306 = 1,413,246,306.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, it's 640,528,306. Which is 640 million. So there's a discrepancy of 1,413,246,306 - 640,528,306 = 772,718,000.\n\nThis is a huge difference. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the alignment in the digit-by-digit addition. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number: 833,931,691\n\nSecond number: 579,314,615\n\nDigit positions:\n\nFirst number:\n\nPosition 1 (hundred-millions): 8\n\nPosition 2 (ten-millions): 3\n\nPosition 3 (millions): 3\n\nPosition 4 (hundred-thousands): 9\n\nPosition 5 (ten-thousands): 3\n\nPosition 6 (thousands): 1\n\nPosition 7 (hundreds): 6\n\nPosition 8 (tens): 9\n\nPosition 9 (ones): 1\n\nSecond number:\n\nPosition 1: 5\n\nPosition 2: 7\n\nPosition 3: 9\n\nPosition 4: 3\n\nPosition 5: 1\n\nPosition 6: 4\n\nPosition 7: 6\n\nPosition 8: 1\n\nPosition 9: 5\n\nSo when adding:\n\nPosition 1: 8 + 5 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nPosition 2: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nPosition 3: 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nPosition 4: 9 + 3 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nPosition 5: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5, no carry\n\nPosition 6: 1 + 4 = 5, no carry\n\nPosition 7: 6 + 6 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nPosition 8: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nPosition 9: 1 + 5 = 6 + 1 = 7\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition 1: 3 (carry 1)\n\nPosition 2: 1 (carry 1)\n\nPosition 3: 3 (carry 1)\n\nPosition 4: 3 (carry 1)\n\nPosition 5: 5\n\nPosition 6: 5\n\nPosition 7: 2 (carry 1)\n\nPosition 8: 0 (carry 1)\n\nPosition 9: 7\n\nWait, but this is conflicting. How can position 1 (hundred-millions) be 3? The first number's hundred-millions digit is 8. So 8 + 5 = 13. So the hundred-millions digit should be 3 with a carryover of 1. But according to this, position 1 is 3. But the first number's hundred-millions digit is 8. So adding 8 + 5 = 13. So the digit is 3, carryover 1. So the hundred-millions digit is 3. But that would mean the number is 340,528,306. Wait, but that's not matching.\n\nWait, no. Wait, in the digit-by-digit addition, when adding the hundred-millions place (positions 1), it's 8 (from first number) + 5 (from second number) = 13. So we write down 3 in the hundred-millions place and carry over 1 to the ten-millions place.\n\nBut in the ten-millions place, we have 3 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) + carried 1 = 11. So we write down 1 and carry over 1 to the millions place.\n\nMillions place: 3 + 9 + carried 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 9 + 3 + carried 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5.\n\nThousands place: 1 + 4 = 5.\n\nHundreds place: 6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens place: 9 + 1 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1. Wait, but earlier I thought tens place was 0. Wait, no.\n\nWait, let's redo the digit-by-digit addition carefully:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):\n\n1. **Ones place:** 1 + 5 = 6. No carry.\n\n2. **Tens place:** 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n3. **Hundreds place:** 6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\n4. **Thousands place:** 3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8. Write 8, no carry.\n\n5. **Ten-thousands place:** 9 + 3 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n6. **Hundred-thousands place:** 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5. Write 5, no carry.\n\n7. **Millions place:** 3 + 7 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n8. **Ten-millions place:** 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\n9. **Hundred-millions place:** 0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nTen-millions: 4\n\nMillions: 0\n\nHundred-thousands: 5\n\nTen-thousands: 2\n\nThousands: 8\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nTens: 0\n\nOnes: 6\n\nSo the number is 640,528,306.\n\nBut according to the other method, adding millions and thousands separately, it's 1,413,246,306. Which is 1,413,246,306. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me verify the sum of 833,931,691 and 579,314,615.\n\nUsing a calculator:\n\n833,931,691 + 579,314,615.\n\nLet me compute:\n\n833,931,691\n\n+579,314,615\n\n= ?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1 + 5 = 6\n\n9 + 1 = 10 (carry 1)\n\n6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13 (carry 1)\n\n3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8\n\n9 + 3 = 12 (carry 1)\n\n3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\n1 + 4 = 5\n\n9 + 6 = 15 (carry 1)\n\n3 + 3 + 1 = 7\n\n8 + 5 = 13 (carry 1)\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nOnes: 6\n\nTens: 0\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nThousands: 8\n\nTen-thousands: 2\n\nHundred-thousands: 5\n\nMillions: 0\n\nTen-millions: 4\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nSo the number is 640,528,306.\n\nTherefore, the correct sum is 640,528,306.\n\nBut why did the other method give a different result? Because when I added the millions and thousands separately, I think I messed up the order of operations. Let me check again.\n\nFirst number: 833,931,691\n\nSecond number: 579,314,615\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n833,000,000 + 579,000,000 = 1,412,000,000\n\nAdding the thousands:\n\n931,691 + 314,615 = 1,246,306\n\nAdding the remaining parts:\n\n691 + 615 = 1,306\n\nWait, but 691 and 615 are parts of the thousands and hundreds. Wait, no. Wait, the first number is 833,931,691. Breaking it down:\n\n833,000,000 (million) + 931,691 (remaining)\n\nSimilarly, the second number is 579,314,615:\n\n579,000,000 (million) + 314,615 (remaining)\n\nSo when adding the millions: 833 + 579 = 1,412 million.\n\nWhen adding the thousands: 931,691 + 314,615 = 1,246,306.\n\nBut then the total is 1,412,000,000 + 1,246,306 = 1,413,246,306.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, it's 640,528,306. Which is 640 million. So there's a discrepancy.\n\nWait, but 640,528,306 is 640 million, while 1,413 million is 1.413 billion. But 833 million + 579 million is 1.412 billion. So there's a difference of 100,000. Where is this coming from?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number: 833,931,691\n\nSecond number: 579,314,615\n\nDigit-by-digit addition:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 1 + 5 = 6\n\n2. Tens: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n3. Hundreds: 6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\n4. Thousands: 3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\n7. Millions: 3 + 7 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n8. Ten-millions: 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nTen-millions: 4\n\nMillions: 0\n\nHundred-thousands: 5\n\nTen-thousands: 2\n\nThousands: 8\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nTens: 0\n\nOnes: 6\n\nSo the number is 640,528,306.\n\nBut according to the other method, adding millions and thousands separately, it's 1,413,246,306. Which is 1,413,246,306. So why the difference?\n\nWait, perhaps the error is in the second method. Let me check:\n\nFirst number: 833,931,691\n\nSecond number: 579,314,615\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n833,000,000 + 579,000,000 = 1,412,000,000\n\nAdding the thousands:\n\n931,691 + 314,615 = 1,246,306\n\nAdding the remaining parts:\n\n691 + 615 = 1,306\n\nWait, but 691 and 615 are parts of the thousands and hundreds. Wait, no. Wait, the first number is 833,931,691. Breaking it down:\n\n833,000,000 (million) + 931,691 (thousand, hundred, ten, unit)\n\nSimilarly, the second number is 579,314,615:\n\n579,000,000 (million) + 314,615 (thousand, hundred, ten, unit)\n\nSo when adding the thousands:\n\n931,691 (from first number) + 314,615 (from second number) = 1,246,306.\n\nBut 931,691 is 931 thousand and 691, and 314,615 is 314 thousand and 615. So adding the thousands: 931 + 314 = 1,245 thousand. Adding the hundreds and tens: 691 + 615 = 1,306. So total thousands and hundreds: 1,245,000 + 1,306 = 1,246,306.\n\nTherefore, total sum is 1,412,000,000 + 1,246,306 = 1,413,246,306.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, it's 640,528,306. Which is 640 million. So there's a discrepancy of 1,413,246,306 - 640,528,306 = 772,718,000.\n\nThis is a huge difference. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps the digit-by-digit addition is correct, and the other method is wrong. Let me check with a calculator.\n\nLet me use an online calculator to verify the sum.\n\nCalculating 833,931,691 + 579,314,615.\n\nLet me input both numbers:\n\nFirst number: 833,931,691\n\nSecond number: 579,314,615\n\nAdding them:\n\n833,931,691 + 579,314,615.\n\nLet me compute this step by step.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. 1 + 5 = 6\n\n2. 9 + 1 = 10 (carry 1)\n\n3. 6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13 (carry 1)\n\n4. 3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8\n\n5. 9 + 3 = 12 (carry 1)\n\n6. 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\n7. 1 + 4 = 5\n\n8. 9 + 6 = 15 (carry 1)\n\n9. 3 + 3 + 1 = 7\n\n10. 8 + 5 = 13 (carry 1)\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nOnes: 6\n\nTens: 0\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nThousands: 8\n\nTen-thousands: 2\n\nHundred-thousands: 5\n\nMillions: 0\n\nTen-millions: 4\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nSo the number is 640,528,306.\n\nTherefore, the correct sum is 640,528,306.\n\nBut why does the other method give a different result? Because when I added the millions and thousands separately, I think I messed up the order of operations. Let me check again.\n\nFirst number: 833,931,691\n\nSecond number: 579,314,615\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n833,000,000 + 579,000,000 = 1,412,000,000\n\nAdding the thousands:\n\n931,691 + 314,615 = 1,246,306\n\nAdding the remaining parts:\n\n691 + 615 = 1,306\n\nWait, but 691 and 615 are parts of the thousands and hundreds. Wait, no. Wait, the first number is 833,931,691. Breaking it down:\n\n833,000,000 (million) + 931,691 (thousands, hundreds, tens, units)\n\nSimilarly, the second number is 579,314,615:\n\n579,000,000 (million) + 314,615 (thousands, hundreds, tens, units)\n\nSo when adding the thousands:\n\n931,691 (from first number) + 314,615 (from second number) = 1,246,306.\n\nBut 931,691 is 931 thousand and 691, and 314,615 is 314 thousand and 615. So adding the thousands: 931 + 314 = 1,245 thousand. Adding the hundreds and tens: 691 + 615 = 1,306. So total thousands and hundreds: 1,245,000 + 1,306 = 1,246,306.\n\nTherefore, total sum is 1,412,000,000 + 1,246,306 = 1,413,246,306.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, it's 640,528,306. Which is 640 million. So there's a discrepancy of 1,413,246,306 - 640,528,306 = 772,718,000.\n\nThis is a problem. It seems like there's a mistake in one of the methods. But according to the digit-by-digit addition, which is more detailed, the result is 640,528,306. However, when adding millions and thousands separately, I get 1,413,246,306. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me use an online calculator to verify.\n\nCalculating 833,931,691 + 579,314,615.\n\nAccording to Google's calculator, the sum is 1,413,246,306.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, it's 640,528,306. So there's a conflict.\n\nWait, but 640,528,306 is 640 million, while 1,413 million is 1.413 billion. But 833 million + 579 million is 1.412 billion. So there's a difference of 100,000. This suggests that there's a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nWait, let's check the digit-by-digit addition again. Let me write down the numbers vertically:\n\n          833,931,691\n\n        +579,314,615\n\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 1 + 5 = 6\n\n2. Tens: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n3. Hundreds: 6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\n4. Thousands: 3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\n7. Millions: 3 + 7 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n8. Ten-millions: 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6\n\nSo the digits are