ing. How can hundred-millions be 3? The first number's hundred-millions digit is 8. Adding 8 + 5 = 13. So the hundred-millions digit should be 3, carry 1. But according to this, the hundred-millions digit is 3, but the ten-millions digit is also 3. That can't be.\n\nWait, no. Wait, in the digit-by-digit addition, when adding the hundred-millions place (position 9), it's 8 (from first number) + 5 (from second number) = 13. So we write down 3 in the hundred-millions place and carry over 1 to the ten-millions place.\n\nThen, in the ten-millions place (position 8), we have 3 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) + carried 1 = 11. So we write down 1 in the ten-millions place and carry over 1 to the millions place.\n\nMillions place (position 7): 3 + 9 + carried 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nHundred-thousands place (position 6): 9 + 3 + carried 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTen-thousands place (position 5): 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5.\n\nThousands place (position 4): 3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8.\n\nHundreds place (position 3): 6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens place (position 2): 9 + 1 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nOnes place (position 1): 1 + 5 = 6 + 1 = 7.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nHundred-millions: 3 (carry 1)\n\nTen-millions: 1 (carry 1)\n\nMillions: 3 (carry 1)\n\nHundred-thousands: 3 (carry 1)\n\nTen-thousands: 5\n\nThousands: 8\n\nHundreds: 3 (carry 1)\n\nTens: 1 (carry 1)\n\nOnes: 7\n\nWait, but this is impossible. How can there be multiple carries over? Each carry affects the next higher digit. So when adding the hundred-millions place, we have a carryover of 1 to the ten-millions place. Then adding the ten-millions place, we have a carryover of 1 to the millions place. Similarly, adding the millions place, carryover to hundred-thousands, etc. But in reality, each carryover affects the next digit to the left. So the carries should propagate through each digit.\n\nBut in the digit-by-digit addition, we process each digit from right to left, but the carries aff