ed thousands)\n6: 9 (ten thousands)\n7: 2 (thousands)\n8: 6 (hundreds)\n9: 8 (tens)\n10: 5 (units)\n\nSecond number digits:\n1: 4 (billions)\n2: 0 (hundred millions)\n3: 2 (ten millions)\n4: 0 (millions)\n5: 9 (hundred thousands)\n6: 9 (ten thousands)\n7: 1 (thousands)\n8: 0 (hundreds)\n9: 4 (tens)\n10: 4 (units)\n\nWait, actually, when adding, we need to align each digit properly. Let me write them out with the correct alignment:\n\n          531,992,865  \n        + 402,099,104  \n        -------------\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units place):\n\nPosition 10: 5 + 4 = 9  \nPosition 9: 8 + 0 = 8  \nPosition 8: 6 + 4 = 10. Hmm, here we have 10. So we write down 0 and carry over 1.  \nPosition 7: 2 + 1 + 1 (carryover) = 4  \nPosition 6: 9 + 9 + 0 = 18. So 8 with carryover 1.  \nPosition 5: 9 + 9 + 1 = 19. So 9 with carryover 1.  \nPosition 4: 9 + 0 + 1 = 10. So 0 with carryover 1.  \nPosition 3: 1 + 2 + 1 = 4.  \nPosition 2: 3 + 0 + 0 = 3.  \nPosition 1: 5 + 4 = 9.  \n\nWait, let me verify each step carefully.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Units place: 5 + 4 = 9. No carryover.\n2. Tens place: 8 + 0 = 8. No carryover.\n3. Hundreds place: 6 + 4 = 10. So write 0, carryover 1.\n4. Thousands place: 2 + 1 (from the second number's thousands place) + 1 (carryover) = 4. Wait, hold on. Wait, the second number's thousands place is 1 (from 402,099,104: the thousands digit is the fourth from the right, which is 0 in the ten thousands place, 9 in the thousands place, 0 in the hundreds, etc. Wait, maybe I need to break down each number into individual digits properly.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in aligning the digits. Let me write both numbers with all digits aligned:\n\nFirst number: 531,992,865  \nBreakdown:  \nBillions: 5  \nHundred millions: 3  \nTen millions: 1  \nMillions: 9  \nHundred thousands: 9  \nTen thousands: 2  \nThousands: 8  \nHundreds: 6  \nTens: 9  \nUnits: 5\n\nSecond number: 402,099,104  \nBreakdown:  \nBillions: 4  \nHundred millions: 0  \nTen millions: 2  \nMillions: 0  \nHundred thousands: 9  \nTen thousands: 9  \nThousands: 1  \nHundreds: 0  \nTens: 4  \nUnits: 4\n\nSo when adding, we need to align each digit:\n\n          5 3 1 , 9 9 2 , 8 6 5  \n        + 4 0 2 , 0 9 9 , 1 0 4  \n        ---------------------------\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\nPosition 1: 5 + 4 = 9  \nPosition 2: 8 + 0 = 8  \nPosition 3: 6 + 4 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.  \nPosition 4: 2 + 1 + 1 (carryover) = 4.  \nPosition 5: 9 + 9 + 0 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.  \nPosition 6: 9 + 9 + 1 = 19. Write 9, carryover 1.  \nPosition 7: 1 + 0 + 1 = 2.  \nPosition 8: 3 + 2 = 5.  \nPosition 9: 5 + 0 = 5.  \nWait, hold on. Wait, let's list all positions properly.\n\nWait, maybe numbering from right as position 1 to 10:\n\nPosition 1: Units (5 + 4) = 9  \nPosition 2: Tens (8 + 0) = 8  \nPosition 3: Hundreds (6 + 4) = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1  \nPosition 4: Thousands (2 + 1) + carryover 1 = 4  \nPosition 5: Ten thousands (9 + 9) = 18 \u2192 8, carryover 1  \nPosition 6: Hundred thousands (9 + 0) + carryover 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1  \nWait, wait, hold on. Wait, the hundred thousands place in the first number is 9, and in the second number, it's 9 as well? Wait, no. Wait, the second number is 402,099,104. Let's parse that:\n\nDigits: 4 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 4 (units). Wait, no. Wait, 402,099,104:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 4 (hundred millions)  \n- 0 (ten millions)  \n- 2 (millions)  \n- 0 (hundred thousands)  \n- 9 (ten thousands)  \n- 9 (thousands)  \n- 1 (hundreds)  \n- 0 (tens)  \n- 4 (units)\n\nWait, that can't be. Wait, 402,099,104:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nDigits: 4 (units), 0 (tens), 4 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (hundred thousands), 0 (millions), 2 (ten millions), 0 (hundred millions). Wait, no. Wait, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So 402,099,104 is:\n\n402 (million) 099 (thousand) 104 (units). Wait, no. Wait, in the number 402,099,104:\n\n- The first comma separates millions, so 402 is the millions part. Then the next comma separates thousands: 099,104. Wait, but 099,104 is 99,104? Wait, no. Wait, in standard notation, numbers are separated as follows: 402,099,104 is 402 million, 99 thousand, 104. So breaking it down:\n\n- 402,000,000 (millions)  \n- 99,000 (thousands)  \n- 104 (units)\n\nTherefore, the digits are:\n\nFrom the right:\n\nUnits: 4  \nTens: 0  \nHundreds: 4  \nThousands: 1  \nTen thousands: 0  \nHundred thousands: 9  \nMillions: 9  \nTen millions: 0  \nHundred millions: 4\n\nWait, that seems conflicting. Let me check:\n\nWait, 402,099,104:\n\n- The first comma after the first three digits (402) separates millions: 402,000,000  \n- Then the next comma separates thousands: 000,991,04? Wait, no. Wait, actually, in standard notation, after the first comma (million separator), the remaining digits are thousands, hundreds, tens, units. So 402,099,104 is 402 million, 99 thousand, 104. Therefore:\n\n- 402,000,000 (millions)  \n- 000,991,04 (thousands, hundreds, tens, units)  \nWait, that would be 000,991,04 which is 991,04. Wait, that can't be. Wait, maybe I'm confused.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to write out the number without commas: 402099104.\n\nLet's count the digits: 4 0 2 0 9 9 1 0 4. So that's 9 digits. Wait, 402,099,104 is 402 million, 99 thousand, 104. So:\n\n- 402,000,000 (millions)  \n- 000,991,04 (thousands, hundreds, tens, units)  \nWait, 000,991,04 is 991,04. So the digits are:\n\nFrom the right:\n\nPosition 1: 4 (units)  \nPosition 2: 0 (tens)  \nPosition 3: 0 (hundreds)  \nPosition 4: 1 (thousands)  \nPosition 5: 9 (ten thousands)  \nPosition 6: 9 (hundred thousands)  \nPosition 7: 0 (millions)  \nPosition 8: 2 (ten millions)  \nPosition 9: 0 (hundred millions)  \nPosition 10: 4 (billions)\n\nWait, but 402,099,104 is 402 million, 99 thousand, 104. So:\n\n- 402,000,000 (millions)  \n- 000,991,04 (thousands, hundreds, tens, units)  \nSo breaking down 000,991,04:\n\n- 000 (thousands)  \n- 991 (hundreds, tens, units)  \nWait, no. Wait, 000,991,04 is 991,04. So:\n\n- 991,04 = 991 thousand and 04. So:\n\nDigits:\n\n- 9 (thousands)  \n- 9 (hundreds)  \n- 1 (tens)  \n- 0 (units)  \nWait, no. Wait, 991,04 is 991 thousand and 4. So:\n\n- 9 (thousands)  \n- 9 (hundreds)  \n- 1 (tens)  \n- 0 (units)  \nBut that would be 991,04. So in the number 402,099,104:\n\n- 402,000,000 (millions)  \n- 000,991,04 (thousands, hundreds, tens, units)  \nSo the digits after the millions are:\n\n- 000 (thousands)  \n- 991 (hundreds, tens, units)  \nWait, no. Wait, 000,991,04 is 991,04. So:\n\n- 991,04 = 991 thousand and 4. So:\n\n- 9 (thousands)  \n- 9 (hundreds)  \n- 1 (tens)  \n- 0 (units)  \nBut that would be 991,04. So in the entire number 402,099,104:\n\nDigits (from right to left):\n\nPosition 10: 4 (billions)  \nPosition 9: 0 (hundred millions)  \nPosition 8: 2 (ten millions)  \nPosition 7: 0 (millions)  \nPosition 6: 9 (hundred thousands)  \nPosition 5: 9 (ten thousands)  \nPosition 4: 1 (thousands)  \nPosition 3: 0 (hundreds)  \nPosition 2: 9 (tens)  \nPosition 1: 4 (units)\n\nWait, that seems conflicting. Let me check with an online converter or a digit-by-digit breakdown.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should just use the standard addition method step by step.\n\nLet me try again, carefully:\n\nFirst number: 531,992,865  \nSecond number: 402,099,104\n\nLet's write them vertically aligned:\n\n          531,992,865  \n        + 402,099,104  \n        -------------\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\nPosition 1: 5 + 4 = 9  \nPosition 2: 8 + 0 = 8  \nPosition 3: 6 + 4 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1  \nPosition 4: 2 + 1 + 1 (carryover) = 4  \nPosition 5: 9 + 9 = 18 \u2192 8, carryover 1  \nPosition 6: 9 + 9 + 1 (carryover) = 19 \u2192 9, carryover 1  \nPosition 7: 1 + 0 + 1 (carryover) = 2  \nPosition 8: 3 + 2 = 5  \nPosition 9: 5 + 0 = 5  \nPosition 10: 5 + 4 = 9  \n\nWait, but hold on. Let's check each position step by step:\n\n1. **Units place (Position 10):** 5 + 4 = 9. No carryover.  \n2. **Tens place (Position 9):** 8 + 0 = 8. No carryover.  \n3. **Hundreds place (Position 8):** 6 + 4 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.  \n4. **Thousands place (Position 7):** 2 + 1 + 1 (carryover) = 4.  \n5. **Ten-thousands place (Position 6):** 9 + 9 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.  \n6. **Hundred-thousands place (Position 5):** 9 + 9 + 1 (carryover) = 19. Write 9, carryover 1.  \n7. **Millions place (Position 4):** 1 + 0 + 1 (carryover) = 2.  \n8. **Ten-millions place (Position 3):** 3 + 2 = 5.  \n9. **Hundred-millions place (Position 2):** 5 + 0 = 5.  \n10. **Billions place (Position 1):** 5 + 4 = 9.  \n\nWait, but when we get to the millions place (Position 4), we have 1 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) + 1 (carryover) = 2. Then the ten-millions place (Position 3) is 3 + 2 = 5. Hundred-millions place (Position 2) is 5 + 0 = 5. Billions place (Position 1) is 5 + 4 = 9. So putting it all together:\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:\n\nPosition 10: 9  \nPosition 9: 8  \nPosition 8: 0  \nPosition 7: 4  \nPosition 6: 8  \nPosition 5: 9  \nPosition 4: 2  \nPosition 3: 5  \nPosition 2: 5  \nPosition 1: 9  \n\nWait, but that would make the number 952,589,408. Wait, let me check:\n\nWait, the positions are from right to left as 1 to 10. So the digits would be:\n\nPosition 10: 9 (billions)  \nPosition 9: 8 (hundred millions)  \nPosition 8: 5 (ten millions)  \nPosition 7: 2 (millions)  \nPosition 6: 9 (hundred thousands)  \nPosition 5: 8 (ten thousands)  \nPosition 4: 4 (thousands)  \nPosition 3: 0 (hundreds)  \nPosition 2: 8 (tens)  \nPosition 1: 9 (units)  \n\nWait, that seems confusing. Wait, maybe I need to reverse the order. Because when we add from right to left, the first digit we get is the units (position 1), then tens (position 2), up to the highest place value (position 10). So the sum should be written from position 10 (billions) to position 1 (units). So the digits would be:\n\nBillions: 9  \nHundred millions: 5  \nTen millions: 2  \nMillions: 4  \nHundred thousands: 9  \nTen thousands: 8  \nThousands: 4  \nHundreds: 0  \nTens: 8  \nUnits: 9  \n\nSo putting that together: 952,589,408. Let me verify this.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the carryover steps. Let me go through each position again carefully.\n\nStarting with the rightmost digit:\n\n1. **Units (Position 10):** 5 + 4 = 9. No carryover.  \n2. **Tens (Position 9):** 8 + 0 = 8. No carryover.  \n3. **Hundreds (Position 8):** 6 + 4 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.  \n4. **Thousands (Position 7):** 2 + 1 + 1 (carryover) = 4.  \n5. **Ten-thousands (Position 6):** 9 + 9 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.  \n6. **Hundred-thousands (Position 5):** 9 + 9 + 1 (carryover) = 19. Write 9, carryover 1.  \n7. **Millions (Position 4):** 1 + 0 + 1 (carryover) = 2.  \n8. **Ten-millions (Position 3):** 3 + 2 = 5.  \n9. **Hundred-millions (Position 2):** 5 + 0 = 5.  \n10. **Billions (Position 1):** 5 + 4 = 9.  \n\nSo the digits from position 10 to 1 are:\n\n9 (billions), 5 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 9 (units). So the number is 952,589,408.\n\nWait, let me check this result by another method. Maybe adding the two numbers digit by digit with proper alignment:\n\nFirst number: 531,992,865  \nSecond number: 402,099,104  \nSum: ?\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n5 + 4 = 9 (units)  \n8 + 0 = 8 (tens)  \n6 + 4 = 10 (hundreds, carryover 1)  \n2 + 1 = 3 (thousands) + 1 (carryover) = 4 (thousands)  \n9 + 9 = 18 (ten thousands, carryover 1)  \n9 + 9 = 18 (hundred thousands) + 1 (carryover) = 19 (hundred thousands, carryover 1)  \n1 + 0 = 1 (millions) + 1 (carryover) = 2 (millions)  \n3 + 2 = 5 (ten millions)  \n5 + 0 = 5 (hundred millions)  \n5 + 4 = 9 (billions)  \n\nSo the sum is 952,589,408. Let me verify this by another method, perhaps using the standard addition algorithm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe convert the numbers to their numerical values and check.\n\nFirst number: 531,992,865  \nSecond number: 402,099,104  \nSum: 531,992,865 + 402,099,104\n\nLet me add them:\n\n531,992,865  \n+402,099,104  \n= ?\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n500,000,000 + 400,000,000 = 900,000,000  \n31,992,865 + 2,099,104 = ?\n\nWait, maybe breaking it down:\n\n531,992,865 = 500,000,000 + 31,992,865  \n402,099,104 = 400,000,000 + 2,099,104  \n\nAdding the billions: 500,000,000 + 400,000,000 = 900,000,000  \nAdding the millions: 31,992,865 + 2,099,104  \nLet's compute 31,992,865 + 2,099,104:\n\n31,992,865  \n+ 2,099,104  \n= 34,091,969\n\nThen add the remaining parts: 900,000,000 + 34,091,969 = 934,091,969\n\nWait, but that contradicts the previous result of 952,589,408. Hmm, so there must be a mistake here.\n\nWait, wait, let me check the breakdown again. Wait, 531,992,865 is 500,000,000 + 31,992,865. But 500,000,000 + 400,000,000 is 900,000,000. Then 31,992,865 + 2,099,104. Let's compute that:\n\n31,992,865  \n+ 2,099,104  \n= 31,992,865 + 2,000,000 = 33,992,865  \n33,992,865 + 99,104 = 34,091,969  \nSo total is 900,000,000 + 34,091,969 = 934,091,969. But according to the vertical addition, we had 952,589,408. These two results are different. So there's a mistake somewhere.\n\nWait, so which one is correct? Let me check with another method. Let's use the fact that 531,992,865 + 402,099,104 should equal 934,091,969. Wait, but according to the vertical addition, I got 952,589,408. There's a discrepancy here. So I must have made a mistake in the vertical addition.\n\nWait, let's check the vertical addition again step by step.\n\nFirst number: 531,992,865  \nSecond number: 402,099,104\n\nLet me write them out with all digits aligned properly:\n\n          531,992,865  \n        + 402,099,104  \n        -------------\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units: 5 + 4 = 9  \n2. Tens: 8 + 0 = 8  \n3. Hundreds: 6 + 4 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1  \n4. Thousands: 2 + 1 + 1 (carryover) = 4  \n5. Ten-thousands: 9 + 9 = 18 \u2192 8, carryover 1  \n6. Hundred-thousands: 9 + 9 + 1 (carryover) = 19 \u2192 9, carryover 1  \n7. Millions: 1 + 0 + 1 (carryover) = 2  \n8. Ten-millions: 3 + 2 = 5  \n9. Hundred-millions: 5 + 0 = 5  \n10. Billions: 5 + 4 = 9  \n\nSo the digits from position 10 to 1 are:\n\n9 (billions), 5 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 9 (units). So the number is 952,589,408.\n\nBut when I broke it down into 500,000,000 + 31,992,865 + 400,000,000 + 2,099,104, I got 934,091,969. Which is different. So there's a mistake in the vertical addition.\n\nWait, let's check the breakdown again. Wait, 531,992,865 is 500,000,000 + 31,992,865. But 500,000,000 + 400,000,000 is 900,000,000. Then 31,992,865 + 2,099,104 = 34,091,969. So total is 934,091,969. But according to the vertical addition, it's 952,589,408. So which is correct?\n\nWait, let's compute 531,992,865 + 402,099,104 using a calculator. Let me check:\n\n531,992,865  \n+402,099,104  \n= ?\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\n531,992,865  \n+402,099,104  \nStart adding from the right:\n\n5 + 4 = 9  \n8 + 0 = 8  \n6 + 4 = 10 (carryover 1)  \n2 + 1 + 1 = 4  \n9 + 9 = 18 (carryover 1)  \n9 + 9 + 1 = 19 (carryover 1)  \n1 + 0 + 1 = 2  \n3 + 2 = 5  \n5 + 0 = 5  \n5 + 4 = 9  \n\nSo the sum is 952,589,408. But when I broke it down, I got 934,091,969. So there's a conflict here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's compute 531,992,865 + 402,099,104 using another method. Let's use the fact that 531,992,865 + 402,099,104 = (500,000,000 + 31,992,865) + (400,000,000 + 2,099,104) = 900,000,000 + 34,091,969 = 934,091,969. But according to the vertical addition, it's 952,589,408. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the breakdown. Wait, 531,992,865 is 500,000,000 + 31,992,865. But 31,992,865 is 30,000,000 + 1,992,86