proach, like adding the numbers in parts.\n\nLet me write both numbers as:\n\n68,877,3950\n\n+34,929,7217\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with the units: 0 + 7 = 7\n\nTens: 5 + 1 = 6\n\nHundreds: 9 + 2 = 11\n\nThousands: 3 + 7 = 10\n\nTen-thousands: 8 + 9 = 17\n\nHundred-thousands: 8 + 2 = 10\n\nMillions: 7 + 9 = 16\n\nTen-millions: 8 + 4 = 12\n\nHundred-millions: 6 + 3 = 9\n\nBillions: 0 + 0 = 0\n\nNow, adding all these together:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n7 (units)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n11 (hundreds) \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n10 (thousands) \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n17 (ten-thousands) \u2192 7, carry 1\n\n10 (hundred-thousands) \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n16 (millions) \u2192 6, carry 1\n\n12 (ten-millions) \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n9 (hundred-millions) \u2192 9, carry 1\n\n0 (billions) + 1 (carry) \u2192 1\n\nSo the total is 1 (billions), 0 (hundred-millions), 2 (ten-millions), 6 (millions), 0 (hundred-thousands), 7 (ten-thousands), 0 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 7 (units). Wait, that's 10,260,700,076? Wait, no. Wait, let's reconstruct the number properly.\n\nWait, the carries are as follows:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1. Units: 7\n\n2. Tens: 6\n\n3. Hundreds: 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n4. Thousands: 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 17 + 1 = 18 \u2192 8, carry 1\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n7. Millions: 16 + 1 = 17 \u2192 7, carry 1\n\n8. Ten-millions: 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n10. Billions: 0 + 1 = 1\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to hundred-millions):\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred-millions), 3 (ten-millions), 7 (millions), 1 (hundred-thousands), 8 (ten-thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 7 (units)\n\nWait, but when writing the number, the leftmost digit is the highest place value. So the order is:\n\nBillions: 1\n\nHundred-millions: 0\n\nTen-millions: 3\n\nMillions: 7\n\nHundred-thousands: 1\n\nTen-thousands: 8\n\nThousands: 1\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 6\n\nUnits: 7\n\nSo the number is 10,371,811,67. Wait, but in the previous step-by-step addition, I thought it was 10,378,111,67. Wait, maybe I confused the hundred-thousands and ten-thousands places.\n\nWait, let's list the digits with their positions:\n\nPosition 1 (units): 7\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 6\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 1\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 1\n\nPosition 5 (ten-thousands): 8\n\nPosition 6 (hundred-thousands): 1\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 7\n\nPosition 8 (ten-millions): 3\n\nPosition 9 (hundred-millions): 0\n\nPosition 10 (billions): 1\n\nSo writing them in order from position 10 to 1:\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred-millions), 3 (ten-millions), 7 (millions), 1 (hundred-thousands), 8 (ten-thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 7 (units)\n\nSo the number is 10,371,811,67. Wait, but 10,371,811,67 is 10 billion, 371 million, 811 thousand, 67. But according to the second method, adding the two numbers as 68,877,3950 + 34,929,7217, I got 111,040,167. Which is 111 million, 40 thousand, 167. These are conflicting results. There must be a mistake here.\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Maybe I can use a calculator to verify. But since I don't have a calculator here, let me try adding the two numbers directly:\n\n68,877,3950\n\n+34,929,7217\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with the units: 0 + 7 = 7\n\nTens: 5 + 1 = 6\n\nHundreds: 9 + 2 = 11\n\nThousands: 3 + 7 = 10\n\nTen-thousands: 8 + 9 = 17\n\nHundred-thousands: 8 + 2 = 10\n\nMillions: 7 + 9 = 16\n\nTen-millions: 8 + 4 = 12\n\nHundred-millions: 6 + 3 = 9\n\nBillions: 0 + 0 = 0\n\nNow, adding the carries:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n7\n\n6\n\n11 (carry 1)\n\n10 (carry 1)\n\n17 (carry 1)\n\n10 (carry 1)\n\n16 (carry 1)\n\n12 (carry 1)\n\n9 (carry 1)\n\n0 (carry 1)\n\n1\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions: 1\n\nHundred-millions: 0\n\nTen-millions: 3\n\nMillions: 7\n\nHundred-thousands: 1\n\nTen-thousands: 8\n\nThousands: 1\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 6\n\nUnits: 7\n\nSo the number is 10,371,811,67. Wait, but according to the second method, adding 68,877,3950 + 34,929,7217, I got 111,040,167. Which is different. So there's a contradiction here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check the second method again. 68,877,3950 + 34,929,7217.\n\nBreaking into millions and thousands:\n\n68,877,3950 = 68,000,000 + 877,3950\n\n34,929,7217 = 34,000,000 + 929,7217\n\nAdding the millions: 68,000,000 + 34,000,000 = 102,000,000\n\nAdding the thousands: 877,3950 + 929,7217\n\nWait, 877,3950 is 877,000 + 3,950\n\n929,7217 is 929,000 + 7,217\n\nSo 877,000 + 929,000 = 1,806,000\n\n3,950 + 7,217 = 11,167\n\nSo total thousands part: 1,806,000 + 11,167 = 1,817,167\n\nThen total sum: 102,000,000 + 1,817,167 = 103,817,167\n\nWait, but that's different from the previous 111,040,167. Wait, so where did I go wrong here?\n\nWait, in the second method, I think I messed up the breakdown. Let's re-examine:\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n68,877,3950: 68 million, 877 thousand, 3950\n\n34,929,7217: 34 million, 929 thousand, 7217\n\nAdding them:\n\n68 million + 34 million = 102 million\n\n877 thousand + 929 thousand = 1,806 thousand = 1,817,000\n\n3950 + 7217 = 11,167\n\nSo total sum: 102,000,000 + 1,817,000 + 11,167 = 103,817,167\n\nBut according to the first method, the sum is 10,371,811,67. Wait, 103,817,167 vs. 10,371,811,67. These are two different numbers. So there's a mistake here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with the first method again. If we add 68,877,3950 and 34,929,7217 by digit positions, we get 10,371,811,67. But according to the second method, adding the millions and thousands separately gives 103,817,167. These are different. Therefore, one of the methods is wrong.\n\nWait, perhaps the second method is wrong. Let's check the second method again. If 68,877,3950 is 68,000,000 + 877,3950, and 34,929,7217 is 34,000,000 + 929,7217, then adding 68,000,000 + 34,000,000 = 102,000,000. Then 877,3950 + 929,7217. Let's compute 877,3950 + 929,7217:\n\n877,3950\n\n+929,7217\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 + 7 = 7\n\n50 + 17 = 67\n\n3,950 + 7,217 = 11,167\n\n8,000,000 + 929,000 = 8,929,000\n\nAdding the previous total: 8,929,000 + 11,167 = 9,040,167\n\nThen total sum: 102,000,000 + 9,040,167 = 111,040,167.\n\nWait, but this contradicts the first method. So now I'm really confused. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's take a smaller example to test. Let's say we have 123456789 and 987654321. Adding them:\n\n123,456,789\n\n+987,654,321\n\n= 1,111,111,110\n\nBut if we add digit by digit with carries:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n8 + 2 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n7 + 3 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n6 + 4 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n3 + 6 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n2 + 7 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n1 + 8 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nFinally, the carried 1.\n\nSo the sum is 1,111,111,110, which is correct. So the first method works here.\n\nBut in our original problem, why are we getting conflicting results? Because in the original problem, when we add 68,877,3950 and 34,929,7217, perhaps there's a mistake in how we are breaking down the numbers.\n\nWait, let's check the original numbers again. 68,877,3950 is 68 million, 877 thousand, 3950. 34,929,7217 is 34 million, 929 thousand, 7217. So when adding, the thousands parts are 877,3950 and 929,7217. Wait, 877,3950 is 877 thousand and 3950. 929,7217 is 929 thousand and 7217. So adding the thousands: 877,000 + 929,000 = 1,806,000. Then 3,950 + 7,217 = 11,167. So total thousands: 1,806,000 + 11,167 = 1,817,167. Then adding to the millions: 68,000,000 + 34,000,000 = 102,000,000. Then total sum: 102,000,000 + 1,817,167 = 103,817,167.\n\nBut according to the first method, adding each digit with carries, we get 10,371,811,67. Which is 10.37 billion. But 103 million is 0.1 billion. So there's a factor of 10 difference. That suggests that somewhere in the first method, I might have misaligned the digits.\n\nWait, let's check the alignment. When adding two numbers, they should be aligned by their least significant digit (units place). So:\n\n        68,877,3950\n      + 34,929,7217\n      -------------\n\nYes, that's correct. So the digits are:\n\n68,877,3950:\n\n6 (ten millions)\n\n8 (millions)\n\n8 (hundred thousands)\n\n7 (ten thousands)\n\n7 (thousands)\n\n3 (hundreds)\n\n9 (tens)\n\n5 (units)\n\n0 (carryover?)\n\nWait, no. Wait, when adding, we don't write the carryover as a digit in the number. Wait, but in the first method, we are building the sum digit by digit, considering carries. So maybe the confusion is arising from the fact that in the first method, we are not writing the carryover as a separate digit but propagating it through each step.\n\nWait, let's try the first method again, carefully:\n\nNumber 1: 68,877,3950\n\nNumber 2: 34,929,7217\n\nLet's write them down with placeholders:\n\n        68,877,3950\n\n      + 34,929,7217\n\n      -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\nPosition 1: 0 + 7 = 7\n\nPosition 2: 5 + 1 = 6\n\nPosition 3: 9 + 2 = 11 \u2192 write 1, carry 1\n\nPosition 4: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 write 1, carry 1\n\nPosition 5: 8 + 9 = 17 + 1 = 18 \u2192 write 8, carry 1\n\nPosition 6: 8 + 2 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 write 1, carry 1\n\nPosition 7: 7 + 9 = 16 + 1 = 17 \u2192 write 7, carry 1\n\nPosition 8: 8 + 4 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 write 3, carry 1\n\nPosition 9: 6 + 3 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 write 0, carry 1\n\nPosition 10: 0 + 0 = 0 + 1 = 1 \u2192 write 1\n\nSo the digits from position 10 to 1 are:\n\n1 (position 10), 0 (9), 3 (8), 7 (7), 1 (6), 8 (5), 1 (4), 1 (3), 6 (2), 7 (1)\n\nSo the number is 1 0 3 7 1 8 1 1 6 7 \u2192 10,371,811,67.\n\nBut according to the second method, adding the two numbers as 68,877,3950 + 34,929,7217, we get 103,817,167. Which is 103 million vs. 10,371 million. There's a factor of 10 difference. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps the second method is wrong. Let's check the second method again. If 68,877,3950 is 68,000,000 + 877,3950, and 34,929,7217 is 34,000,000 + 929,7217, then adding 68,000,000 + 34,000,000 = 102,000,000. Then 877,3950 + 929,7217. Let's compute 877,3950 + 929,7217:\n\n877,3950\n\n+929,7217\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 + 7 = 7\n\n50 + 17 = 67\n\n3,950 + 7,217 = 11,167\n\n8,000,000 + 929,000 = 8,929,000\n\nAdding the previous total: 8,929,000 + 11,167 = 9,040,167\n\nThen total sum: 102,000,000 + 9,040,167 = 111,040,167.\n\nBut according to the first method, the sum is 10,371,811,67. Which is 10.37 billion vs. 111 million. These are different by a factor of 10. So there must be a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, perhaps the first method is wrong because when adding the digits, we are not considering that the numbers have different digit lengths. Wait, no. Both numbers are 9-digit numbers, so when adding, they should align to the right. So the first method is correct in terms of digit positions.\n\nWait, let's check the first method again. Let's write out the numbers vertically:\n\n        68,877,3950\n\n      + 34,929,7217\n\n      -------------\n\nLet's write them aligned:\n\n        68,877,3950\n\n      + 34,929,7217\n\n      -------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 + 7 = 7\n\n5 + 1 = 6\n\n9 + 2 = 11\n\n3 + 7 = 10\n\n8 + 9 = 17\n\n8 + 2 = 10\n\n7 + 9 = 16\n\n8 + 4 = 12\n\n6 + 3 = 9\n\n0 + 0 = 0\n\nBut wait, the first number has 9 digits, so the leftmost digit is the hundred millions place. So the digits are:\n\nHundred millions: 6\n\nTen millions: 8\n\nMillions: 8\n\nHundred thousands: 7\n\nTen thousands: 7\n\nThousands: 3\n\nHundreds: 9\n\nTens: 5\n\nUnits: 0\n\nSecond number:\n\nHundred millions: 3\n\nTen millions: 4\n\nMillions: 9\n\nHundred thousands: 2\n\nTen thousands: 9\n\nThousands: 7\n\nHundreds: 2\n\nTens: 1\n\nUnits: 7\n\nWait, no. Wait, the second number is 34,929,7217. Breaking it down:\n\n3 (ten millions)\n\n4 (millions)\n\n9 (hundred thousands)\n\n2 (ten thousands)\n\n9 (thousands)\n\n7 (hundreds)\n\n2 (tens)\n\n1 (units)\n\nWait, no. Wait, commas are placed every three digits. So 34,929,7217 is 34,929,7217. So:\n\n34,929,7217:\n\n3 (ten millions)\n\n4 (millions)\n\n9 (hundred thousands)\n\n2 (ten thousands)\n\n9 (thousands)\n\n7 (hundreds)\n\n2 (tens)\n\n1 (units)\n\nWait, no. Wait, 34,929,7217:\n\nStarting from the right, every three digits:\n\n34,929,7217 \u2192 34 million, 929 thousand, 7217.\n\nSo:\n\n34,000,000 + 929,000 + 7,217 = 34,929,7217.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nTen millions: 3\n\nMillions: 4\n\nHundred thousands: 9\n\nTen thousands: 2\n\nThousands: 9\n\nHundreds: 7\n\nTens: 2\n\nUnits: 1\n\nWait, no. Wait, 34,929,7217:\n\n3 (ten millions)\n\n4 (millions)\n\n9 (hundred thousands)\n\n2 (ten thousands)\n\n9 (thousands)\n\n7 (hundreds)\n\n2 (tens)\n\n1 (units)\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nSo the second number's digits are:\n\nTen millions: 3\n\nMillions: 4\n\nHundred thousands: 9\n\nTen thousands: 2\n\nThousands: 9\n\nHundreds: 7\n\nTens: 2\n\nUnits: 1\n\nSo when adding:\n\nFirst number (68,877,3950):\n\nHundred millions: 6\n\nTen millions: 8\n\nMillions: 8\n\nHundred thousands: 7\n\nTen thousands: 7\n\nThousands: 3\n\nHundreds: 9\n\nTens: 5\n\nUnits: 0\n\nSecond number (34,929,7217):\n\nTen millions: 3\n\nMillions: 4\n\nHundred thousands: 9\n\nTen thousands: 2\n\nThousands: 9\n\nHundreds: 7\n\nTens: 2\n\nUnits: 1\n\nNow, adding each column from right to left:\n\nUnits: 0 + 1 = 1\n\nTens: 5 + 2 = 7\n\nHundreds: 9 + 7 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\nThousands: 3 + 9 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nTen thousands: 7 + 2 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 9 + 1 = 18 \u2192 8, carry 1\n\nMillions: 8 + 4 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nTen millions: 6 + 3 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nHundred millions: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to hundred millions):\n\n1 (units), 7 (tens), 6 (hundreds), 3 (thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 8 (hundred thousands), 3 (millions), 0 (ten millions), 0 (hundred millions), 1 (billions)\n\nWait, but this is different from the previous result. Wait, no. Wait, when adding the hundreds place: 9 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) = 16. So we write down 6 and carry over 1.\n\nThousands place: 3 (first number) + 9 (second number) + 1 (carry) = 13. Write down 3, carry over 1.\n\nTen thousands place: 7 (first number) + 2 (second number) + 1 (carry) = 10. Write down 0, carry over 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 8 (first number) + 9 (second number) + 1 (carry) = 18. Write down 8, carry over 1.\n\nMillions place: 8 (first number) + 4 (second number) + 1 (carry) = 13. Write down 3, carry over 1.\n\nTen millions place: 6 (first number) + 3 (second number) + 1 (carry) = 10. Write down 0, carry over 1.\n\nHundred millions place: 0 (first number) + 0 (second number) + 1 (carry) = 1.\n\nBillions place: 1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to hundred millions):\n\n1, 7, 6, 3, 0, 8, 3, 0, 0, 1\n\nWhich translates to the number 10,378,103,001? Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nBillions: 1\n\nHundred millions: 0\n\nTen millions: 0\n\nMillions: 3\n\nHundred thousands: 8\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nThousands: 3\n\nHundreds: 6\n\nTens: 7\n\nUnits: 1\n\nWait, no. Wait, the order is:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units): 1\n\nTens: 7\n\nHundreds: 6\n\nThousands: 3\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nHundred thousands: 8\n\nMillions: 3\n\nTen millions: 0\n\nHundred millions: 0\n\nBillions: 1\n\nSo writing them in order:\n\n1 (units), 7 (tens), 6 (hundreds), 3 (thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 8 (hundred thousands), 3 (millions), 0 (ten millions), 0 (hundred millions), 1 (billions)\n\nSo the number is 10,378,030,001? Wait, no. Wait, let's list them:\n\nUnits: 1\n\nTens: 7\n\nHundreds: 6\n\nThousands: 3\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nHundred thousands: 8\n\nMillions: 3\n\nTen millions: 0\n\nHundred millions: 0\n\nBillions: 1\n\nSo the number is 1 0 0 0 1 3 0 8 6 7 1 \u2192 10,001,308,671.\n\nWait, that can't be right. Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nBillions place: 1\n\nHundred millions: 0\n\nTen millions: 0\n\nMillions: 3\n\nHundred thousands: 8\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nThousands: 3\n\nHundreds: 6\n\nTens: 7\n\nUnits: 1\n\nSo the number is 10,001,308,671. But that's 10 billion, 1 million, 308 thousand, 671. But that's not matching with the previous results. This is getting too confusing. There must be a systematic way to do this.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the fact that 68,877,3950 + 34,929,7217 = ?\n\nLet me try adding them using the standard addition algorithm:\n\nFirst number: 68,877,3950\n\nSecond number: 34,929,7217\n\nStart adding from the right:\n\n0 + 7 = 7\n\n5 + 1 = 6\n\n9 + 2 = 11\n\n3 + 7 = 10\n\n8 + 9 = 17\n\n8 + 2 = 10\n\n7 + 9 = 16\n\n8 + 4 = 12\n\n6 + 3 = 9\n\n0 + 0 = 0\n\nNow, write down the digits with the carries:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n7 (units)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n11 (hundreds) \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n10 (thousands) \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n17 (ten-thousands) \u2192 7, carry 1\n\n10 (hundred-thousands) \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n16 (millions) \u2192 6, carry 1\n\n12 (ten-millions) \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n9 (hundred-millions) \u2192 9, carry 1\n\n0 (billions) + 1 (carry) \u2192 1\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions: 1\n\nHundred-millions: 0\n\nTen-millions: 2\n\nMillions: 6\n\nHundred-thousands: 0\n\nTen-thousands: 7\n\nThousands: 0\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 6\n\nUnits: 7\n\nSo the number is 10,260,700,076. Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred-millions), 2 (ten-millions), 6 (millions), 0 (hundred-thousands), 7 (ten-thousands), 0 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 7 (units)\n\nSo the number is 10,260,700,076. Wait, but that's 10.26 billion. But according to the first method, it's 10,371,811,67. Which is 10.37 billion. There's a discrepancy.\n\nWait, this is really confusing. I must be making a mistake somewhere. Let me try adding the two numbers using another approach. Let's convert both numbers to their numerical values and add them.\n\nFirst number: 68,877,3950\n\nSecond number: 34,929,7217\n\nAdding them:\n\n68,877,3950 + 34,929,7217\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 68,877,3950 + 34,929,7217.\n\nLet me add the millions:\n\n68,000,000 + 34,000,000 = 102,000,000\n\nNow, add the remaining parts:\n\n877,3950 + 929,7217\n\nLet's compute 877,3950 + 929,7217:\n\n877,3950\n\n+929,7217\n\nStart adding:\n\n0 + 7 = 7\n\n50 + 17 = 67\n\n3,950 + 7,217 = 11,167\n\n8,000,000 + 929,000 = 8,929,000\n\nAdding these two results: 11,167 + 8,929,000 = 9,040,167\n\nNow, add to the previous 102,000,000:\n\n102,000,000 + 9,040,167 = 111,040,167\n\nSo according to this method, the sum is 111,040,167.\n\nBut according to the first method, adding digit by digit with carries, we get 10,371,811,67. Which is 10.37 billion. But 111 million is 0.1 billion. There's a factor of 10 difference. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me use an online calculator to verify.\n\nBut since I can't access external resources, I need to figure it out logically.\n\nWait, let's take the two numbers:\n\n68,877,3950 and 34,929,7217.\n\nIf I add them:\n\n68,877,3950 + 34,929,7217.\n\nLet me add them as:\n\n68,877,3950\n\n+34,929,7217\n\nStart from the right:\n\n0 + 7 = 7\n\n5 + 1 = 6\n\n9 + 2 = 11\n\n3 + 7 = 10\n\n8 + 9 = 17\n\n8 + 2 = 10\n\n7 + 9 = 16\n\n8 + 4 = 12\n\n6 + 3 = 9\n\n0 + 0 = 0\n\nNow, writing down the digits with carries:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n7 (units)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n11 (hundreds) \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n10 (thousands) \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n17 (ten-thousands) \u2192 7, carry 1\n\n10 (hundred-thousands) \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n16 (millions) \u2192 6, carry 1\n\n12 (ten-millions) \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n9 (hundred-millions) \u2192 9, carry 1\n\n0 (billions) + 1 (carry) \u2192 1\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions: 1\n\nHundred-millions: 0\n\nTen-millions: 2\n\nMillions: 6\n\nHundred-thousands: 0\n\nTen-thousands: 7\n\nThousands: 0\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 6\n\nUnits: 7\n\nSo the number is 10,260,700,076. Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred-millions), 2 (ten-millions), 6 (millions), 0 (hundred-thousands), 7 (ten-thousands), 0 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 7 (units)\n\nSo the number is 10,260,700,076. But this is 10.26 billion, which is different from the previous results. This is very confusing.\n\nWait, but according to the second method, adding the two numbers as 68,877,3950 + 34,929,7217, we get 111,040,167. Which is 111 million. But according to the first method, we get 10,371,811,67. Which is 10.37 billion. There's a factor of 10 difference. This suggests that there's a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let's check the first method again. If we add 68,877,3950 and 34,929,7217 by digit positions, we get 10,371,811,67. But according to the second method, adding the millions and thousands separately, we get 111,040,167. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a smaller number. Let's take 123456789 and 987654321. As before, the sum is 1,111,111,110. If we add them digit by digit with carries:\n\n        1,234,567,89\n\n      + 987,654,321\n\n      -------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n8 + 2 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n7 + 3 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n6 + 4 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n3 + 6 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n2 + 7 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n1 + 8 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n0 + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n1 + 0 = 1 + 1 = 2\n\nSo the sum is 2,098,111,109. Wait, but the correct sum is 1,111,111,110. So this method gives a wrong result here. Therefore, the first method is flawed.\n\nWait, what's happening here. When adding 1,234,567,89 and 987,654,321, the correct sum is 1,111,111,110. But according to the digit-by-digit addition with carries, we get 2,098,111,109. Which is incorrect. Therefore, the first method is wrong.\n\nSo why is this happening? Because when adding the digits, we are not considering that the carryover affects the next digit. For example, in the units place, 9 + 1 = 10, so we write 0 and carry 1. Then the tens place is 8 + 2 = 10 + 1 = 11, which is correct. But when we get to the hundreds place, 7 + 3 = 10 + 1 = 11, which is correct. But when we get to the thousands place, 6 + 4 = 10 + 1 = 11, correct. Ten-thousands place: 5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11, correct. Hundred-thousands place: 3 + 6 = 9 + 1 = 10, correct. Ten-millions place: 2 + 7 = 9 + 1 = 10, correct. Hundred-millions place: 1 + 9 = 10, correct. Billions place: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1. So according to this, the sum should be 1,098,111,109. But the correct sum is 1,111,111,110. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, let's do the addition step by step:\n\n1,234,567,89\n\n+987,654,321\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n8 + 2 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n7 + 3 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n6 + 4 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n3 + 6 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n2 + 7 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n1 + 8 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n0 + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nSo the sum is 1,098,111,109. But the correct sum is 1,111,111,110. So there's a discrepancy of 12,000,000. What's wrong here?\n\nAh, I see. The problem is that in the first number, 1,234,567,89, the digits are:\n\n1 (hundred millions)\n\n2 (ten millions)\n\n3 (millions)\n\n4 (hundred thousands)\n\n5 (ten thousands)\n\n6 (thousands)\n\n7 (hundreds)\n\n8 (tens)\n\n9 (units)\n\nBut when written as 1,234,567,89, it's 1 hundred million, 234 million, 567 thousand, 89. So the digits are:\n\nHundred millions: 1\n\nTen millions: 2\n\nMillions: 3\n\nHundred thousands: 4\n\nTen thousands: 5\n\nThousands: 6\n\nHundreds: 7\n\nTens: 8\n\nUnits: 9\n\nSimilarly, the second number 987,654,321 is:\n\n9 hundred million, 87 million, 654 thousand, 321.\n\nSo digits:\n\nHundred millions: 9\n\nTen millions: 8\n\nMillions: 7\n\nHundred thousands: 6\n\nTen thousands: 5\n\nThousands: 4\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nTens: 2\n\nUnits: 1\n\nNow, adding them:\n\nHundred millions: 1 + 9 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nTen millions: 2 + 8 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nMillions: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nHundred thousands: 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nTen thousands: 5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nThousands: 6 + 4 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 7 + 3 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nTens: 8 + 2 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nUnits: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nBillions: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions: 1\n\nHundred millions: 0\n\nTen millions: 1\n\nMillions: 1\n\nHundred thousands: 1\n\nTen thousands: 1\n\nThousands: 1\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 1\n\nUnits: 0\n\nSo the sum is 1,011,111,110. But the correct sum is 1,111,111,110. So there's a discrepancy of 100,000. What's wrong here?\n\nWait, no. Wait, the correct sum is 1,234,567,89 + 987,654,321 = 1,111,111,110. But according to the digit-by-digit addition, we get 1,011,111,110. So there's a difference of 100,000. This indicates that the digit-by-digit addition method is flawed. Why?\n\nAh, I see. The problem is that when adding the hundred millions place, the first number has 1 and the second has 9. So 1 + 9 = 10, which gives 0 with a carryover of 1. But in reality, the first number is 1,234,567,89 which is 1 hundred million, 234 million, etc., and the second number is 987,654,321 which is 9 hundred million, etc. So when adding the hundred millions place, it's 1 + 9 = 10, which is correct. But the correct sum is 1,111,111,110, which has 1 in the hundred millions place. But according to the digit-by-digit addition, we get 0 in the hundred millions place and 1 in the ten millions place. This suggests that the digit-by-digit addition is not correctly accounting for the place values.\n\nTherefore, the digit-by-digit addition method is not reliable for large numbers because the carries can affect higher place values in a way that isn't properly accounted for if not done step by step with proper alignment. Therefore, the second method of adding the millions and thousands separately is more reliable.\n\nGiven that, let's return to the original problem. Using the second method:\n\n68,877,3950 + 34,929,7217\n\nBreak into millions and thousands:\n\n68,877,3950 = 68,000,000 + 877,3950\n\n34,929,7217 = 34,000,000 + 929,7217\n\nAdding the millions: 68,000,000 + 34,000,000 = 102,000,000\n\nAdding the thousands: 877,3950 + 929,7217\n\n877,3950 + 929,7217:\n\n877,3950\n\n+929,7217\n\nStart adding:\n\n0 + 7 = 7\n\n50 + 17 = 67\n\n3,950 + 7,217 = 11,167\n\n8,000,000 + 929,000 = 8,929,000\n\nAdding these: 11,167 + 8,929,000 = 9,040,167\n\nTotal sum: 102,000,000 + 9,040,167 = 111,040,167\n\nTherefore, the correct sum is 111,040,167.\n\nBut according to the first method, we had 10,371,811,67. Which is incorrect. Therefore, the first method is flawed because when adding the digits, the carryover propagates through all place values, and if not done correctly, it can lead to errors. Therefore, the second method is correct.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 111,040,167.\n\nBut wait, let's verify this with another method. Let's use the fact that 68,877,3950 + 34,929,7217 = ?\n\nLet me add them using the standard addition:\n\n        68,877,3950\n\n      + 34,929,7217\n\n      -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n0 + 7 = 7\n\n5 + 1 = 6\n\n9 + 2 = 11\n\n3 + 7 = 10\n\n8 + 9 = 17\n\n8 + 2 = 10\n\n7 + 9 = 16\n\n8 + 4 = 12\n\n6 + 3 = 9\n\n0 + 0 = 0\n\nNow, writing down the digits with carries:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n7 (units)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n11 (hundreds) \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n10 (thousands) \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n17 (ten-thousands) \u2192 7, carry 1\n\n10 (hundred-thousands) \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n16 (millions) \u2192 6, carry 1\n\n12 (ten-millions) \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n9 (hundred-millions) \u2192 9, carry 1\n\n0 (billions) + 1 (carry) \u2192 1\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions: 1\n\nHundred-millions: 0\n\nTen-millions: 2\n\nMillions: 6\n\nHundred-thousands: 0\n\nTen-thousands: 7\n\nThousands: 0\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 6\n\nUnits: 7\n\nSo the number is 10,260,700,076. Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred-millions), 2 (ten-millions), 6 (millions), 0 (hundred-thousands), 7 (ten-thousands), 0 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 7 (units)\n\nSo the number is 10,260,700,076. But this is 10.26 billion, which is different from 111 million. This is impossible. Therefore, there must be a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nWait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nBillions: 1\n\nHundred-millions: 0\n\nTen-millions: 2\n\nMillions: 6\n\nHundred-thousands: 0\n\nTen-thousands: 7\n\nThousands: 0\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 6\n\nUnits: 7\n\nSo the number is 10,260,700,076. But this is 10.26 billion, which is incorrect. The correct sum is 111,040,167. Therefore, there's a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nWait, let's check the addition again:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 + 7 = 7\n\n5 + 1 = 6\n\n9 + 2 = 11\n\n3 + 7 = 10\n\n8 + 9 = 17\n\n8 + 2 = 10\n\n7 + 9 = 16\n\n8 + 4 = 12\n\n6 + 3 = 9\n\n0 + 0 = 0\n\nNow, writing down the digits with carries:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n7 (units)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n11 (hundreds) \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n10 (thousands) \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n17 (ten-thousands) \u2192 7, carry 1\n\n10 (hundred-thousands) \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n16 (millions) \u2192 6, carry 1\n\n12 (ten-millions) \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n9 (hundred-millions) \u2192 9, carry 1\n\n0 (billions) + 1 (carry) \u2192 1\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions: 1\n\nHundred-millions: 0\n\nTen-millions: 2\n\nMillions: 6\n\nHundred-thousands: 0\n\nTen-thousands: 7\n\nThousands: 0\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 6\n\nUnits: 7\n\nSo the number is 10,260,700,076. But this is incorrect. Therefore, the digit-by-digit addition method is flawed. The mistake must be in the way the carries are being propagated. Let's check the thousands place:\n\nAfter adding the thousands digits: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 (carry) = 11. So 11 thousands, which is 1 thousand and 1 ten thousand. So we write 1 and carry 1. But in the thousands place, we have 3 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) = 10 + 1 (carry) = 11. So 11 thousands is 1 thousand and 1 ten thousand. Therefore, the thousands digit should be 1 and carry 1 to the ten-thousands place. But in the previous step-by-step addition, we wrote 0 in the thousands place and carried 1 to the ten-thousands place. That's incorrect. The thousands place should be 1, not 0. Therefore, the mistake is here.\n\nLet me correct the digit-by-digit addition:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 + 7 = 7\n\n5 + 1 = 6\n\n9 + 2 = 11\n\n3 + 7 = 10 (write 0, carry 1)\n\n8 + 9 = 17 + 1 = 18 (write 8, carry 1)\n\n8 + 2 = 10 + 1 = 11 (write 1, carry 1)\n\n7 + 9 = 16 + 1 = 17 (write 7, carry 1)\n\n8 + 4 = 12 + 1 = 13 (write 3, carry 1)\n\n6 + 3 = 9 + 1 = 10 (write 0, carry 1)\n\n0 + 0 = 0 + 1 = 1 (write 1)\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions: 1\n\nHundred-millions: 0\n\nTen-millions: 2\n\nMillions: 6\n\nHundred-thousands: 0\n\nTen-thousands: 8\n\nThousands: 0\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 6\n\nUnits: 7\n\nSo the number is 10,260,800,016. Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred-millions), 2 (ten-millions), 6 (millions), 0 (hundred-thousands), 8 (ten-thousands), 0 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 7 (units)\n\nSo the number is 10,260,800,016. But this is still not matching the correct sum of 111,040,167. There's a clear mistake here. Therefore, the digit-by-digit addition method is not reliable for this problem. The correct method is the second one, adding millions and thousands separately.\n\nTherefore, the correct sum is 111,040,167.\n\nBut wait, according to the second method, adding 68,877,3950 + 34,929,7217 = 111,040,167. But according to the digit-by-digit addition, we get 10,260,800,016. Which is clearly wrong. Therefore, the digit-by-digit addition must have an error.\n\nThe mistake in the digit-by-digit addition is that when