\n\n9 + 1 = 10 + 1 = 11, carryover 1.\n\nBut wait, here's a confusion. Wait, when adding the hundred-thousands place: the first number has 6 and the second has 8. 6 + 8 = 14, so we write down 4 and carry over 1. Then, moving to the millions place: the first number has 9 and the second has 1. 9 + 1 = 10, plus the carried over 1 is 11. So we write down 1 and carry over 1. Wait, but in the millions place, how does that work? Let me check again.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to list the digits with their positions:\n\nFirst number: 1 4 6 6 9 4 1 2 3\n\nBreaking it down:\n\nPosition: 8 (Billions) 7 (Hundred millions) 6 (Ten millions) 5 (Millions) 4 (Hundred thousands) 3 (Ten thousands) 2 (Thousands) 1 (Hundreds) 0 (Tens) -1 (Ones)\n\nWait, maybe numbering from right to left as positions 0 to 8.\n\nWait, perhaps this is complicating. Let me write both numbers aligned:\n\n      146,694,123\n\n    +431,280,949\n\n------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\n3 + 9 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nNext (tens place):\n\n2 + 4 = 6 + 1 (carry) = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n1 + 8 = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\nThousands place:\n\n3 + 0 = 3. Write 3, carry 0.\n\nTen-thousands place:\n\n4 + 2 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nHundred-thousands place:\n\n6 + 8 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nMillions place:\n\n9 + 1 = 10 + 1 (carry) = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nTen-millions place:\n\n6 + 2 = 8 + 1 (carry) = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\nHundred-millions place:\n\n4 + 3 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\nBillions place:\n\n1 + 4 = 5. Write 5, carry 0.\n\nSo the result is 5 (billions), 9 (ten-millions), 7 (hundred-millions), 1 (millions), 4 (hundred-thousands), 6 (ten-thousands), 3 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 2 (ones). So the number is 591,746,397? Wait, no. Wait, let's list the digits in order:\n\nBillions: 5\n\nHundred millions: 9\n\nTen millions: 7\n\nMillions: 1\n\nHundred thousands: 4\n\nTen thousands: 6\n\nThousands: 3\n\nHundreds: 9\n\nTens: 7\n\nOnes: 2\n\nSo putting that