\n\nWait, maybe I need to use a calculator to verify. Let me check:\n\n248,708,728\n\n+378,220,428\n\n= ?\n\nLet's add them step by step:\n\nFirst, add the millions:\n\n248,708,728 has 248 million\n\n378,220,428 has 378 million\n\n248 + 378 = 626 million\n\nThen add the remaining parts:\n\n248,708,728 = 248,000,000 + 708,728\n\n378,220,428 = 378,000,000 + 220,428\n\nAdding the remaining parts:\n\n708,728 + 220,428 = 929,156\n\nAdding to the 626 million:\n\n626,000,000 + 929,156 = 626,929,156\n\nSo the correct sum is 626,929,156.\n\nBut according to my previous addition, I got 5,610,501,0556, which is way off. So where did I go wrong?\n\nAh! I see. When I did the digit-by-digit addition, I messed up the order of the digits. The correct sum is 626,929,156, which is 626 million, 929 thousand, 156. So the digits are:\n\n6 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 6 (units)\n\nWait, no. Wait, 626,929,156 is:\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n2 (ten millions)\n\n6 (millions)\n\n9 (hundred thousands)\n\n2 (ten thousands)\n\n9 (thousands)\n\n1 (hundreds)\n\n5 (tens)\n\n6 (units)\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to hundred millions):\n\n6, 5, 9, 1, 2, 9, 6, 2, 6, 6\n\nBut when writing the number, we need to arrange them in the correct order. So the number is 6 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 6 (units). So the number is 626,929,156.\n\nBut according to my previous digit-by-digit addition, I had 610,501,0556, which is incorrect. So where is the mistake?\n\nAh! I see. When I did the digit-by-digit addition, I incorrectly reversed the order of the digits. The correct way is to take the digits as computed from right to left and then reverse them to get the correct left-to-right order. Wait, no. Wait, when you add numbers, the digits are added starting from the right, so the sum is written