n\n1 (ten millions) + 3 (ten millions) = 4, plus carried 1 is 5. Write down 5.\n\nHundred millions place:\n\n5 (hundred millions) + 1 (hundred millions) = 6, plus no carryover. Write down 6.\n\nBillions place:\n\n4 (billions) + 6 (billions) = 10, plus no carryover. Write down 0, carry over 1.\n\nTen billions place:\n\n7 (ten billions) + 9 (ten billions) = 16, plus carried 1 is 17. Write down 7, carry over 1.\n\nHundred billions place:\n\n0 (hundred billions from first number) + 9 (hundred billions from second number) = 9, plus carried 1 is 10. Write down 0, carry over 1.\n\nWait, but there are no more digits. So we have a carryover of 1 at the end. So the total sum would be 100,170,6558? Wait, that doesn't make sense. Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the columns. Let me list all the digits with their positions:\n\nFirst number (74,513,8857):\n\nPosition 9 (hundred billions): 7\n\nPosition 8 (ten billions): 4\n\nPosition 7 (billions): 5\n\nPosition 6 (hundred millions): 1\n\nPosition 5 (ten millions): 3\n\nPosition 4 (millions): 8\n\nPosition 3 (hundred thousands): 8\n\nPosition 2 (ten thousands): 8\n\nPosition 1 (thousands): 7\n\nSecond number (99,613,7931):\n\nPosition 9: 9\n\nPosition 8: 9\n\nPosition 7: 6\n\nPosition 6: 1\n\nPosition 5: 3\n\nPosition 4: 7\n\nPosition 3: 9\n\nPosition 2: 3\n\nPosition 1: 1\n\nSo adding each position:\n\nPosition 1: 7 + 1 = 8\n\nPosition 2: 8 + 3 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nPosition 3: 8 + 9 = 17 + 1 = 18 \u2192 8, carryover 1\n\nPosition 4: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nPosition 5: 1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\nPosition 6: 5 + 9 = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1\n\nPosition 7: 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nPosition 8: 7 + 9 = 16 + 1 = 17 \u2192 7, carryover 1\n\nPosition 9: 0 (since first number doesn't have a hundred billions place) + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n\nBut since there are no more positions, we need to add the final carryover. So the total sum is 100,170,6558? Wait, but that would be 100,170,6558? Wait, but let me check the carryover steps again.\n\nWait, starting from the right:\n\nPosition 1: 7 + 1 = 8\n\nPosition 2: 8 + 3 = 11. So write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 3: 8 + 9 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 4: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 5: 1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5.\n\nPosition 6: 5 + 9 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 7: 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 8: 7 + 9 = 16 + 1 = 17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 9: 0 (from first number) + 9 (from second number) = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nBut since there's a carryover 1 left, we need to add that as a new digit. So the total sum is 100,170,6558? Wait, but 10 is 10, so adding that carryover 1 at the end would make it 100,170,6558? Wait, but 10 in the hundred billions place? Wait, no. Wait, the carryover from position 9 (hundred billions place) is added as a new digit. Wait, but in the initial setup, both numbers have nine digits. So when adding, the hundred billions place is the first digit from the left. So adding the hundred billions place digits:\n\n7 (from first number) + 9 (from second number) = 16. Then, since we started from the right, the carryover from position 9 is added to the hundred billions place. Wait, maybe I confused the order.\n\nWait, actually, when adding numbers, we start from the rightmost digit (units), move left, carrying over as needed. So the carryover from each position affects the next position to the left. So in this case, after adding all the digits from the right, the last carryover is added as a new digit to the left. So in this case, after adding all the digits, we have a carryover of 1 at the end. So the total sum would be 100,170,6558? Wait, but 10 is 10, so adding 1 to the hundred billions place. Wait, but 10 in the hundred billions place would be 100,000,000,000. But the numbers we are adding are around 75 million and 99.6 million, so the sum should be around 174.6 million. Wait, 75 million + 99.6 million is 174.6 million. So 174,600,000. But according to my previous calculation, I ended up with 100,170,6558? That can't be right. There must be a mistake here.\n\nWait, let's check the addition step by step again, perhaps I made an error in the positions.\n\nLet me write both numbers vertically aligned properly:\n\n          74,513,8857\n        + 99,613,7931\n        -------------\n\nLet me separate each digit:\n\nFirst number:\n\n7 4 5 1 3 8 8 5 7\n\nSecond number:\n\n9 9 6 1 3 7 9 3 1\n\nNow, adding each column from right to left:\n\nColumn 1 (units): 7 + 1 = 8\n\nColumn 2 (tens): 5 + 3 = 8\n\nColumn 3 (hundreds): 8 + 9 = 17 \u2192 7, carryover 1\n\nColumn 4 (thousands): 8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\nColumn 5 (ten thousands): 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nColumn 6 (hundred thousands): 1 + 7 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nColumn 7 (millions): 5 + 3 = 8\n\nColumn 8 (ten millions): 4 + 1 = 5\n\nColumn 9 (hundred millions): 7 + 9 = 16\n\nColumn 10 (billions): 4 + 6 = 10\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, there are only nine digits in each number, so when adding, the leftmost digit is the hundred millions place for the first number and the hundred billions place for the second number. Wait, no. Wait, the first number is 74,513,8857:\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n- 7: hundred billions\n\n- 4: ten billions\n\n- 5: billions\n\n- 1: hundred millions\n\n- 3: ten millions\n\n- 8: millions\n\n- 8: hundred thousands\n\n- 8: ten thousands\n\n- 7: thousands\n\nWait, no. Wait, commas separate the thousands, millions, billions, etc. So in the first number: 74,513,8857.\n\n- 74 billion\n\n- 513 million\n\n- 885 thousand\n\n- 7\n\nWait, no. Wait, in the US numbering system:\n\n- The first comma separates the billions: 74,513,8857 is 74,513,8857.\n\nSo:\n\n74,513,8857 = 74 billion + 513 million + 885 thousand + 7\n\nSimilarly, 99,613,7931 = 99,613,7931.\n\nSo:\n\n99,613,7931 = 99 billion + 613 million + 793 thousand + 1\n\nTherefore, when adding:\n\nFirst number:\n\n74,513,8857\n\n= 74,000,000,000 + 513,000,000 + 885,000 + 7\n\nSecond number:\n\n99,613,7931\n\n= 99,000,000,000 + 613,000,000 + 793,000 + 1\n\nAdding them together:\n\n74,000,000,000 + 99,000,000,000 = 173,000,000,000\n\n513,000,000 + 613,000,000 = 1,126,000,000\n\n885,000 + 793,000 = 1,678,000\n\n7 + 1 = 8\n\nSo total sum: 173,000,000,000 + 1,126,000,000 + 1,678,000 + 8\n\nCalculating:\n\n173,000,000,000 + 1,126,000,000 = 174,126,000,000\n\n174,126,000,000 + 1,678,000 = 174,127,678,000\n\n174,127,678,000 + 8 = 174,127,678,008\n\nWait, so according to this breakdown, the sum is 174,127,678,008. But when I added the digits column by column, I ended up with 100,170,6558? That can't be. There's a mistake here. Let me figure out where.\n\nWait, perhaps my initial column-by-column addition was incorrect because I misaligned the digits. Let me try adding them step by step again, carefully.\n\nFirst number: 74,513,8857\n\nSecond number: 99,613,7931\n\nLet me write them as:\n\n          74,513,8857\n\n        + 99,613,7931\n\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n7 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) = 8\n\nNext, tens place: 5 + 3 = 8\n\nHundreds place: 8 + 9 = 17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place: 8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 1 + 7 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nMillions place: 5 + 3 = 8\n\nTen-millions place: 4 + 1 = 5\n\nHundred-millions place: 7 + 9 = 16\n\nBillions place: 4 + 6 = 10\n\nTen-billions place: 7 + 9 = 16\n\nHundred-billions place: 0 (since first number doesn't have a hundred-billions place) + 9 = 9\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait, the hundred-billions place: the first number has 7 in the hundred-billions place, and the second number has 9. But when adding, we start from the right, so the hundred-billions place is the leftmost digit. So when we add the hundred-billions place digits (7 + 9), that's 16. Then, since