 (billions place): 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 (carryover) = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\n- Then, the final carryover 1 becomes the next digit.\n\nSo writing the digits from left to right (highest place to lowest):\n\nThe carryover at the end is 1, which becomes the leftmost digit. Then, the digits we wrote are:\n\nFrom column 10 (billions place): 1\n\nColumn 9: 6\n\nColumn 8: 5\n\nColumn 7: 8\n\nColumn 6: 9\n\nColumn 5: 3\n\nColumn 4: 6\n\nColumn 3: 7\n\nColumn 2: 8\n\nColumn 1: 8\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait, when we add the columns from right to left, the digits we write are from right to left. But when constructing the number, we need to place the digits from left to right. So the first digit we get is the carryover from column 10 (billions place), which is 1. Then, the next digits are the results from column 9 to column 1, but reversed.\n\nWait, no. Wait, when adding, the first digit we get is the rightmost (units), which is 8. Then moving left, the tens is 8, hundreds is 7, thousands is 6, ten thousands is 3, hundred thousands is 9, millions is 8, ten millions is 5, hundred millions is 6, and finally the billions place is 1, with a carryover of 1 which becomes the hundred billions place.\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me try writing the digits step by step, including the carryovers:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\nDigit 1: 7 + 1 = 8\n\nDigit 2: 5 + 3 = 8\n\nDigit 3: 8 + 9 = 17 \u2192 7, carryover 1\n\nDigit 4: 8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\nDigit 5: 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nDigit 6: 1 + 7 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nDigit 7: 5 + 3 = 8\n\nDigit 8: 4 + 1 = 5\n\nDigit 9: 7 + 9 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\nDigit 10 (billions place): 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nSince there are no more digits, we add the final carryover 1.\n\nSo the digits from left to right (highest place to lowest) are:\n\n1 (carryover), 1 (billions place), 6 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 7 (units).\n\nWait, that seems reversed. Wait, no. Wait, when adding from right to left, the first digit is the units, then tens, etc. So the digits we have are:\n\nUnits: 8\n\nTens: 8\n\nHundreds: 7\n\nThousands: 6\n\nTen thousands: 3\n\nHundred thousands: 9\n\nMillions: 8\n\nTen millions: 5\n\nHundred millions: 6\n\nBillions: 1\n\nBut when constructing the number, we need to place the largest place value first. So starting from the left:\n\nHundred billions place: 1 (carryover)\n\nBillions place: 1\n\nHundred millions place: 6\n\nTen millions place: 5\n\nMillions place: 8\n\nHundred thousands place: 3\n\nTen thousands place: 6\n\nThousands place: 7\n\nHundreds place: 8\n\nTens place: 8\n\nUnits place: 8\n\nWait, that can't be right. Wait, no. Wait, the carryover from the hundred millions place addition is 1, which becomes the hundred billions place. Then, the 1 from the billions place addition is the billions place. Then, the 6 from the hundred millions place is the hundred millions place, and so on. Wait, this is getting confusing. Let me try writing the digits in order:\n\nAfter adding all columns, the digits from left to right (highest to lowest place value) are:\n\n1 (hundred billions place)\n\n1 (billions place)\n\n6 (hundred millions place)\n\n5 (ten millions place)\n\n8 (millions place)\n\n3 (hundred thousands place)\n\n9 (ten thousands place)\n\n6 (thousands place)\n\n8 (hundreds place)\n\n8 (tens place)\n\n7 (units place)\n\nWait, but that would make the number 1,165,836,586,878. But that doesn't make sense. Wait, but according to the breakdown earlier, the total sum should be 174,127,678,008. Let's see:\n\n174,127,678,008 is 174 billion, 127 million, 678 thousand, 800, 008.\n\nBut according to the column-by-column addition, we have:\n\n1 (hundred billions)\n\n1 (billions)\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n5 (ten millions)\n\n8 (millions)\n\n3 (hundred thousands)\n\n9 (ten thousands)\n\n6 (thousands)\n\n8 (hundreds)\n\n8 (tens)\n\n7 (units)\n\nSo that's 116,583,658,687,8. Wait, that's way off. There's a clear mistake here. Let me figure out where I went wrong.\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion comes from mixing up the place values. Let me try writing the numbers with their place values:\n\nFirst number: 74,513,8857\n\n- 7 * 10^10 (ten billions)\n\n- 4 * 10^9 (billions)\n\n- 5 * 10^8 (hundred millions)\n\n- 1 * 10^7 (ten millions)\n\n- 3 * 10^6 (millions)\n\n- 8 * 10^5 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 8 * 10^4 (ten thousands)\n\n- 5 * 10^3 (thousands)\n\n- 7 * 10^2 (hundreds)\n\nSecond number: 99,613,7931\n\n- 9 * 10^10 (ten billions)\n\n- 9 * 10^9 (billions)\n\n- 6 * 10^8 (hundred millions)\n\n- 1 * 10^7 (ten millions)\n\n- 3 * 10^6 (millions)\n\n- 7 * 10^5 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 9 * 10^4 (ten thousands)\n\n- 3 * 10^3 (thousands)\n\n- 1 * 10^2 (hundreds)\n\nAdding them:\n\nFirst number + second number:\n\n(7 + 9) * 10^10 = 16 * 10^10\n\n(4 + 9) * 10^9 = 13 * 10^9\n\n(5 + 6) * 10^8 = 11 * 10^8\n\n(1 + 1) * 10^7 = 2 * 10^7\n\n(3 + 3) * 10^6 = 6 * 10^6\n\n(8 + 7) * 10^5 = 15 * 10^5\n\n(8 + 9) * 10^4 = 17 * 10^4\n\n(5 + 3) * 10^3 = 8 * 10^3\n\n(7 + 1) * 10^2 = 8 * 10^2\n\nAdding all these together:\n\n16*10^10 + 13*10^9 + 11*10^8 + 2*10^7 + 6*10^6 + 15*10^5 + 17*10^4 + 8*10^3 + 8*10^2\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n16*10^10 = 1,600,000,000,000\n\n13*10^9 = 130,000,000,000\n\n11*10^8 = 11,000,000,000\n\n2*10^7 = 20,000,000\n\n6*10^6 = 6,000,000\n\n15*10^5 = 150,000\n\n17*10^4 = 17,000\n\n8*10^3 = 8,000\n\n8*10^2 = 800\n\nNow, adding them all up:\n\n1,600,000,000,000\n\n+ 130,000,000,000 = 1,730,000,000,000\n\n+ 11,000,000,000 = 1,741,000,000,000\n\n+ 20,000,000 = 1,741,020,000,000\n\n+ 6,000,000 = 1,741,026,000,000\n\n+ 150,000 = 1,741,026,150,000\n\n+ 17,000 = 1,741,026,167,000\n\n+ 8,000 = 1,741,026,167,800\n\n+ 800 = 1,741,026,167,808\n\nSo the total sum is 1,741,026,167,808. Which is 174,102,616,7808. Wait, but in numerical terms, that's 174,102,616,7808. But this conflicts with the previous column-by-column result. Clearly, there's a mistake in the column-by-column addition.\n\nWait, let's check the column-by-column addition again. Let me write down the numbers with their digits aligned:\n\n          74,513,8857\n\n        + 99,613,7931\n\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nDigit 1: 7 + 1 = 8\n\nDigit 2: 5 + 3 = 8\n\nDigit 3: 8 + 9 = 17 \u2192 7, carryover 1\n\nDigit 4: 8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\nDigit 5: 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nDigit 6: 1 + 7 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nDigit 7: 5 + 3 = 8\n\nDigit 8: 4 + 1 = 5\n\nDigit 9: 7 + 9 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\nDigit 10: 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nFinal carryover: 1\n\nSo the digits from left to right (including the final carryover) are:\n\n1 (from final carryover), 1 (from digit 10), 6 (digit 9), 5 (digit 8), 8 (digit 7), 3 (digit 6), 9 (digit 5), 6 (digit 4), 8 (digit 3), 8 (digit 2), 7 (digit 1)\n\nWait, but that would mean the number is 1,165,836,586,878. But according to the term-by-term addition, it's 1,741,026,167,808. These are vastly different. There's a clear disconnect here. What's wrong?\n\nAh! I think I see the mistake. When adding the hundred millions place, the first number has 7 and the second number has 9. So 7 + 9 = 16. Then, we have a carryover of 1 to the next higher place. But in the column-by-column addition, I added the hundred millions place first, which is incorrect. Because when adding from right to left, we should add each column and carryover, moving left. So the hundred millions place is the 9th digit from the right. So after adding the units to the hundred millions place, we have already added all previous carryovers. Wait, no. Wait, carryover is added after each digit is added. So when adding the hundred millions place, we have to add the digits there and any carryover from the previous (ten millions place). Wait, but in the initial step-by-step addition, I added the hundred millions place first, which is incorrect. That's the mistake.\n\nSo the correct way is:\n\nStart from the rightmost digit (units), move left, adding each digit and any carryover, and carrying over to the next left digit.\n\nSo let's do it correctly:\n\nDigit 1 (units): 7 + 1 = 8. No carryover.\n\nDigit 2 (tens): 5 + 3 = 8. No carryover.\n\nDigit 3 (hundreds): 8 + 9 = 17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 4 (thousands): 8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 5 (ten thousands): 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 6 (hundred thousands): 1 + 7 = 8 + 1 = 9. No carryover.\n\nDigit 7 (millions): 5 + 3 = 8. No carryover.\n\nDigit 8 (ten millions): 4 + 1 = 5. No carryover.\n\nDigit 9 (hundred millions): 7 + 9 = 16 + 1 (carryover) = 17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 10 (billions): 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nFinal carryover: 1.\n\nSo the digits from left to right are:\n\n1 (from final carryover), 1 (digit 10), 7 (digit 9), 5 (digit 8), 8 (digit 7), 3 (digit 6), 9 (digit 5), 6 (digit 4), 8 (digit 3), 8 (digit 2), 7 (digit 1).\n\nWait, but that would be 1,175,836,586,878. But according to the term-by-term addition, it's 1,741,026,167,808. These are different. So there's a mistake here. Let me check the term-by-term addition again.\n\nWait, term-by-term addition:\n\n16*10^10 = 1,600,000,000,000\n\n13*10^9 = 130,000,000,000\n\n11*10^8 = 11,000,000,000\n\n2*10^7 = 20,000,000\n\n6*10^6 = 6,000,000\n\n15*10^5 = 150,000\n\n17*10^4 = 17,000\n\n8*10^3 = 8,000\n\n8*10^2 = 800\n\nAdding these:\n\n1,600,000,000,000\n\n+ 130,000,000,000 = 1,730,000,000,000\n\n+ 11,000,000,000 = 1,741,000,000,000\n\n+ 20,000,000 = 1,741,020,000,000\n\n+ 6,000,000 = 1,741,026,000,000\n\n+ 150,000 = 1,741,026,150,000\n\n+ 17,000 = 1,741,026,167,000\n\n+ 8,000 = 1,741,026,167,800\n\n+ 800 = 1,741,026,167,808\n\nSo the correct sum is 1,741,026,167,808.\n\nBut according to the column-by-column addition, we have 1,175,836,586,878. Which is way off. So where is the mistake?\n\nAh! I think the mistake is in the column-by-column addition. Let me try again, carefully:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nDigit 1 (units): 7 + 1 = 8\n\nDigit 2 (tens): 5 + 3 = 8\n\nDigit 3 (hundreds): 8 + 9 = 17 \u2192 7, carryover 1\n\nDigit 4 (thousands): 8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\nDigit 5 (ten thousands): 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nDigit 6 (hundred thousands): 1 + 7 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nDigit 7 (millions): 5 + 3 = 8\n\nDigit 8 (ten millions): 4 + 1 = 5\n\nDigit 9 (hundred millions): 7 + 9 = 16 + 1 = 17 \u2192 7, carryover 1\n\nDigit 10 (billions): 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nFinal carryover: 1\n\nSo the digits from left to right are:\n\n1 (final carryover), 1 (digit 10), 7 (digit 9), 5 (digit 8), 8 (digit 7), 3 (digit 6), 9 (digit 5), 6 (digit 4), 8 (digit 3), 8 (digit 2), 7 (digit 1)\n\nWait, but that would be 1,175,836,586,878. But according to term-by-term, it's 1,741,026,167,808. So there's a discrepancy. What's wrong here?\n\nWait, perhaps the error is in the alignment of the digits. Let me check the original numbers again:\n\nFirst number: 74,513,8857\n\nSecond number: 99,613,7931\n\nLet me write them out with all digits:\n\nFirst number: 7 4 5 1 3 8 8 5 7\n\nSecond number: 9 9 6 1 3 7 9 3 1\n\nNow, adding each column from right to left:\n\nColumn 1: 7 + 1 = 8\n\nColumn 2: 5 + 3 = 8\n\nColumn 3: 8 + 9 = 17 \u2192 7, carryover 1\n\nColumn 4: 8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\nColumn 5: 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nColumn 6: 1 + 7 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nColumn 7: 5 + 3 = 8\n\nColumn 8: 4 + 1 = 5\n\nColumn 9: 7 + 9 = 16 + 1 = 17 \u2192 7, carryover 1\n\nColumn 10: 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nFinal carryover: 1\n\nSo the digits from left to right are:\n\n1 (from final carryover), 1 (column 10), 7 (column 9), 5 (column 8), 8 (column 7), 3 (column 6), 9 (column 5), 6 (column 4), 8 (column 3), 8 (column 2), 7 (column 1)\n\nWait, but that would be 1,175,836,586,878. But according to term-by-term, it's 1,741,026,167,808. These are different. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps the error is in the term-by-term addition. Let me check:\n\nFirst number: 74,513,8857\n\nSecond number: 99,613,7931\n\nAdding term-by-term:\n\nFirst number:\n\n74,513,8857 = 7*10^10 + 4*10^9 + 5*10^8 + 1*10^7 + 3*10^6 + 8*10^5 + 8*10^4 + 5*10^3 + 7*10^2\n\nSecond number:\n\n99,613,7931 = 9*10^10 + 9*10^9 + 6*10^8 + 1*10^7 + 3*10^6 + 7*10^5 + 9*10^4 + 3*10^3 + 1*10^2\n\nAdding:\n\n(7+9)*10^10 = 16*10^10\n\n(4+9)*10^9 = 13*10^9\n\n(5+6)*10^8 = 11*10^8\n\n(1+1)*10^7 = 2*10^7\n\n(3+3)*10^6 = 6*10^6\n\n(8+7)*10^5 = 15*10^5\n\n(8+9)*10^4 = 17*10^4\n\n(5+3)*10^3 = 8*10^3\n\n(7+1)*10^2 = 8*10^2\n\nSo that's correct. Then adding each term:\n\n16*10^10 = 1,600,000,000,000\n\n13*10^9 = 130,000,000,000\n\n11*10^8 = 11,000,000,000\n\n2*10^7 = 20,000,000\n\n6*10^6 = 6,000,000\n\n15*10^5 = 150,000\n\n17*10^4 = 17,000\n\n8*10^3 = 8,000\n\n8*10^2 = 800\n\nAdding these:\n\n1,600,000,000,000\n\n+ 130,000,000,000 = 1,730,000,000,000\n\n+ 11,000,000,000 = 1,741,000,000,000\n\n+ 20,000,000 = 1,741,020,000,000\n\n+ 6,000,000 = 1,741,026,000,000\n\n+ 150,000 = 1,741,026,150,000\n\n+ 17,000 = 1,741,026,167,000\n\n+ 8,000 = 1,741,026,167,800\n\n+ 800 = 1,741,026,167,808\n\nSo the correct sum is 1,741,026,167,808.\n\nBut according to the column-by-column addition, we have 1,175,836,586,878. Which is incorrect. Therefore, the column-by-column addition must have an error.\n\nLet me try the column-by-column addition again, carefully:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nDigit 1 (units): 7 + 1 = 8\n\nDigit 2 (tens): 5 + 3 = 8\n\nDigit 3 (hundreds): 8 + 9 = 17 \u2192 7, carryover 1\n\nDigit 4 (thousands): 8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\nDigit 5 (ten thousands): 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nDigit 6 (hundred thousands): 1 + 7 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nDigit 7 (millions): 5 + 3 = 8\n\nDigit 8 (ten millions): 4 + 1 = 5\n\nDigit 9 (hundred millions): 7 + 9 = 16 + 1 = 17 \u2192 7, carryover 1\n\nDigit 10 (billions): 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nFinal carryover: 1\n\nSo the digits from left to right are:\n\n1 (final carryover), 1 (digit 10), 7 (digit 9), 5 (digit 8), 8 (digit 7), 3 (digit 6), 9 (digit 5), 6 (digit 4), 8 (digit 3), 8 (digit 2), 7 (digit 1)\n\nWait, but that would be 1,175,836,586,878. But according to term-by-term, it's 1,741,026,167,808. So there's a discrepancy. What's the issue here?\n\nWait, perhaps the error is in the alignment of the digits. Let me check the original numbers again:\n\nFirst number: 74,513,8857\n\nSecond number: 99,613,7931\n\nBreaking them down:\n\nFirst number:\n\n7 (ten billions)\n\n4 (billions)\n\n5 (hundred millions)\n\n1 (ten millions)\n\n3 (millions)\n\n8 (hundred thousands)\n\n8 (ten thousands)\n\n5 (thousands)\n\n7 (hundreds)\n\nSecond number:\n\n9 (ten billions)\n\n9 (billions)\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n1 (ten millions)\n\n3 (millions)\n\n7 (hundred thousands)\n\n9 (ten thousands)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n1 (hundreds)\n\nWait, hold on! The second number is 99,613,7931. Let's parse this correctly:\n\nDigits: 9,9,6,1,3,7,9,3,1\n\nSo:\n\n9 (ten billions)\n\n9 (billions)\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n1 (ten millions)\n\n3 (millions)\n\n7 (hundred thousands)\n\n9 (ten thousands)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n1 (hundreds)\n\nBut the first number is 74,513,8857:\n\nDigits: 7,4,5,1,3,8,8,5,7\n\nSo:\n\n7 (hundred billions)\n\n4 (ten billions)\n\n5 (billions)\n\n1 (hundred millions)\n\n3 (ten millions)\n\n8 (millions)\n\n8 (hundred thousands)\n\n8 (ten thousands)\n\n7 (thousands)\n\nWait, no. Wait, the first number:\n\n74,513,8857:\n\n- 7: hundred billions\n\n- 4: ten billions\n\n- 5: billions\n\n- 1: hundred millions\n\n- 3: ten millions\n\n- 8: millions\n\n- 8: hundred thousands\n\n- 8: ten thousands\n\n- 7: thousands\n\nWait, but the second number is 99,613,7931:\n\n- 9: ten billions\n\n- 9: billions\n\n- 6: hundred millions\n\n- 1: ten millions\n\n- 3: millions\n\n- 7: hundred thousands\n\n- 9: ten thousands\n\n- 3: thousands\n\n- 1: hundreds\n\nWait, so when adding, the hundred millions place:\n\nFirst number has 1 (hundred millions), second number has 6 (hundred millions). So 1 + 6 = 7, no carryover.\n\nWait, but in the column-by-column addition, I added the hundred millions place as 7 + 9 = 16. That's incorrect. Because the second number's hundred millions place is 6, not 9. Wait, no. Wait, the second number is 99,613,7931. So the digits are:\n\n9 (ten billions)\n\n9 (billions)\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n1 (ten millions)\n\n3 (millions)\n\n7 (hundred thousands)\n\n9 (ten thousands)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n1 (hundreds)\n\nWait, so the hundred millions place in the second number is 6, not 9. The 9 is in the billions place. So my mistake was in parsing the second number's digits. I thought the second number was 99,613,7931, which is 99 billion, 613 million, 793 thousand, 1. So:\n\n- 99,000,000,000 (99 billion)\n\n- 613,000,000 (613 million)\n\n- 793,000 (793 thousand)\n\n- 1\n\nTherefore, the digits are:\n\n9 (ten billions)\n\n9 (billions)\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n1 (ten millions)\n\n3 (millions)\n\n7 (hundred thousands)\n\n9 (ten thousands)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n1 (hundreds)\n\nSo the hundred millions place in the second number is 6, not 9. Therefore, when adding the hundred millions place:\n\nFirst number: 1 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number: 6 (hundred millions)\n\n1 + 6 = 7. No carryover.\n\nBut in my previous column-by-column addition, I added 7 (hundred millions from first number) + 9 (hundred millions from second number) = 16. That was incorrect. The second number's hundred millions place is 6, not 9. I must have miscounted the digits.\n\nWait, let's parse the second number again: 99,613,7931.\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n- The first comma separates the billions: 99,613,7931 is 99,613,7931.\n\nSo:\n\n99,613,7931 = 99,000,000,000 + 613,000,000 + 793,000 + 1\n\nTherefore, the digits are:\n\n9 (ten billions)\n\n9 (billions)\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n1 (ten millions)\n\n3 (millions)\n\n7 (hundred thousands)\n\n9 (ten thousands)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n1 (hundreds)\n\nSo the hundred millions place is 6, not 9. Therefore, in the column-by-column addition, the hundred millions place addition should be 1 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) = 7, no carryover.\n\nBut in my previous calculation, I added 7 (hundred millions from first number) + 9 (hundred millions from second number) = 16. That was incorrect. The second number's hundred millions place is 6, not 9. I must have miscounted the digits when parsing the second number.\n\nThis was the key mistake. So correcting that:\n\nColumn 9 (hundred millions place):\n\nFirst number: 1\n\nSecond number: 6\n\n1 + 6 = 7. No carryover.\n\nThen, moving to the next column (ten millions place):\n\nColumn 8 (ten millions place):\n\nFirst number: 4\n\nSecond number: 1\n\n4 + 1 = 5. No carryover.\n\nMillions place:\n\nColumn 7 (millions place):\n\nFirst number: 5\n\nSecond number: 3\n\n5 + 3 = 8. No carryover.\n\nTen thousands place:\n\nColumn 6 (ten thousands place):\n\nFirst number: 8\n\nSecond number: 9\n\n8 + 9 = 17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nWait, no. Wait, ten thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. Let me list all columns with their digits:\n\nColumns from right to left (units to hundred millions):\n\n1: 7 (units)\n\n2: 5 (tens)\n\n3: 8 (hundreds)\n\n4: 8 (thousands)\n\n5: 3 (ten thousands)\n\n6: 1 (hundred thousands)\n\n7: 5 (millions)\n\n8: 4 (ten millions)\n\n9: 1 (hundred millions)\n\n10: 9 (billions)\n\nWait, no. Wait, the columns are as follows:\n\nWait, confusion arises from different numbering. Let me clarify:\n\nWhen adding the two numbers:\n\nFirst number: 74,513,8857\n\nDigits from right to left (units to hundred millions):\n\n1: 7 (units)\n\n2: 5 (tens)\n\n3: 8 (hundreds)\n\n4: 8 (thousands)\n\n5: 3 (ten thousands)\n\n6: 1 (hundred thousands)\n\n7: 5 (millions)\n\n8: 4 (ten millions)\n\n9: 1 (hundred millions)\n\n10: 7 (billions)\n\nWait, no. Wait, the first number is 74,513,8857:\n\nBreaking it 