: 6 + 8 = 14\n\nDigit 9: 4 + 8 = 12\n\nNow, handling the carryovers:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nDigit 1: 5\n\nDigit 2: 7\n\nDigit 3: 0\n\nDigit 4: 5\n\nDigit 5: 14 \u2192 4 with carryover 1\n\nDigit 6: 3 + 1 = 4\n\nDigit 7: 8 + 0 = 8\n\nDigit 8: 6 + 0 = 6\n\nDigit 9: 4 + 0 = 4\n\nWait, but wait, let's do this step by step with carryovers:\n\nStart with digit 1 (units): 5\n\nNo carryover.\n\nDigit 2 (tens): 7\n\nNo carryover.\n\nDigit 3 (hundreds): 0\n\nNo carryover.\n\nDigit 4 (thousands): 2 + 3 = 5\n\nNo carryover.\n\nDigit 5 (ten thousands): 9 + 5 = 14 \u2192 4 with carryover 1\n\nDigit 6 (hundred thousands): 3 + 5 = 8\n\nNo carryover.\n\nDigit 7 (millions): 8 + 3 = 11 \u2192 1 with carryover 1\n\nDigit 8 (ten millions): 6 + 8 = 14 \u2192 4 with carryover 1\n\nDigit 9 (hundred millions): 4 + 8 = 12 + 1 (carryover) = 13 \u2192 3 with carryover 1\n\nFinally, the carryover 1 is added as the next digit.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to hundred millions):\n\n5, 7, 0, 5, 4, 8, 1, 4, 3\n\nWait, no. Wait, the carryover propagates to the left. So the final digits are:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units): 5\n\nTens: 7\n\nHundreds: 0\n\nThousands: 5\n\nTen thousands: 4 (with carryover 1)\n\nHundred thousands: 8\n\nMillions: 1 (with carryover 1)\n\nTen millions: 4 (with carryover 1)\n\nHundred millions: 3 (with carryover 1)\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to hundred millions):\n\n5, 7, 0, 5, 4, 8, 1, 4, 3\n\nBut when written as a number, it's read from left to right (hundred millions to units):\n\n3 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 5 (units)\n\nSo the number is 341,481,475.\n\nWait, but according to the previous method where we broke down the numbers into millions and thousands, we had 132,287,375. Now, according to this digit-by-digit addition, we have 341,481,475. These are different results. There must be a mistake somewhere.\n\