+ 0 = 2\n\nPlus the final carryover 1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to ten millions):\n\n7, 1, 5, 1, 7, 1, 1, 2, 1\n\nWait, but how does this translate to the number? Let's list them in order:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units): 7\n\nTens place: 1 (from position 7)\n\nHundreds place: 5 (position 6)\n\nThousands place: 1 (position 5)\n\nTen thousands place: 7 (position 4)\n\nHundred thousands place: 1 (position 3)\n\nMillions place: 1 (position 2)\n\nTen millions place: 2 (position 1)\n\nPlus the final carryover 1.\n\nSo the digits from left to right (billions to units):\n\n1 (carryover) 2 (ten millions) 1 (millions) 1 (hundred thousands) 0 (ten thousands) 7 (thousands) 5 (hundreds) 1 (tens) 7 (units)\n\nWait, that would be 1,211,075,177. But this still doesn't match the previous result of 132,287,375. There's a clear inconsistency here.\n\nWait, perhaps the initial assumption about the digits of the numbers is wrong. Let's verify the numbers again.\n\nFirst number: 45,395,2075\n\nIf this is 45 million, 395 thousand, 2075, then in digits:\n\n45,000,000 + 395,000 + 2,075 = 45,395,2075. So the digits are 4,5,3,9,5,2,0,7,5. That's 9 digits. Similarly, the second number: 86,885,5300 is 86 million, 885 thousand, 5300. So digits: 8,6,8,8,5,5,3,0,0. That's 9 digits.\n\nSo when adding two 9-digit numbers, the result can be up to 10 digits. The correct sum is 132,287,375, which is 8 digits. Wait, 132,287,375 is 8 digits: 1,3,2,2,8,7,3,7,5. Wait, no. Wait, 132,287,375 is 1 followed by 3, 2, 2, 8, 7, 3, 7, 5. That's 9 digits. Wait, no. Wait, 132 million is 9 digits: 1,3,2,2,8,7,3,7,5. So 132,287,375 is indeed 9 digits. But adding two 9-digit numbers can result in a 10-digit number. However, 45,395,2075 + 86,885,5300 = 132,287,375, which is 9 digits. So the digit-by-digit addition must have an error.\n\nLet me try adding the numbers using the standard addition method:\n\n      45,395,2075\n\n    +86,885,5300\n\n    -------------\n\nStarting from the right