. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nColumn 3 (hundreds): 5 + 6 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nColumn 4 (thousands): 7 + 4 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nColumn 5 (ten thousands): 5 + 2 = 7 + 0 = 7.\n\nColumn 6 (hundred thousands): 8 + 1 = 9.\n\nColumn 7 (millions): 2 + 2 = 4.\n\nColumn 8 (ten millions): 6 + 1 = 7.\n\nColumn 9 (hundred millions): 6 + 2 = 8.\n\nSo the sum is 874,982,243.\n\nBut according to the calculator, it's 275,498,207. There's a discrepancy of 874,982,243 - 275,498,207 = 600,484,036. That's a huge difference. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps I miscounted the digits in the numbers. Let me verify the numbers again.\n\nFirst number: 62,865,7595.\n\nIs this 62,865,7595 or 62,865,7595? Wait, commas are placed every four digits. So 62,865,7595 would be 62 million, 865 thousand, 7595. So in digits: 6 2 8 6 5 7 5 9 5. That's nine digits. Correct.\n\nSecond number: 212,124,648.\n\n212 million, 124 thousand, 648. In digits: 2 1 2 1 2 4 6 4 8. Nine digits. Correct.\n\nSo adding two nine-digit numbers. The sum should be up to ten digits. 62,865,7595 is approximately 63 million, and 212,124,648 is approximately 212 million. So their sum should be approximately 275 million. Which matches the calculator result of 275,498,207. But according to the vertical addition, it's 874,982,243. Which is way higher. So there's a mistake in the vertical addition.\n\nWait, this is impossible. How can adding two numbers around 275 million give a result over a billion? That doesn't make sense. So clearly, there's a mistake in the vertical addition process.\n\nLet me try adding the numbers digit by digit again, carefully.\n\nFirst number: 62,865,7595\n\nSecond number: 212,124,648\n\nLet me write them as:\n\n        62,865,7595\n\n      + 212,124,648\n\n      -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n5 (units) + 8 (units) = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n9 (tens) + 4 (tens) = 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\n5 (hundreds) + 6 (hundreds) = 11 + 1 =