\n\nNext (hundred-thousands):\n\n5 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) = 8. Write 8.\n\nNext (millions):\n\n0 (from first number) + 4 (from second number) = 4. Write 4.\n\nNext (ten-millions):\n\n3 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nNext (hundred-millions):\n\n7 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nFinally, the hundred-millions place:\n\n0 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 1. Write 1.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, the hundred-millions place: first number has 7, second number has 3, and we have a carryover of 1 from the ten-millions place. So 7 + 3 + 1 = 11. So we write 1 and carry over 1. But since there are no more digits, we just add the carryover 1. So the hundred-millions place is 1.\n\nPutting all the digits together from the highest to lowest:\n\n1 (hundred-millions), 1 (ten-hundred-millions), 0 (hundred-millions), 3 (ten-millions), 1 (millions), 4 (hundred-thousands), 5 (ten-thousands), 8 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 6 (units).\n\nWait, that seems confusing. Let me list them step by step:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units: 6\n\n2. Tens: 4\n\n3. Hundreds: 0\n\n4. Thousands: 4\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 5\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 8\n\n7. Millions: 4\n\n8. Ten-millions: 1\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 1\n\n10. Ten-hundred-millions: 0\n\nWait, but when writing the number, the leftmost digit is the highest place value. So the digits are:\n\nTen-hundred-millions: 0\n\nHundred-millions: 1\n\nTen-millions: 1\n\nMillions: 4\n\nHundred-thousands: 8\n\nTen-thousands: 5\n\nThousands: 4\n\nHundreds: 0\n\nTens: 4\n\nUnits: 6\n\nSo the number is 0114854046.\n\nWait, but leading zeros are not typically written, so it would be 114,854,046.\n\nWait, let me check that. If the digits are 0 (ten-hundred), 1 (hundred-million), 1 (ten-million), 4 (million), 8 (hundred-thousand), 5 (ten-thousand), 4 (thousand), 0 (hundred), 4 (ten), 6 (unit). So that's 114,854,046.\n\nWait, but let me verify this result by another method. Maybe adding the numbers digit by digit with proper alignment.\n\nFirst number: 73,502,0915\n\nSecond number: 30,843,3311\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n5 + 1 = 6\n\n1 + 3 = 4\n\n9 + 1 = 10 (0, carry 1)\n\n0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\n2 + 3 = 5\n\n0 + 3 = 3\n\n9 + 3 = 12 (2, carry 1)\n\n1 + 3 + 1 = 5\n\n7 + 3 = 10 (0, carry 1)\n\nBut wait, here's a discrepancy. Wait, in the ten-hundred-millions place, the first number has 7 and the second has 3, plus the carryover 1. So 7 + 3 + 1 = 11. So we write 1 and carry over 1. But since there are no more digits, we add the carryover 1. So the hundred-millions place is 1, and the ten-hundred-millions place is 0.\n\nWait, but in the previous step-by-step addition, when adding the ten-millions place: 3 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 11. So we write 1 and carry over 1. Then, in the hundred-millions place: 7 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 11. So we write 1 and carry over 1. But since there are no more digits, the carryover 1 becomes the hundred-millions place. Wait, but in the hundred-millions place, the first number is 7, the second is 3, and the carryover is 1. So 7 + 3 + 1 = 11. So we write 1 and carry over 1. But since there's no higher place, the carryover 1 becomes the hundred-millions digit. Wait, but in the hundred-millions place, the first number is 7, the second is 3, and the carryover is 1. So 7 + 3 + 1 = 11. So we write 1 in the hundred-millions place and carry over 1. But since there are no more digits, the carryover 1 is added to the hundred-millions place. Wait, but the hundred-millions place is the third digit from the right. Wait, maybe I need to visualize the columns properly.\n\nLet me list the digits with their positions:\n\nPosition: 10^8 (hundred-millions): 7 (first number) + 3 (second number) = 10 + carryover 1? Wait, no. Wait, when adding, each column is independent. Let me list the numbers vertically aligned:\n\n        7 3 5 0 2 0 9 1 5\n\n      + 3 0 8 4 3 3 3 1 1\n\n      ---------------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\nDigit 1: 5 + 1 = 6\n\nDigit 2: 1 + 3 = 4\n\nDigit 3: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nDigit 4: 0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\nDigit 5: 2 + 3 = 5\n\nDigit 6: 0 + 3 = 3\n\nDigit 7: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nDigit 8: 1 + 3 + 1 = 5\n\nDigit 9: 7 + 3 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nDigit 10: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nSo the result is 1 0 3 4 5 2 0 4 6 5 6? Wait, no. Wait, the digits from left to right (highest to lowest place value) are:\n\nDigit 10: 1 (hundred-millions)\n\nDigit 9: 0 (ten-millions)\n\nDigit 8: 3 (millions)\n\nDigit 7: 4 (hundred-thousands)\n\nDigit 6: 5 (ten-thousands)\n\nDigit 5: 2 (thousands)\n\nDigit 4: 0 (hundreds)\n\nDigit 3: 4 (tens)\n\nDigit 2: 6 (units)\n\nWait, that seems confusing. Wait, when adding, the rightmost digit is units (10^0), so the digits are:\n\n10^0: 6\n\n10^1: 4\n\n10^2: 0\n\n10^3: 4\n\n10^4: 5\n\n10^5: 2\n\n10^6: 0\n\n10^7: 3\n\n10^8: 1\n\n10^9: 0\n\nWait, no. Wait, the first number is 73,502,0915, which is:\n\n7 (10^8), 3 (10^7), 5 (10^6), 0 (10^5), 2 (10^4), 0 (10^3), 9 (10^2), 1 (10^1), 5 (10^0)\n\nThe second number is 30,843,3311:\n\n3 (10^8), 0 (10^7), 8 (10^6), 4 (10^5), 3 (10^4), 3 (10^3), 3 (10^2), 3 (10^1), 1 (10^0)\n\nSo when adding, each digit is added as follows:\n\n10^8: 7 + 3 = 10 \u2192 write 0, carry 1\n\n10^7: 3 + 0 + 1 = 4 \u2192 write 4, no carry\n\n10^6: 5 + 8 = 13 \u2192 write 3, carry 1\n\n10^5: 0 + 4 + 1 = 5 \u2192 write 5, no carry\n\n10^4: 2 + 3 = 5 \u2192 write 5, no carry\n\n10^3: 0 + 3 = 3 \u2192 write 3, no carry\n\n10^2: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 write 2, carry 1\n\n10^1: 1 + 3 + 1 = 5 \u2192 write 5, no carry\n\n10^0: 5 + 1 = 6 \u2192 write 6, no carry\n\nBut wait, this is a different approach. If we process each digit from left to right, considering the carryover:\n\nStarting with 10^8 (hundred-millions place):\n\n7 + 3 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n10^7 (ten-millions place):\n\n3 + 0 + 1 (carry) = 4. Write 4, no carry.\n\n10^6 (millions place):\n\n5 + 8 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\n10^5 (hundred-thousands place):\n\n0 + 4 + 1 (carry) = 5. Write 5, no carry.\n\n10^4 (ten-thousands place):\n\n2 + 3 = 5. Write 5, no carry.\n\n10^3 (thousands place):\n\n0 + 3 = 3. Write 3, no carry.\n\n10^2 (hundreds place):\n\n9 + 3 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n10^1 (tens place):\n\n1 + 3 + 1 (carry) = 5. Write 5, no carry.\n\n10^0 (units place):\n\n5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, no carry.\n\nSo the digits from 10^8 to 10^0 are: 0, 4, 3, 5, 5, 3, 2, 5, 6.\n\nBut writing them in order from highest to lowest place value:\n\n0 (hundred-millions), 4 (ten-millions), 3 (millions), 5 (hundred-thousands), 5 (ten-thousands), 3 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 6 (units).\n\nSo the number is 043,553,2556? Wait, that can't be. Wait, leading zeros are not written, so it should be 43,553,2556? Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\n0 (hundred-millions), 4 (ten-millions), 3 (millions), 5 (hundred-thousands), 5 (ten-thousands), 3 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 6 (units).\n\nSo combining them: 43,553,2556? Wait, but 043,553,2556 is 43,553,2556. But that seems incorrect. Wait, let's check with another method.\n\nAlternatively, maybe convert both numbers to their numerical values and add them.\n\nFirst number: 73,502,0915\n\nSecond number: 30,843,3311\n\nAdding them:\n\n73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311.\n\nLet me add them step by step.\n\n73,502,0915\n\n+30,843,3311\n\nStart from the right:\n\n5 + 1 = 6\n\n1 + 3 = 4\n\n9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\n2 + 3 = 5\n\n0 + 3 = 3\n\n9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n1 + 3 + 1 = 5\n\n7 + 3 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nBut since there are no more digits, add the carryover 1 \u2192 1.\n\nSo the result is 104,345,4326? Wait, no. Wait, the digits from left to right would be:\n\nStarting from the hundred-millions place:\n\n7 + 3 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nNext (ten-millions): 3 + 0 + 1 = 4\n\nMillions: 5 + 8 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nHundred-thousands: 0 + 4 + 1 = 5\n\nTen-thousands: 2 + 3 = 5\n\nThousands: 0 + 3 = 3\n\nHundreds: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nTens: 1 + 3 + 1 = 5\n\nUnits: 5 + 1 = 6\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n0 (hundred-millions), 4 (ten-millions), 3 (millions), 5 (hundred-thousands), 5 (ten-thousands), 3 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 6 (units).\n\nSo the number is 043,553,2556. But leading zeros are not written, so it's 43,553,2556. Wait, but 043,553,2556 is 43,553,2556. But that seems like a 9-digit number. Wait, 043,553,2556 is 0 4 3 5 5 3 2 5 6. Wait, that's 9 digits. So 043,553,2556 is 43,553,2556. But 43,553,2556 is 43 million something. Wait, but 73 million plus 30 million is 103 million. So 103,345,4326? Wait, maybe I messed up the commas.\n\nWait, 73,502,0915 is 73 million, 502 thousand, 915.\n\n30,843,3311 is 30 million, 843 thousand, 311.\n\nAdding 73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311:\n\n73,502,0915\n\n+30,843,3311\n\n=104,345,4326?\n\nWait, but 73 million + 30 million is 103 million. Then 502 thousand + 843 thousand is 1,345 thousand. Then 915 + 311 is 1,226. So total is 103,345,226. Wait, that's different from what I was getting before. So maybe my previous step-by-step addition was wrong.\n\nWait, let's do it again:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 5 + 1 = 6\n\nTens: 1 + 3 = 4\n\nHundreds: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nThousands: 0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\nTen-thousands: 2 + 3 = 5\n\nHundred-thousands: 0 + 3 = 3\n\nMillions: 5 + 8 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nTen-millions: 3 + 0 + 1 = 4\n\nHundred-millions: 7 + 3 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nTen-hundred-millions: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nTen-hundred-millions: 0\n\nHundred-millions: 1\n\nTen-millions: 0\n\nMillions: 3\n\nHundred-thousands: 3\n\nTen-thousands: 5\n\nThousands: 4\n\nHundreds: 0\n\nTens: 4\n\nUnits: 6\n\nSo the number is 103,345,4046. Wait, but that's 103,345,4046. But 103,345,4046 is 103 million, 345 thousand, 4046. But 73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311 should be 104,345,4326? Wait, maybe I need to check with actual addition.\n\nLet me compute 73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311.\n\nFirst, 73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311.\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\n73,502,0915\n\n+30,843,3311\n\nStart adding digit by digit:\n\nUnits: 5 + 1 = 6\n\nTens: 1 + 3 = 4\n\nHundreds: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nThousands: 0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\nTen-thousands: 2 + 3 = 5\n\nHundred-thousands: 0 + 3 = 3\n\nMillions: 5 + 8 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nTen-millions: 3 + 0 + 1 = 4\n\nHundred-millions: 7 + 3 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nTen-hundred-millions: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nSo the digits from ten-hundred-millions to units:\n\n1, 0, 4, 3, 3, 5, 4, 0, 4, 6.\n\nWait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nTen-hundred-millions: 1\n\nHundred-millions: 0\n\nTen-millions: 4\n\nMillions: 3\n\nHundred-thousands: 3\n\nTen-thousands: 5\n\nThousands: 4\n\nHundreds: 0\n\nTens: 4\n\nUnits: 6\n\nSo the number is 103,345,4046. Wait, but 103,345,4046 is 103 million, 345 thousand, 4046. But 73 million + 30 million is 103 million. Then 502 thousand + 843 thousand is 1,345 thousand. Then 915 + 311 is 1,226. So total is 103,345,226. Wait, but according to the digit-by-digit addition, it's 103,345,4046. There's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's do the addition step by step with the carryovers:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nDigit 1 (units): 5 + 1 = 6. No carryover.\n\nDigit 2 (tens): 1 + 3 = 4. No carryover.\n\nDigit 3 (hundreds): 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 4 (thousands): 0 + 3 + 1 (carry) = 4. No carryover.\n\nDigit 5 (ten-thousands): 2 + 3 = 5. No carryover.\n\nDigit 6 (hundred-thousands): 0 + 3 = 3. No carryover.\n\nDigit 7 (millions): 5 + 8 = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 8 (ten-millions): 3 + 0 + 1 (carry) = 4. No carryover.\n\nDigit 9 (hundred-millions): 7 + 3 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 10 (ten-hundred-millions): 0 + 0 + 1 (carry) = 1.\n\nSo the digits from left to right (highest to lowest place value):\n\n1 (ten-hundred-millions), 0 (hundred-millions), 4 (ten-millions), 3 (millions), 3 (hundred-thousands), 5 (ten-thousands), 4 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 6 (units).\n\nSo the number is 103,345,4046. But when I do the direct addition of the two numbers:\n\n73,502,0915\n\n+30,843,3311\n\n=104,345,4326?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the digit positions. Let me check with an example.\n\nWait, 73,502,0915 is 73,502,0915.\n\n30,843,3311 is 30,843,3311.\n\nAdding them:\n\n73,502,0915\n\n+30,843,3311\n\nLet me add them as:\n\n73,502,0915\n\n+30,843,3311\n\nStart adding from the right:\n\n5 + 1 = 6\n\n1 + 3 = 4\n\n9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\n2 + 3 = 5\n\n0 + 3 = 3\n\n9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n1 + 3 + 1 = 5\n\n7 + 3 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nBut since there are no more digits, add the carryover 1 \u2192 1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n1 (ten-hundred-millions), 0 (hundred-millions), 4 (ten-millions), 3 (millions), 3 (hundred-thousands), 5 (ten-thousands), 4 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 6 (units).\n\nSo the number is 103,345,4046.\n\nBut when I compute 73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311:\n\nLet me compute 73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311.\n\nFirst, 73,502,0915 + 30,000,000 = 103,502,0915\n\nThen, 103,502,0915 + 843,3311 = 104,345,4326.\n\nWait, so there's a discrepancy here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, 73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311.\n\nLet me compute 73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311:\n\nStart with 73,502,0915\n\nAdd 30,843,3311:\n\n73,502,0915\n\n+30,843,3311\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\n73,502,0915\n\n+30,843,3311\n\n= (73,502,0000 + 30,843,3000) + (2,0915 + 3,3311)\n\n= 104,345,3000 + (5,4226)\n\n= 104,345,35,4226\n\nWait, that doesn't make sense. Wait, 73,502,0000 + 30,843,3000 = 104,345,3000.\n\nThen 2,0915 + 3,3311 = 5,4226.\n\nSo total is 104,345,35,4226. But that's 104,345,422,566? Wait, no. Wait, 104,345,35,4226 is not a standard number. Wait, commas are separators every three digits. So 104,345,354,226. But that's 104,345,354,226. But that's 104 billion, 345 million, 354 thousand, 226. But 73 million + 30 million is 103 million. So this can't be right.\n\nWait, clearly, there's a mistake here. Let me check with a calculator approach.\n\nLet me write both numbers:\n\nFirst number: 73,502,0915\n\nSecond number: 30,843,3311\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n5 + 1 = 6\n\n1 + 3 = 4\n\n9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\n2 + 3 = 5\n\n0 + 3 = 3\n\n9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n1 + 3 + 1 = 5\n\n7 + 3 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nBut since there are no more digits, add the carryover 1 \u2192 1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n1 (ten-hundred-millions), 0 (hundred-millions), 4 (ten-millions), 3 (millions), 3 (hundred-thousands), 5 (ten-thousands), 4 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 6 (units).\n\nSo the number is 103,345,4046.\n\nBut when I do the direct addition, I get 104,345,4326. Which is conflicting.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the digit positions. Let me check with smaller numbers.\n\nFor example, 10 + 10 = 20.\n\nBut according to the digit-wise addition:\n\n10: 1 0\n\n10: 1 0\n\nSum: 2 0. Correct.\n\nBut if I do 10 + 10:\n\nStarting from the right: 0 + 0 = 0\n\nNext digit: 0 + 0 = 0. So sum is 20. Correct.\n\nBut if I do 73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311, according to the digit-wise addition, it's 103,345,4046. But when I do the addition step by step, I get 104,345,4326. Which is different.\n\nWait, let's do the addition manually:\n\n73,502,0915\n\n+30,843,3311\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n      73,502,0915\n\n    + 30,843,3311\n\n    -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n5 + 1 = 6\n\n1 + 3 = 4\n\n9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\n2 + 3 = 5\n\n0 + 3 = 3\n\n9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n1 + 3 + 1 = 5\n\n7 + 3 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nBut since there are no more digits, add the carryover 1 \u2192 1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n1 (ten-hundred-millions), 0 (hundred-millions), 4 (ten-millions), 3 (millions), 3 (hundred-thousands), 5 (ten-thousands), 4 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 6 (units).\n\nSo the number is 103,345,4046.\n\nBut when I compute 73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311, I get:\n\n73,502,0915\n\n+30,843,3311\n\n=104,345,4326.\n\nWait, how is this possible? There must be a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let's check with smaller numbers. Let's take 735 + 3084.\n\n735 + 3084:\n\n5 + 4 = 9\n\n1 + 8 = 9\n\n3 + 0 = 3\n\n7 + 3 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\nSo the sum is 4099.\n\nBut according to the digit-wise addition:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n5 + 4 = 9\n\n1 + 8 = 9\n\n3 + 0 = 3\n\n7 + 3 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\nSo the digits are 4 (ten-thousands), 0 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 9 (units). So 4099. Correct.\n\nBut if I do 735 + 3084:\n\n700 + 3000 = 3735\n\n30 + 84 = 108\n\n5 + 4 = 9\n\nTotal: 3735 + 108 = 3843 + 9 = 3852. Wait, that's different. Wait, no. Wait, 735 + 3084:\n\n700 + 3000 = 3735\n\n30 + 84 = 114\n\n5 + 4 = 9\n\nTotal: 3735 + 114 = 3849 + 9 = 3858. Wait, no. Wait, 735 + 3084:\n\n700 + 3000 = 3735\n\n30 + 84 = 114\n\n5 + 4 = 9\n\nTotal: 3735 + 114 = 3849 + 9 = 3858. But according to digit-wise addition, it's 3858. So that's correct.\n\nBut if I do 735 + 3084:\n\n735\n\n+3084\n\n=3858.\n\nBut if I do 73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311, according to the digit-wise addition, it's 103,345,4046. But when I do the direct addition, I get 104,345,4326. Which is conflicting.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the digit positions. Let me check with the actual numbers.\n\nFirst number: 73,502,0915\n\nSecond number: 30,843,3311\n\nLet me write them out:\n\nFirst number: 7 3 5 0 2 0 9 1 5\n\nSecond number: 3 0 8 4 3 3 3 1 1\n\nAdding each digit:\n\nPositions (from right, 1 to 9):\n\n1: 5 + 1 = 6\n\n2: 1 + 3 = 4\n\n3: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n4: 0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\n5: 2 + 3 = 5\n\n6: 0 + 3 = 3\n\n7: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n8: 1 + 3 + 1 = 5\n\n9: 7 + 3 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nResulting digits (from left to right, positions 9 to 1):\n\nPosition 9: 0 (carryover)\n\nPosition 8: 5\n\nPosition 7: 1 (carryover)\n\nPosition 6: 2\n\nPosition 5: 3\n\nPosition 4: 4\n\nPosition 3: 0\n\nPosition 2: 4\n\nPosition 1: 6\n\nWait, no. Wait, when adding, the first digit (leftmost) is the highest place value. So the digits are:\n\nPosition 9 (hundred-millions): 0\n\nPosition 8 (ten-millions): 5\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 1\n\nPosition 6 (hundred-thousands): 2\n\nPosition 5 (ten-thousands): 3\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 4\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 0\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 4\n\nPosition 1 (units): 6\n\nSo the number is 051,234,54046. Wait, that can't be. Wait, leading zeros are not written, so it's 51,234,54046. But that's not correct.\n\nWait, this is getting confusing. Let me use another approach. Let's convert both numbers to numbers and add them.\n\nFirst number: 73,502,0915\n\nSecond number: 30,843,3311\n\nConvert to numerical values:\n\n73,502,0915 = 7.35020915 x 10^8\n\n30,843,3311 = 3.08433311 x 10^8\n\nAdding them: 7.35020915 x 10^8 + 3.08433311 x 10^8 = (7.35020915 + 3.08433311) x 10^8 = 10.43454226 x 10^8 = 104,345,422.6\n\nBut since we are dealing with whole numbers, this would be 104,345,4226. Wait, but 10.43454226 x 10^8 is 104,345,422.6, which is 104,345,422.6. But since we are adding two whole numbers, the result should be 104,345,4326. But this is conflicting with the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nWait, but 73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311:\n\nLet me compute 73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311:\n\nStart with 73,502,0915 + 30,000,000 = 103,502,0915\n\nThen add 843,3311 to 103,502,0915:\n\n103,502,0915 + 800,000 = 104,302,0915\n\n104,302,0915 + 43,3311 = 104,345,4326\n\nYes, so the correct sum is 104,345,4326.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, I got 103,345,4046. Which is different. So where is the mistake?\n\nAh! I see the mistake. When I did the digit-by-digit addition, I think I misaligned the digits. Let me check again.\n\nFirst number: 73,502,0915\n\nWhich is:\n\n7 (ten-hundred-millions)\n\n3 (hundred-millions)\n\n5 (ten-millions)\n\n0 (millions)\n\n2 (hundred-thousands)\n\n0 (ten-thousands)\n\n9 (thousands)\n\n1 (hundreds)\n\n5 (tens)\n\n6 (units)\n\nSecond number: 30,843,3311\n\nWhich is:\n\n3 (ten-hundred-millions)\n\n0 (hundred-millions)\n\n8 (ten-millions)\n\n4 (millions)\n\n3 (hundred-thousands)\n\n3 (ten-thousands)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n3 (hundreds)\n\n1 (tens)\n\n1 (units)\n\nWait, no. Wait, 30,843,3311 is:\n\n3 (ten-hundred-millions)\n\n0 (hundred-millions)\n\n8 (ten-millions)\n\n4 (millions)\n\n3 (hundred-thousands)\n\n3 (ten-thousands)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n3 (hundreds)\n\n1 (tens)\n\n1 (units)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nFirst number digits (left to right):\n\n7, 3, 5, 0, 2, 0, 9, 1, 5\n\nSecond number digits (left to right):\n\n3, 0, 8, 4, 3, 3, 3, 3, 1, 1\n\nSo when adding digit by digit:\n\nPosition 1 (units): 5 + 1 = 6\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 1 + 3 = 4\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\nPosition 5 (ten-thousands): 2 + 3 = 5\n\nPosition 6 (hundred-thousands): 0 + 3 = 3\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 5 + 4 = 9\n\nPosition 8 (ten-millions): 3 + 8 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nPosition 9 (hundred-millions): 7 + 0 + 1 = 8\n\nPosition 10 (ten-hundred-millions): 0\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, there are 10 digits. Let me list them:\n\nPositions 1 to 10:\n\n1: 5 + 1 = 6\n\n2: 1 + 3 = 4\n\n3: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n4: 0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\n5: 2 + 3 = 5\n\n6: 0 + 3 = 3\n\n7: 5 + 4 = 9\n\n8: 3 + 8 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n9: 7 + 0 + 1 = 8\n\n10: 0\n\nSo the digits from position 10 to 1:\n\n0 (position 10), 8 (position 9), 1 (position 8), 9 (position 7), 5 (position 6), 3 (position 5), 4 (position 4), 2 (position 3), 4 (position 2), 6 (position 1).\n\nSo the number is 081,953,4246. Wait, that can't be. Wait, leading zeros are not written, so it's 81,953,4246. But that's 81,953,4246. But 73 million + 30 million is 103 million. So this is wrong.\n\nWait, clearly, there's a mistake in the digit positions. Let me check again.\n\nWait, the first number is 73,502,0915. Breaking it down:\n\n- 7 (ten-hundred-millions)\n\n- 3 (hundred-millions)\n\n- 5 (ten-millions)\n\n- 0 (millions)\n\n- 2 (hundred-thousands)\n\n- 0 (ten-thousands)\n\n- 9 (thousands)\n\n- 1 (hundreds)\n\n- 5 (tens)\n\n- 6 (units)\n\nSecond number: 30,843,3311\n\n- 3 (ten-hundred-millions)\n\n- 0 (hundred-millions)\n\n- 8 (ten-millions)\n\n- 4 (millions)\n\n- 3 (hundred-thousands)\n\n- 3 (ten-thousands)\n\n- 3 (thousands)\n\n- 3 (hundreds)\n\n- 1 (tens)\n\n- 1 (units)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nPosition 1 (units): 5 + 1 = 6\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 1 + 3 = 4\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\nPosition 5 (ten-thousands): 2 + 3 = 5\n\nPosition 6 (hundred-thousands): 0 + 3 = 3\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 5 + 4 = 9\n\nPosition 8 (ten-millions): 3 + 8 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nPosition 9 (hundred-millions): 7 + 0 + 1 = 8\n\nPosition 10 (ten-hundred-millions): 0\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition 10: 0\n\nPosition 9: 8\n\nPosition 8: 1\n\nPosition 7: 9\n\nPosition 6: 5\n\nPosition 5: 3\n\nPosition 4: 4\n\nPosition 3: 2\n\nPosition 2: 4\n\nPosition 1: 6\n\nSo the number is 081,953,4246. But leading zeros are not written, so it's 81,953,4246. But 81,953,4246 is 81 million, 953 thousand, 4246. But 73 million + 30 million is 103 million. So this is wrong.\n\nWait, this is impossible. There must be a mistake in my digit positions. Let me check with the original numbers.\n\nFirst number: 73,502,0915\n\nSecond number: 30,843,3311\n\nAdding them:\n\n73,502,0915\n\n+30,843,3311\n\nLet me use a calculator to verify.\n\n73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311:\n\n73,502,0915 + 30,843,3311 = 104,345,4326.\n\nYes, according to the calculator, the sum is 104,345,4326.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, I get 081,953,4246, which is 81,953,4246. Which is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, my digit-by-digit addition must have an error. Let me check again.\n\nFirst number: 73,502,0915\n\nDigits: 7, 3, 5, 0, 2, 0, 9, 1, 5\n\nSecond number: 30,843,3311\n\nDigits: 3, 0, 8, 4, 3, 3, 3, 3, 1, 1\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nPo