, no, let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in mapping the digits. Let me try again.\n\nFirst number: 8 6 7 5 3 6 9 1 0\n\nPositions (from right to left):\n\n1: 0\n\n2: 1\n\n3: 9\n\n4: 6\n\n5: 3\n\n6: 5\n\n7: 7\n\n8: 6\n\n9: 8\n\nSecond number: 8 1 7 0 2 1 9 6 3\n\nPositions (from right to left):\n\n1: 3\n\n2: 6\n\n3: 9\n\n4: 1\n\n5: 2\n\n6: 0\n\n7: 7\n\n8: 1\n\n9: 8\n\nWait, now I'm really confused. If the second number is 81,702,1963, then in digits without commas, it's 8 1 7 0 2 1 9 6 3. So from left to right: 8,1,7,0,2,1,9,6,3. But when written without commas, the digits are 8,1,7,0,2,1,9,6,3. So the rightmost digit is 3 (units), then 6 (tens), 9 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 1 (hundred thousands), 7 (millions), 0 (ten millions), 8 (hundred millions). Wait, that can't be. Wait, 81,702,1963 is 8 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (units), 3 (ten units). So in digits: 8,1,7,0,2,1,9,6,3. So from right to left (units to ten millions):\n\nPosition 1: 3 (ten units)\n\nPosition 2: 6 (units)\n\nPosition 3: 9 (tens)\n\nPosition 4: 1 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 5: 2 (thousands)\n\nPosition 6: 1 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition 7: 7 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition 8: 0 (millions)\n\nPosition 9: 8 (ten millions)\n\nWait, that makes sense. So the second number's digits from right to left (units to ten millions) are:\n\n3 (position 1), 6 (2), 9 (3), 1 (4), 2 (5), 1 (6), 7 (7), 0 (8), 8 (9).\n\nTherefore, when adding:\n\nPosition 1: 0 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) = 3\n\nPosition 2: 1 + 6 = 7\n\nPosition 3: 9 + 9 = 18\n\nPosition 4: 6 + 1 = 7\n\nPosition 5: 3 + 2 = 5\n\nPosition 6: 7 + 1 = 8\n\nPosition 7: 5 + 7 = 12\n\nPosition 8: 8 + 0 = 8\n\nPosition 9: 8 + 8 = 16\n\nWait, but hold on, this is conflicting with the previous result. Let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I need to reverse the digits of the second number to align properly. Because when adding, the right