      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the sum of two really big numbers: 327,649,514 and 227,975,781. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. Adding them directly might be tricky because of all those digits, but maybe there's a way to break it down step by step to make it easier. Let me write down the numbers one above the other, aligning the digits properly. That usually helps with addition.\n\nSo, first, let's write both numbers with their commas for clarity:\n\n327,649,514  \n+227,975,781  \n------------\n\nNow, starting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let me add each column:\n\n4 (ones place) + 1 (ones place) = 5. That's straightforward.\n\nNext, the tens place: 1 + 8 = 9. Easy.\n\nHundreds place: 5 + 7 = 12. Hmm, I need to carry over the 1 here.\n\nThousands place: 9 + 5 = 14, plus the carried 1 makes 15. Another carryover.\n\nTen-thousands place: 6 + 8 = 14, plus the carried 1 is 15. Carryover again.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 4 + 7 = 11, plus the carried 1 is 12. Carryover.\n\nMillions place: 2 + 9 = 11, plus the carried 1 is 12. Carryover.\n\nTen-millions place: 7 + 2 = 9, plus the carried 1 is 10. Wait, hold on, here we have a carryover of 1, so that would make the next digit 10. But let me check again.\n\nWait, let me list out each digit position from right to left with the numbers:\n\nFirst number (327,649,514):\n- 3 (millions)\n- 2 (hundred-thousands)\n- 7 (ten-thousands)\n- 6 (thousands)\n- 4 (hundreds)\n- 9 (tens)\n- 5 (ones)\n\nWait, actually, maybe I should index them properly. Let me write them out with their places:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 4\n2. Tens: 1\n3. Hundreds: 5\n4. Thousands: 9\n5. Ten-thousands: 6\n6. Hundred-thousands: 4\n7. Millions: 2\n8. Ten-millions: 3\n\nWait, no, that's not right. Wait, the number 327,649,514 is:\n\n3 (hundred millions)  \n2 (ten millions)  \n7 (millions)  \n6 (hundred thousands)  \n4 (ten thousands)  \n9 (thousands)  \n5 (hundreds)  \n1 (tens)  \n4 (ones)\n\nWait,