00. Then 0 gives 846,160,000. Then 400 gives 846,160,400. Then 80 gives 846,160,480. Then 8 gives 846,160,488. So the total is 846,160,488.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, I had 84,616,048. Which is 84.6 million vs 846.16 million. So clearly, there's a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nWait, so where did I go wrong?\n\nAh! Wait, I think I messed up the number of digits. The first number is 72,178,5238, which is 9 digits. The second number is 122,585,810, also 9 digits. When adding two 9-digit numbers, the result can be a 10-digit number. So 846,160,488 is a 9-digit number? Wait, 846,160,488 is 846 million, which is a 9-digit number. Wait, 846,160,488 is 8 digits? Let me count: 8-4-6-1-6-0-4-8-8. Wait, no, commas are placed every three digits. So 846,160,488 is 846 million, 160 thousand, 488. So that's 846,160,488. Which is 846 million. But 72 million + 122 million is 194 million. So why is the digit-by-digit addition giving 846 million?\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me check with another method. Let's use the standard addition algorithm step by step.\n\nFirst number: 72,178,5238\n\nSecond number: 122,585,810\n\nLet me write them down:\n\n        7 2 1 7 8 5 2 3 8\n\n      + 1 2 2 5 8 5 8 1 0\n\n      ---------------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\n8 + 0 = 8\n\nTens place: 3 + 1 = 4\n\nHundreds place: 2 + 8 = 10. Write down 0, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place: 5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write down 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place: 7 + 8 = 15 + 1 = 16. Write down 6, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 1 + 2 = 3 + 1 = 4\n\nMillions place: 2 + 2 = 4\n\nTen millions place: 7 + 1 = 8\n\nSo the result is 84,616,048. But according to the component addition, it's 194,763,6048. Which is conflicting.\n\nWait, but 84,616,048 is 84.6 million, and 194,763,6048 is 194.76 million. But 72 million + 122 million is 194 million. So why is the digit-by-digit addition giving 84 million?\n\nWait, this is a problem. There must be a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nWait, let's check the alignment again. When adding the two numbers:\n\nFirst number: 72,178,5238\n\nSecond number: 122,585,810\n\nSo, the digits are:\n\nFirst number: 7 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 3 (ones), 8.\n\nSecond number: 1 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 2 (millions), 5 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 1 (ones), 0.\n\nWait, hold on! Wait, the second number is 122,585,810. Breaking down:\n\n1 (ten millions)\n\n2 (millions)\n\n2 (millions)\n\n5 (ten thousands)\n\n8 (thousands)\n\n5 (hundreds)\n\n8 (tens)\n\n1 (ones)\n\n0.\n\nWait, so the digits are:\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 0\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 1\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 8\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 5\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 2\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 2\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 5\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 1\n\nWait, no. Wait, commas are every three digits. So 122,585,810 is 122 million, 585 thousand, 810. So breaking into digits:\n\n1 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 2 (millions), 5 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 1 (ones), 0.\n\nWait, no. Wait, 122,585,810:\n\n- 122 million: 1, 2, 2\n\n- 585 thousand: 5, 8, 5\n\n- 810: 8, 1, 0\n\nSo the digits are: 1, 2, 2, 5, 8, 5, 8, 1, 0.\n\nSo positions:\n\n1: 1 (ten millions)\n\n2: 2 (millions)\n\n3: 2 (millions)\n\n4: 5 (ten thousands)\n\n5: 8 (thousands)\n\n6: 5 (hundreds)\n\n7: 8 (tens)\n\n8: 1 (ones)\n\n9: 0 (ten ones?)\n\nWait, no. Wait, commas are placed every three digits. So 122,585,810 is split as 122,585,810. So:\n\n- 122,000,000 (122 million)\n\n- 585,000 (585 thousand)\n\n- 810 (810)\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n1 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 2 (millions), 5 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 1 (ones), 0.\n\nWait, but when written out without commas: 1 2 2 5 8 5 8 1 0.\n\nSo positions 1-9:\n\n1: 1\n\n2: 2\n\n3: 2\n\n4: 5\n\n5: 8\n\n6: 5\n\n7: 8\n\n8: 1\n\n9: 0\n\nTherefore, when adding:\n\nFirst number: 7 2 1 7 8 5 2 3 8\n\nSecond number: 1 2 2 5 8 5 8 1 0\n\nSo aligning:\n\nPosition 1: 8 + 0 = 8\n\nPosition 2: 3 + 1 = 4\n\nPosition 3: 2 + 2 = 4\n\nPosition 4: 5 + 5 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n\nPosition 5: 7 + 8 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\nPosition 6: 1 + 5 = 6 + 1 = 7\n\nPosition 7: 2 + 8 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n\nPosition 8: 7 + 2 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n\nPosition 9: 1 + 1 = 2 + 1 = 3\n\nWait, hold on! I think I messed up the positions earlier. Let's do this step by step.\n\nLet me list the digits of both numbers with their positions:\n\nFirst number (72,178,5238):\n\nPosition: 1 (ones) | 2 (tens) | 3 (hundreds) | 4 (thousands) | 5 (ten thousands) | 6 (hundred thousands) | 7 (millions) | 8 (ten millions)\n\nDigits:      8     | 3      | 2        | 5        | 7        | 1        | 2        | 2        |\n\nWait, no. Wait, the first number is 72,178,5238. Breaking into digits:\n\n7 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 3 (ones), 8 (ten thousands? Wait, no. Wait, commas are every three digits. So:\n\n72,178,5238 = 72 million, 178 thousand, 5238.\n\nDigits: 7, 2, 1, 7, 8, 5, 2, 3, 8.\n\nSo positions 1-9:\n\n1: 8 (ones)\n\n2: 3 (tens)\n\n3: 2 (hundreds)\n\n4: 5 (thousands)\n\n5: 7 (ten thousands)\n\n6: 1 (hundred thousands)\n\n7: 7 (ten millions)\n\nWait, no. Wait, commas are placed every three digits. So starting from the right:\n\nThe number is 72,178,5238.\n\nDigits from right to left (ones to ten millions):\n\n8 (ones)\n\n3 (tens)\n\n2 (hundreds)\n\n5 (thousands)\n\n7 (ten thousands)\n\n1 (hundred thousands)\n\n7 (ten millions)\n\n2 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, no. Wait, commas are placed every three digits. So:\n\n72,178,5238:\n\n- The first comma separates millions: 72,178,5238\n\n- So, 72 million, 178 thousand, 5238.\n\nDigits breakdown:\n\n72,000,000 + 178,000 + 5,238 = 72,178,5238.\n\nSo the digits are:\