,000,000 + 60,000,000 = 140,000,000. Then added 140,000,000 to 1,740,000,000 to get 1,740,000,000 + 140,000,000 = 1,780,000,000. Wait, no. Wait, in the component addition, I added the ten millions components first: 80,000,000 + 60,000,000 = 140,000,000. Then added to the previous total of 1,740,000,000. So 1,740,000,000 + 140,000,000 = 1,780,000,000. But in my previous component addition, I wrote:\n\n1,600,000,000 (billions)\n\n+ 140,000,000 (ten millions) = 1,740,000,000\n\nThen added millions, etc. So that's correct.\n\nWait, but according to vertical addition, the sum is 175,180,712. Which is 175 million. But according to component addition, it's 1,751,074,032. Which is 1.75 billion. The difference is 1,751,074,032 - 175,180,712 = 1,575,893,320. This is a huge difference. There must be a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the vertical addition. Let me try adding the numbers digit by digit again, carefully.\n\nFirst number: 981,574,076\n\nSecond number: 769,507,636\n\nLet's write them down:\n\n          981,574,076\n\n        + 769,507,636\n\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n6 + 6 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n7 + 3 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n0 + 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7, no carry.\n\n4 + 7 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n7 + 0 = 7. Write 7, no carry.\n\n5 + 5 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n1 + 9 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n8 + 6 + 1 = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n\n9 + 7 + 1 = 17. Write 7, carry 1.\n\nFinally, write the carried 1.\n\nSo the sum is 175,180,712.\n\nBut according to component addition, it's 1,751,074,032. Which is 1.75 billion. This is a factor of 10 difference. This suggests that there's a mistake in the component addition. But where?\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the number of digits. Let's check the original numbers again.\n\nFirst number: 981,574,076\n\nDigits: 9 8 1 5 7 4 0 7 6\n\nSecond number: 769,507,636\n\nDigits: 7 6 9 5 0 7 6 3 6\n\nSo when adding, the digits are:\n\nFirst number: 9 8 1 5 7 4 0