rry over 1. Wait, the first number here is 5, and the second is 6. 5 + 6 = 11. Then adding the carryover 1 gives 12. So 2 with a carryover of 1.\n\nFinally, the hundred-millions place (which is actually the billions place in this case, but let's see):\nWait, hold on. Let me check the places again. The numbers are 835,547,970 and 684,380,506. Let me break them down digit by digit:\n\nFirst number: 8 3 5 5 4 7 9 7 0\nPositions (from right): 1:0, 2:7, 3:9, 4:7, 5:4, 6:5, 7:3, 8:5, 9:8\n\nWait, maybe I should label the places properly. Let me write them vertically with place values:\n\n          8 3 5 5 4 7 9 7 0\n        + 6 8 4 3 8 0 5 0 6\n        ---------------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units place):\n\nPosition 1 (units): 0 + 6 = 6\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 7 + 0 = 7\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 9 + 5 = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 7 + 0 = 7 + 1 (carryover) = 8\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 5 + 8 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 4 + 3 = 7 + 1 (carryover) = 8\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 3 + 4 = 7\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 5 + 8 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 8 + 6 = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, the first number is 835,547,970. Let me list each digit with its position:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition 1: 0 (units)\nPosition 2: 7 (tens)\nPosition 3: 9 (hundreds)\nPosition 4: 7 (thousands)\nPosition 5: 4 (ten thousands)\nPosition 6: 5 (hundred thousands)\nPosition 7: 3 (millions)\nPosition 8: 5 (ten millions)\nPosition 9: 8 (hundred millions)\n\nSimilarly, the second number is 684,380,506:\n\nPosition 1: 6 (units)\nPosition 2: 0 (tens)\nPosition 3: 5 (hundreds)\nPosition 4: 0 (thousands)\nPosition 5: 8 (ten thousands)\nPosition 6: 3 (hundred thousands)\nPosition 7: 4 (millions)\nPosition 8: 8 (ten millions)\nPosition 9: 6 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the positions. Let me check again. Let's write both numbers with all 9 digits:\n\nFirst number: 8 3 5 5 4 7 9 7 0\n\nSecond number: 6 8 4 3 8 0 5 0 6\n\nSo, aligning them:\n\nPositions (from right, starting at 1):\n\n1: 0 + 6 = 6\n\n2: 7 + 0 = 7\n\n3: 9 + 5 = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1\n\n4: 7 + 0 = 7 + 1 = 8\n\n5: 4 + 8 = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 1\n\n6: 5 + 3 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\n7: 3 + 4 = 7\n\n8: 5 + 8 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\n9: 8 + 6 = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1\n\nBut wait, the carryover from position 8 (ten millions place) is 1, but position 9 (hundred millions place) is 8 + 6 = 14, so 4 with a carryover of 1. But since there are only 9 digits, the carryover would actually go to the next position, but since there are no more positions, we just add it. Wait, but in reality, when adding two numbers, each column can only have a carryover of 0 or 1, because the maximum sum in any column is 9 + 9 = 18, which gives a digit 8 and carryover 1. So, in this case, the carryover from the hundred millions place (position 9) would be added to the next position, but since there is no next position, we just add it as a new digit. Wait, but in the case of adding two numbers, the carryover from the hundred millions place (position 9) would actually be added to the billions place (position 10), but since we only have 9 digits, maybe we just ignore that? Wait, no. Wait, when adding two numbers, each column can only have a carryover of 1, so when we add the hundred millions place (position 9), the sum is 8 + 6 = 14, which is 4 with a carryover of 1. But since there is no position beyond that, we just write down 4 in the hundred millions place and carryover 1. But since there are no more digits, we just add that carryover as a new digit in the next position. Wait, but in reality, when adding two numbers, the carryover from the hundred millions place would go to the trillions place, but since we are only adding two numbers here, the maximum possible carryover is 1. So, in this case, the sum would be 835,547,970 + 684,380,506 = 1,519,928,476. Wait, but let me ch