rry 1.\n8. Ten millions place: 7 + 8 = 15, plus carry 1 = 16. So 6, carry 1.\n9. Hundred millions place: 0 + 8 = 8, plus carry 1 = 9.\n\nPutting all those together: 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 3 (units). So the sum is 167,177,883. Wait, let me check that again.\n\nWait, when I wrote down the digits from left to right, the first digit was 9 (hundred millions), then 6 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 3 (units). So the number is 967,177,883. Wait, hold on, maybe I mixed up the positions. Let me list them properly.\n\nWait, positions are from right to left as units, tens, hundreds, etc. But when we write the number, the rightmost digit is units, then tens, etc. So when we added the digits in each position, the first digit we added was the units (position 1), then tens (position 2), up to the ninth position (hundred millions). So the digits from left to right (highest to lowest place value) would be:\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 9\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 6\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 7\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 1\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 7\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 8\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 3\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 8\n\nPosition 1 (units): 3\n\nWait, that can't be. Wait, no. Wait, when adding the digits in each column, starting from the rightmost (units), the first digit added is 4 (from first number) + 9 (second number) = 13, so we write 3 and carry 1. Then moving left, the next digit is 4 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 8, no carry. Then 0 + 3 + 0 = 3. Then 0 + 8 = 8. Then 2 + 5 = 7. Then 6 + 5 = 11, write 1, carry 1. Then 8 + 8 + 1 = 17, write 7, carry 1. Then 7 + 8 + 1 = 16, write 6, carry 1. Then 0 + 8 + 1 = 9. So the digits from right to left (units to hundred millions) are: 3, 8, 3, 8, 7, 1, 7, 6, 9. Wait, but when writing the number, the rightmost digit is units, so the digits are:\n\nPosition 1 (units): 3\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 8\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 3\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 8\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 7\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 1\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 7\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 6\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 9\n\nSo the number is 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 3 (units). So written out: 967,177,883. Wait, let me check that again.\n\nWait, the positions are:\n\n1. Units: 3\n\n2. Tens: 8\n\n3. Hundreds: 3\n\n4. Thousands: 8\n\n5. Ten thousands: 7\n\n6. Hundred thousands: 1\n\n7. Millions: 7\n\n8. Ten millions: 6\n\n9. Hundred millions: 9\n\nSo when writing the number, starting from the highest place (hundred millions), it's 9, then ten millions: 6, millions: 7, hundred thousands: 1, ten thousands: 7, thousands: 8, hundreds: 3, tens: 8, units: 3. So the number is 967,177,883. Let me verify this with another method.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can add the numbers digit by digit with more attention to the carries.\n\nFirst number: 78,624,0044\n\nSecond number: 88,535,8339\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n      78,624,0044\n\n    + 88,535,8339\n\n--------------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n4 + 9 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nNext digit (tens place): 4 + 3 = 7, plus carry 1 = 8. Write 8, no carry.\n\nNext (hundreds): 0 + 3 = 3. Write 3.\n\nNext (thousands): 0 + 8 = 8. Write 8.\n\nNext (ten thousands): 2 + 5 = 7. Write 7.\n\nNext (hundred thousands): 6 + 5 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nNext (millions): 8 + 8 = 16, plus carry 1 = 17. Write 7, carry 1.\n\nNext (ten millions): 7 + 8 = 15, plus carry 1 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\nNext (hundred millions): 0 + 8 = 8, plus carry 1 = 9. Write 9.\n\nSo the result is 967,177,883. Let me check this with another approach. Maybe adding the numbers step by step.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the fact