 = 14.\n\nSo we write 4 and carry 1.\n\nBut since there are no more digits, we add that 1 as a new digit. So the total is 14 followed by the other digits.\n\nTherefore, the digits from left to right are:\n\n1 (carryover), 4 (hundred-millions), 6 (ten-millions), 0 (millions), 7 (hundred-thousands), 6 (ten-thousands), 0 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 1 (units).\n\nSo the number is 14,607,60011. Wait, but 14,607,60011 is 14,607,60011, which is 14,607,60011. But according to the numerical addition, it's 147,655,611. So there's a discrepancy. What's wrong here?\n\nWait, no. Wait, the carryover after the hundred-millions place is added as a new digit. So the digits are:\n\n- 1 (carryover from hundred-millions place)\n\n- 4 (hundred-millions place)\n\n- 6 (ten-millions place)\n\n- 0 (millions place)\n\n- 7 (hundred-thousands place)\n\n- 6 (ten-thousands place)\n\n- 0 (thousands place)\n\n- 0 (hundreds place)\n\n- 1 (tens place)\n\n- 1 (units place)\n\nSo written out, that's 14,607,60011. But 14,607,60011 is 14,607,60011, which is 14 million, 607 million, 60011. But according to the numerical addition, it's 147,655,611. So there's a mistake here. Wait, maybe I made a mistake in the numerical addition.\n\nWait, let's do the numerical addition again step by step:\n\nFirst number: 75,080,0466\n\nSecond number: 65,185,0945\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n6 + 5 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n6 + 4 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n4 + 5 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n0 + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n0 + 0 = 0 + 1 = 1. Write 1.\n\n8 + 8 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\n5 + 1 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7.\n\n0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6.\n\n7 + 6 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nFinally, write the carried 1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to hundred millions):\n\n1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 6, 7, 6, 3, 1.\n\nWait, but when written from left to right (hundred millions to units):\n\n1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 