5,0945 = 103,1411\n\nSo total is 140,000,000 + 103,1411 = 140,103,1411\n\nWait, but 140,103,1411 is 140,103,1411. But according to the column addition, it's 13,076,0011. This is a huge difference. Therefore, the column addition must be incorrect. But why?\n\nAh! I think I see the mistake. When I did the column addition earlier, I wrote the digits from right to left, but when constructing the final number, I wrote them from left to right. However, in the column addition, the digits from left to right are:\n\n1 (carryover), 4 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 1 (units).\n\nBut according to the numerical addition, the result is 140,265,1411. So there's a discrepancy. Therefore, my column addition must have messed up the order of the digits.\n\nWait, no. Wait, the column addition is correct if we interpret the digits from left to right as the order they are added. But in reality, when adding two numbers, the digits are added from right to left, but the result is written from left to right. So the column addition process is correct, but the final number is written in the order of the digits as they were added. However, when I did the numerical addition, I got 140,265,1411, which is different from the column addition result of 13,076,0011. Therefore, there must be a mistake in the column addition.\n\nWait, let's do the column addition again, carefully:\n\nFirst number: 75,080,0466\n\nSecond number: 65,185,0945\n\nAligning:\n\n      75,080,0466\n    +  65,185,0945\n    -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1. Units: 6 + 5 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n2. Tens: 6 + 4 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n3. Hundreds: 4 + 5 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n4. Thousands: 0 + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 0 + 0 = 0 + 1 = 1. Write 1.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 8 + 8 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\n7. Millions: 5 + 1 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7.\n\n8. Ten-m