      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the sum of two really big numbers: 66,653,7775 and 33,803,9615. Hmm, let me think. Adding them directly might be tricky because they're so large, but maybe there's a way to break them down to make the addition easier. Let me write them down one above the other to visualize the place values.\n\nFirst, let me make sure I have the numbers right. The first number is 66,653,7775. Wait, is that 66 million, 653 thousand, 7775? Or is it 66,653,7775? Wait, commas are usually placed every three digits, so maybe it's 66,653,7775. Let me check. If I separate them into groups of three from the right, 66,653,7775 would be 66,653,7775. Wait, that doesn't make sense. Wait, maybe it's 66,653,7775? Let me count the digits. 66,653,7775: 6-6-6-5-3-7-7-7-5. That's 9 digits. So 66,653,7775 is 66 million, 653 thousand, and 7775. Similarly, 33,803,9615 would be 33 million, 803 thousand, 9615. Let me confirm:\n\nFirst number: 66,653,7775\nSecond number: 33,803,9615\n\nWait, but commas are placed every three digits, so starting from the right, the first comma separates thousands, then millions, then billions. So 66,653,7775 is 66 million (since 66*10^6), then 653 thousand (653*10^3), then 7775. Similarly, 33,803,9615 is 33 million, 803 thousand, 9615. Let me verify the digits:\n\nFirst number: 6 6 6 5 3 7 7 7 5\nSecond number: 3 3 8 0 3 9 6 1 5\n\nWait, but if they are 9-digit numbers, then adding them would be straightforward. Let me write them aligned properly:\n\n      66,653,7775\n    + 33,803,9615\n    -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the units place. Let's add each column:\n\n5 (units) + 5 (units) = 10. Write down 0, carry over 1.\n\n7 (tens) + 1 (tens) = 8, plus the carried 1 makes 9. Write down 9, no carry.\n\n7 (hundreds) + 6 (hundreds) = 13. Write down 3, carry over 1.\n\n3 (thousands) + 9 (thousands) = 12, plus the carried 1 = 13. Write down 3, carry over 1.\n\n5 (ten thousands) + 3 (ten thousands) = 8, plus 1 = 9. Write down 9, no carry.\n\n6 (hundred thousands) + 8 (hundred thousands) = 14. Write down 4, carry over 1.\n\n6 (millions) + 3 (millions) = 9, plus 1 = 10. Write down 0, carry over 1.\n\nFinally, the remaining carried 1 is written down.\n\nWait, let me check again step by step:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nPosition 1 (units): 5 + 5 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 7 + 1 = 8 + 1 (carry) = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 7 + 6 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 (carry) = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 5 + 3 = 8 + 1 (carry) = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 6 + 8 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 6 + 3 = 9 + 1 (carry) = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 0 (since first number has only 9 digits) + 0 (second number has 9 digits) + carry 1 = 1. Write 1.\n\nSo putting it all together, starting from the left (after the carry):\n\n1 (ten millions) 0 (millions) 4 (hundred thousands) 3 (ten thousands) 9 (thousands) 3 (hundreds) 9 (tens) 0 (units). Wait, but that seems reversed. Wait, when adding, we start from the right, so the first digit we wrote was the units, then tens, etc. So the final result should be 100,439,390.\n\nWait, let me check again. Let me write the digits in order:\n\nAfter adding each position:\n\nUnits: 0\n\nTens: 9\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nThousands: 3\n\nTen thousands: 9\n\nHundred thousands: 4\n\nMillions: 0\n\nTen millions: 1\n\nHundred millions: 0 (since both numbers are 9-digit, the first digit is 6 and 3, so 0 carried over)\n\nWait, no. Wait, the first number is 66,653,7775, which is 6 digits before the comma? Wait, no. Wait, confusion here. Let me clarify.\n\nWait, 66,653,7775 is 66 million, 653 thousand, 7775. So in digits, that's 6 6 6 5 3 7 7 7 5. Wait, that's 9 digits. Similarly, 33,803,9615 is 3 3 8 0 3 9 6 1 5. So both numbers are 9-digit numbers.\n\nSo when written out:\n\nFirst number: 6 6 6