      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the sum of two really big numbers: 226,614,242 and 631,969,374. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. Adding them directly might be tricky because of all those digits, but maybe there's a way to break it down step by step. Let me write them down one above the other to visualize the addition:\n\n  226,614,242\n+ 631,969,374\n------------\n?\n\nAlright, starting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let me add those first. The ones digits are 2 and 4. 2 + 4 is 6. That's straightforward. So the ones place of the sum is 6.\n\nMoving to the tens place. The digits are 2 and 7. 2 + 7 equals 9. So the tens digit is 9. No carryover here since 2 + 7 = 9, which is less than 10.\n\nNext, the hundreds place. The digits are 4 and 3. 4 + 3 is 7. So that's 7 in the hundreds place. Still no carryover.\n\nThousands place: 2 and 9. 2 + 9 = 11. Hmm, so that's 11. I need to write down 1 and carry over 1 to the next column. Wait, but hold on, let me check again. The thousands place in the first number is 2 and in the second number it's 9. Yes, 2 + 9 = 11. So we put 1 in the thousands place and carry over 1 to the ten-thousands place.\n\nMoving to the ten-thousands place. The digits are 6 and 6. But we have a carryover of 1 from the previous step. So 6 + 6 = 12, plus 1 is 13. So that's 3 in the ten-thousands place and another carryover of 1. Wait, 6 + 6 is 12, plus the carried 1 makes 13. So yes, 13. So we write 3 and carry over 1.\n\nNext, the hundred-thousands place. The digits are 1 and 9. But again, we have a carryover of 1. So 1 + 9 = 10, plus 1 is 11. So that's 1 in the hundred-thousands place and another carryover of 1. Hmm, but wait, the first number here is 1 (from 226,614,242) and the second number is 9 (from 631,969,374). So 1 + 9 = 10, plus the carried 1 is 11. So yes, 11. So we write 1 and carry over 1.\n\nNow, the millions place. The digits are 4 and 6. But we have a carryover of 1. So 4 + 6 = 10, plus 1 is 11. So that's 1 in the millions place and another carryover of 1. Wait, 4 + 6 is 10, plus 1 is 11. So 11. So we write 1 and carry over 1.\n\nNext, the ten-millions place. The digits are 6 and 3. But we have a carryover of 1. So 6 + 3 = 9, plus 1 is 10. So that's 0 in the ten-millions place and a carryover of 1. Wait, 6 + 3 is 9, plus 1 is 10. So 10. So 0 with a carryover of 1.\n\nNow, the hundred-millions place. The digits are 2 and 3. But we have a carryover of 1. So 2 + 3 = 5, plus 1 is 6. So that's 6 in the hundred-millions place and no carryover. 5 + 3 = 8, plus 1 is 9. Wait, hold on, maybe I messed up here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, the hundred-millions place in the first number is 2 (since the number is 226,614,242) and in the second number, it's 3 (from 631,969,374). So 2 + 3 = 5, plus the carried over 1 is 6. So 6 in the hundred-millions place. That seems right.\n\nFinally, the billions place. The digits are 2 and 6. But we have a carryover of 1 from the previous step. So 2 + 6 = 8, plus 1 is 9. So 9 in the billions place. No carryover since 8 is less than 10.\n\nPutting all those together, starting from the right:\n\nOnes: 6\n\nTens: 9\n\nHundreds: 7\n\nThousands: 1 (with carryover)\n\nTen-thousands: 3 (with carryover)\n\nHundred-thousands: 3 (with carryover)\n\nMillions: 1 (with carryover)\n\nTen-millions: 0 (with carryover)\n\nHundred-millions: 6 (with carryover)\n\nBillions: 9\n\nWait, but let me check again. Let me list each place step by step with the carries:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 2 + 4 = 6. No carryover.\n\n2. Tens: 2 + 7 = 9. No carryover.\n\n3. Hundreds: 4 + 3 = 7. No carryover.\n\n4. Thousands: 2 + 9 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 6 + 6 + 1 (carry) = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 1 + 9 + 1 (carry) = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n7. Millions: 4 + 6 + 1 (carry) = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n8. Ten-millions: 6 + 3 + 1 (carry) = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 2 + 3 + 1 (carry) = 6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\n10. Billions: 2 + 6 + 1 (carry) = 9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nSo putting all those digits together from right to left:\n\n6 (ones), 9 (tens), 7 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 3 (ten-thousands), 1 (hundred-thousands), 1 (millions), 0 (ten-millions), 6 (hundred-millions), 9 (billions).\n\nWait, but when we write the number, we need to make sure the digits are in the correct places. Let me list them in the order they were calculated:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nTen-millions: 0\n\nHundred-thousands: 1\n\nTen-thousands: 3\n\nThousands: 1\n\nHundreds: 7\n\nTens: 9\n\nOnes: 6\n\nWait, no. Wait, when adding, the first digit we calculated was the ones place (rightmost), so the order is:\n\nOnes, Tens, Hundreds, Thousands, Ten-thousands, Hundred-thousands, Millions, Ten-millions, Hundred-millions, Billions.\n\nBut when writing the number, we need to place each digit in its respective position. So the sum is:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nTen-millions: 0\n\nHundred-thousands: 1\n\nTen-thousands: 3\n\nThousands: 1\n\nHundreds: 7\n\nTens: 9\n\nOnes: 6\n\nWait, but that seems reversed. Wait, no. Let me think again. When adding the numbers, the first digit added is the ones place (rightmost), so the sum's digits from right to left are:\n\n6 (ones), 9 (tens), 7 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 3 (ten-thousands), 1 (hundred-thousands), 1 (millions), 0 (ten-millions), 6 (hundred-millions), 9 (billions).\n\nBut when writing the number, we need to place each digit in the corresponding column. So starting from the right:\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 6\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 9\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 7\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 1\n\nPosition 5 (ten-thousands): 3\n\nPosition 6 (hundred-thousands): 1\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 1\n\nPosition 8 (ten-millions): 0\n\nPosition 9 (hundred-millions): 6\n\nPosition 10 (billions): 9\n\nSo the number is 9, 6, 0, 1, 3, 1, 1, 0, 6, 9. Wait, but that would be 9601311069? Wait, let me check:\n\nWait, positions 10 to 1 are:\n\n10: 9\n\n9: 6\n\n8: 0\n\n7: 1\n\n6: 3\n\n5: 1\n\n4: 1\n\n3: 0\n\n2: 9\n\n1: 6\n\nWait, no. Wait, when writing the number, the leftmost digit is the highest place value. So the digits from left to right (billions to ones) would be:\n\n9 (billions), 6 (hundred-millions), 0 (ten-millions), 1 (hundred-thousands), 3 (ten-thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 6 (ones). Wait, that can't be right. Wait, no. Wait, the positions are:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nTen-millions: 0\n\nHundred-thousands: 1\n\nTen-thousands: 3\n\nThousands: 1\n\nHundreds: 0\n\nTens: 9\n\nOnes: 6\n\nWait, but how does that sequence go? Let me list them in order from highest to lowest place value:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nTen-millions: 0\n\nHundred-thousands: 1\n\nTen-thousands: 3\n\nThousands: 1\n\nHundreds: 0\n\nTens: 9\n\nOnes: 6\n\nSo the number is 960,131,1069? Wait, that doesn't make sense. Wait, no. Wait, when writing the number, each digit is placed in its respective column. So starting from the right:\n\nOnes: 6\n\nTens: 9\n\nHundreds: 0\n\nThousands: 1\n\nTen-thousands: 3\n\nHundred-thousands: 1\n\nMillions: 1\n\nTen-millions: 0\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nBillions: 9\n\nSo the number is 9 (billions), 6 (hundred-millions), 0 (ten-millions), 1 (hundred-thousands), 3 (ten-thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 6 (ones). Wait, but that would be 960,131,1069? Wait, no. Wait, let's break it down:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition 1: 6 (ones)\n\nPosition 2: 9 (tens)\n\nPosition 3: 0 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 4: 1 (thousands)\n\nPosition 5: 3 (ten-thousands)\n\nPosition 6: 1 (hundred-thousands)\n\nPosition 7: 1 (millions)\n\nPosition 8: 0 (ten-millions)\n\nPosition 9: 6 (hundred-millions)\n\nPosition 10: 9 (billions)\n\nSo when written out, it's 9 (billions), 6 (hundred-millions), 0 (ten-millions), 1 (hundred-thousands), 3 (ten-thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 6 (ones). So the number is 960,131,1069? Wait, but 960,131,1069 would be 960 million, 131 thousand, 1069. But according to our digit positions, it's 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 6 (ones). So that would be 960,131,1069. Wait, but 960,131,1069 is 960 million, 131 thousand, 1069. But according to the digits we have:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nTen-millions: 0\n\nHundred-thousands: 1\n\nTen-thousands: 3\n\nThousands: 1\n\nHundreds: 0\n\nTens: 9\n\nOnes: 6\n\nSo the number is 960,131,1069. Wait, but 960,131,1069 is 960 million, 131 thousand, 1069. But according to the digits we calculated, it's 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones). Wait, that would be 960,131,1069. Wait, but 960,131,1069 is 960 million, 131 thousand, 1069. But according to the digits, it's 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones). So that's 960,131,1069. Wait, but 960,131,1069 is 960 million, 131 thousand, 1069. But according to the digits, it's 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones). So that's 960,131,1069. Wait, but 960,131,1069 is 960 million, 131 thousand, 1069. But according to the digits, it's 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones). So that's 960,131,1069. Wait, but 960,131,1069 is 960 million, 131 thousand, 1069. But according to the digits, it's 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones). So that's 960,131,1069. Wait, but 960,131,1069 is 960 million, 131 thousand, 1069. But according to the digits, it's 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones). So that's 960,131,1069. Wait, but 960,131,1069 is 960 million, 131 thousand, 1069. But according to the digits, it's 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones). So that's 960,131,1069. Wait, but 960,131,1069 is 960 million, 131 thousand, 1069. But according to the digits, it's 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones). So that's 960,131,1069. Wait, but 960,131,1069 is 960 million, 131 thousand, 1069. But according to the digits, it's 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones). So that's 960,131,1069. Wait, but 960,131,1069 is 960 million, 131 thousand, 1069. But according to the digits, it's 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones). So that's 960,131,1069. Wait, but 960,131,1069 is 960 million, 131 thousand, 1069. But according to the digits, it's 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones). So that's 960,131,1069. Wait, but 960,131,1069 is 960 million, 131 thousand, 1069. But according to the digits, it's 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones). So that's 960,131,1069. Wait, but 960,131,1069 is 960 million, 131 thousand, 1069. But according to the digits, it's 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones). So that's 960,131,1069. Wait, but 