mponents are just the sum of each corresponding digit place. For example, the ten thousands place in the first number is 800,000, and in the second number, it's 500,000. So adding those gives 1,300,000. But in the vertical addition, the ten thousands place was 8 + 5 = 13, which is 130,000. So that's correct. So why is there a discrepancy?\n\nWait, perhaps the error is in the component breakdown when adding the hundred thousands place. Let's check:\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands place: 800,000\n\nSecond number's hundred thousands place: 2,000,000\n\nSo 800,000 + 2,000,000 = 2,800,000\n\nBut in the component breakdown, I added 800,000 + 500,000 = 1,300,000. That's incorrect. So the error was in the component breakdown's hundred thousands place addition. Therefore, the component breakdown was wrong because it incorrectly added 500,000 instead of 2,000,000.\n\nTherefore, the correct component breakdown should be:\n\nFirst number:\n\n- 200,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n- 80,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n- 2,000,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 800,000 (ten thousands)\n\n- 70,000 (thousands)\n\n- 2,000 (hundreds)\n\n- 0 (tens)\n\n- 0 (units)\n\n- 1 (units)\n\nSecond number:\n\n- 500,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n- 80,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n- 2,000,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 500,000 (ten thousands)\n\n- 20,000 (thousands)\n\n- 6,000 (hundreds)\n\n- 800 (tens)\n\n- 4 (units)\n\n- 3 (units)\n\nNow, adding components correctly:\n\nHundred millions: 200,000,000 + 500,000,000 = 700,000,000\n\nTen millions: 80,000,000 + 80,000,000 = 160,000,000\n\nHundred thousands: 2,000,000 + 2,000,000 = 4,000,000\n\nTen thousands: 800,000 + 500,000 = 1,300,000\n\nThousands: 70,000 + 20,000 = 90,000\n\nHundreds: 2,000 + 6,000 = 8,000\n\nTens: 0 + 800 = 800\n\nUnits: 1 + 3 = 4\n\nNow, adding all components:\n\n700,000,000 + 160,000,000 = 860,000,000\n\n860,000,000 + 4,000,000 = 864,000,000\n\n864,000,000 + 1,300,000 = 865,300,000\n\n865,300,000 + 90,000 = 865,390,000\n\n865,390,000 + 8,000 = 