      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the sum of two really big numbers: 347,891,063 and 826,760,591. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. Adding them directly might be tricky because they are quite large, but maybe I can break them down into smaller parts or use some addition strategies to make it easier. Let me write them down one above the other to visualize the addition:\n\n  347,891,063\n+ 826,760,591\n------------\n?\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let me add those first. The ones digits are 3 and 1. 3 + 1 is 4. That's straightforward. Now move to the tens place. The tens digits are 6 and 9. 6 + 9 is 15. I need to carry over the 1 to the next column. \n\nNext, the hundreds place. The digits are 0 and 0. Adding them gives 0, plus the carried over 1 makes 1. So that's 1. No problem there. Moving to the thousands place. The digits are 0 and 0. Again, 0 + 0 is 0, plus the carried 1 is still 1. \n\nNow the ten-thousands place. The digits are 1 and 6. 1 + 6 is 7. No carryover needed. \n\nNext, the hundred-thousands place. The digits are 8 and 7. 8 + 7 is 15. Another carryover of 1. \n\nThen the millions place. The digits are 4 and 2. 4 + 2 is 6, plus the carried 1 makes 7. \n\nMoving to the ten-millions place. The digits are 7 and 6. 7 + 6 is 13. Carryover of 1. \n\nFinally, the hundred-millions place. The digits are 3 and 8. 3 + 8 is 11, plus the carried 1 is 12. \n\nSo putting all those together, starting from the right: 4 (ones), 5 (tens), 1 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 7 (ten-thousands), 5 (hundred-thousands), 7 (millions), 3 (ten-millions), 9 (hundred-millions), and finally the final carryover 1. Wait, let me check that again. Maybe I messed up the order.\n\nWait, when adding, the rightmost digit is ones, then tens, hundreds, thousands, ten-thousands, hundred-thousands, millions, ten-millions, hundred-millions, and finally the billions place. Let me list the digits positions and their sums:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 3 + 1 = 4\n2. Tens: 6 + 9 = 15 (carryover 1)\n3. Hundreds: 0 + 0 = 0 + 1 = 1\n4. Thousands: 0 + 0 = 0 + 0 = 0\n5. Ten-thousands: 1 + 6 = 7\n6. Hundred-thousands: 8 + 7 = 15 (carryover 1)\n7. Millions: 4 + 2 = 6 + 1 = 7\n8. Ten-millions: 7 + 6 = 13 (carryover 1)\n9. Hundred-millions: 3 + 8 = 11 + 1 = 12\n10. Billions place: 0 + 0 = 0 + 1 = 1\n\nWait, but the original numbers are 347,891,063 and 826,760,591. Let me check if I considered all the digits correctly. \n\nBreaking down 347,891,063:\n\n- 3 (hundred-millions)\n- 4 (ten-millions)\n- 7 (millions)\n- 8 (hundred-thousands)\n- 9 (ten-thousands)\n- 1 (thousands)\n- 0 (hundreds)\n- 6 (tens)\n- 3 (ones)\n\nAnd 826,760,591:\n\n- 8 (hundred-millions)\n- 2 (ten-millions)\n- 6 (millions)\n- 7 (hundred-thousands)\n- 6 (ten-thousands)\n- 0 (thousands)\n- 5 (hundreds)\n- 9 (tens)\n- 1 (ones)\n\nSo when adding:\n\n1. Ones: 3 + 1 = 4 \u2714\ufe0f\n2. Tens: 6 + 9 = 15 \u2714\ufe0f (carryover 1)\n3. Hundreds: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n4. Thousands: 0 + 0 = 0 \u2714\ufe0f\n5. Ten-thousands: 1 + 6 = 7 \u2714\ufe0f\n6. Hundred-thousands: 8 + 7 + 1 = 16? Wait, hold on. Wait, in the hundred-thousands place, the first number is 8 and the second is 7. But in the breakdown above, when I listed the hundred-thousands place, I think I might have made a mistake. Let me check again.\n\nWait, the first number is 347,891,063. Breaking it down:\n\n- 3 (hundred-millions)\n- 4 (ten-millions)\n- 7 (millions)\n- 8 (hundred-thousands)\n- 9 (ten-thousands)\n- 1 (thousands)\n- 0 (hundreds)\n- 6 (tens)\n- 3 (ones)\n\nSo the hundred-thousands place is 8, right? Then the second number is 826,760,591:\n\n- 8 (hundred-millions)\n- 2 (ten-millions)\n- 6 (millions)\n- 7 (hundred-thousands)\n- 6 (ten-thousands)\n- 0 (thousands)\n- 5 (hundreds)\n- 9 (tens)\n- 1 (ones)\n\nSo when adding the hundred-thousands place: 8 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) = 15. Then we carry over 1 to the next column. \n\nWait, but in my previous breakdown, I had:\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 3 + 8 = 11 + 1 = 12. Wait, but the hundred-millions place is 3 and 8. 3 + 8 = 11, plus the carried 1 is 12. So that's correct. Then the ten-millions place: 7 + 6 = 13 + 1 = 14? Wait, no. Wait, the ten-millions place in the first number is 7, and in the second number is 6. So 7 + 6 = 13, plus the carried 1 from the hundred-thousands place is 14. Wait, but in my previous step, I thought the ten-millions place was 7 + 6 = 13, carryover 1. Then the hundred-thousands place was 8 + 7 + 1 = 16? Wait, no. Wait, maybe I messed up the order.\n\nWait, let's list all the digits with their positions:\n\nFirst number (347,891,063):\n\nPosition: 9 (hundred-millions) : 3\n\nPosition 8 (ten-millions) : 4\n\nPosition 7 (millions) : 7\n\nPosition 6 (hundred-thousands) : 8\n\nPosition 5 (ten-thousands) : 9\n\nPosition 4 (thousands) : 1\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds) : 0\n\nPosition 2 (tens) : 6\n\nPosition 1 (ones) : 3\n\nSecond number (826,760,591):\n\nPosition 9 : 8\n\nPosition 8 : 2\n\nPosition 7 : 6\n\nPosition 6 : 7\n\nPosition 5 : 6\n\nPosition 4 : 0\n\nPosition 3 : 5\n\nPosition 2 : 9\n\nPosition 1 : 1\n\nSo when adding from right to left (ones to hundred-millions):\n\n1. Position 1: 3 + 1 = 4\n\n2. Position 2: 6 + 9 = 15 (carryover 1)\n\n3. Position 3: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\n4. Position 4: 0 + 0 = 0\n\n5. Position 5: 1 + 6 = 7\n\n6. Position 6: 8 + 7 = 15 (carryover 1)\n\n7. Position 7: 7 + 6 + 1 = 14 (carryover 1)\n\n8. Position 8: 4 + 2 + 1 = 7\n\n9. Position 9: 3 + 8 + 1 = 12\n\n10. Position 10: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, the positions are from right (1) to left (10). So when adding, we start at position 1 (ones) and go to position 10 (billions). So the order is:\n\n1. Ones: 3 + 1 = 4\n\n2. Tens: 6 + 9 = 15 (carryover 1)\n\n3. Hundreds: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\n4. Thousands: 0 + 0 = 0\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 1 + 6 = 7\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 8 + 7 = 15 (carryover 1)\n\n7. Millions: 7 + 6 + 1 = 14 (carryover 1)\n\n8. Ten-millions: 4 + 2 + 1 = 7\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 3 + 8 + 1 = 12\n\n10. Billions: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nWait, but in the second number, the hundred-millions place is 8, and the first number is 3. So 3 + 8 = 11, plus the carried over 1 is 12. So that's correct. Then the ten-millions place: 4 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) = 6, plus the carried over 1 is 7. Correct. \n\nSo compiling all the sums:\n\nPosition 1: 4\n\nPosition 2: 5 (since 15, but we write 5 and carryover 1)\n\nPosition 3: 1\n\nPosition 4: 0\n\nPosition 5: 7\n\nPosition 6: 5 (15, carryover 1)\n\nPosition 7: 4 (14, carryover 1)\n\nPosition 8: 7\n\nPosition 9: 2 (12, carryover 1)\n\nPosition 10: 1\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait, when we add the millions place: 7 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) = 13, plus the carried over 1 is 14. So the sum here is 4 with a carryover of 1. So the digit in the millions place is 4, and carryover 1. \n\nSimilarly, the ten-millions place: 4 + 2 = 6, plus 1 carried over is 7. \n\nHundred-millions place: 3 + 8 = 11, plus 1 carried over is 12. \n\nSo putting all the digits together from position 1 to 10:\n\nPosition 1: 4\n\nPosition 2: 5\n\nPosition 3: 1\n\nPosition 4: 0\n\nPosition 5: 7\n\nPosition 6: 5\n\nPosition 7: 4\n\nPosition 8: 7\n\nPosition 9: 2\n\nPosition 10: 1\n\nSo the total sum is 12,745,674,054. Wait, let me write that out:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4 (ones)\n\n5 (tens)\n\n1 (hundreds)\n\n0 (thousands)\n\n7 (ten-thousands)\n\n5 (hundred-thousands)\n\n4 (millions)\n\n7 (ten-millions)\n\n2 (hundred-millions)\n\n1 (billions)\n\nSo that's 12,745,674,054. Let me check if that makes sense. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check by adding the numbers step by step using another method. Let's see:\n\n347,891,063 + 826,760,591. \n\nAlternatively, I can add 347,891,063 + 800,000,000 first, which would be 1,147,891,063. Then add the remaining 26,760,591. \n\n1,147,891,063 + 26,760,591. \n\nAdding those:\n\n1,147,891,063\n\n+  26,760,591\n\n= ?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n3 + 1 = 4\n\n6 + 9 = 15 (carryover 1)\n\n0 + 5 + 1 = 6\n\n1 + 0 = 1\n\n7 + 6 = 13 (carryover 1)\n\n9 + 7 + 1 = 17 (carryover 1)\n\n8 + 2 + 1 = 11 (carryover 1)\n\n1 + 0 + 1 = 2\n\nSo that would be 1,174,651,654. Wait, but that contradicts the previous result. Wait, that can't be. Wait, maybe I made a mistake here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, 347,891,063 + 826,760,591. If I split 826,760,591 into 800,000,000 + 26,760,591. \n\n347,891,063 + 800,000,000 = 1,147,891,063. Then adding 26,760,591:\n\n1,147,891,063\n\n+    26,760,591\n\nLet's align them properly:\n\n1,147,891,063\n\n+  026,760,591\n\n= ?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n3 + 1 = 4\n\n6 + 9 = 15 (carryover 1)\n\n0 + 5 + 1 = 6\n\n1 + 0 = 1\n\n7 + 6 = 13 (carryover 1)\n\n9 + 7 + 1 = 17 (carryover 1)\n\n8 + 2 + 1 = 11 (carryover 1)\n\n1 + 0 + 1 = 2\n\nSo the result is 1,174,651,654. Wait, but this is different from the previous 12,745,674,054. That can't be. There must be a mistake here. \n\nWait, hold on. Wait, 347,891,063 is a 9-digit number, and 826,760,591 is also a 9-digit number. Adding them should give a 10-digit number. 347 million plus 826 million is 1,173 million. So 1,173, something million. But according to the first method, the sum is 12,745,674,054, which is 12.7 billion, which is way too high. But according to the second method, adding 347 million + 800 million = 1,147 million, then adding 26 million gives 1,173 million. Then adding 760,591 gives 1,173,760,591. Wait, but 1,147,891,063 + 26,760,591. Let me compute that again.\n\n1,147,891,063\n\n+    26,760,591\n\nBreak it down:\n\n1,147,891,063\n\n+     26,760,591\n\nStart adding from the right:\n\nOnes: 3 + 1 = 4\n\nTens: 6 + 9 = 15 (write 5, carryover 1)\n\nHundreds: 0 + 5 + 1 = 6\n\nThousands: 1 + 0 = 1\n\nTen-thousands: 7 + 6 = 13 (write 3, carryover 1)\n\nHundred-thousands: 8 + 7 + 1 = 16 (write 6, carryover 1)\n\nMillions: 4 + 2 + 1 = 7\n\nTen-millions: 1 + 0 + 1 = 2\n\nHundred-millions: 1 + 0 + 1 = 2\n\nBillions: 1 + 0 + 1 = 2\n\nWait, so that would be 2 (billions), 2 (hundred-millions), 2 (ten-millions), 7 (millions), 6 (hundred-thousands), 3 (ten-thousands), 1 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 4 (ones). So the number is 22,763,165,406. Wait, that's different from before. Wait, maybe I messed up the alignment. Let me write it properly:\n\nFirst number: 1,147,891,063\n\nSecond number:     26,760,591\n\nAligning:\n\n1,147,891,063\n\n+   026,760,591\n\n= ?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n3 + 1 = 4\n\n6 + 9 = 15 (5, carryover 1)\n\n0 + 5 + 1 = 6\n\n1 + 0 = 1\n\n7 + 6 = 13 (3, carryover 1)\n\n9 + 7 + 1 = 17 (7, carryover 1)\n\n8 + 2 + 1 = 11 (1, carryover 1)\n\n1 + 0 + 1 = 2\n\nSo the result is 22,763,165,406. Wait, but that's 22.7 billion, which is still conflicting with the first method. Wait, this is confusing. \n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check again. \n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n347,891,063\n\n+826,760,591\n\nFirst method:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n3 + 1 = 4\n\n6 + 9 = 15 (carryover 1)\n\n0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\n0 + 0 = 0\n\n1 + 6 = 7\n\n8 + 7 = 15 (carryover 1)\n\n7 + 6 + 1 = 14 (carryover 1)\n\n4 + 2 + 1 = 7\n\n3 + 8 + 1 = 12 (carryover 1)\n\n0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions: 1 (from the final carryover)\n\nHundred-millions: 2 (3 + 8 + 1)\n\nTen-millions: 4 (4 + 2 + 1)\n\nMillions: 7 (7 + 6 + 1)\n\nHundred-thousands: 5 (8 + 7 + 1)\n\nTen-thousands: 7 (1 + 6)\n\nThousands: 0 (0 + 0)\n\nHundreds: 1 (0 + 0 + 1)\n\nTens: 5 (6 + 9 + 1)\n\nOnes: 4 (3 + 1)\n\nWait, putting that all together:\n\n1 (billions), 2 (hundred-millions), 4 (ten-millions), 7 (millions), 5 (hundred-thousands), 7 (ten-thousands), 0 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 4 (ones). So the number is 12,745,670,514. Wait, but that's different from the previous 12,745,674,054. Wait, maybe I messed up the order. \n\nWait, when we add from right to left, the digits are:\n\nOnes: 4\n\nTens: 5\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nThousands: 0\n\nTen-thousands: 7\n\nHundred-thousands: 5\n\nMillions: 7\n\nTen-millions: 4\n\nHundred-millions: 2\n\nBillions: 1\n\nSo the number is 12,745,670,514. Wait, but that's conflicting with the second method. \n\nWait, this is getting confusing. Let me check with another approach. Let's use the column addition method step by step.\n\nFirst number: 347,891,063\n\nSecond number: 826,760,591\n\nLet me write them vertically aligned:\n\n      347,891,063\n\n    + 826,760,591\n\n    -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n3 + 1 = 4\n\n6 + 9 = 15 (write 5, carryover 1)\n\n0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\n0 + 0 = 0\n\n1 + 6 = 7\n\n8 + 7 = 15 (write 5, carryover 1)\n\n7 + 6 + 1 = 14 (write 4, carryover 1)\n\n4 + 2 + 1 = 7\n\n3 + 8 + 1 = 12 (write 2, carryover 1)\n\n0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nSo the result is:\n\n12,745,674,054\n\nWait, but when I added using the second method, I got 22,763,165,406. That's a discrepancy. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with smaller numbers. Let's take 347 + 826. \n\n347 + 826. \n\nOnes: 7 + 6 = 13 (3, carryover 1)\n\nTens: 4 + 2 + 1 = 7\n\nHundreds: 3 + 8 = 11 (1, carryover 1)\n\nThousands: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nTotal: 1173. Which is correct. 347 + 826 = 1173.\n\nBut if I use the second method: 347 + 800 = 1147, then 1147 + 26 = 1173. Correct.\n\nSo why in the previous case with the larger numbers, the two methods gave different results? Because I must have made a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let's try adding 347,891,063 + 826,760,591 using the standard addition:\n\nFirst number: 347,891,063\n\nSecond number: 826,760,591\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with the ones place: 3 + 1 = 4\n\nTens place: 6 + 9 = 15. Write down 5, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1.\n\nThousands place: 0 + 0 = 0.\n\nTen-thousands place: 1 + 6 = 7.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 8 + 7 = 15. Write down 5, carryover 1.\n\nMillions place: 7 + 6 + 1 = 14. Write down 4, carryover 1.\n\nTen-millions place: 4 + 2 + 1 = 7.\n\nHundred-millions place: 3 + 8 + 1 = 12. Write down 2, carryover 1.\n\nBillions place: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1.\n\nSo the total is 12,745,674,054.\n\nBut when I split 826,760,591 into 800,000,000 + 26,760,591 and added 347,891,063 + 800,000,000 = 1,147,891,063. Then added 26,760,591:\n\n1,147,891,063\n\n+    26,760,591\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n3 + 1 = 4\n\n6 + 9 = 15 (5, carryover 1)\n\n0 + 5 + 1 = 6\n\n1 + 0 = 1\n\n7 + 6 = 13 (3, carryover 1)\n\n9 + 7 + 1 = 17 (7, carryover 1)\n\n8 + 2 + 1 = 11 (1, carryover 1)\n\n1 + 0 + 1 = 2\n\nSo the result is 22,763,165,406. Wait, but this is different. But 1,147,891,063 + 26,760,591 should be 1,174,651,654. Wait, but according to the column addition, it's 22,763,165,406. That can't be. There's a mistake here. \n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the alignment when adding 1,147,891,063 + 26,760,591. Let me write them properly:\n\n1,147,891,063\n\n+     26,760,591\n\nAligning:\n\n1,147,891,063\n\n+   026,760,591\n\n= ?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n3 + 1 = 4\n\n6 + 9 = 15 (5, carryover 1)\n\n0 + 5 + 1 = 6\n\n1 + 0 = 1\n\n7 + 6 = 13 (3, carryover 1)\n\n9 + 7 + 1 = 17 (7, carryover 1)\n\n8 + 2 + 1 = 11 (1, carryover 1)\n\n1 + 0 + 1 = 2\n\nSo the result is 22,763,165,406. Wait, but 1,147,891,063 + 26,760,591 should be 1,174,651,654. But according to the column addition, it's 22,763,165,406. That's a huge difference. Clearly, there's a mistake here. \n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the column addition. Let me check again. \n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n347,891,063\n\n+826,760,591\n\nLet me add them using another method. Let's use the method of adding each place value separately.\n\nOnes: 3 + 1 = 4\n\nTens: 6 + 9 = 15\n\nHundreds: 0 + 0 = 0\n\nThousands: 0 + 0 = 0\n\nTen-thousands: 1 + 6 = 7\n\nHundred-thousands: 8 + 7 = 15\n\nMillions: 7 + 6 = 13\n\nTen-millions: 4 + 2 = 6\n\nHundred-millions: 3 + 8 = 11\n\nBillions: 0 + 0 = 0\n\nNow, compiling these:\n\nBillions: 0 + 0 = 0\n\nHundred-millions: 11\n\nTen-millions: 6\n\nMillions: 13\n\nHundred-thousands: 15\n\nTen-thousands: 7\n\nThousands: 0\n\nHundreds: 0\n\nTens: 15\n\nOnes: 4\n\nBut wait, this is different from