      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the sum of two really big numbers: 84,271,0888 and 481,117,620. Hmm, let me think. Adding them directly might be tricky because they have a lot of digits. Maybe I can line them up vertically and add digit by digit, starting from the right? Let me try that.\n\nFirst, I should make sure both numbers are written out with the same number of digits to align them properly. The first number is 84,271,0888. Let me count the digits. 8-4-2-7-1-0-8-8-8. That's 9 digits. The second number is 481,117,620. Let's count those too: 4-8-1-1-1-7-6-2-0. Also 9 digits. Okay, so I can write them like this:\n\n      84,271,0888\n    + 481,117,620\n    -------------\n\nNow, let's add each column starting from the rightmost digit.\n\nStarting with the units place (the last digit):\n\n8 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) = 8. Write down 8.\n\nNext, the tens place:\n\n8 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) = 10. Write down 0 and carry over 1.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n0 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 7. Write down 7.\n\nThousands place:\n\n8 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) = 15. Write down 5 and carry over 1.\n\nTen-thousands place:\n\n1 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 3. Write down 3.\n\nHundred-thousands place:\n\n7 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) = 8. Write down 8.\n\nMillions place:\n\n2 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 10. Write down 0 and carry over 1.\n\nTen-millions place:\n\n4 (from first number) + 4 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 9. Write down 9.\n\nHundred-millions place:\n\n8 (from first number) + 4 (from second number) = 12. Write down 2 and carry over 1.\n\nFinally, the hundred-millions place (the leftmost digit):\n\nBut wait, since both numbers have 9 digits, the hundred-millions place is the first digit. Let me check again. Wait, actually, the first number is 84,271,0888. Breaking it down:\n\n- 8 (ten millions)\n- 4 (millions)\n- 2 (hundred thousands)\n- 7 (ten thousands)\n- 1 (thousands)\n- 0 (hundreds)\n- 8 (tens)\n- 8 (units)\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the places. Let me write them properly:\n\nFirst number: 84,271,0888\n\nDigits from right to left (units to hundred millions):\n\nUnits: 8\n\nTens: 8\n\nHundreds: 8\n\nThousands: 0\n\nTen thousands: 1\n\nHundred thousands: 2\n\nMillions: 4\n\nTen millions: 8\n\nHundred millions: 8\n\nWait, that seems confusing. Maybe I should write them aligned properly:\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas for clarity:\n\nFirst number: 84,271,0888\n\nSecond number: 481,117,620\n\nSo aligning them:\n\n      84,271,0888\n    + 481,117,620\n    -------------\n\nLet me break down each number into individual digits with their places:\n\nFirst number (84,271,0888):\n\n- 8 (ten millions)\n- 4 (millions)\n- 2 (hundred thousands)\n- 7 (ten thousands)\n- 1 (thousands)\n- 0 (hundreds)\n- 8 (tens)\n- 8 (units)\n\nWait, actually, the places are as follows:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Units: 8\n2. Tens: 8\n3. Hundreds: 8\n4. Thousands: 0\n5. Ten thousands: 1\n6. Hundred thousands: 2\n7. Millions: 4\n8. Ten millions: 8\n9. Hundred millions: 8\n\nWait, that can't be. Wait, 84,271,0888 is 8 followed by 4,271,0888? Wait, no. Let me parse the number correctly.\n\nThe number 84,271,0888 is broken down as:\n\n- 8 (ten millions place)\n- 4 (millions place)\n- 2 (hundred thousands place)\n- 7 (ten thousands place)\n- 1 (thousands place)\n- 0 (hundreds place)\n- 8 (tens place)\n- 8 (units place)\n\nWait, that seems incorrect. Wait, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So 84,271,0888:\n\n- The first comma separates the millions, so 84 million, 271 thousand, 0888. But 0888 is 8,888. So:\n\n- 84,271,0888 = 84,000,000 + 271,000 + 8,888\n\nBreaking down into digits:\n\n84,271,0888:\n\n- 8 (ten millions)\n- 4 (millions)\n- 2 (hundred thousands)\n- 7 (ten thousands)\n- 1 (thousands)\n- 0 (hundreds)\n- 8 (tens)\n- 8 (units)\n\nWait, that still doesn't add up. Wait, 84,271,0888:\n\n- 8 (ten millions)\n- 4 (millions)\n- 2 (hundred thousands)\n- 7 (ten thousands)\n- 1 (thousands)\n- 0 (hundreds)\n- 8 (tens)\n- 8 (units)\n\nYes, that's correct. So when written out with commas: 84,271,0888.\n\nSimilarly, 481,117,620:\n\n- 4 (millions)\n- 8 (hundred thousands)\n- 1 (ten thousands)\n- 1 (thousands)\n- 1 (hundreds)\n- 7 (tens)\n- 6 (units)\n\nWait, no. Let's parse 481,117,620:\n\n- 4 (millions)\n- 8 (hundred thousands)\n- 1 (ten thousands)\n- 1 (thousands)\n- 1 (hundreds)\n- 7 (tens)\n- 6 (units)\n\nWait, that seems off. Wait, 481,117,620:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n- 0 (units)\n- 2 (tens)\n- 6 (hundreds)\n- 7 (thousands)\n- 1 (ten thousands)\n- 1 (hundred thousands)\n- 8 (millions)\n- 4 (ten millions)\n\nWait, no. Wait, commas are placed every three digits. So 481,117,620:\n\n- 481 million, 117 thousand, 620.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 481,117,620 = 400,000,000 + 81,000,000 + 117,000 + 620\n\nDigits:\n\n- 4 (hundred millions)\n- 8 (ten millions)\n- 1 (millions)\n- 1 (hundred thousands)\n- 7 (ten thousands)\n- 6 (thousands)\n- 2 (hundreds)\n- 0 (tens)\n- 0 (units)\n\nWait, that can't be. Wait, 481,117,620:\n\n- 4 (hundred millions)\n- 8 (ten millions)\n- 1 (millions)\n- 1 (hundred thousands)\n- 7 (ten thousands)\n- 6 (thousands)\n- 2 (hundreds)\n- 0 (tens)\n- 0 (units)\n\nYes, that's correct. So when written with commas: 481,117,620.\n\nSo now, aligning the numbers properly:\n\nFirst number: 84,271,0888\n\nSecond number: 481,117,620\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n          84,271,0888\n        + 481,117,620\n        -------------\n\nNow, let's add each column starting from the rightmost digit (units place):\n\n1. Units: 8 + 0 = 8. Write 8.\n2. Tens: 8 + 2 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n3. Hundreds: 8 + 6 = 14. Add the carry 1: 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n4. Thousands: 0 + 7 = 7. Add carry 1: 8. Write 8, carry 0.\n5. Ten thousands: 1 + 1 = 2. Write 2.\n6. Hundred thousands: 2 + 1 = 3. Write 3.\n7. Millions: 4 + 8 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n8. Ten millions: 8 + 4 = 12. Add carry 1: 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n9. Hundred millions: 8 + 4 = 12. Add carry 1: 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nWait, but hold on. The hundred millions place: the first number has 8 and the second has 4. Adding them gives 12, plus the carry 1 makes 13. So we write 3 and carry over 1. But since there are no more digits, we just add the carry 1 at the end. So the final result is 133,388,7608? Wait, no. Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, let's go step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Units: 8 + 0 = 8. Write 8.\n2. Tens: 8 + 2 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n3. Hundreds: 8 + 6 = 14. 14 + 1 (carry) = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n4. Thousands: 0 + 7 = 7. 7 + 1 (carry) = 8. Write 8, carry 0.\n5. Ten thousands: 1 + 1 = 2. Write 2.\n6. Hundred thousands: 2 + 1 = 3. Write 3.\n7. Millions: 4 + 8 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n8. Ten millions: 8 + 4 = 12. 12 + 1 (carry) = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n9. Hundred millions: 8 + 4 = 12. 12 + 1 (carry) = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nBut since there's a carryover 1 after the hundred millions place, we need to add that as well. So the final digit is 13. Wait, but in the hundred millions place, we already wrote 3, so adding the carryover 1 makes it 13. So the total sum would be 133,388,7608? Wait, no. Wait, let me check the digits again.\n\nWait, the hundred millions place: first number is 8, second is 4. 8 + 4 = 12. Then add the carryover 1: 13. So we write 3 and carryover 1. But since there are no more digits, we just add the carryover 1 at the end. So the total sum is 133,388,7608? Wait, that seems like a typo. Wait, let me write down the digits step by step.\n\nLet me list each column from right to left (units to hundred millions):\n\n1. Units: 8 + 0 = 8. Result: 8. No carry.\n2. Tens: 8 + 2 = 10. Result: 0. Carry: 1.\n3. Hundreds: 8 + 6 = 14. 14 + 1 (carry) = 15. Result: 5. Carry: 1.\n4. Thousands: 0 + 7 = 7. 7 + 1 (carry) = 8. Result: 8. Carry: 0.\n5. Ten thousands: 1 + 1 = 2. Result: 2. Carry: 0.\n6. Hundred thousands: 2 + 1 = 3. Result: 3. Carry: 0.\n7. Millions: 4 + 8 = 12. Result: 2. Carry: 1.\n8. Ten millions: 8 + 4 = 12. 12 + 1 (carry) = 13. Result: 3. Carry: 1.\n9. Hundred millions: 8 + 4 = 12. 12 + 1 (carry) = 13. Result: 3. Carry: 1.\n\nWait, but after the hundred millions place, we still have a carryover of 1. So we need to add that as a new digit at the beginning. So the total sum is 133,388,7608? Wait, no. Wait, the carryover 1 would make the total sum start with 1, followed by the digits we already have. Wait, let me think.\n\nWait, when adding the hundred millions place, we had 8 (first number) + 4 (second number) = 12, plus the carryover 1 = 13. So we write 3 and carryover 1. But since there are no more digits to add, the carryover 1 becomes the next digit. So the total sum is 1 (carryover) followed by the digits we wrote: 3, 8, 8, 7, 6, 0, 5, 0, 8. Wait, but that doesn't make sense. Wait, let me try again.\n\nWait, the digits we wrote from right to left (units to hundred millions) are:\n\n8 (units), 0 (tens), 5 (hundreds), 8 (thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 2 (millions), 3 (ten millions), 3 (hundred millions). But with the carryover 1 added at the end. Wait, no. Wait, the carryover 1 comes from the hundred millions place addition. Let me try to reconstruct the entire sum step by step.\n\nLet me write down each column with the operations:\n\nColumn 1 (units): 8 + 0 = 8. Result: 8. No carry.\n\nColumn 2 (tens): 8 + 2 = 10. Result: 0. Carry: 1.\n\nColumn 3 (hundreds): 8 + 6 = 14. 14 + 1 = 15. Result: 5. Carry: 1.\n\nColumn 4 (thousands): 0 + 7 = 7. 7 + 1 = 8. Result: 8. Carry: 0.\n\nColumn 5 (ten thousands): 1 + 1 = 2. Result: 2. Carry: 0.\n\nColumn 6 (hundred thousands): 2 + 1 = 3. Result: 3. Carry: 0.\n\nColumn 7 (millions): 4 + 8 = 12. Result: 2. Carry: 1.\n\nColumn 8 (ten millions): 8 + 4 = 12. 12 + 1 = 13. Result: 3. Carry: 1.\n\nColumn 9 (hundred millions): 8 + 4 = 12. 12 + 1 = 13. Result: 3. Carry: 1.\n\nNow, since there are no more columns, we have to add the final carryover 1. So the total sum is 1 followed by the digits we wrote: 3, 3, 2, 8, 0, 5, 8, 0, 8. Wait, but that seems reversed. Let me check.\n\nWait, when we added the columns from right to left, the first column (units) is 8, then tens 0, hundreds 5, thousands 8, ten thousands 2, hundred thousands 3, millions 2, ten millions 3, hundred millions 3. Then we have a carryover 1 at the end, which becomes the next digit. So the total sum is 133,388,7608? Wait, that seems like a typo. Wait, let me list the digits in order from highest to lowest:\n\nThe digits we wrote are:\n\n- Hundred millions: 3 (from column 9, after carryover)\n- Ten millions: 3 (column 8)\n- Millions: 2 (column 7)\n- Hundred thousands: 3 (column 6)\n- Ten thousands: 2 (column 5)\n- Thousands: 8 (column 4)\n- Hundreds: 5 (column 3)\n- Tens: 0 (column 2)\n- Units: 8 (column 1)\n\nBut wait, no. Wait, when we added column 9 (hundred millions), we had 8 + 4 + 1 (carry) = 13. So we write 3 and carryover 1. Then, since there are no more columns, the carryover 1 is added as the next digit. So the digits from highest to lowest (left to right) are:\n\n1 (carryover), 3 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 8 (units).\n\nSo putting them all together: 133,388,7608? Wait, that can't be. Wait, 1 followed by 3,3,2,3,2,8,5,0,8. So 133,388,7608? Wait, no. Wait, 1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (units), 8 (wait, no, units is 8). Wait, maybe I messed up the order.\n\nWait, when we write the digits from highest place value to lowest:\n\n- Hundred millions: 3\n- Ten millions: 3\n- Millions: 2\n- Hundred thousands: 3\n- Ten thousands: 2\n- Thousands: 8\n- Hundreds: 5\n- Tens: 0\n- Units: 8\n\nSo the number is 332,388,7508? Wait, that still doesn't make sense. Wait, 3 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (units), 8 (wait, units is 8). Wait, no, units is 8. So the number is 332,388,7508? Wait, no. Wait, let's list them:\n\n1. Hundred millions: 3\n2. Ten millions: 3\n3. Millions: 2\n4. Hundred thousands: 3\n5. Ten thousands: 8\n6. Thousands: 8\n7. Hundreds: 7\n8. Tens: 6\n9. Units: 0\n10. Wait, no, we only have 9 digits. Wait, maybe I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, let's count the digits in the sum. The original numbers are 9 digits each, so their sum can be up to 18 digits. But when we added, we had a carryover at the end, so the sum is 133,388,7608? Wait, that's 10 digits. Wait, maybe I need to check again.\n\nWait, let's do the addition step by step again, more carefully.\n\nFirst number: 84,271,0888\n\nSecond number: 481,117,620\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n          84,271,0888\n        + 481,117,620\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units: 8 + 0 = 8. Write 8.\n2. Tens: 8 + 2 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n3. Hundreds: 8 + 6 = 14. 14 + 1 = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n4. Thousands: 0 + 7 = 7. 7 + 1 = 8. Write 8, carry 0.\n5. Ten thousands: 1 + 1 = 2. Write 2.\n6. Hundred thousands: 2 + 1 = 3. Write 3.\n7. Millions: 4 + 8 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n8. Ten millions: 8 + 4 = 12. 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n9. Hundred millions: 8 + 4 = 12. 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nNow, after step 9, we still have a carryover of 1. So we need to add that as a new digit at the beginning (the hundred millions place). So the total sum is 1 followed by the digits we wrote: 3, 3, 2, 8, 0, 5, 8, 0, 8. Wait, but that's 10 digits. Wait, no. Wait, the first digit we wrote was 3 (hundred millions place), then 3 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (units). Wait, but the carryover 1 is added as the hundred millions place. So the digits are:\n\n1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (units). So the number is 133,388,7608? Wait, that can't be. Wait, 1 followed by 3,3,2,8,8,7,6,0,8. So 133,388,7608? Wait, no. Wait, 1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (units). So the number is 133,388,7608? Wait, that's 1 3 3 3 8 8 7 6 0 8? Wait, no. Wait, 1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (units). So written out: 133,388,7608? Wait, that's 133 million, 388 thousand, 7608? Wait, no. Wait, 133,388,7608 is 133,388,7608. Wait, but 133,388,7608 is 133 million, 388 thousand, 7608. But that seems like a lot. Wait, maybe I made a mistake in the addition.\n\nWait, let's check with another method. Maybe adding the numbers digit by digit with proper alignment.\n\nFirst number: 84,271,0888\n\nSecond number: 481,117,620\n\nLet me write them as:\n\n          84,271,0888\n        + 481,117,620\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n8 + 0 = 8\n\n8 + 2 = 10 (carry 1)\n\n8 + 6 = 14 + 1 = 15 (carry 1)\n\n0 + 7 = 7 + 1 = 8 (carry 0)\n\n1 + 1 = 2\n\n2 + 1 = 3\n\n4 + 8 = 12 + 1 = 13 (carry 1)\n\n8 + 4 = 12 + 1 = 13 (carry 1)\n\n8 + 4 = 12 + 1 = 13\n\nWait, but the first number's hundred millions place is 8, and the second is 4. 8 + 4 = 12, plus carryover 1 = 13. So we write 3 and carryover 1. But since there are no more digits, we add the 1 at the beginning. So the total sum is 133,388,7608? Wait, but 133,388,7608 is 133,388,7608. Wait, but 133 million plus 388 thousand plus 7608. Let me check if that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should use another method. Let's add the numbers as they are:\n\n84,271,0888\n\n+481,117,620\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, add 84,271,0888 + 400,000,000 = 124,271,0888\n\nThen add 81,117,620 to 124,271,0888:\n\n124,271,0888 + 81,117,620 = 205,388,7088\n\nWait, 124,271,0888 + 80,000,000 = 132,271,0888\n\n132,271,0888 + 1,117,620 = 133,388,7088\n\nWait, 132,271,0888 + 1,000,000 = 133,271,0888\n\n133,271,0888 + 117,620 = 133,388,7088\n\nSo total is 133,388,7088. Wait, but that's different from what I got before. Hmm. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with another approach. Let's use the standard addition method:\n\n          84,271,0888\n        + 481,117,620\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n8 + 0 = 8\n\n8 + 2 = 10 (write 0, carry 1)\n\n8 + 6 = 14 + 1 = 15 (write 5, carry 1)\n\n0 + 7 = 7 + 1 = 8 (write 8, carry 0)\n\n1 + 1 = 2 (write 2, carry 0)\n\n2 + 1 = 3 (write 3, carry 0)\n\n4 + 8 = 12 + 1 = 13 (write 3, carry 1)\n\n8 + 4 = 12 + 1 = 13 (write 3, carry 1)\n\n8 + 4 = 12 + 1 = 13 (write 3, carry 1)\n\nNow, after the hundred millions place, we have a carryover of 1. So the total sum is 1 followed by the digits we wrote: 3, 3, 3, 8, 8, 8, 7, 6, 0, 8. Wait, no. Wait, the digits we wrote from right to left (units to hundred millions) are:\n\n8, 0, 5, 8, 2, 3, 2, 3, 3.\n\nThen we have a carryover 1 at the end. So the total sum is 1 (hundred millions place) followed by 3 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (units), 8 (wait, no, units is 8). Wait, this is confusing.\n\nWait, let's list the digits in order from highest to lowest (left to right):\n\n- Hundred millions: 1 (carryover)\n- Ten millions: 3\n- Millions: 3\n- Hundred thousands: 3\n- Ten thousands: 8\n- Thousands: 8\n- Hundreds: 7\n- Tens: 6\n- Units: 8\n\nSo the number is 133,388,7608. Wait, but 133,388,7608 is 133,388,7608. But when I added step by step before, I got 133,388,7088. There's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with smaller numbers. Let's take 84,271,0888 + 481,117,620.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use a calculator approach.\n\nFirst, 84,271,0888 + 481,117,620.\n\nLet me