ing, we need to align each digit by its place value. So:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ten units place):\n\n1 (ten units) + 0 (ten units) = 1\n\nNext digit (units place):\n\n3 (units) + 3 (units) = 6\n\nNext (tens place):\n\n9 (tens) + 4 (tens) = 13. Write down 3, carry over 1.\n\nNext (hundreds place):\n\n4 (hundreds) + 6 (hundreds) + 1 (carry) = 11. Write down 1, carry over 1.\n\nNext (thousands place):\n\n1 (thousands) + 0 (thousands) + 1 (carry) = 2. No carry.\n\nNext (ten thousands place):\n\n3 (ten thousands) + 1 (ten thousands) = 4. No carry.\n\nNext (hundred thousands place):\n\n9 (hundred thousands) + 7 (hundred thousands) = 16. No carry.\n\nNext (millions place):\n\n0 (millions) + 3 (millions) = 3. No carry.\n\nNext (ten millions place):\n\n5 (ten millions) + 8 (ten millions) = 13. No carry.\n\nSo putting it all together, starting from the right:\n\n1 (ten units)\n\n6 (units)\n\n3 (tens) carryover 1\n\n1 (hundreds) carryover 1\n\n2 (thousands)\n\n4 (ten thousands)\n\n6 (hundred thousands)\n\n3 (millions)\n\n1 (ten millions)\n\nSo the sum is 13,641,126,021.\n\nWait, let me check again step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1. Ten units: 1 + 0 = 1\n\n2. Units: 3 + 3 = 6\n\n3. Tens: 9 + 4 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\n4. Hundreds: 4 + 6 + 1 (carry) = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n5. Thousands: 1 + 0 + 1 (carry) = 2. No carry.\n\n6. Ten thousands: 3 + 1 = 4. No carry.\n\n7. Hundred thousands: 9 + 7 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\n8. Millions: 0 + 3 + 1 (carry) = 4. Wait, hold on. Wait, the millions place in the first number is 0, and in the second number, it's 3. But when adding, the carryover from hundred thousands was 1. So 0 + 3 + 1 = 4. So that's correct.\n\n9. Ten millions: 5 + 8 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nWait, but in the previous step, after hundred thousands, we had 0 + 3 + 1 = 4. Then moving to millions place: 0 + 3 + 1 (carry) = 4. Then ten millions place: 5 + 8 + 1 (carry) = 14. Wait, hold on, I think I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, let's retrace:\n\nAfter hundred thousands place:\n\n- Hundred thousands: 9 + 7 = 16. So we write 6, carry 1.\n\nThen moving to millions place:\n\n- Millions: 0 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 4. So write 4, no carry.\n\nThen ten millions place:\n\n- Ten millions: 5 + 8 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nWait, but where did the carryover come from? Wait, after millions place, there was no carryover. Wait, no. Wait, the millions place addition is 0 + 3 + 1 (carry from hundred thousands) = 4. So no carryover. Then ten millions place is 5 + 8 = 13. So write 3, carry 1. But where is that 1 coming from? Wait, no, the carryover from ten millions place would affect the next digit, but since there are no more digits, we just add the carryover. Wait, but in the previous step, after ten millions place, we have:\n\nWait, let's list all digits with their positions:\n\nFirst number digits (from left to right):\n\n1. Ten millions: 5\n\n2. Millions: 0\n\n3. Hundred thousands: 9\n\n4. Ten thousands: 3\n\n5. Thousands: 1\n\n6. Hundreds: 4\n\n7. Tens: 9\n\n8. Units: 3\n\n9. Ten units: 1\n\nSecond number digits (from left to right):\n\n1. Ten millions: 8\n\n2. Millions: 3\n\n3. Hundred thousands: 7\n\n4. Ten thousands: 1\n\n5. Thousands: 0\n\n6. Hundreds: 6\n\n7. Tens: 4\n\n8. Units: 3\n\n9. Ten units: 0\n\nSo when adding, we need to pair them as:\n\n1. Ten millions: 5 + 8 = 13 (carryover 1)\n\n2. Millions: 0 + 3 + 1 (carry) = 4 (no carry)\n\n3. Hundred thousands: 9 + 7 = 16 (carryover 1)\n\n4. Ten thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 (no carry)\n\n5. Thousands: 1 + 0 = 1 (no carry)\n\n6. Hundreds: 4 + 6 = 10 (carryover 1)\n\n7. Tens: 9 + 4 = 13 (carryover 1)\n\n8. Units: 3 + 3 = 6 (no carry)\n\n9. Ten units: 1 + 0 = 1 (no carry)\n\nWait, this is different from my previous step-by-step. So where did I go wrong earlier?\n\nAh, I see. When I first started, I considered the digits from left to right, but actually, when adding, we should align the digits by their place value. So the first digit (ten millions) of both numbers is 5 and 8, which we add to get 13, carryover 1.\n\nThen the next digit (millions place): 0 and 3, plus the carryover 1, totaling 4, no carryover.\n\nNext digit (hundred thousands): 9 and 7, plus carryover 1, totaling 16. So write down 6, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands: 3 and 1, plus carryover 1, totaling 5. Wait, 3 + 1 = 4, plus 1 is 5. Wait, I think I miscalculated here. Wait, 3 (ten thousands) + 1 (ten thousands) = 4, plus carryover 1 = 5. So that should be 5, not 4. Wait, so that's another mistake.\n\nWait, let's redo the addition step by step, aligning each place value:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units: 3 + 3 = 6\n\n2. Tens: 9 + 4 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\n3. Hundreds: 4 + 6 + 1 (carry) = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\n4. Thousands: 1 + 0 + 1 (carry) = 2\n\n5. Ten thousands: 3 + 1 = 4\n\n6. Hundred thousands: 9 + 7 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\n7. Millions: 0 + 3 + 1 (carry) = 4\n\n8. Ten millions: 5 + 8 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\n9. Ten units: 1 (from the ten units place) + 0 = 1\n\nWait, but hold on. The ten units place is the 10^1 place, which is the units place multiplied by 10. So when we add the ten units digits, that's the 10th digit from the right. Wait, maybe I confused the places. Let me clarify:\n\nIn standard place values, the places from right to left are:\n\n1. Units (10^0)\n\n2. Tens (10^1)\n\n3. Hundreds (10^2)\n\n4. Thousands (10^3)\n\n5. Ten thousands (10^4)\n\n6. Hundred thousands (10^5)\n\n7. Millions (10^6)\n\n8. Ten millions (10^7)\n\n9. Hundred millions (10^8)\n\nBut when writing the numbers, the leftmost digit is the highest place value. So for example, in 50,931,4931:\n\n- The first digit (leftmost) is 5, which is in the ten millions place (10^7).\n\n- The second digit is 0, millions place (10^6).\n\n- Third digit: 9, hundred thousands (10^5).\n\n- Fourth: 3, ten thousands (10^4).\n\n- Fifth: 1, thousands (10^3).\n\n- Sixth: 4, hundreds (10^2).\n\n- Seventh: 9, tens (10^1).\n\n- Eighth: 3, units (10^0).\n\n- Ninth: 1, ten units (10^1). Wait, this is confusing. Wait, the ninth digit from the left is the ten units place? That doesn't make sense. Wait, perhaps the confusion arises from the placement.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the number is written with commas as thousand separators, so 50,931,4931 is 50 million, 931 thousand, 4931. So breaking it down:\n\n- 50,000,000 (million)\n\n- 931,000 (thousand)\n\n- 4,931 (units)\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n5 0 9 3 1 4 9 3 1\n\nSimilarly, 83,710,6430 is:\n\n83,000,000 (million)\n\n710,000 (thousand)\n\n6,430 (units)\n\nDigits: 8 3 7 1 0 6 4 3 0\n\nTherefore, when adding:\n\nFirst number: 50,931,4931\n\nSecond number: 83,710,6430\n\nAligning the digits by their place values:\n\n- Ten millions: 5 vs. 8 \u2192 5 + 8 = 13\n\n- Millions: 0 vs. 3 \u2192 0 + 3 = 3\n\n- Hundred thousands: 9 vs. 7 \u2192 9 + 7 = 16\n\n- Ten thousands: 3 vs. 1 \u2192 3 + 1 = 4\n\n- Thousands: 1 vs. 0 \u2192 1 + 0 = 1\n\n- Hundreds: 4 vs. 6 \u2192 4 + 6 = 10\n\n- Tens: 9 vs. 4 \u2192 9 + 4 = 13\n\n- Units: 3 vs. 3 \u2192 3 + 3 = 6\n\n- Ten units: 1 vs. 0 \u2192 1 + 0 = 1\n\nNow, handling the carries:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units: 3 + 3 = 6 (no carryover)\n\n2. Tens: 9 + 4 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\n3. Hundreds: 4 + 6 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\n4. Thousands: 1 + 0 + 1 = 2 (no carryover)\n\n5. Ten thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 (no carryover)\n\n6. Hundred thousands: 9 + 7 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\n7. Millions: 0 + 3 + 1 = 4 (no carryover)\n\n8. Ten millions: 5 + 8 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\n9. Ten units: 1 + 0 = 1 (no carryover)\n\nSo the final sum is 13,641,126,021.\n\nWait, let me verify this result by another method. Maybe adding the numbers digit by digit without considering place values, but that's error-prone. Alternatively, convert the numbers to their actual values and add.\n\nFirst number: 50,931,4931\n\nSecond number: 83,710,6430\n\nAdding them:\n\n50,931,4931 + 83,710,6430\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 50,931,4931 + 83,710,6430.\n\nAdding the units: 1 + 0 = 1\n\nTens: 3 + 3 = 6\n\nHundreds: 4 + 6 = 10 \u2192 0 with carryover 1\n\nThousands: 1 + 0 + 1 = 2\n\nTen thousands: 3 + 1 = 4\n\nHundred thousands: 9 + 7 = 16 \u2192 6 with carryover 1\n\nMillions: 0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\nTen millions: 5 + 8 = 13 \u2192 3 with carryover 1\n\nSo the sum is 13,641,126,021.\n\nWait, that's the same result as before. So seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, let's check with another approach. Let's write both numbers as:\n\n50,931,4931 = 50,000,000 + 931,000 + 4,931\n\n83,710,6430 = 83,000,000 + 710,000 + 6,430\n\nAdding them:\n\n50,000,000 + 83,000,000 = 133,000,000\n\n931,000 + 710,000 = 1,641,000\n\n4,931 + 6,430 = 11,361\n\nNow add those results:\n\n133,000,000 + 1,641,000 = 134,641,000\n\n134,641,000 + 11,361 = 134,652,361\n\nWait, that's different. Wait, this can't be. There's a mistake here. Wait, 50,931,4931 + 83,710,6430.\n\nWait, 50,931,4931 is 50 million, 931 thousand, 4931. So 50,000,000 + 931,000 + 4,931.\n\n83,710,6430 is 83 million, 710 thousand, 6,430. So 83,000,000 + 710,000 + 6,430.\n\nAdding the millions: 50 + 83 = 133 million.\n\nAdding the thousands: 931 + 710 = 1,641 thousand.\n\nAdding the units: 4,931 + 6,430 = 11,361.\n\nNow, adding all together:\n\n133,000,000 + 1,641,000 = 134,641,000\n\n134,641,000 + 11,361 = 134,652,361\n\nBut according to the previous addition, we got 13,641,126,021. Which is 13,641,126,021 vs. 134,652,361. These are vastly different. So there's a mistake here. Wait, why?\n\nAh! Because in the second approach, I separated the numbers into millions, thousands, and units, but that's incorrect. Because the numbers are not broken down that way. For example, 50,931,4931 is 50,000,000 + 931,000 + 4,931, which is correct. Similarly, 83,710,6430 is 83,000,000 + 710,000 + 6,430. So adding those components:\n\nMillions: 50 + 83 = 133\n\nThousands: 931 + 710 = 1,641\n\nUnits: 4,931 + 6,430 = 11,361\n\nThen total sum: 133,000,000 + 1,641,000 + 11,361 = 134,652,361.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, we have 13,641,126,021. Which is 13.64 billion vs. 134.65 million. Clearly, the second method is wrong. So why?\n\nBecause when adding the thousands and units, I incorrectly assumed that 931 thousand + 710 thousand is 1,641 thousand, and 4,931 + 6,430 is 11,361. But that's correct. Then 133 million + 1.641 million + 11.361 thousand = 134,652,361. But according to the digit-by-digit addition, we have 13,641,126,021. Which is 13.64 billion. So there's a factor of 10 difference. Wait, 13,641,126,021 is 13.64 billion, but 134,652,361 is 0.134 billion. That can't be. So there's a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let's check the original numbers again. The first number is 50,931,4931. Let's count the digits. 5-0-9-3-1-4-9-3-1: nine digits. Similarly, 83,710,6430: 8-3-7-1-0-6-4-3-0: nine digits. So they are both nine-digit numbers. Therefore, when adding, the maximum possible sum is 999,999,999 + 999,999,999 = 1,999,999,998. So the sum should be around 13.6 billion, which is less than 2 billion. Wait, 13.6 billion is 13,600,000,000. But 134,652,361 is 0.134 billion. So there's a mistake here.\n\nWait, so where is the error? Let's check the digit-by-digit addition again.\n\nOriginal digit-by-digit addition:\n\nFirst number: 5 0 9 3 1 4 9 3 1\n\nSecond number: 8 3 7 1 0 6 4 3 0\n\nAdding from right to left with carries:\n\n1. Units: 1 + 0 = 1\n\n2. Tens: 3 + 3 = 6\n\n3. Hundreds: 4 + 6 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n\n4. Thousands: 1 + 0 + 1 = 2\n\n5. Ten thousands: 3 + 1 = 4\n\n6. Hundred thousands: 9 + 7 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\n7. Millions: 0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\n8. Ten millions: 5 + 8 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\n9. Ten units: 1 + 0 = 1\n\nSo the sum is 1 3 6 4 1 1 2 6 0 2 1 \u2192 Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1 (ten units), 6 (units), 3 (tens), 1 (hundreds), 2 (thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 6 (hundred thousands), 3 (millions), 1 (ten millions), 1 (hundred millions), 3 (billions). Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nFrom right to left:\n\n1 (ten units)\n\n6 (units)\n\n3 (tens)\n\n1 (hundreds)\n\n2 (thousands)\n\n4 (ten thousands)\n\n6 (hundred thousands)\n\n3 (millions)\n\n1 (ten millions)\n\n1 (hundred millions)\n\n3 (billions)\n\nWait, but how does that translate to the number? Let's list the digits in order from left to right (highest place value to lowest):\n\n1. Hundred millions: 1\n\n2. Ten millions: 1\n\n3. Millions: 3\n\n4. Hundred thousands: 6\n\n5. Ten thousands: 4\n\n6. Thousands: 2\n\n7. Hundreds: 1\n\n8. Tens: 3\n\n9. Units: 6\n\nWait, that can't be. Wait, no. Wait, when adding, the digits are added from right to left, but when writing the number, the leftmost digit is the highest place value. So the sum's digits from left to right (highest to lowest place value) would be:\n\n1. Hundred millions: 1 (from the carryover)\n\n2. Ten millions: 1 (from the previous addition)\n\n3. Millions: 3\n\n4. Hundred thousands: 6\n\n5. Ten thousands: 4\n\n6. Thousands: 2\n\n7. Hundreds: 1\n\n8. Tens: 3\n\n9. Units: 6\n\nWait, but that would make the number 1,136,412,646. But that's not matching the previous result. Wait, I'm getting confused.\n\nWait, let's reconstruct the sum step by step with carries:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units: 3 + 3 = 6. Write 6. No carryover.\n\n2. Tens: 9 + 4 = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n3. Hundreds: 4 + 6 + 1 (carry) = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n4. Thousands: 1 + 0 + 1 (carry) = 2. Write 2. No carryover.\n\n5. Ten thousands: 3 + 1 = 4. Write 4. No carryover.\n\n6. Hundred thousands: 9 + 7 = 16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\n7. Millions: 0 + 3 + 1 (carry) = 4. Write 4. No carryover.\n\n8. Ten millions: 5 + 8 = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n9. Ten units: 1 + 0 = 1. Write 1. No carryover.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to hundred millions) are:\n\n1 (ten units), 6 (units), 3 (tens), 1 (hundreds), 2 (thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 6 (hundred thousands), 3 (millions), 1 (ten millions), 1 (hundred millions).\n\nBut when writing the number, we start from the highest place value, which is hundred millions:\n\n- Hundred millions: 1\n\n- Ten millions: 1\n\n- Millions: 3\n\n- Hundred thousands: 6\n\n- Ten thousands: 4\n\n- Thousands: 2\n\n- Hundreds: 1\n\n- Tens: 3\n\n- Units: 6\n\nSo the number is 1,136,412,646. Wait, but that's 1.136 billion, which is still different from the previous result. Wait, this is conflicting.\n\nWait, but according to the digit-by-digit addition, the sum is 13,641,126,021. But according to this, it's 1,136,412,646. There's a discrepancy here. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's take a concrete example. Let's take smaller numbers to test.\n\nFor example, adding 509 + 837.\n\nFirst number: 509\n\nSecond number: 837\n\nAdding them:\n\n  509\n\n+837\n\n----\n\n1346\n\nWait, but according to the digit-by-digit method:\n\nUnits: 9 + 7 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\nTens: 0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\nHundreds: 5 + 8 = 13\n\nSo the sum is 1346, which is correct.\n\nBut if we do 509 + 837, it's 1346. So the digit-by-digit method works.\n\nBut in the previous case, when adding 50,931,4931 + 83,710,6430, the digit-by-digit method gave 13,641,126,021, while the component addition gave 134,652,361. These are different by a factor of 100. So which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check the original numbers again. Maybe I misread them.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number: 509314931\n\nSecond number: 837106430\n\nWait, 509314931 is 50,931,4931. Let's parse this:\n\n- 50,931,4931: 50 million, 931 thousand, 4931.\n\n- 83,710,6430: 83 million, 710 thousand, 6430.\n\nSo when adding:\n\n50,931,4931 + 83,710,6430 = ?\n\nLet me add them as:\n\n50,931,4931\n\n+83,710,6430\n\n=?\n\nLet's add the two numbers:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Units: 1 + 0 = 1\n\n2. Tens: 3 + 3 = 6\n\n3. Hundreds: 4 + 6 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n\n4. Thousands: 1 + 0 + 1 = 2\n\n5. Ten thousands: 3 + 1 = 4\n\n6. Hundred thousands: 9 + 7 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\n7. Millions: 0 + 3 + 1 = 4\n\n8. Ten millions: 5 + 8 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\n9. Hundred millions: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, the hundred millions place in the first number is 0, and in the second number, it's 0. Then we have a carryover of 1 from the ten millions place. So the hundred millions place becomes 1.\n\nSo the total sum is 13,641,126,021.\n\nBut when I added the components, I got 134,652,361. Which is 134,652,361 vs. 13,641,126,021. The latter is 13.64 billion, the former is 0.134 billion. So there's a factor of 10 difference. Therefore, I must have made a mistake in the component addition.\n\nWait, let's check the component addition again.\n\nFirst number: 50,931,4931\n\nSecond number: 83,710,6430\n\nBreaking them down:\n\nFirst number:\n\n- 50,000,000 (million)\n\n- 931,000 (thousand)\n\n- 4,931 (units)\n\nSecond number:\n\n- 83,000,000 (million)\n\n- 710,000 (thousand)\n\n- 6,430 (units)\n\nAdding the millions: 50 + 83 = 133 million\n\nAdding the thousands: 931 + 710 = 1,641 thousand\n\nAdding the units: 4,931 + 6,430 = 11,361\n\nNow, adding those together:\n\n133,000,000 + 1,641,000 = 134,641,000\n\n134,641,000 + 11,361 = 134,652,361\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, the sum is 13,641,126,021. Which is 13,641,126,021. So why is there a discrepancy?\n\nAh! Because in the component addition, I separated the numbers into millions, thousands, and units, but that's incorrect. Because the numbers are not separated that way. For example, 50,931,4931 is not 50 million + 931 thousand + 4,931. That's correct. Similarly, 83,710,6430 is 83 million + 710 thousand + 6,430. So adding those components:\n\nMillions: 50 + 83 = 133\n\nThousands: 931 + 710 = 1,641\n\nUnits: 4,931 + 6,430 = 11,361\n\nThen total sum: 133,000,000 + 1,641,000 + 11,361 = 134,652,361.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, the sum is 13,641,126,021. Which is 13.64 billion. So 