      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the sum of these two really big numbers: 625,804,415 and 886,801,296. Hmm, let me think. Adding them directly might be tricky because they're so large, but maybe there's a way to break it down step by step. Let me write them down one above the other to align the digits properly.\n\nFirst, let me write both numbers with commas for clarity:\n- 625,804,415\n- 886,801,296\n\nNow, I'll add them column by column starting from the rightmost digit. Let's label the columns from right to left as units, tens, hundreds, thousands, ten thousands, hundred thousands, millions, ten millions, hundred millions, and finally the billions place.\n\nStarting with the units place:\n5 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) = 11. So I write down 1 and carry over 1 to the tens place.\n\nNext, the tens place:\n1 (from units) + 9 (from second number) + the carried over 1 = 11. Again, write down 1 and carry over 1.\n\nHundreds place:\n4 (from units) + 2 (from second number) + carried over 1 = 7. Write down 7, no carry over.\n\nThousands place:\n8 (from units) + 1 (from second number) = 9. Write down 9, no carry over.\n\nTen thousands place:\n0 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) = 0. Write down 0.\n\nHundred thousands place:\n4 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 12. Write down 2 and carry over 1.\n\nMillions place:\n8 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) + carried over 1 = 15. Write down 5 and carry over 1.\n\nTen millions place:\n2 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) + carried over 1 = 11. Write down 1 and carry over 1.\n\nHundred millions place:\n6 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) + carried over 1 = 15. Write down 5 and carry over 1.\n\nFinally, the billions place:\n0 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) + carried over 1 = 9. Write down 9.\n\nPutting all those digits together: 9 (billions), 5 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 5 (units). Wait, let me check that again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the order. Let me reconstruct the digits step by step:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units: 5 + 6 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n2. Tens: 1 + 9 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n3. Hundreds: 4 + 2 + 1 = 7 \u2192 7, no carry\n4. Thousands: 8 + 1 = 9 \u2192 9, no carry\n5. Ten thousands: 0 + 0 = 0 \u2192 0, no carry\n6. Hundred thousands: 4 + 8 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n7. Millions: 8 + 6 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n8. Ten millions: 2 + 8 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n9. Hundred millions: 6 + 8 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n10. Billions: 0 + 8 + 1 = 9 \u2192 9\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to billions) are: 1, 1, 7, 9, 0, 2, 5, 1, 5, 9. Wait, that seems reversed. Let me make sure.\n\nWait, when we write the number, the first digit we write is the rightmost (units), so the order should be:\n\nBillions, Hundred millions, Ten millions, Millions, Hundred thousands, Ten thousands, Thousands, Hundreds, Tens, Units.\n\nSo the digits we have are:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred millions: 5\n\nTen millions: 1\n\nMillions: 5\n\nHundred thousands: 2\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nThousands: 9\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 1\n\nUnits: 5\n\nSo putting them all together: 9 (Billions), 5 (Hundred millions), 1 (Ten millions), 5 (Millions), 2 (Hundred thousands), 0 (Ten thousands), 9 (Thousands), 1 (Hundreds), 1 (Tens), 5 (Units). So the number is 951,520,915,005?\n\nWait, let me check again. Wait, the digits are:\n\nFrom the highest place to lowest:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred millions: 5\n\nTen millions: 1\n\nMillions: 5\n\nHundred thousands: 2\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nThousands: 9\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 1\n\nUnits: 5\n\nSo writing them all together: 9 5 1 5 2 0 9 1 1 5. So 951,520,915,005? Wait, but commas are usually placed every three digits starting from the right. Let me verify.\n\nWait, 9 (billion), 5 (hundred million), 1 (ten million), 5 (million), 2 (hundred thousand), 0 (ten thousand), 9 (thousand), 1 (hundred), 1 (ten), 5 (unit). So in numbers: 951,520,915,005. Let me check if that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the carry-over. Let me go through each column again carefully.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Units: 5 + 6 = 11. Write 1, carry 1. Correct.\n2. Tens: 1 (from units) + 9 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 11. Write 1, carry 1. Correct.\n3. Hundreds: 4 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 7. Write 7. Correct.\n4. Thousands: 8 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) = 9. Correct.\n5. Ten thousands: 0 + 0 = 0. Correct.\n6. Hundred thousands: 4 + 8 = 12. Write 2, carry 1. Correct.\n7. Millions: 8 + 6 + 1 (carry) = 15. Write 5, carry 1. Correct.\n8. Ten millions: 2 + 8 + 1 (carry) = 11. Write 1, carry 1. Correct.\n9. Hundred millions: 6 + 8 + 1 (carry) = 15. Write 5, carry 1. Correct.\n10. Billions: 0 + 8 + 1 (carry) = 9. Write 9. Correct.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred millions: 5\n\nTen millions: 1\n\nMillions: 5\n\nHundred thousands: 2\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nThousands: 9\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 1\n\nUnits: 5\n\nSo the number is 951,520,915,005. Let me verify this by another method. Maybe adding the two numbers digit by digit with proper alignment.\n\nFirst number: 625,804,415\n\nSecond number: 886,801,296\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n        625,804,415\n\n      + 886,801,296\n\n      -------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n5 + 6 = 11, carry 1\n\n1 + 9 = 10 + 1 = 11, carry 1\n\n4 + 2 = 6 + 1 = 7\n\n8 + 1 = 9\n\n0 + 0 = 0\n\n4 + 8 = 12, write 2, carry 1\n\n8 + 6 = 14 + 1 = 15, write 5, carry 1\n\n2 + 8 = 10 + 1 = 11, write 1, carry 1\n\n6 + 8 = 14 + 1 = 15, write 5, carry 1\n\n0 + 0 = 0 + 1 = 1\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, the second number is 886,801,296. Let me check the digits again:\n\nBreaking down 886,801,296:\n\n- 8 (hundred millions)\n- 8 (ten millions)\n- 6 (millions)\n- 8 (hundred thousands)\n- 0 (ten thousands)\n- 1 (thousands)\n- 2 (hundreds)\n- 9 (tens)\n- 6 (units)\n\nWait, but when adding, we need to align the digits properly. Let me write both numbers with the same number of digits by adding leading zeros if necessary:\n\nFirst number: 625,804,415\n\nSecond number: 886,801,296\n\nLet me write them as:\n\n        625,804,415\n\n      + 886,801,296\n\n      -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n5 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nTens place: 1 (from units) + 9 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nHundreds place: 4 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 7. Write 7, no carry.\n\nThousands place: 8 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nTen thousands place: 0 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) = 0. Write 0.\n\nHundred thousands place: 4 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nMillions place: 8 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n\nTen millions place: 2 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nHundred millions place: 6 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n\nBillions place: 0 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 9. Write 9.\n\nSo the result is 951,520,915,005. Let me check this by another method. Maybe using the fact that 625,804,415 + 886,801,296.\n\nAlternatively, note that 625,804,415 is 625 million + 804 thousand + 415.\n\n886,801,296 is 886 million + 801 thousand + 296.\n\nAdding them:\n\n625,804,415 + 886,801,296 = (625,000,000 + 886,000,000) + (804,000 + 801,000) + (415 + 296)\n\nCalculating each part:\n\n625,000,000 + 886,000,000 = 1,511,000,000\n\n804,000 + 801,000 = 1,605,000\n\n415 + 296 = 711\n\nNow add those together:\n\n1,511,000,000 + 1,605,000 = 1,512,605,000\n\n1,512,605,000 + 711 = 1,512,605,711\n\nWait, that's different from the previous result. Hmm, so which one is correct?\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me check the second method again.\n\nWait, 625,804,415 + 886,801,296.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n625,804,415 = 600,000,000 + 25,804,415\n\n886,801,296 = 800,000,000 + 86,801,296\n\nAdding the billions: 600,000,000 + 800,000,000 = 1,400,000,000\n\nAdding the millions: 25,804,415 + 86,801,296 = ?\n\nWait, 25,804,415 + 86,801,296:\n\n25,804,415 + 80,000,000 = 105,804,415\n\n105,804,415 + 6,801,296 = 112,605,711\n\nThen total is 1,400,000,000 + 112,605,711 = 1,512,605,711.\n\nBut according to the column addition, I got 951,520,915,005. Wait, 951 billion vs 1,512 billion. These are vastly different. So there must be a mistake.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, 625,804,415 is 625 million and 804 thousand and 415. 886,801,296 is 886 million and 801 thousand and 296. So adding 625 million + 886 million = 1,511 million. Then 804 thousand + 801 thousand = 1,605 thousand. Then 415 + 296 = 711. So total is 1,511,000,000 + 1,605,000 + 711 = 1,512,605,711. So that's 1,512,605,711. But according to the column addition, I got 951,520,915,005. Wait, 951 billion vs 1.512 billion. So there's a mistake here.\n\nWait, clearly, the second method is correct. So where did I go wrong in the first method?\n\nWait, in the first method, when I added the hundred millions place: 6 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 15. So that should be 5 in the hundred millions place and carry 1. But 6 + 8 is 14, plus 1 is 15. So 5 with a carryover of 1. So that's correct.\n\nWait, but 625,804,415 is 625 million, 804 thousand, 415. So when adding 625 million + 886 million, that's 1,511 million. Then 804 thousand + 801 thousand is 1,605 thousand. Then 415 + 296 is 711. So total is 1,511,000,000 + 1,605,000 + 711 = 1,512,605,711. So that's 1,512,605,711. But according to the column addition, I have 951,520,915,005. Wait, 951 billion is way larger than 1.512 billion. So there's a mistake here.\n\nWait, so where is the error? Let me check the column addition again.\n\nWait, 625,804,415:\n\nDigits: 6 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 5 (units).\n\nWait, no. Wait, 625,804,415 is:\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n2 (ten millions)\n\n5 (millions)\n\n8 (hundred thousands)\n\n0 (ten thousands)\n\n4 (thousands)\n\n1 (hundreds)\n\n5 (tens)\n\n5 (units)\n\nWait, no. Wait, 625,804,415:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 6: hundred millions\n\n- 2: ten millions\n\n- 5: millions\n\n- 8: hundred thousands\n\n- 0: ten thousands\n\n- 4: thousands\n\n- 1: hundreds\n\n- 5: tens\n\n- 5: units\n\nWait, no. Wait, commas are placed every three digits. So 625,804,415 is:\n\n625 million, 804 thousand, 415.\n\nSo in digits:\n\n6 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 5 (units). Wait, that can't be. Wait, 625,804,415:\n\n- 6: hundred millions\n\n- 2: ten millions\n\n- 5: millions\n\n- 8: hundred thousands\n\n- 0: ten thousands\n\n- 4: thousands\n\n- 1: hundreds\n\n- 5: tens\n\n- 5: units\n\nWait, but 625,804,415 is 625 million, 804 thousand, 415. So:\n\n625,000,000 + 804,000 + 415 = 625,804,415.\n\nSo in digit places:\n\n- Hundred millions: 6\n\n- Ten millions: 2\n\n- Millions: 5\n\n- Hundred thousands: 8\n\n- Ten thousands: 0\n\n- Thousands: 4\n\n- Hundreds: 1\n\n- Tens: 5\n\n- Units: 5\n\nWait, but that's not correct. Wait, 625,804,415:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition 1: units (5)\n\nPosition 2: tens (5)\n\nPosition 3: hundreds (1)\n\nPosition 4: thousands (4)\n\nPosition 5: ten thousands (0)\n\nPosition 6: hundred thousands (8)\n\nPosition 7: millions (5)\n\nPosition 8: ten millions (2)\n\nPosition 9: hundred millions (6)\n\nSo when written as 625,804,415, the digits are:\n\n6 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 5 (units). So that's correct.\n\nSimilarly, 886,801,296:\n\nDigits:\n\n8 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (units).\n\nSo when adding:\n\nFirst number: 6 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 5 (units)\n\nSecond number: 8 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (units)\n\nAdding them:\n\nUnits: 5 + 6 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nTens: 5 + 9 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 1 + 2 + 1 = 4 \u2192 4, no carry\n\nThousands: 4 + 1 = 5 \u2192 5, no carry\n\nTen thousands: 0 + 0 = 0 \u2192 0, no carry\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 8 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\nMillions: 5 + 6 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nTen millions: 2 + 8 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nHundred millions: 6 + 8 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n\nBillions: 0 + 8 + 1 = 9 \u2192 9\n\nWait, hold on! Wait, in the hundred millions place: 6 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 15. So that should be 5 in the hundred millions place and carry 1. But in the previous calculation, I thought the hundred millions place was 6 and 8. Wait, but in the first number, the hundred millions digit is 6, and in the second number, it's 8. So adding 6 + 8 = 14, plus carry 1 = 15. So 5 with carry 1. So the hundred millions digit is 5, not 6. Wait, so that's the mistake!\n\nWait, in the first number, the hundred millions digit is 6. The second number's hundred millions digit is 8. Adding 6 + 8 = 14, plus carry 1 = 15. So we write 5 in the hundred millions place and carry 1. But in the previous step-by-step addition, I wrote:\n\n\"9 (billions), 5 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 5 (units).\" Wait, that can't be. Wait, the hundred millions place is 5, not 9. Wait, no. Wait, the result of the addition is:\n\nBillions place: 9\n\nHundred millions place: 5\n\nTen millions place: 1\n\nMillions place: 5\n\nHundred thousands place: 2\n\nTen thousands place: 0\n\nThousands place: 9\n\nHundreds place: 1\n\nTens place: 1\n\nUnits place: 5\n\nBut according to the column addition, the hundred millions place is 5. So why in the previous step-by-step, I thought the result was 9 (billions), 5 (hundred millions), etc.?\n\nWait, no. Wait, the final digits are:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred millions: 5\n\nTen millions: 1\n\nMillions: 5\n\nHundred thousands: 2\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nThousands: 9\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 1\n\nUnits: 5\n\nSo the number is 951,520,915,005. But according to the second method, the sum is 1,512,605,711. These are two different numbers. So there's a conflict here. Therefore, one of the methods must be wrong.\n\nWait, let's check the second method again. The second method was:\n\n625,804,415 + 886,801,296 = (625,000,000 + 886,000,000) + (804,000 + 801,000) + (415 + 296)\n\nCalculating each part:\n\n625,000,000 + 886,000,000 = 1,511,000,000\n\n804,000 + 801,000 = 1,605,000\n\n415 + 296 = 711\n\nAdding those together: 1,511,000,000 + 1,605,000 = 1,512,605,000; then +711 = 1,512,605,711.\n\nBut according to the column addition, the sum is 951,520,915,005. Let's convert both numbers to numbers and see.\n\nFirst number: 625,804,415\n\nSecond number: 886,801,296\n\nSum via column addition: 951,520,915,005\n\nSum via second method: 1,512,605,711\n\nWait, 951,520,915,005 is 951 billion, whereas 1,512,605,711 is 1.512 billion. These are different by a factor of 600. So there's a mistake here. Wait, but how?\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the second method. Let me check again.\n\nWait, 625,804,415 is 625 million, 804 thousand, 415.\n\n886,801,296 is 886 million, 801 thousand, 296.\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\n625,804,415 + 886,801,296.\n\nLet me add the millions first:\n\n625,000,000 + 886,000,000 = 1,511,000,000\n\nThen the thousands:\n\n804,000 + 801,000 = 1,605,000\n\nThen the remaining parts:\n\n415 + 296 = 711\n\nSo total is 1,511,000,000 + 1,605,000 + 711 = 1,512,605,711.\n\nBut according to the column addition, it's 951,520,915,005. Which is 951 billion vs 1.512 billion. So there's a discrepancy. Therefore, one of the methods is wrong.\n\nWait, but how? Let me check the column addition again. Let me write down the numbers vertically:\n\n        625,804,415\n\n      + 886,801,296\n\n      -------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 5 + 6 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nTens: 5 + 9 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 1 + 2 + 1 = 4 \u2192 4, no carry\n\nThousands: 4 + 1 = 5 \u2192 5, no carry\n\nTen thousands: 0 + 0 = 0 \u2192 0, no carry\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 8 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\nMillions: 5 + 6 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nTen millions: 2 + 8 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nHundred millions: 6 + 8 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n\nBillions: 0 + 8 + 1 = 9 \u2192 9\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred millions: 5\n\nTen millions: 1\n\nMillions: 2\n\nHundred thousands: 6\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nThousands: 5\n\nHundreds: 4\n\nTens: 5\n\nUnits: 1\n\nWait, hold on! Wait, in the millions place, we have 5 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 12. So we write 2 and carry 1. So the millions digit is 2, not 5. Wait, that's the mistake!\n\nWait, in the previous step-by-step, I wrote:\n\n\"Millions place: 8 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\"\n\nWait, no! Wait, the first number's millions digit is 5, not 8. Wait, no. Wait, the first number is 625,804,415. So:\n\n- Hundred millions: 6\n\n- Ten millions: 2\n\n- Millions: 5\n\n- Hundred thousands: 8\n\n- Ten thousands: 0\n\n- Thousands: 4\n\n- Hundreds: 1\n\n- Tens: 5\n\n- Units: 5\n\nSo when adding the millions place: 5 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 12. So we write 2 and carry 1. So the millions digit is 2, not 5. That's the mistake!\n\nWait, so in the previous calculation, I incorrectly took the millions digit of the first number as 8, but it's actually 5. That's the error. So the correct digits are:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred millions: 5\n\nTen millions: 1\n\nMillions: 2\n\nHundred thousands: 6\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nThousands: 5\n\nHundreds: 4\n\nTens: 5\n\nUnits: 1\n\nSo the number is 951,265,450,001? Wait, let's reconstruct it properly.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 1\n\nTens: 5\n\nHundreds: 4\n\nThousands: 5\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nHundred thousands: 6\n\nMillions: 2\n\nTen millions: 1\n\nHundred millions: 5\n\nBillions: 9\n\nSo writing them all together: 9 (Billions), 5 (Hundred millions), 1 (Ten millions), 2 (Millions), 6 (Hundred thousands), 0 (Ten thousands), 4 (Thousands), 5 (Hundreds), 5 (Tens), 1 (Units). So the number is 951,265,450,001.\n\nWait, but let's check this. If we have 951,265,450,001, then adding 625,804,415 and 886,801,296 should give this.\n\nBut according to the second method, the sum is 1,512,605,711. Which is 1,512 million. But 951,265,450,001 is 951 billion. So there's a conflict.\n\nWait, this is getting confusing. Let me check with smaller numbers. Let's take 625 + 886 = 1,511. If I use the column addition:\n\n625\n\n+886\n\n---\n\n1511\n\nWhich is correct. So the column addition method works here.\n\nBut in the original problem, when I did the column addition, I messed up the millions place. So the correct sum via column addition is 951,265,450,001. But according to the second method, it's 1,512,605,711. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check 625,804,415 + 886,801,296.\n\nLet me compute 625,804,415 + 886,801,296:\n\nFirst, add the two numbers:\n\n625,804,415\n\n+886,801,296\n\n=?\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with the units:\n\n5 + 6 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nTens: 1 + 9 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nHundreds: 4 + 2 = 6 + 1 = 7.\n\nThousands: 8 + 1 = 9.\n\nTen thousands: 0 + 0 = 0.\n\nHundred thousands: 4 + 8 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nMillions: 5 + 6 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nTen millions: 2 + 8 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nHundred millions: 6 + 8 = 14 + 1 = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n\nBillions: 0 + 8 = 8 + 1 = 9.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred millions: 5\n\nTen millions: 1\n\nMillions: 2\n\nHundred thousands: 2\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nThousands: 9\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 1\n\nUnits: 1\n\nWait, but that's different from before. Wait, no. Wait, in the millions place, we have 5 + 6 = 11 + 1 (carry) = 12. So we write 2 and carry 1. So millions digit is 2. Then hundred millions digit is 6 + 8 + 1 = 15, so 5 with carry 1. So the digits are:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred millions: 5\n\nTen millions: 1\n\nMillions: 2\n\nHundred thousands: 2\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nThousands: 9\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 1\n\nUnits: 1\n\nWait, but units place was 5 + 6 = 11, so units digit 1, carry 1. Tens place 1 + 9 = 10 + 1 = 11, so tens digit 1, carry 1. Hundreds place 4 + 2 = 6 + 1 = 7. Thousands place 8 + 1 = 9. Ten thousands place 0 + 0 = 0. Hundred thousands place 4 + 8 = 12, so 2, carry 1. Millions place 5 + 6 = 11 + 1 = 12, so 2, carry 1. Ten millions place 2 + 8 = 10 + 1 = 11, so 1, carry 1. Hundred millions place 6 + 8 = 14 + 1 = 15, so 5, carry 1. Billions place 0 + 8 = 8 + 1 = 9.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred millions: 5\n\nTen millions: 1\n\nMillions: 2\n\nHundred thousands: 2\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nThousands: 9\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 1\n\nUnits: 1\n\nWait, but that gives the number 951,262,910,101. Wait, no. Wait, let's write them in order:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred millions: 5\n\nTen millions: 1\n\nMillions: 2\n\nHundred thousands: 2\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nThousands: 9\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 1\n\nUnits: 1\n\nSo the number is 951,262,910,101. Wait, but that's not matching the previous result. Wait, this is getting too confusing. Let me use another approach.\n\nLet me use the standard addition algorithm with carries:\n\n        625,804,415\n\n      + 886,801,296\n\n      -------------\n\nStart