096,626\n\nStart from the right:\n\n4 + 6 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n6 + 2 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\n8 + 6 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\n2 + 9 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n8 + 0 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\n1 + 0 = 1\n\n9 + 4 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\n2 + 2 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\n9 + 9 = 18 \u2192 8, carry 1\n\n2 + 0 = 2 + 1 = 3\n\nSo the sum is 1,215,384,890.\n\nBut according to my previous addition, I got 1,358,194,900. So there's a discrepancy of 1,142,715,000. That's a huge difference. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, let me check the digits again. Maybe I added the digits in the wrong order.\n\nWait, when adding vertically, the digits are added from right to left, but the place values are left to right. So the first digit added is the ones (rightmost), then tens, etc., but when writing the result, the first digit you get is the ones, then tens, etc., but in reality, the first digit you write is the ones, then tens, etc., but when you add the numbers, the order is preserved. Wait, no. When you add two numbers, the digits are added in the same order they are written. So if you have:\n\n      2 9 1 2 8 8 2 6 4\n\n    + 9 2 4 0 9 6 6 2 6\n\nThe rightmost digits are added first, then moving left. So the result should be written from the leftmost digit (billions place) to the rightmost digit (ones place). So the final sum should be 1,215,384,890.\n\nBut according to my previous step-by-step addition, I ended up with 1,358,194,900. So clearly, there's a mistake in the process.\n\nWait, let me try adding the numbers using another method. Let's break them down:\n\nFirst number: 291,288,264\n\nSecond number: 924,096,626\n\nLet me add them as:\n\n291,288,264 + 924,096,626 = ?\n\nLet me add the billions: 200 million + 900 million = 1,100 million\n\nThen the hundred millions: 90 million + 20 million = 110 million\n\nThen the ten millions: 10 million + 40 million = 50 million\n\nThen the millions: 2 million + 9 million = 11 million\n\nThen the hundred thousands: 800 thousand + 0 thousand = 800 thousand\n\nThen the