      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to add two really big numbers: 863,636,349 and 637,729,581. Hmm, how do I do this? Well, I remember that adding large numbers can be done column by column, starting from the rightmost digits and moving left, carrying over if needed. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, I'll write down the numbers one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly:\n\n```\n 863,636,349\n+637,729,581\n------------\n```\n\nNow, let's start adding from the rightmost digit. Let me list out the digits vertically:\n\nStarting from the units place:\n\n9 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) = 10. So I write down 0 and carry over 1.\n\nNext, the tens place:\n\n4 + 8 = 12, plus the carried 1 makes 13. Write down 3, carry over 1.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n3 + 9 = 12, plus 1 = 13. Write down 3, carry over 1.\n\nThousands place:\n\n6 + 7 = 13, plus 1 = 14. Write down 4, carry over 1.\n\nTen-thousands place:\n\n6 + 2 = 8, plus 1 = 9. Write down 9, no carry over.\n\nHundred-thousands place:\n\n3 + 9 = 12. Write down 2, carry over 1.\n\nMillions place:\n\n3 + 7 = 10, plus 1 = 11. Write down 1, carry over 1.\n\nTen-millions place:\n\n6 + 3 = 9, plus 1 = 10. Write down 0, carry over 1.\n\nHundred-millions place:\n\n8 + 6 = 14, plus 1 = 15. Write down 5, carry over 1.\n\nFinally, the billions place:\n\n0 (since the first number doesn't have a billion digit) + 6 = 6, plus 1 = 7. Write down 7.\n\nWait, let me check that again. The first number is 863,636,349. Breaking it down:\n\n- 8 (hundred millions)\n- 6 (ten millions)\n- 3 (millions)\n- 6 (hundred thousands)\n- 3 (ten thousands)\n- 6 (thousands)\n- 3 (hundreds)\n- 4 (tens)\n- 9 (units)\n\nThe second number is 637,729,581:\n\n- 6 (hundred millions)\n- 3 (ten millions)\n- 7 (millions)\n- 7 (hundred thousands)\n- 2 (ten thousands)\n- 9 (thousands)\n- 5 (hundreds)\n- 8 (tens)\n- 1 (units)\n\nSo when adding the hundred millions place: 8 + 6 = 14, plus the carryover 1 is 15. So that's 5 in the hundred millions place and carryover 1.\n\nTen millions place: 6 + 3 = 9, plus 1 is 10. So 0 in ten millions, carryover 1.\n\nMillions place: 3 + 7 = 10, plus 1 is 11. So 1 in millions, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 6 + 7 = 13, plus 1 is 14. So 4 in hundred thousands, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place: 6 + 2 = 8, plus 1 is 9.\n\nThousands place: 3 + 9 = 12, plus 1 is 13. So 3 with carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place: 3 + 9 = 12, plus 1 is 13. So 3 with carryover 1.\n\nTens place: 4 + 8 = 12, plus 1 is 13. So 3 with carryover 1.\n\nUnits place: 9 + 1 = 10, so 0 with carryover 1. Wait, but earlier I thought the first digit was 9. Wait, maybe I messed up the order.\n\nWait, let me re-examine the columns properly. Let's list all digits from right to left:\n\nFirst number (863,636,349):\n\nPositions (from right):\n\n1. Units: 9\n\n2. Tens: 4\n\n3. Hundreds: 3\n\n4. Thousands: 6\n\n5. Ten thousands: 3\n\n6. Hundred thousands: 6\n\n7. Millions: 3\n\n8. Ten millions: 6\n\n9. Hundred millions: 8\n\nSecond number (637,729,581):\n\nPositions from right:\n\n1. Units: 1\n\n2. Tens: 8\n\n3. Hundreds: 5\n\n4. Thousands: 9\n\n5. Ten thousands: 2\n\n6. Hundred thousands: 7\n\n7. Millions: 7\n\n8. Ten millions: 3\n\n9. Hundred millions: 6\n\nSo adding them column by column:\n\nColumn 1 (units): 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n\nColumn 2 (tens): 4 + 8 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nColumn 3 (hundreds): 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nColumn 4 (thousands): 6 + 7 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1\n\nColumn 5 (ten thousands): 3 + 2 = 5 + 1 = 6 \u2192 6, no carryover\n\nColumn 6 (hundred thousands): 6 + 7 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nColumn 7 (millions): 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nColumn 8 (ten millions): 6 + 3 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n\nColumn 9 (hundred millions): 8 + 6 = 14 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carryover 1\n\nColumn 10 (billions): 0 + 6 = 6 + 1 = 7 \u2192 7\n\nSo putting all the digits together from left to right (the order from the highest place value):\n\nBillions place: 7\n\nHundred millions: 5\n\nTen millions: 0\n\nMillions: 1\n\nHundred thousands: 3\n\nTen thousands: 6\n\nThousands: 4\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nTens: 3\n\nUnits: 0\n\nSo the sum is 750,136,430.\n\nWait, let me verify that. Let me write out the digits in order:\n\nStarting from the leftmost (billions place):\n\n7 (billions)\n\n5 (hundred millions)\n\n0 (ten millions)\n\n1 (millions)\n\n3 (hundred thousands)\n\n6 (ten thousands)\n\n4 (thousands)\n\n3 (hundreds)\n\n3 (tens)\n\n0 (units)\n\nSo the number is 750,136,430.\n\nLet me check this with another method to be sure. Maybe adding the numbers digit by digit another way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe adding the numbers as they are:\n\n863,636,349\n\n+637,729,581\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\nStart with the units: 9 + 1 = 10. Write down 0, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 4 + 8 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write down 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write down 3, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 6 + 7 = 13 + 1 = 14. Write down 4, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands: 3 + 2 = 5 + 1 = 6. Write down 6.\n\nHundred thousands: 6 + 7 = 13. Write down 3, carryover 1.\n\nMillions: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write down 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen millions: 6 + 3 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write down 0, carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions: 8 + 6 = 14 + 1 = 15. Write down 5, carryover 1.\n\nBillions: 0 + 6 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write down 7.\n\nSo the result is 750,136,430. That seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check by subtracting one of the numbers from the sum. For example, if I subtract 863,636,349 from 750,136,430, it should give 637,729,581.\n\nLet's try that:\n\n750,136,430\n\n-863,636,349\n\nSubtracting digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 - 9. Can't do that, need to borrow. 30 - 9 = 21, but the next digit is 3, which becomes 2 after borrowing. Wait, maybe this is getting complicated. Alternatively, maybe use another method.\n\nAlternatively, maybe add the two numbers again:\n\n863,636,349\n\n+637,729,581\n\nLet me add the numbers step by step:\n\nFirst, 863,636,349 + 600,000,000 = 1,463,636,349\n\nThen, 1,463,636,349 + 37,729,581 = ?\n\n1,463,636,349 + 30,000,000 = 1,493,636,349\n\n1,493,636,349 + 7,729,581 = ?\n\n1,493,636,349 + 7,000,000 = 1,499,636,349\n\n1,499,636,349 + 729,581 = ?\n\n1,499,636,349 + 700,000 = 1,500,336,349\n\n1,500,336,349 + 29,581 = 1,500,365,931\n\nWait, that doesn't seem right. Wait, maybe I messed up the steps. Let me try again.\n\nWait, 863,636,349 + 637,729,581.\n\nAlternatively, break down the numbers:\n\n800,000,000 + 600,000,000 = 1,400,000,000\n\n60,000,000 + 30,000,000 = 90,000,000\n\n3,000,000 + 7,000,000 = 10,000,000\n\n600,000 + 200,000 = 800,000\n\n30,000 + 9,000 = 39,000\n\n600 + 500 = 1,100\n\n30 + 80 = 110\n\n9 + 1 = 10\n\nWait, this is getting confusing. Maybe adding the numbers step by step:\n\nStart with 863,636,349 + 637,729,581.\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\nFirst, align them:\n\n863,636,349\n\n+637,729,581\n\nStart from the right:\n\nUnits: 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nTens: 4 + 8 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nHundreds: 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nThousands: 6 + 7 = 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nTen thousands: 3 + 2 = 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6.\n\nHundred thousands: 6 + 7 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nMillions: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nTen millions: 6 + 3 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nHundred millions: 8 + 6 = 14 + 1 = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n\nBillions: 0 + 6 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7.\n\nSo the total is 750,136,430. That seems consistent with the previous result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another check. Let's compute 863,636,349 + 637,729,581.\n\nAlternatively, note that 863,636,349 + 637,729,581 = (800,000,000 + 600,000,000) + (60,000,000 + 30,000,000) + (3,000,000 + 7,000,000) + (600,000 + 200,000) + (30,000 + 9,000) + (600 + 500) + (30 + 80) + (9 + 1).\n\nCalculating each part:\n\n800,000,000 + 600,000,000 = 1,400,000,000\n\n60,000,000 + 30,000,000 = 90,000,000\n\n3,000,000 + 7,000,000 = 10,000,000\n\n600,000 + 200,000 = 800,000\n\n30,000 + 9,000 = 39,000\n\n600 + 500 = 1,100\n\n30 + 80 = 110\n\n9 + 1 = 10\n\nNow add all these together:\n\n1,400,000,000\n\n+90,000,000 = 1,490,000,000\n\n+10,000,000 = 1,500,000,000\n\n+800,000 = 1,500,800,000\n\n+39,000 = 1,500,839,000\n\n+1,100 = 1,500,840,100\n\n+110 = 1,500,840,210\n\n+10 = 1,500,840,220\n\nWait, that's 1,500,840,220. But that contradicts the previous result of 750,136,430. Hmm, so there must be a mistake here. Wait, but how?\n\nWait, no, because when adding the numbers, I think I messed up the commas. Wait, 863,636,349 is 863 million 636 thousand 349. Similarly, 637,729,581 is 637 million 729 thousand 581. So when adding these, the total should be in the billions. But according to the column addition, it's 750,136,430, which is 750 million. But according to this step-by-step addition, I ended up with 1,500,840,220, which is 1.5 billion. That's a huge discrepancy. So clearly, I made a mistake in the step-by-step addition.\n\nWait, let's check again. Let's do the step-by-step addition properly.\n\nStarting with 863,636,349 + 637,729,581.\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n      863,636,349\n\n    +637,729,581\n\n    -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nUnits: 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nTens: 4 + 8 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nHundreds: 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nThousands: 6 + 7 = 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nTen thousands: 3 + 2 = 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, no carry.\n\nHundred thousands: 6 + 7 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nMillions: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nTen millions: 6 + 3 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nHundred millions: 8 + 6 = 14 + 1 = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n\nBillions: 0 + 6 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7.\n\nSo the result is 750,136,430. But when I tried adding the components, I messed up the commas. For example, 863,636,349 is 863 million, and 637,729,581 is 637 million. Adding those gives 863 + 637 = 1,500 million. Then adding the rest (636,349 + 729,581) would be 636,349 + 729,581 = 1,365,930. So total would be 1,500,000,000 + 1,365,930 = 1,501,365,930. Wait, that's still different. Hmm, so where is the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps the mistake is in the way I broke down the numbers. Let's see:\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n863,636,349\n\n+637,729,581\n\nIf I add 863,636,349 + 637,729,581, the correct way is to do column-wise addition. But when I tried breaking it down into components, I might have misapplied the commas. Let's try again:\n\n863,636,349 is 800,000,000 + 60,000,000 + 3,000,000 + 600,000 + 30,000 + 600 + 349.\n\n637,729,581 is 600,000,000 + 30,000,000 + 7,000,000 + 200,000 + 9,000 + 500 + 80 + 1.\n\nAdding these together:\n\n800,000,000 + 600,000,000 = 1,400,000,000\n\n60,000,000 + 30,000,000 = 90,000,000\n\n3,000,000 + 7,000,000 = 10,000,000\n\n600,000 + 200,000 = 800,000\n\n30,000 + 9,000 = 39,000\n\n600 + 500 = 1,100\n\n349 + 80 = 429\n\nSo adding all these:\n\n1,400,000,000 + 90,000,000 = 1,490,000,000\n\n1,490,000,000 + 10,000,000 = 1,500,000,000\n\n1,500,000,000 + 800,000 = 1,500,800,000\n\n1,500,800,000 + 39,000 = 1,500,839,000\n\n1,500,839,000 + 1,100 = 1,500,840,100\n\n1,500,840,100 + 429 = 1,500,840,529\n\nWait, that's 1,500,840,529. But according to the column addition, it's 750,136,430. There's a discrepancy here. So clearly, my initial column addition must be wrong. But how?\n\nWait, no. Wait, 863,636,349 is 863 million, and 637,729,581 is 637 million. Adding those gives 1,500,000,000. Then adding the remaining parts: 636,349 + 729,581 = 1,365,930. So total is 1,500,000,000 + 1,365,930 = 1,501,365,930. But according to the column addition, it's 750,136,430. So there's a factor of 2 difference. That means I made a mistake in the column addition.\n\nWait, let's check the column addition again step by step.\n\nFirst number: 863,636,349\n\nSecond number: 637,729,581\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n      8 6 3 6 3 6 3 4 9\n\n    +6 3 7 7 2 9 5 8 1\n\n    ---------------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nTens place: 4 + 8 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nThousands: 6 + 7 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\nTen thousands: 3 + 2 = 5 + 1 = 6 \u2192 6, no carry\n\nHundred thousands: 6 + 7 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nMillions: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nTen millions: 6 + 3 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nHundred millions: 8 + 6 = 14 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n\nBillions: 0 + 6 = 6 + 1 = 7 \u2192 7\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions: 7\n\nHundred millions: 5\n\nTen millions: 0\n\nMillions: 1\n\nHundred thousands: 3\n\nTen thousands: 6\n\nThousands: 4\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nTens: 3\n\nUnits: 0\n\nSo the number is 750,136,430.\n\nBut according to the component addition, I got 1,500,840,529. Which is way off. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the commas in the component addition. Let's check:\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n863,636,349 is 800,000,000 + 60,000,000 + 3,000,000 + 600,000 + 30,000 + 600 + 349.\n\nWait, 800,000,000 is 8 hundred millions.\n\n60,000,000 is 6 ten millions.\n\n3,000,000 is 3 millions.\n\n600,000 is 6 hundred thousands.\n\n30,000 is 3 ten thousands.\n\n600 is 6 hundreds.\n\n349 is 349.\n\nSimilarly, 637,729,581 is 600,000,000 + 30,000,000 + 7,000,000 + 200,000 + 9,000 + 500 + 80 + 1.\n\nSo adding these components:\n\n800,000,000 + 600,000,000 = 1,400,000,000\n\n60,000,000 + 30,000,000 = 90,000,000\n\n3,000,000 + 7,000,000 = 10,000,000\n\n600,000 + 200,000 = 800,000\n\n30,000 + 9,000 = 39,000\n\n600 + 500 = 1,100\n\n349 + 80 = 429\n\nNow adding all these:\n\n1,400,000,000 + 90,000,000 = 1,490,000,000\n\n1,490,000,000 + 10,000,000 = 1,500,000,000\n\n1,500,000,000 + 800,000 = 1,500,800,000\n\n1,500,800,000 + 39,000 = 1,500,839,000\n\n1,500,839,000 + 1,100 = 1,500,840,100\n\n1,500,840,100 + 429 = 1,500,840,529\n\nBut according to the column addition, the result is 750,136,430. So there's a factor of 2 difference. That means somewhere in the component addition, I added a million instead of a hundred million or something. Wait, 1,500,840,529 is 1.5 billion, but the column addition says 750 million. So there's a mistake in the component addition.\n\nWait, let's check the components again. The first number is 863,636,349. The second is 637,729,581. Adding these two:\n\n863,636,349 + 637,729,581.\n\nBut 863,636,349 is 863 million, and 637,729,581 is 637 million. So 863 + 637 = 1,500 million. Then adding the remaining parts: 636,349 + 729,581 = 1,365,930. So total is 1,500,000,000 + 1,365,930 = 1,501,365,930. But according to the column addition, it's 750,136,430. So there's a discrepancy of a factor of 2. That suggests that somewhere in the component addition, I messed up the commas. Wait, 1,501,365,930 is 1.5 billion, but the column addition says 750 million. So there's a mistake in the component addition.\n\nWait, perhaps I added the components incorrectly. Let's check:\n\nFirst number: 863,636,349\n\nSecond number: 637,729,581\n\nAdding these two:\n\n863,636,349 + 637,729,581.\n\nBut 863,636,349 is 863 million, 636 thousand, 349.\n\n637,729,581 is 637 million, 729 thousand, 581.\n\nAdding these together:\n\n863,000,000 + 637,000,000 = 1,500,000,000\n\n636,349 + 729,581 = 1,365,930\n\nSo total is 1,500,000,000 + 1,365,930 = 1,501,365,930.\n\nBut according to the column addition, it's 750,136,430. So there's a problem here. Therefore, my column addition must be wrong. But how?\n\nWait, let's check the column addition again step by step.\n\nFirst number: 863,636,349\n\nSecond number: 637,729,581\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n      8 6 3 6 3 6 3 4 9\n\n    +6 3 7 7 2 9 5 8 1\n\n    ---------------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1. Units: 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n2. Tens: 4 + 8 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\n3. Hundreds: 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\n4. Thousands: 6 + 7 = 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\n5. Ten thousands: 3 + 