      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the sum of two really big numbers: 997,960,631 and 463,643,229. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. Adding them directly might be tricky because of all those digits, but maybe there's a way to break it down to make it easier. Let me write them down one above the other to visualize the addition:\n\n```\n 997,960,631\n+463,643,229\n------------\n```\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let me add those first. The ones digits are 1 and 9. 1 + 9 is 10. So I write down 0 and carry over 1 to the tens place.\n\nNext, the tens place: 3 and 2. Adding those gives 5, plus the carried over 1 makes 6. So that's 6 in the tens place, no carryover.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 6 and 3. 6 + 3 is 9. No carryover here.\n\nThousands place: 0 and 4. 0 + 4 is 4. Still no carryover.\n\nTen-thousands place: 0 and 6. 0 + 6 is 6. No problem.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 9 and 3. 9 + 3 is 12. So that's 2 in the hundred-thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place.\n\nMillions place: 9 and 6. 9 + 6 is 15, plus the carried over 1 makes 16. So that's 6 in the millions place and carry over 1 to the ten-millions place.\n\nTen-millions place: 7 and 4. 7 + 4 is 11, plus the carried over 1 is 12. So that's 2 in the ten-millions place and carry over 1 to the hundred-millions place.\n\nHundred-millions place: 9 and 6. 9 + 6 is 15, plus the carried over 1 is 16. So that's 6 in the hundred-millions place and carry over 1 to the billions place.\n\nFinally, the billions place: 9 and 4. 9 + 4 is 13, plus the carried over 1 is 14. So that's 4 in the billions place and carry over 1 to the next place. But since there are no more digits, that carried over 1 becomes a new digit in the next place, which would be the ten-billions place. Wait, but actually, since these are both 1-digit numbers, adding 9 and 4 gives 13, plus 1 carried over makes 14. So we write 4 and carry over 1. But since there are no more digits, the 1 becomes a new digit. So the final result is 14 followed by the digits we've already calculated.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right:\n\nOnes: 0\n\nTens: 6\n\nHundreds: 9\n\nThousands: 4\n\nTen-thousands: 6\n\nHundred-thousands: 2\n\nMillions: 6\n\nTen-millions: 2\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nBillions: 4\n\nAnd then the carried over 1 in the ten-billions place. Wait, but let me check again. Wait, when we added the hundred-millions place: 9 + 6 = 15, plus 1 carried over is 16. So that's 6 in hundred-millions and 1 carried over. Then the billions place: 9 + 4 = 13, plus 1 carried over is 14. So 4 in billions and 1 carried over. So the carried over 1 becomes the next digit. So the final sum should be 14 followed by the digits we've written. Let me list all the digits in order:\n\nStarting from the rightmost:\n\nOnes: 0\n\nTens: 6\n\nHundreds: 9\n\nThousands: 4\n\nTen-thousands: 6\n\nHundred-thousands: 2\n\nMillions: 6\n\nTen-millions: 2\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nBillions: 4\n\nTen-billions: 1\n\nSo putting all those digits together: 1 4 6 2 6 9 4 6 0. Wait, let me check the order again. Wait, when we added the digits from right to left, the first digit we got was the ones place (0), then tens (6), hundreds (9), thousands (4), ten-thousands (6), hundred-thousands (2), millions (6), ten-millions (2), hundred-millions (6), billions (4), and then ten-billions (1). So the number is 14,626,946,0. Wait, let me verify that.\n\nWait, the digits in order from left to right (highest to lowest place value) would be:\n\n- Ten-billions: 1\n\n- Billions: 4\n\n- Hundred-millions: 6\n\n- Ten-millions: 2\n\n- Millions: 6\n\n- Hundred-thousands: 2\n\n- Ten-thousands: 6\n\n- Thousands: 4\n\n- Hundreds: 9\n\n- Tens: 6\n\n- Ones: 0\n\nSo combining them: 1 (ten-billions), 4 (billions), 6 (hundred-millions), 2 (ten-millions), 6 (millions), 2 (hundred-thousands), 6 (ten-thousands), 4 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (ones). So the number is 14,626,946,0.\n\nWait, let me check this with another method to make sure. Maybe adding the numbers digit by digit with proper alignment:\n\nLet me write both numbers aligned properly:\n\n```\n    997,960,631\n  + 463,643,229\n  -------------\n```\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1 + 9 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n3 + 2 + 1 = 6 \u2192 6, no carry\n\n6 + 3 = 9 \u2192 9, no carry\n\n0 + 4 = 4 \u2192 4, no carry\n\n0 + 6 = 6 \u2192 6, no carry\n\n9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n9 + 6 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\n7 + 4 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n9 + 3 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\n4 + 9 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\nFinally, the leftmost digit: 9 + 4 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1. Wait, but in the first number, the leftmost digit is 9 (hundred millions place), and the second number has 4 (billions place). Wait, maybe I messed up the places.\n\nWait, let's break down each number into their place values.\n\nFirst number: 997,960,631\n\nDigits: 9 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nWait, actually, let's parse it correctly:\n\nPosition: 100,000,000 | 10,000,000 | 1,000,000 | 100,000 | 10,000 | 1,000 | 100 | 10 | 1\n\nDigits:           9     | 9      | 7      | 9      | 6      | 0      | 6      | 3      | 1\n\nSecond number: 463,643,229\n\nPosition: 100,000,000 | 10,000,000 | 1,000,000 | 100,000 | 10,000 | 1,000 | 100 | 10 | 1\n\nDigits:           4     | 6      | 3      | 6      | 4      | 3      | 2      | 2      | 9\n\nNow, adding each position:\n\nStarting from the ones (1st position):\n\n1 + 9 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nTens (10th position):\n\n3 + 2 = 5 + 1 (carry) = 6 \u2192 6, no carry\n\nHundreds (100th position):\n\n6 + 3 = 9 \u2192 9, no carry\n\nThousands (1,000th position):\n\n0 + 4 = 4 \u2192 4, no carry\n\nTen-thousands (10,000th position):\n\n0 + 6 = 6 \u2192 6, no carry\n\nHundred-thousands (100,000th position):\n\n9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nMillions (1,000,000th position):\n\n9 + 6 = 15 + 1 (carry) = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\nTen-millions (10,000,000th position):\n\n7 + 4 = 11 + 1 (carry) = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nHundred-millions (100,000,000th position):\n\n9 + 6 = 15 + 1 (carry) = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\nBillions (100,000,000,000th position):\n\n4 + 9 = 13 + 1 (carry) = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\nTen-billions (10,000,000,000,000th position):\n\n9 + 4 = 13 + 1 (carry) = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\nWait, but hold on. The first number is 997,960,631. Let's check the digits again:\n\nFrom left to right (hundred millions down to ones):\n\n9 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nThe second number is 463,643,229:\n\nFrom left to right (hundred millions down to ones):\n\n4 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 9 (ones).\n\nSo when adding, the positions are:\n\n1. Ones: 1 + 9 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n2. Tens: 3 + 2 = 5 + 1 = 6 \u2192 6, carry 0\n\n3. Hundreds: 6 + 3 = 9 \u2192 9, carry 0\n\n4. Thousands: 0 + 4 = 4 \u2192 4, carry 0\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 0 + 6 = 6 \u2192 6, carry 0\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n7. Millions: 9 + 6 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\n8. Ten-millions: 7 + 4 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 9 + 6 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\n10. Billions: 4 + 9 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\n11. Ten-billions: 9 + 4 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\n12. Hundred-billions: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1 \u2192 1\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, the first number has a hundred millions place (9) and the second number also has a hundred millions place (4). So when adding the hundred millions place: 9 + 4 = 13 + 1 (carry from ten millions place) = 14. So that's 4 in the hundred millions place and carry 1. Then, moving to the ten-billions place: the first number doesn't have a ten-billions place, but the second number does. Wait, the second number's digits are 4 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 9 (ones). So the ten-billions place in the second number is 0, right? Because the places are:\n\n1. Hundred millions\n\n2. Ten millions\n\n3. Millions\n\n4. Hundred thousands\n\n5. Ten thousands\n\n6. Thousands\n\n7. Hundreds\n\n8. Tens\n\n9. Ones\n\nSo the second number doesn't have a ten-billions place. Therefore, when adding, the first number's hundred millions place (9) plus the second number's hundred millions place (4) gives 13, plus the carryover 1 from the billions place gives 14. So we write 4 in the hundred millions place and carry 1. But since there are no more digits, that 1 becomes the next digit. So the final result is 14 followed by the digits we've calculated.\n\nWait, but let's list all the digits in order from left to right:\n\n1. Hundred-billions place: 1 (from the carryover)\n\n2. Ten-billions place: 4\n\n3. Billions place: 4\n\n4. Hundred-millions place: 6\n\n5. Ten-millions place: 2\n\n6. Millions place: 6\n\n7. Hundred-thousands place: 2\n\n8. Ten-thousands place: 6\n\n9. Thousands place: 4\n\n10. Hundreds place: 9\n\n11. Tens place: 6\n\n12. Ones place: 0\n\nSo putting them all together: 1, 4, 4, 6, 2, 6, 2, 6, 4, 9, 6, 0. Which is 14,462,694,60.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought it was 14,626,946,0. Hmm, so which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check again. Let's do the addition step by step carefully.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1. Ones: 1 + 9 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n2. Tens: 3 + 2 = 5. 5 + 1 (carry) = 6. Write 6, no carry.\n\n3. Hundreds: 6 + 3 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\n4. Thousands: 0 + 4 = 4. Write 4, no carry.\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 0 + 6 = 6. Write 6, no carry.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n7. Millions: 9 + 6 = 15. 15 + 1 (carry) = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\n8. Ten-millions: 7 + 4 = 11. 11 + 1 (carry) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 9 + 6 = 15. 15 + 1 (carry) = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\n10. Billions: 4 + 9 = 13. 13 + 1 (carry) = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\n11. Ten-billions: 9 + 4 = 13. 13 + 1 (carry) = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\n12. Hundred-billions: 0 + 0 + 1 (carry) = 1. Write 1.\n\nSo the digits from left to right are:\n\n1 (hundred-billions), 4 (ten-billions), 4 (billions), 6 (hundred-millions), 2 (ten-millions), 6 (millions), 2 (hundred-thousands), 6 (ten-thousands), 4 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 14,462,694,60.\n\nWait, but when I first added the hundred-millions place, I thought it was 9 + 6 = 15 + 1 = 16, so 6 with a carryover 1. Then the ten-billions place is 9 (from the first number) + 4 (from the second number) + 1 (carryover) = 14, so 4 with a carryover 1. Then the hundred-billions place is 0 + 0 + 1 = 1. So yes, the digits are 1,4,4,6,2,6,2,6,4,9,6,0. So 14,462,694,60.\n\nBut earlier, when I first added, I thought the hundred-millions place was 6. Wait, no. Wait, the hundred-millions place in the first number is 9, and in the second number, it's 4. Adding those gives 13, plus the carryover 1 gives 14. So we write 4 in the hundred-millions place and carry 1. But since there are no more digits, that 1 becomes the next digit. So the hundred-millions place is 4, not 6. Wait, no. Wait, the hundred-millions place is the 6th digit from the right. Let me list all the digits in order:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 0\n\n2. Tens: 6\n\n3. Hundreds: 9\n\n4. Thousands: 4\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 6\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 2\n\n7. Millions: 6\n\n8. Ten-millions: 2\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 4\n\n10. Billions: 4\n\n11. Ten-billions: 1\n\nWait, no. Wait, when adding, the digits from right to left are:\n\nPosition 12 (ones): 0\n\n11 (tens): 6\n\n10 (hundreds): 9\n\n9 (thousands): 4\n\n8 (ten-thousands): 6\n\n7 (hundred-thousands): 2\n\n6 (millions): 6\n\n5 (ten-millions): 2\n\n4 (hundred-millions): 4\n\n3 (billions): 4\n\n2 (ten-billions): 1\n\n1 (hundred-billions): 0\n\nWait, this is getting confusing. Let me try a different approach. Let's write down the two numbers with their digits aligned:\n\nFirst number: 997,960,631\n\nSecond number: 463,643,229\n\nLet's write them vertically:\n\n```\n    997,960,631\n  + 463,643,229\n  -------------\n```\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1. Ones: 1 + 9 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n2. Tens: 3 + 2 = 5 + 1 = 6 \u2192 6, carry 0\n\n3. Hundreds: 6 + 3 = 9 \u2192 9, carry 0\n\n4. Thousands: 0 + 4 = 4 \u2192 4, carry 0\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 0 + 6 = 6 \u2192 6, carry 0\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n7. Millions: 9 + 6 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\n8. Ten-millions: 7 + 4 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 9 + 6 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\n10. Billions: 4 + 9 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\n11. Ten-billions: 9 + 4 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\n12. Hundred-billions: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1 \u2192 1\n\nSo the digits from left to right (highest place to lowest) are:\n\n1 (hundred-billions), 4 (ten-billions), 4 (billions), 6 (hundred-millions), 2 (ten-millions), 6 (millions), 2 (hundred-thousands), 6 (ten-thousands), 4 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nTherefore, the sum is 14,462,694,60.\n\nWait, but let me verify this with another method. Let's use the standard addition algorithm step by step.\n\nFirst, write the numbers one above the other:\n\n```\n    997,960,631\n  + 463,643,229\n  -------------\n```\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1. Ones place: 1 + 9 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n2. Tens place: 3 + 2 = 5. 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, no carry.\n\n3. Hundreds place: 6 + 3 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\n4. Thousands place: 0 + 4 = 4. Write 4, no carry.\n\n5. Ten-thousands place: 0 + 6 = 6. Write 6, no carry.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands place: 9 + 3 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n7. Millions place: 9 + 6 = 15. 15 + 1 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\n8. Ten-millions place: 7 + 4 = 11. 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n9. Hundred-millions place: 9 + 6 = 15. 15 + 1 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\n10. Billions place: 4 + 9 = 13. 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\n11. Ten-billions place: 9 + 4 = 13. 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\n12. Hundred-billions place: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1. Write 1.\n\nSo the result is 14,462,694,60.\n\nTo confirm, let's check the addition using another approach. Let's add the numbers digit by digit with proper place values.\n\nFirst number: 997,960,631\n\nSecond number: 463,643,229\n\nAdding them:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 1 + 9 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n2. Tens: 3 + 2 = 5 + 1 = 6 \u2192 6, carry 0\n\n3. Hundreds: 6 + 3 = 9 \u2192 9, carry 0\n\n4. Thousands: 0 + 4 = 4 \u2192 4, carry 0\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 0 + 6 = 6 \u2192 6, carry 0\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n7. Millions: 9 + 6 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\n8. Ten-millions: 7 + 4 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 9 + 6 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\n10. Billions: 4 + 9 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\n11. Ten-billions: 9 + 4 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\n12. Hundred-billions: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1 \u2192 1\n\nSo the digits from left to right are:\n\n1 (hundred-billions), 4 (ten-billions), 4 (billions), 6 (hundred-millions), 2 (ten-millions), 6 (millions), 2 (hundred-thousands), 6 (ten-thousands), 4 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nTherefore, the sum is 14,462,694,60.\n\nTo cross-verify, let's compute the sum using another method. Let's break down the numbers into smaller parts.\n\nFirst, 997,960,631 can be written as 900,000,000 + 97,960,631.\n\nSimilarly, 463,643,229 is 400,000,000 + 63,643,229.\n\nAdding these parts:\n\n900,000,000 + 400,000,000 = 1,300,000,000\n\n97,960,631 + 63,643,229 = ?\n\nLet's compute 97,960,631 + 63,643,229:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1 + 9 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n3 + 2 = 5 + 1 = 6\n\n6 + 3 = 9\n\n0 + 4 = 4\n\n0 + 6 = 6\n\n9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n7 + 6 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\n9 + 4 + 1 = 