90791271. Let's count the digits: 3 9 0 7 9 1 2 7 1. That's 9 digits. So 39,079,1271 is a 9-digit number. Similarly, 64,790,9971 is 647909971. Let's count: 6 4 7 9 0 9 9 7 1. That's also 9 digits. So both numbers are 9-digit numbers. So when adding them, we need to align them properly. Let me write them down:\n\n      390791271\n    + 647909971\n    -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, we add each column. Let's do this step by step.\n\nFirst, let's write both numbers with the same number of digits by padding the first number with a leading zero if necessary. Wait, but both numbers are 9 digits. So we can just add them as they are.\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n      39,079,1271\n    + 64,790,9971\n    -------------\n\nBut to add, we can ignore the commas and just add the numbers as they are. Let's convert them into numerical values. 39,079,1271 is 39,079,1271 and 64,790,9971 is 64,790,9971. Let's add them.\n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking them down into smaller parts would help. Let's see:\n\n39,079,1271 + 64,790,9971.\n\nLet me split 39,079,1271 into 30,000,000 + 9,000,000 + 700,000 + 90,000 + 1,000 + 200 + 70 + 1.\n\nSimilarly, 64,790,9971 is 60,000,000 + 4,000,000 + 700,000 + 90,000 + 0,000 + 900 + 90 + 1.\n\nWait, maybe that's too tedious. Alternatively, use column addition.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically:\n\n          39,079,1271\n        + 64,790,9971\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\n1 + 1 = 2. Write down 2.\n\nNext column (tens place): 7 + 7 = 14. Write down 4, carry over 1.\n\nNext (hundreds place): 2 + 9 = 11, plus the carryover 1 = 12. Write down 2, carry over 1.\n\nThousands place: 1 + 0 = 1, plus carryover 1 = 2. Write down 2, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands place: 9 + 9 = 18. Write down 8, carry over 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 0 + 7 = 7, plus carryover 1 = 8. Write down 8, no carryover.\n\nMillions place: 9 + 4 = 13. Write down 3, carry over 1.\n\nTen millions place: 3 + 6 = 9, plus carryover 1 = 10. Write down 0, carry over 1.\n\nHundred millions place: 0 (since first number is 39 million) + 6 (second number is 64 million) = 6, plus carryover 1 = 7. Write down 7.\n\nSo putting it all together, starting from the right:\n\nOnes: 2\n\nTens: 4\n\nHundreds: 2\n\nThousands: 2\n\nTen thousands: 8\n\nHundred thousands: 8\n\nMillions: 3\n\nTen millions: 0\n\nHundred millions: 7\n\nSo the sum is 70,838,282. Wait, let me check again step by step.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the order. Let me write the digits properly.\n\nFirst number: 39,079,1271\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 3 (ten millions)\n- 9 (millions)\n- 0 (hundred millions)\n- 7 (ten thousands)\n- 9 (thousands)\n- 1 (hundreds)\n- 2 (tens)\n- 7 (ones)\n- 1 (wait, hold on, that can't be. Wait, 39,079,1271 is 3 ten millions, 9 millions, 0 hundred millions, 7 ten thousands, 9 thousands, 1 hundred, 2 tens, 7 ones. Wait, but that's 9 digits. Wait, 39,079,1271: commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So 39,079,1271 is 39 million, 79 thousand, and 1271? Wait, that seems inconsistent. Wait, maybe I need to parse it correctly.\n\nWait, 39,079,1271: starting from the right, the first three digits are 1271, then the next three are 079, then the remaining two digits are 39. Wait, that would make it 39 million, 79 thousand, and 1271. But 39 million, 79 thousand, 1271 is 39,079,1271. So yes, that's correct. So the digits are:\n\n3 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 0 (hundred millions), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 7 (ones), 1 (wait, no, hold on. Wait, 39,079,1271 is 39,079,1271. Let's separate the digits:\n\nPosition: 1 (rightmost) to 9 (leftmost):\n\n1: 1\n\n2: 7\n\n3: 2\n\n4: 1\n\n5: 9\n\n6: 0\n\n7: 7\n\n8: 9\n\n9: 3\n\nWait, no. Wait, if the number is 39,079,1271, then breaking it down:\n\n- The first comma is after the first three digits: 39,079,1271. So the digits are:\n\n3 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 0 (hundred millions), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 7 (ones), 1 (wait, no, that's only 8 digits. Wait, maybe I'm confused.\n\nWait, 39,079,1271: Let's remove the commas. So it's 390791271. Let's count the digits: 3 9 0 7 9 1 2 7 1. That's 9 digits. So positions 1 to 9:\n\n1: 1\n\n2: 7\n\n3: 2\n\n4: 1\n\n5: 9\n\n6: 0\n\n7: 7\n\n8: 9\n\n9: 3\n\nWait, no. Wait, 390791271:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition 1: 1 (units)\n\nPosition 2: 7 (tens)\n\nPosition 3: 2 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 4: 1 (thousands)\n\nPosition 5: 9 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition 6: 0 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition 7: 7 (millions)\n\nPosition 8: 9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition 9: 3 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, that makes sense. So the digits are:\n\n3 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 1 (units).\n\nWait, no. Wait, if the number is 39,079,1271, then:\n\n- The first comma separates the thousands: 39,079,1271.\n\nSo starting from the right, the digits are:\n\n1 (units), 7 (tens), 2 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 0 (hundred thousands), 7 (millions), 9 (ten millions), 3 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, that seems correct. So the digits are:\n\n3 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 1 (units).\n\nSimilarly, 64,790,9971 is 647909971. Breaking down:\n\n6 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 1 (units).\n\nSo now, let's write both numbers with their digits aligned:\n\n          39,079,1271\n        + 64,790,9971\n        -------------\n\nAligning the digits:\n\n          3 9 0 7 9 1 2 7 1\n        + 6 4 7 9 0 9 9 7 1\n        ---------------------------\n\nNow, adding each column starting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1 (units) + 1 = 2\n\n7 (tens) + 7 = 14 \u2192 write 4, carryover 1\n\n2 (hundreds) + 9 = 11 + 1 (carryover) = 12 \u2192 write 2, carryover 1\n\n1 (thousands) + 0 = 1 + 1 (carryover) = 2 \u2192 write 2, carryover 0\n\n9 (ten thousands) + 9 = 18 \u2192 write 8, carryover 1\n\n0 (hundred thousands) + 7 = 7 + 1 (carryover) = 8 \u2192 write 8, carryover 0\n\n7 (millions) + 4 = 11 \u2192 write 1, carryover 1\n\n9 (ten millions) + 6 = 15 + 1 (carryover) = 16 \u2192 write 6, carryover 1\n\n3 (hundred millions) + 0 = 3 + 1 (carryover) = 4 \u2192 write 4\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, the leftmost digit in the first number is 3 (hundred millions), and in the second number, it's 6 (hundred millions). So adding 3 + 6 = 9, plus the carryover 1 gives 10. Wait, but in my previous step, I wrote 4. That's a mistake.\n\nWait, let's redo the columns properly:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1 (units) + 1 = 2. Write 2.\n\nTens place: 7 + 7 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place: 2 + 9 = 11 + 1 (carryover) = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place: 1 + 0 = 1 + 1 (carryover) = 2. Write 2, carryover 0.\n\nTen thousands place: 9 + 9 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 0 + 7 = 7 + 1 (carryover) = 8. Write 8, carryover 0.\n\nMillions place: 7 + 4 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen millions place: 9 + 6 = 15 + 1 (carryover) = 16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions place: 3 + 0 = 3 + 1 (carryover) = 4. Write 4.\n\nWait, but the first number's hundred millions place is 3, and the second number's hundred millions place is 0. So 3 + 0 = 3, plus carryover 1 is 4. So the final result is 4 followed by the previous digits: 6, 8, 2, 4, 2, 8, 4, 6, 2. Wait, no. Wait, let's list the digits from left to right as we built them:\n\nHundred millions: 4\n\nTen millions: 6\n\nMillions: 1\n\nHundred thousands: 8\n\nTen thousands: 8\n\nThousands: 2\n\nHundreds: 2\n\nTens: 4\n\nUnits: 2\n\nSo putting them all together: 4,6,1,8,8,2,2,4,2. So the number is 46,188,2242? Wait, that can't be right. Wait, 46,188,2242 is 10 digits. But our original numbers are 9 digits. So something's wrong here.\n\nWait, let's check again. Maybe I messed up the order. Let me write the digits step by step:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units: 2\n2. Tens: 4\n3. Hundreds: 2\n4. Thousands: 2\n5. Ten thousands: 8\n6. Hundred thousands: 8\n7. Millions: 1\n8. Ten millions: 6\n9. Hundred millions: 4\n\nSo the number is 4 (hundred millions) 6 (ten millions) 1 (millions) 8 (hundred thousands) 8 (ten thousands) 2 (thousands) 2 (hundreds) 4 (tens) 2 (units). So written out: 46,188,2242. Wait, but that's 10 digits. Wait, no. Wait, 4 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 2 (units). So that's 9 digits: 46,188,2242. Wait, 46 million, 188 thousand, 2242? But 46,188,2242 is 46,188,2242. Let me check if that's the sum.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should use another method. Let's add the two numbers directly:\n\n39,079,1271\n\n+64,790,9971\n\nLet me add them step by step.\n\nFirst, add 39,079,1271 + 64,790,9971.\n\nLet me add the millions:\n\n39,079,1271 is 39 million, 79 thousand, 1271.\n\n64,790,9971 is 64 million, 790 thousand, 9971.\n\nAdding the millions: 39 + 64 = 103 million.\n\nAdding the thousands: 79,000 + 790,000 = 869,000.\n\nAdding the hundreds, tens, and ones: 1,271 + 9,971 = 11,242.\n\nSo total sum: 103,000,000 + 869,000 + 11,242 = 103,869,000 + 11,242 = 103,880,242.\n\nWait, that's different from the previous result. So according to this method, the sum is 103,880,242. But according to the column addition, I had 46,188,2242. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, clearly, there's a mistake in the column addition. Let me check again.\n\nWait, let's do the column addition properly.\n\nFirst, write both numbers without commas:\n\n390791271\n\n647909971\n\nAdding these two numbers:\n\nLet's align them:\n\n      390791271\n\n    + 647909971\n\n    -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1 + 1 = 2\n\n7 + 7 = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1\n\n2 + 9 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 1\n\n1 + 0 = 1 + 1 = 2 \u2192 2, carryover 0\n\n9 + 9 = 18 \u2192 8, carryover 1\n\n0 + 7 = 7 + 1 = 8 \u2192 8, carryover 0\n\n7 + 4 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\n9 + 6 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\n3 + 0 = 3 + 1 = 4 \u2192 4\n\nSo the result is 46,188,2242. Wait, but according to the other method, it's 103,880,242. These two numbers are different. There must be a mistake here.\n\nWait, let's check the second method again. Adding 39,079,1271 and 64,790,9971.\n\n39,079,1271 + 64,790,9971.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n39,079,1271 = 39,000,000 + 79,1271\n\nWait, no. Wait, 39,079,1271 is 39 million plus 079 thousand plus 1271.\n\nSimilarly, 64,790,9971 is 64 million plus 790 thousand plus 9971.\n\nAdding the millions: 39 + 64 = 103 million.\n\nAdding the thousands: 079,000 + 790,000 = 869,000.\n\nAdding the hundreds, tens, and ones: 1,271 + 9,971 = 11,242.\n\nTotal: 103,000,000 + 869,000 + 11,242 = 103,869,000 + 11,242 = 103,880,242.\n\nBut according to the column addition, it's 46,188,2242. Which is 46 million vs 103 million. There's a discrepancy here. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with smaller numbers. Let's take 39 + 64 = 103. If I do column addition:\n\n39\n\n+64\n\n---\n\n103\n\nWhich is correct. So why is there a discrepancy?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the alignment in the column addition. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n39,079,1271\n\n64,790,9971\n\nLet me write them out with all digits:\n\nFirst number: 3 9 0 7 9 1 2 7 1\n\nSecond number: 6 4 7 9 0 9 9 7 1\n\nAdding these:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1 + 1 = 2\n\n7 + 7 = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1\n\n2 + 9 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 1\n\n1 + 0 = 1 + 1 = 2 \u2192 2, carryover 0\n\n9 + 9 = 18 \u2192 8, carryover 1\n\n0 + 7 = 7 + 1 = 8 \u2192 8, carryover 0\n\n7 + 4 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\n9 + 6 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\n3 + 0 = 3 + 1 = 4 \u2192 4\n\nSo the sum is 4 6 1 8 8 2 2 4 2 \u2192 46,188,2242.\n\nBut according to the other method, it's 103,880,242. These are different. So which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with another method. Let's compute 39,079,1271 + 64,790,9971.\n\nFirst, 39,079,1271 + 64,790,9971.\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\n39,079,1271\n\n+64,790,9971\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with the units: 1 + 1 = 2\n\nTens: 7 + 7 = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1\n\nHundreds: 2 + 9 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 1\n\nThousands: 1 + 0 = 1 + 1 = 2 \u2192 2, carryover 0\n\nTen thousands: 9 + 9 = 18 \u2192 8, carryover 1\n\nHundred thousands: 0 + 7 = 7 + 1 = 8 \u2192 8, carryover 0\n\nMillions: 7 + 4 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nTen millions: 9 + 6 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\nHundred millions: 3 + 0 = 3 + 1 = 4 \u2192 4\n\nSo the sum is 46,188,2242. Wait, but according to the other method, it's 103,880,242. There's a conflict here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me verify the sum using a calculator.\n\nFirst, 39,079,1271 + 64,790,9971.\n\nLet me compute 39,079,1271 + 64,790,9971.\n\n39,079,1271 + 64,790,9971.\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\n39,079,1271\n\n+64,790,9971\n\nStart adding from the right:\n\n1 + 1 = 2\n\n7 + 7 = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1\n\n2 + 9 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 1\n\n1 + 0 = 1 + 1 = 2 \u2192 2, carryover 0\n\n9 + 9 = 18 \u2192 8, carryover 1\n\n0 + 7 = 7 + 1 = 8 \u2192 8, carryover 0\n\n7 + 4 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\n9 + 6 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carryover 1\n\n3 + 0 = 3 + 1 = 4 \u2192 4\n\nSo the sum is 46,188,2242. But according to the other method, it's 103,880,242. There's a problem here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check the second method again. 39,079,1271 + 64,790,9971.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n39,079,1271 = 39,000,000 + 79,1271\n\nWait, no. Wait, 39,079,1271 is 39 million plus 079 thousand plus 1271.\n\nSimilarly, 64,790,9971 is 64 million plus 790 thousand plus 9971.\n\nAdding the millions: 39 + 64 = 103 million.\n\nAdding the thousands: 079,000 + 790,000 = 869,000.\n\nAdding the hundreds, tens, and ones: 1,271 + 9,971 = 11,242.\n\nTotal: 103,000,000 + 869,000 + 11,242 = 103,869,000 + 11,242 = 103,880,242.\n\nBut according to column addition, it's 46,188,2242. Which is 46 million vs 103 million. There's a discrepancy of 57 million. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the breakdown. Let's check:\n\n39,079,1271: 39 million, 079 thousand, 1271.\n\nWait, 079 thousand is 79,000. So 39 million + 79,000 + 1,271 = 39,079,1271. Correct.\n\n64,790,9971: 64 million, 790 thousand, 9,971. Wait, 790 thousand is 790,000. 9,971 is 9 thousand, 9 hundred, 71. So 64 million + 790,000 + 9,971 = 64,790,9971. Correct.\n\nAdding millions: 39 + 64 = 103 million. Correct.\n\nAdding thousands: 79,000 + 790,000 = 869,000. Correct.\n\nAdding hundreds, tens, ones: 1,271 + 9,971 = 11,242. Correct.\n\nTotal: 103,000,000 + 869,000 + 11,242 = 103,869,000 + 11,242 = 103,880,242. Correct.\n\nBut according to column addition, it's 46,188,2242. Which is 46 million. So there's a mistake in the column addition.\n\nWait, let's check the column addition again. Maybe I messed up the digits.\n\nFirst number: 39,079,1271.\n\nDigits from left to right: 3,9,0,7,9,1,2,7,1.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, 39,079,1271 is 3 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 1 (units). Wait, no. Wait, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So 39,079,1271 is 39 million, 79 thousand, 1271. So the digits are:\n\n3 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 1 (units). So the digits are:\n\n1. 1 (units)\n\n2. 7 (tens)\n\n3. 2 (hundreds)\n\n4. 1 (thousands)\n\n5. 9 (ten thousands)\n\n6. 0 (hundred thousands)\n\n7. 7 (millions)\n\n8. 9 (ten millions)\n\n9. 3 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, that's correct. So when adding, the leftmost digit is 3 (hundred millions), then 9 (ten millions), then 0 (millions