      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to add two really big numbers: 891,053,187 and 153,410,476. Hmm, how do I do this step by step? Maybe I can line them up vertically like how we do it on paper. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, I'll write both numbers down, making sure the digits are aligned properly by place value. So, starting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let me check the number of digits in each number. 891,053,187 has 8 digits, and 153,410,476 also has 8 digits. That means they can be aligned without any leading zeros. Let me write them down:\n\n          891,053,187\n        + 153,410,476\n        -------------\n\nNow, I need to add each column starting from the right. Let's go digit by digit.\n\nStarting with the ones place:\n7 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) = 13. So I write down 3 and carry over 1.\n\nNext, the tens place:\n8 + 7 = 15, plus the carried 1 makes 16. Write down 6, carry over 1.\n\nHundreds place:\n1 + 4 = 5, plus the carried 1 = 6. Write down 6, no carry.\n\nThousands place:\n3 + 0 = 3. Write down 3, no carry.\n\nTen-thousands place:\n5 + 1 = 6. Write down 6, no carry.\n\nHundred-thousands place:\n0 + 3 = 3. Write down 3, no carry.\n\nMillions place:\n9 + 5 = 14. Write down 4, carry over 1.\n\nTen-millions place:\n8 + 1 = 9, plus the carried 1 = 10. Write down 0, carry over 1.\n\nFinally, the hundred-millions place:\n0 (since the first number doesn't have a hundred-millions place) + 1 = 1, plus the carried 1 = 2. Write down 2.\n\nPutting all those together: 2 0 3 6 4 6 6 3 3. Wait, let me check again. Wait, the digits I wrote down were:\n\nFrom the ones place: 3\n\nTens: 6\n\nHundreds: 6\n\nThousands: 3\n\nTen-thousands: 6\n\nHundred-thousands: 3\n\nMillions: 4\n\nTen-millions: 0\n\nHundred-millions: 2\n\nSo putting them all together: 2 (hundred-millions), 0 (ten-millions), 3 (millions), 6 (hundred-thousands), 4 (ten-thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 3 (ones). So the number is 203,646,633.\n\nWait, let me verify that. Let me add the two numbers another way to check. Maybe adding the numbers digit by digit with more attention to the carries.\n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down the numbers into smaller parts. Let's see:\n\n891,053,187 can be broken into 800,000,000 + 91,000,000 + 105,000 + 3,187.\n\nSimilarly, 153,410,476 is 100,000,000 + 53,000,000 + 410,000 + 476.\n\nAdding them together:\n\n800,000,000 + 100,000,000 = 900,000,000\n\n91,000,000 + 53,000,000 = 144,000,000\n\n105,000 + 410,000 = 515,000\n\n3,187 + 476 = 3,663\n\nNow add those results:\n\n900,000,000 + 144,000,000 = 1,044,000,000\n\n1,044,000,000 + 515,000 = 1,044,515,000\n\n1,044,515,000 + 3,663 = 1,044,518,663\n\nWait, that's different from the previous result. Hmm, so which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check again.\n\nFirst method:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 7 + 6 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nTens: 8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 1 + 4 = 5 + 1 = 6 \u2192 6, carry 0\n\nThousands: 3 + 0 = 3\n\nTen-thousands: 5 + 1 = 6\n\nHundred-thousands: 0 + 3 = 3\n\nMillions: 9 + 5 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\nTen-millions: 8 + 1 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nHundred-millions: 0 + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n2 (hundred-millions), 0 (ten-millions), 3 (millions), 6 (hundred-thousands), 4 (ten-thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 3 (ones). So 203,646,633.\n\nBut when I broke down the numbers, I got 1,044,518,663. Wait, that's a big difference. Which is correct?\n\nWait, 891,053,187 + 153,410,476. Let me check with another method. Let's use the standard addition algorithm step by step.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically:\n\n          891,053,187\n        + 153,410,476\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n7 + 6 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nNext digit (tens): 8 + 7 = 15, plus the carried 1 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\nNext (hundreds): 1 + 4 = 5, plus 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nNext (thousands): 3 + 0 = 3. Write 3, carry 0.\n\nNext (ten-thousands): 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nNext (hundred-thousands): 0 + 3 = 3. Write 3, carry 0.\n\nNext (millions): 9 + 5 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nNext (ten-millions): 8 + 1 = 9, plus 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nNext (hundred-millions): 0 + 1 = 1, plus 1 = 2. Write 2.\n\nSo the total is 203,646,633. Let me check this with another approach. Let's use the fact that 891,053,187 + 153,410,476.\n\nAlternatively, maybe adding 891,053,187 + 153,410,476 by adding the numbers step by step.\n\nFirst, 891,053,187 + 100,000,000 = 991,053,187\n\nThen, 991,053,187 + 50,000,000 = 1,041,053,187\n\nThen, 1,041,053,187 + 3,000,000 = 1,044,053,187\n\nThen, 1,044,053,187 + 400,000 = 1,044,093,187\n\nThen, 1,044,093,187 + 10,000 = 1,044,093,287\n\nThen, 1,044,093,287 + 400 = 1,044,093,687\n\nThen, 1,044,093,687 + 70 = 1,044,093,757\n\nThen, 1,044,093,757 + 6 = 1,044,093,763\n\nWait, that's 1,044,093,763. Hmm, that's different again. Wait, so now I'm getting 203,646,633, 1,044,093,763, and 1,044,093,763. Wait, maybe I messed up the breakdown.\n\nWait, let's check the first method again. Let me do the addition step by step carefully.\n\nFirst, write the numbers:\n\n          891,053,187\n        + 153,410,476\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\n7 + 6 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nNext digit (tens):\n\n8 + 7 = 15, plus carry 1 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\nNext (hundreds):\n\n1 + 4 = 5, plus 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nNext (thousands):\n\n3 + 0 = 3. Write 3, carry 0.\n\nNext (ten-thousands):\n\n5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nNext (hundred-thousands):\n\n0 + 3 = 3. Write 3, carry 0.\n\nNext (millions):\n\n9 + 5 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nNext (ten-millions):\n\n8 + 1 = 9, plus carry 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nNext (hundred-millions):\n\n0 + 1 = 1, plus carry 1 = 2. Write 2.\n\nSo the result is 203,646,633. Let me check this with another method. Let's use the column addition again, but more carefully.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the standard algorithm with more attention to the carries.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically:\n\n          8 9 1 0 5 3 1 8 7\n        + 1 5 3 4 1 0 4 7 6\n        ---------------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n7 + 6 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nNext digit (tens):\n\n8 + 7 = 15, plus 1 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\nNext (hundreds):\n\n1 + 4 = 5, plus 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nNext (thousands):\n\n3 + 0 = 3. Write 3, carry 0.\n\nNext (ten-thousands):\n\n5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nNext (hundred-thousands):\n\n0 + 3 = 3. Write 3, carry 0.\n\nNext (millions):\n\n9 + 5 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nNext (ten-millions):\n\n8 + 1 = 9, plus 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nNext (hundred-millions):\n\n0 + 1 = 1, plus 1 = 2. Write 2.\n\nSo the result is 203,646,633. Let me verify this by subtracting 153,410,476 from 203,646,633 to see if I get 891,053,187.\n\n203,646,633 - 153,410,476.\n\nLet's compute:\n\n203,646,633\n\n-153,410,476\n\n= ?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n3 - 6. Can't do, borrow 1 from the tens place. But tens place is 6, which becomes 5, and the ones becomes 13. 13 -6=7.\n\nTens place: 5 -7. Can't do, borrow 1 from hundreds place. Hundreds place is 6, becomes 5, tens becomes 15. 15-7=8.\n\nHundreds place: 5 -4=1.\n\nThousands place: 6 -0=6.\n\nTen-thousands place: 4 -1=3.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 6 -3=3.\n\nMillions place: 6 -5=1.\n\nTen-millions place: 0 -1. Wait, 203,646,633 has 0 in the ten-millions place? Wait, 203,646,633 is 203 million, 646 thousand, 633. So in terms of places:\n\nDigits: 2 (hundred-millions), 0 (ten-millions), 3 (millions), 6 (hundred-thousands), 4 (ten-thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nSo when subtracting:\n\nStarting from the ones:\n\n3 -6: borrow 1 from tens. Tens is 3, becomes 2, ones becomes 13. 13-6=7.\n\nTens: 2 -7: borrow 1 from hundreds. Hundreds is 6, becomes 5, tens becomes 12. 12-7=5.\n\nHundreds: 5 -4=1.\n\nThousands: 6 -0=6.\n\nTen-thousands: 4 -1=3.\n\nHundred-thousands: 6 -3=3.\n\nMillions: 6 -5=1.\n\nTen-millions: 0 -1. Wait, here we have 0 in the ten-millions place. So 0 -1. Can't do, need to borrow from the hundred-millions place. The hundred-millions place is 2, becomes 1, and the ten-millions place becomes 10. 10 -1=9.\n\nSo putting it all together: 1 (hundred-millions), 9 (ten-millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred-thousands), 3 (ten-thousands), 3 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 7 (ones). Wait, that's 190,133,157. But that's not 891,053,187. Wait, that can't be right. So this suggests that 203,646,633 -153,410,476=190,133,157, which is not equal to 891,053,187. Therefore, my previous calculation must be wrong.\n\nWait, this is confusing. So according to the column addition, the sum is 203,646,633, but when subtracting, it doesn't give back the original number. Therefore, there must be a mistake in the addition.\n\nWait, let's check the subtraction again. Maybe I messed up the digits.\n\nWait, 203,646,633 minus 153,410,476.\n\nLet me write it out:\n\n          203,646,633\n        - 153,410,476\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n3 -6: Can't do, borrow 1 from the tens place. The tens digit is 6, becomes 5, ones becomes 13. 13-6=7.\n\nTens place: 5 -7: Can't do, borrow 1 from hundreds. Hundreds digit is 6, becomes 5, tens becomes 15. 15-7=8.\n\nHundreds place: 5 -4=1.\n\nThousands place: 6 -0=6.\n\nTen-thousands place: 4 -1=3.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 6 -3=3.\n\nMillions place: 6 -5=1.\n\nTen-millions place: 0 -1: Can't do. Need to borrow from hundred-millions place. Hundred-millions digit is 2, becomes 1, ten-millions digit becomes 10. 10 -1=9.\n\nSo the result is 1 (hundred-millions), 9 (ten-millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred-thousands), 3 (ten-thousands), 3 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 7 (ones). So 190,133,157. But that's not 891,053,187. Therefore, my addition must be wrong.\n\nWait, so where is the mistake? Let's check the column addition again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n891,053,187\n\n+153,410,476\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 891,053,187 + 100,000,000 = 991,053,187\n\nThen add 50,000,000: 991,053,187 + 50,000,000 = 1,041,053,187\n\nAdd 3,000,000: 1,041,053,187 + 3,000,000 = 1,044,053,187\n\nAdd 400,000: 1,044,053,187 + 400,000 = 1,044,093,187\n\nAdd 10,000: 1,044,093,187 + 10,000 = 1,044,093,287\n\nAdd 400: 1,044,093,287 + 400 = 1,044,093,687\n\nAdd 70: 1,044,093,687 + 70 = 1,044,093,757\n\nAdd 6: 1,044,093,757 + 6 = 1,044,093,763\n\nWait, so according to this step-by-step addition, the sum is 1,044,093,763. But that's different from both previous results. So now I'm really confused. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with another method. Let's use the fact that 891,053,187 + 153,410,476.\n\nLet me add the numbers digit by digit with more attention to the carries.\n\nFirst, write the numbers:\n\n          8 9 1 0 5 3 1 8 7\n\n        + 1 5 3 4 1 0 4 7 6\n\n        ---------------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\n7 + 6 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens place: 8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\nHundreds place: 1 + 4 = 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nThousands place: 3 + 0 = 3. Write 3, carry 0.\n\nTen-thousands place: 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 0 + 3 = 3. Write 3, carry 0.\n\nMillions place: 9 + 5 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nTen-millions place: 8 + 1 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nHundred-millions place: 0 + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2. Write 2.\n\nSo the result is 2 0 3 6 4 6 6 3 3, which is 203,646,633.\n\nBut when I subtract 153,410,476 from 203,646,633, I get 190,133,157, which is not 891,053,187. Therefore, there must be a mistake in the addition.\n\nWait, maybe I added wrong in the step-by-step method. Let me check that again.\n\nStarting with 891,053,187.\n\nAdd 100,000,000: 891,053,187 + 100,000,000 = 991,053,187. Correct.\n\nAdd 50,000,000: 991,053,187 + 50,000,000 = 1,041,053,187. Correct.\n\nAdd 3,000,000: 1,041,053,187 + 3,000,000 = 1,044,053,187. Correct.\n\nAdd 400,000: 1,044,053,187 + 400,000 = 1,044,093,187. Correct.\n\nAdd 10,000: 1,044,093,187 + 10,000 = 1,044,093,287. Correct.\n\nAdd 400: 1,044,093,287 + 400 = 1,044,093,687. Correct.\n\nAdd 70: 1,044,093,687 + 70 = 1,044,093,757. Correct.\n\nAdd 6: 1,044,093,757 + 6 = 1,044,093,763. Correct.\n\nBut according to this, the sum is 1,044,093,763. But according to the column addition, it's 203,646,633. These two results are different. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the column addition. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n891,053,187\n\n+153,410,476\n\nLet me write them aligned properly:\n\n          891,053,187\n        + 153,410,476\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 7 + 6 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1.\n\nTens: 8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1.\n\nHundreds: 1 + 4 = 5 + 1 = 6, carry 0.\n\nThousands: 3 + 0 = 3, carry 0.\n\nTen-thousands: 5 + 1 = 6, carry 0.\n\nHundred-thousands: 0 + 3 = 3, carry 0.\n\nMillions: 9 + 5 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1.\n\nTen-millions: 8 + 1 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1.\n\nHundred-millions: 0 + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n2 (hundred-millions), 0 (ten-millions), 3 (millions), 6 (hundred-thousands), 4 (ten-thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 3 (ones). So 203,646,633.\n\nBut when I subtract 153,410,476 from 203,646,633, I get 190,133,157. Which is not 891,053,187. Therefore, there's a problem here.\n\nWait, maybe the original numbers were misread. Let me check the original problem again.\n\nThe problem says: What is the sum of 891053187 and 153410476?\n\nYes, 891,053,187 and 153,410,476. Both are 8-digit numbers.\n\nWait, let me verify the column addition once more.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically:\n\n          8 9 1 0 5 3 1 8 7\n        + 1 5 3 4 1 0 4 7 6\n        ---------------------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 7 + 6 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n2. Tens: 8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n3. Hundreds: 1 + 4 = 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n4. Thousands: 3 + 0 = 3. Write 3, carry 0.\n5. Ten-thousands: 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n6. Hundred-thousands: 0 + 3 = 3. Write 3, carry 0.\n7. Millions: 9 + 5 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n8. Ten-millions: 8 + 1 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n9. Hundred-millions: 0 + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2. Write 2.\n\nSo the sum is 203,646,633. But when subtracting, it's not matching. Therefore, the mistake must be in the subtraction step.\n\nWait, let's check the subtraction again. If the sum is 203,646,633, then subtracting 153,410,476 should give 891,053,187. But when I subtract, I get 190,133,157. That's a big difference. Therefore, the addition must be wrong.\n\nWait, maybe I added the numbers incorrectly in the step-by-step method. Let me check:\n\nStarting with 891,053,187.\n\nAdd 100,000,000: 891,053,187 + 100,000,000 = 991,053,187. Correct.\n\nAdd 50,000,000: 991,053,187 + 50,000,000 = 1,041,053,187. Correct.\n\nAdd 3,000,000: 1,041,053,187 + 3,000,000 = 1,044,053,187. Correct.\n\nAdd 400,000: 1,044,053,187 + 400,000 = 1,044,093,187. Correct.\n\nAdd 10,000: 1,044,093,187 + 10,000 = 1,044,093,287. Correct.\n\nAdd 400: 1,044,093,287 + 400 = 1,044,093,687. Correct.\n\nAdd 70: 1,044,093,687 + 70 = 1,044,093,757. Correct.\n\nAdd 6: 1,044,093,757 + 6 = 1,044,093,763. Correct.\n\nBut according to this, the sum is 1,044,093,763. But according to the column addition, it's 203,646,633. These two numbers are different by a factor of 50. So there's a mistake here.\n\nWait, maybe I added the numbers in the step-by-step method incorrectly. Let me check each addition step.\n\nFirst step: 891,053,187 + 100,000,000. 891,053,187 + 100,000,000. 891,053,187 + 100,000,000 = 991,053,187. Correct.\n\nSecond step: 991,053,187 + 50,000,000. 991,053,187 + 50,000,000. 991,053,187 + 50,000,000 = 1,041,053,187. Correct.\n\nThird step: 1,041,053,187 + 3,000,000. 1,041,053,187 + 3,000,000 = 1,044,053,187. Correct.\n\nFourth step: 1,044,053,187 + 400,000. 1,044,053,187 + 400,000 = 1,044,093,187. Correct.\n\nFifth step: 1,044,093,187 + 10,000. 1,044,093,187 + 10,000 = 1,044,093,287. Correct.\n\nSixth step: 1,044,093,287 + 400. 1,044,093,287 + 400 = 1,044,093,687. Correct.\n\nSeventh step: 1,044,093,687 + 70. 1,044,093,687 + 70 = 1,044,093,757. Correct.\n\nEighth step: 1,044,093,757 + 6 = 1,044,093,763. Correct.\n\nSo all the steps in the step-by-step addition are correct. Therefore, the sum must be 1,044,093,763. But according to the column addition, it's 203,646,633. These two numbers are vastly different. Therefore, there must be a mistake in the column addition.\n\nWait, let me check the column addition once again. Maybe I misaligned the digits.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n891,053,187\n\n+153,410,476\n\nLet me write them with commas for clarity:\n\nFirst number: 891,053,187\n\nSecond number: 153,410,476\n\nAdding them:\n\n          891,053,187\n        + 153,410,476\n        -------------\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n          8 9 1 0 5 3 1 8 7\n        + 1 5 3 4 1 0 4 7 6\n        ---------------------------\n\nNow, aligning the digits properly:\n\nHundred-millions: 8 vs. 0\n\nTen-millions: 9 vs. 1\n\nMillions: 0 vs. 5\n\nHundred-thousands: 5 vs. 3\n\nTen-thousands: 3 vs. 1\n\nThousands: 1 vs. 4\n\nHundreds: 8 vs. 0\n\nTens: 7 vs. 7\n\nOnes: 3 vs. 6\n\nWait, hold on! Here's the mistake. The first number is 891,053,187, which is 8 9 1 0 5 3 1 8 7. But when written as 891,053,187, the digits are 8 (hundred-millions), 9 (ten-millions), 1 (millions), 0 (hundred-thousands), 5 (ten-thousands), 3 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nThe second number is 153,410,476, which is 1 5 3 4 1 0 4 7 6. So written out: 1 (hundred-millions), 5 (ten-millions), 3 (millions), 4 (hundred-thousands), 1 (ten-thousands), 0 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nTherefore, when adding, the digits should be aligned as follows:\n\n          8 9 1 0 5 3 1 8 7\n        + 1 5 3 4 1 0 4 7 6\n        ---------------------------\n\nSo:\n\nHundred-millions: 8 vs. 1\n\nTen-millions: 9 vs. 5\n\nMillions: 0 vs. 3\n\nHundred-thousands: 5 vs. 4\n\nTen-thousands: 3 vs. 1\n\nThousands: 1 vs. 0\n\nHundreds: 8 vs. 4\n\nTens: 7 vs. 7\n\nOnes: 7 vs. 6\n\nWait, no! Wait, the second number's thousands place is 0, but in the alignment above, the thousands place in the second number is the third digit from the right. Wait, no. Wait, in the vertical addition, the digits are aligned by their place value. So the rightmost digit is ones, then tens, hundreds, thousands, ten-thousands, hundred-thousands, millions, ten-millions, hundred-millions.\n\nTherefore, the first number: 8 (hundred-millions), 9 (ten-millions), 1 (millions), 0 (hundred-thousands), 5 (ten-thousands), 3 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 1 (hundred-millions), 5 (ten-millions), 3 (millions), 4 (hundred-thousands), 1 (ten-thousands), 0 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nTherefore, when adding:\n\nHundred-millions: 8 + 1 = 9\n\nTen-millions: 9 + 5 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\nMillions: 0 + 3 = 3 + 1 = 4\n\nHundred-thousands: 5 + 4 = 9\n\nTen-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4\n\nThousands: 1 + 0 = 1\n\nHundreds: 8 + 4 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nTens: 7 + 7 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\nOnes: 7 + 6 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nWait, this is different. Wait, no. Wait, in the previous column addition, I think I misaligned the digits. Let me correct this.\n\nWait, the first number is 891,053,187. Breaking down:\n\n- 8 (hundred-millions)\n\n- 9 (ten-millions)\n\n- 0 (millions)\n\n- 5 (hundred-thousands)\n\n- 3 (ten-thousands)\n\n- 1 (thousands)\n\n- 8 (hundreds)\n\n- 7 (tens)\n\n- 3 (ones)\n\nWait, no. Wait, 891,053,187:\n\n- 8 (hundred-millions)\n\n- 9 (ten-millions)\n\n- 0 (millions)\n\n- 5 (hundred-thousands)\n\n- 3 (ten-thousands)\n\n- 1 (thousands)\n\n- 8 (hundreds)\n\n- 7 (tens)\n\n- 3 (ones)\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nThe second number is 153,410,476:\n\n- 1 (hundred-millions)\n\n- 5 (ten-millions)\n\n- 3 (mi