ions) = 9. No carryover.\n2. 7 (millions) + 2 (millions) = 9. No carryover.\n3. 7 (hundred thousands) + 8 (hundred thousands) = 15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n4. 8 (ten thousands) + 6 (ten thousands) + 1 (carryover) = 15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n5. 9 (thousands) + 7 (thousands) + 1 (carryover) = 17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n6. 7 (hundreds) + 2 (hundreds) + 1 (carryover) = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n7. 2 (tens) + 8 (tens) + 1 (carryover) = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n8. 3 (ones) + 5 (ones) + 1 (carryover) = 9. Write 9, no carryover.\n9. 5 (ten ones) + 8 (ten tens) = 13. Wait, no. Wait, the second number's last digit is 8, which is in the tens place. Wait, no, the second number's digits are:\n\nPosition 1: 3 (ten millions)\n\nPosition 2: 2 (millions)\n\nPosition 3: 8 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition 4: 6 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition 5: 7 (thousands)\n\nPosition 6: 2 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 7: 8 (tens)\n\nPosition 8: 5 (ones)\n\nWait, so the second number's tens place is 8, and the first number's tens place is 3. So when adding the tens place: 3 + 8 = 11. Wait, but in my previous calculation, I had 2 + 8 + 1. Wait, no, that was for the hundreds place. Wait, no, let's clarify:\n\nWait, the first number's digits (from left to right, ten millions to ones):\n\n1. 6\n\n2. 7 (millions)\n\n3. 7 (hundred thousands)\n\n4. 8 (ten thousands)\n\n5. 9 (thousands)\n\n6. 7 (hundreds)\n\n7. 2 (tens)\n\n8. 3 (ones)\n\nSecond number's digits (from left to right):\n\n1. 3\n\n2. 2 (millions)\n\n3. 8 (hundred thousands)\n\n4. 6 (ten thousands)\n\n5. 7 (thousands)\n\n6. 2 (hundreds)\n\n7. 8 (tens)\n\n8. 5 (ones)\n\nSo when adding each corresponding digit:\n\n1. 6 + 3 = 9\n\n2. 7 + 2 = 9\n\n3. 7 + 8 = 15 \u2192 5, carryover 1\n\n4. 8 + 6 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carryover 1\n\n5. 9 + 7 + 1 = 17 \u2192 7, carryover 1\n\n6. 7 + 2 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n\n7. 2 + 8 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\n8. 3 + 5 + 1 = 9\n\n9. 5 + 8 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nThen, the final carryover 1 is added as the next digit.\n\nSo putting all the digits together, starting from the highest place:\n\n1. Ten millions: 9\n\n2. Millions: 9\n\n3. Hundred thousands: 5\n\n4. Ten thousands: 5\n\n5. Thousands: 7\n\n6. Hundreds: 0\n\n7. Tens: 1\n\n8. Ones: 3\n\nBut wait, that would be 9,955,701,3? Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nFrom highest place to lowest:\n\n1. Ten millions: 9\n\n2. Millions: 9\n\n3. Hundred thousands: 5\n\n4. Ten thousands: 5\n\n5. Thousands: 7\n\n6. Hundreds: 0\n\n7. Tens: 1\n\n8. Ones: 3\n\nSo the number is 9,955,701,3? Wait, that can't be right. Wait, no, when writing the number, we need to place the digits in their respective places. So:\n\n- The first digit (ten millions place) is 9.\n\n- The second digit (millions place) is 9.\n\n- The third digit (hundred thousands place) is 5.\n\n- The fourth digit (ten thousands place) is 5.\n\n- The fifth digit (thousands place) is 7.\n\n- The sixth digit (hundreds place) is 0.\n\n- The seventh digit (tens place) is 1.\n\n- The eighth digit (ones place) is 3.\n\nSo the number is 9,955,701,3? Wait, no. Wait, 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 3 (ones). So the number is 9,955,701,3? Wait, that's 9 billion, 955 million, 701 thousand, 3. But that seems too large. Wait, but the original numbers are 67,789,7235 and 32,867,2858. Adding two 8-digit numbers should give a 12-digit number. Wait, 67,789,7235 is approximately 67.8 million, and 32,867,2858 is approximately 32.9 million. Adding them gives roughly 100.8 million, which is a 9-digit number. Wait, but 67,789,7235 + 32,867,2858 = 100,656,0003? Wait, that can't be. Wait, maybe I made a mistake in the addition.\n\nWait, let's check with smaller numbers. Let's take 67,789,7235 + 32,867,2858.\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, add the ones: 5 + 8 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens: 3 + 5 + 1 = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\nHundreds: 2 + 8 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nThousands: 9 + 7 + 1 = 17. Write 7, ca