 no next digit, add the carried 1 as a new digit: 1,000,000.\n\nSo combining all the digits:\n\nFrom hundred millions: 1\n\nTen millions: 0 (since we only had 1 in the hundred millions and no carryover beyond that)\n\nMillions: 4\n\nHundred thousands: 4\n\nTen thousands: 2\n\nThousands: 6\n\nHundreds: 0\n\nTens: 0\n\nOnes: 1\n\nWait, that would be 1,000,000 + 5,000,000 + 4,000,000 + 400,000 + 200,000 + 600 + 0 + 0 + 1.\n\nCalculating that:\n\n1,000,000 + 5,000,000 = 6,000,000\n\n6,000,000 + 4,000,000 = 10,000,000\n\n10,000,000 + 400,000 = 10,400,000\n\n10,400,000 + 200,000 = 10,600,000\n\n10,600,000 + 600 = 10,600,600\n\n10,600,600 + 0 + 0 + 1 = 10,600,601.\n\nWait, but that contradicts the previous step-by-step. Hmm, maybe I messed up the order of the digits. Let me check the positions again.\n\nWait, when adding the numbers:\n\nThe first number is 215,890,309.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 2 (hundred millions)\n\n- 1 (ten millions)\n\n- 5 (millions)\n\n- 8 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 9 (ten thousands)\n\n- 0 (thousands)\n\n- 3 (hundreds)\n\n- 0 (tens)\n\n- 9 (ones)\n\nThe second number is 935,846,392.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 9 (hundred millions)\n\n- 3 (ten millions)\n\n- 5 (millions)\n\n- 8 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 4 (ten thousands)\n\n- 6 (thousands)\n\n- 3 (hundreds)\n\n- 9 (tens)\n\n- 2 (ones)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):\n\n9 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nTens place: 0 (first number) + 9 (second number) = 9 + 1 (carry) = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nHundreds place: 3 (first) + 3 (second) = 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\nThousands place: 0 (first) + 6 (second) = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nTen thousands place: 8 (first) + 4 (second) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 9 (first) + 8 (second) = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carry 1.\n\nMillions place: 8 (first) + 5 (second) = 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nTen millions place: 1 (first) + 3 (second) = 4 + 1 = 5. Write 5, carry 0.\n\nHundred millions place: 2 (first) + 9 (second) = 11. Write 1, carry 1. Since there are no more digits, add the carried 1 as a new digit: 1,000,000.\n\nSo putting all the digits together:\n\nFrom hundred millions: 1\n\nTen millions: 0 (since we only added 1 in the hundred millions place and no carryover)\n\nMillions: 4\n\nHundred thousands: 4\n\nTen thousands: 2\n\nThousands: 6\n\nHundreds: 0\n\nTens: 0\n\nOnes: 1\n\nWait, but that would be 1 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (ones). So the number is 1,044,062,001? Wait, no. Wait, let's list the digits in order:\n\nStarting from the left:\n\n1 (hundred millions)\n\n0 (ten millions)\n\n4 (millions)\n\n4 (hundred thousands)\n\n2 (ten thousands)\n\n6 (thousands)\n\n0 (hundreds)\n\n0 (tens)\n\n1 (ones)\n\nSo that's 1,044,062,001. Wait, but let me check again. Wait, the hundred millions place is 1, then ten millions is 0, millions is 4, hundred thousands is 4, ten thousands is 2, thousands is 6, hundreds is 0, tens is 0, ones is 1. So the number is 1,044,062,001. But let me verify this with another method.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can add the numbers digit by digit without breaking them into places. Let's try:\n\n215,890,309\n\n+935,846,392\n\nStart from the right:\n\n9 + 2 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n0 + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n3 + 3 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\n0 + 6 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\n8 + 4 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carry 1.\n\n8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\n1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5. Write 5, carry 0.\n\n2 + 9 = 11. Write 1, carry 1. Add 1 at the beginning.\n\nSo the result is 1,111,111,111. Wait, that can't be right. Wait, but according to this, the sum is 1,111,111,111. But when I broke it down into places, I got 1,044,062,001. There's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let me check with smaller numbers. Let's take 19 + 91. According to the column method:\n\n19\n\n+91\n\n---\n\n100\n\nBut according to the digit-wise addition:\n\n9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n1 + 9 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1. Then add 1. So total 110. Wait, but 19 + 91 is 110. Wait, but according to the column method, it's 110. So that works. But in my previous calculation, when I added 215,890,309 + 935,846,392, according to the column method, the result is 1,111,111,111. But when I broke it down into places, I got 1,044,062,001. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let me check with another example. Let's say 1234 + 9876.\n\nColumn method:\n\n1234\n\n+9876\n\n------\n\n11110\n\nBut according to digit-wise:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4 + 6 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n2 + 8 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n1 + 9 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1. Then add 1.\n\nSo total 11110. Correct. So the column method works.\n\nBut in the previous problem, when I added 215,890,309 + 935,846,392, according to the column method, the result is 1,111,111,111. But when I broke it down into the places, I thought it was 1,044,062,001. Which is conflicting.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the breakdown. Let me check again.\n\nWait, the hundred millions place: 2 + 9 = 11. So that's 11,000,000.\n\nThen ten millions place: 1 + 3 = 4, but since there was a carryover from the millions place, which was 14. Wait, no. Wait, the carryover from millions place was 1, which was added to the hundred millions place. Wait, no. Wait, the carryover from the millions place addition (8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14) was 1, which was added to the hundred millions place. So the hundred millions place is 2 + 9 + 1 (carryover) = 12. Wait, hold on, maybe I messed up the carryover steps.\n\nWait, let's re-examine the addition step by step with proper tracking of the carryover:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):\n\nDigit 1: 9 + 2 = 11. Write 1. Carryover 1.\n\nDigit 2 (tens): 0 + 9 = 9 + 1 (carryover) = 10. Write 0. Carryover 1.\n\nDigit 3 (hundreds): 3 + 3 = 6 + 1 (carryover) = 7. Write 7. Carryover 0.\n\nDigit 4 (thousands): 0 + 6 = 6. Write 6. Carryover 0.\n\nDigit 5 (ten thousands): 8 + 4 = 12. Write 2. Carryover 1.\n\nDigit 6 (hundred thousands): 9 + 8 = 17 + 1 (carryover) = 18. Write 8. Carryover 1.\n\nDigit 7 (millions): 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 (carryover) = 14. Write 4. Carryover 1.\n\nDigit 8 (ten millions): 1 + 3 = 4 + 1 (carryover) = 5. Write 5. Carryover 0.\n\nDigit 9 (hundred millions): 2 + 9 = 11. Write 1. Carryover 1. Since there are no more digits, add the carryover 1 as a new digit: 1,000,000.\n\nSo the digits added up are:\n\n1 (hundred millions)\n\n0 (ten millions) [since there was a carryover from the hundred millions place, but wait, no. Wait, the carryover from the hundred millions place addition (2 + 9 + 1) was 1, which was added as a new digit. So the digits are:\n\nFrom hundred millions: 1\n\nFrom ten millions: 0 (since there was no addition here, just the carryover)\n\nFrom millions: 4\n\nFrom hundred thousands: 4\n\nFrom ten thousands: 2\n\nFrom thousands: 6\n\nFrom hundreds: 0\n\nFrom tens: 0\n\nFrom ones: 1\n\nWait, but how does that translate to the number? Let's list the digits in order from highest to lowest:\n\n1 (hundred millions)\n\n0 (ten millions)\n\n4 (millions)\n\n4 (hundred thousands)\n\n2 (ten thousands)\n\n6 (thousands)\n\n0 (hundreds)\n\n0 (tens)\n\n1 (ones)\n\nSo the number is 1,044,062,001. But according to the column method, it's 1,111,111,111. There's a conflict here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with another method. Let's compute 215,890,309 + 935,846,392 by breaking down the numbers:\n\n215,890,309 = 200,000,000 + 15,890,309\n\n935,846,392 = 900,000,000 + 35,846,392\n\nAdding these together:\n\n200,000,000 + 900,000,000 = 1,100,000,000\n\n15,890,309 + 35,846,392 = 51,736,701\n\nThen total sum is 1,100,000,000 + 51,736,701 = 1,151,736,701.\n\nWait, that's different again. Now I'm really confused. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let's compute 215,890,309 + 935,846,392 directly:\n\n215,890,309\n\n+935,846,392\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with the first number: 215,890,309\n\nAdd 935,846,392:\n\n215,890,309 + 935,846,392\n\nLet me add the millions:\n\n200,000,000 + 900,000,000 = 1,100,000,000\n\n15,890,309 + 35,846,392 = 51,736,701\n\nSo total is 1,100,000,000 + 51,736,701 = 1,151,736,701.\n\nBut according to the column method, I had 1,111,111,111. According to breaking into parts, I get 1,151,736,701. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me compute 215,890,309 + 935,846,392.\n\n215,890,309\n\n+935,846,392\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add them:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n9 + 2 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n0 + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n3 + 3 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\n0 + 6 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\n8 + 4 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carry 1.\n\n8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\n1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5. Write 5, carry 0.\n\n2 + 9 = 11. Write 1, carry 1. Add 1 at the beginning.\n\nSo the result is 1,111,111,111.\n\nBut when I broke it down into parts, I got 1,151,736,701. Which is conflicting.\n\nWait, so which is correct? There must be a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let's check the breakdown again. 215,890,309 is 200,000,000 + 15,890,309. 935,846,392 is 900,000,000 + 35,846,392. Adding 200,000,000 + 900,000,000 = 1,100,000,000. Then 15,890,309 + 35,846,392. Let's compute that:\n\n15,890,309\n\n+35,846,392\n\n= ?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n9 + 2 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n0 + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n3 + 3 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\n0 + 6 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\n8 + 4 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carry 1.\n\n5 + 3 = 8 + 1 = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\n1 + 3 = 4. Write 4, carry 0.\n\nSo the sum is 51,736,701. Then adding to 1,100,000,000 gives 1,151,736,701. But according to the column method, it's 1,111,111,111. So which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with actual addition. Let's write both numbers vertically:\n\n      215,890,309\n\n    + 935,846,392\n\n------------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nDigit 1: 9 + 2 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nDigit 2: 0 + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nDigit 3: 3 + 3 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\nDigit 4: 0 + 6 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nDigit 5: 8 + 4 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nDigit 6: 9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carry 1.\n\nDigit 7: 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nDigit 8: 1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5. Write 5, carry 0.\n\nDigit 9: 2 + 9 = 11. Write 1, carry 1. Add 1 at the beginning.\n\nSo the result is 1,111,111,111.\n\nBut when I broke it down into parts, I got 1,151,736,701. There's a conflict here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me compute 215,890,309 + 935,846,392.\n\n215,890,309\n\n+935,846,392\n\nLet me add them:\n\n215,890,309\n\n+935,846,392\n\n= 1,151,736,701.\n\nWait, so according to the calculator, the correct sum is 1,151,736,701. But according to the column method, it's 1,111,111,111. So there's a mistake in the column method.\n\nWait, where did I go wrong in the column method?\n\nLet me re-examine the column method step by step.\n\nFirst number: 215,890,309\n\nSecond number: 935,846,392\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n      215,890,309\n\n    + 935,846,392\n\n------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):\n\nDigit 1: 9 + 2 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nDigit 2: 0 + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nDigit 3: 3 + 3 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\nDigit 4: 0 + 6 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nDigit 5: 8 + 4 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nDigit 6: 9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carry 1.\n\nDigit 7: 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nDigit 8: 1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5. Write 5, carry 0.\n\nDigit 9: 2 + 9 = 11. Write 1, carry 1. Add 1 at the beginning.\n\nSo the result is 1,111,111,111.\n\nBut according to the calculator, it's 1,151,736,701. So there's a discrepancy. Therefore, my column method must have an error.\n\nWait, let's check the digits again. Maybe I misaligned the numbers.\n\nFirst number: 215,890,309\n\nSecond number: 935,846,392\n\nLet me write them out:\n\nFirst number: 2 1 5 8 9 0 3 0 9\n\nSecond number: 9 3 5 8 4 6 3 9 2\n\nWait, hold on! The first number is 215,890,309, which is 2 1 5 8 9 0 3 0 9.\n\nThe second number is 935,846,392, which is 9 3 5 8 4 6 3 9 2.\n\nBut when adding, we need to align the digits properly. So the first number is 9 digits, the second number is 9 digits. Let me write them with commas for clarity:\n\nFirst number: 215,890,309\n\nSecond number: 935,846,392\n\nSo breaking down into individual digits:\n\nFirst number:\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 2\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 1\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 5\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 8\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 9\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 0\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 3\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 0\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 9\n\nSecond number:\n\nPosition 9: 9\n\nPosition 8: 3\n\nPosition 7: 5\n\nPosition 6: 8\n\nPosition 5: 4\n\nPosition 4: 6\n\nPosition 3: 3\n\nPosition 2: 9\n\nPosition 1: 2\n\nSo when adding, we should align them as follows:\n\nPosition 1: 9 + 2 = 11\n\nPosition 2: 0 + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10\n\nPosition 3: 3 + 3 = 6 + 1 = 7\n\nPosition 4: 0 + 6 = 6\n\nPosition 5: 8 + 4 = 12\n\nPosition 6: 9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18\n\nPosition 7: 5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11\n\nWait, hold on! Wait, the second number's position 7 (millions place) is 5, and the first number's position 7 is 5. So 5 + 5 = 10. Then we write 0 and carry 1.\n\nWait, but in my previous calculation, I had 8 + 5 = 13 + 1. That's incorrect. Wait, the first number's position 7 is 5 (millions place), and the second number's position 7 is 5 (millions place). So 5 + 5 = 10. Then we write 0 and carry 1.\n\nBut in my previous step-by-step, I had:\n\nMillions place: 8 + 5 = 13 + 1. That's wrong. The millions place of the first number is 8, and the second number's millions place is 5. So 8 + 5 = 13. Then add the carryover 1, making it 14. So 14, which would be 4 with a carryover 1. Wait, but in the previous calculation, I had:\n\nWait, let's re-examine the positions:\n\nFirst number:\n\nPosition 9: 2 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition 8: 1 (ten millions)\n\nPosition 7: 5 (millions)\n\nPosition 6: 8 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition 5: 9 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition 4: 0 (thousands)\n\nPosition 3: 3 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 2: 0 (tens)\n\nPosition 1: 9 (ones)\n\nSecond number:\n\nPosition 9: 9 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition 8: 3 (ten millions)\n\nPosition 7: 5 (millions)\n\nPosition 6: 8 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition 5: 4 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition 4: 6 (thousands)\n\nPosition 3: 3 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 2: 9 (tens)\n\nPosition 1: 2 (ones)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nPosition 1: 9 + 2 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nPosition 2: 0 + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nPosition 3: 3 + 3 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\nPosition 4: 0 + 6 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nPosition 5: 8 + 4 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nPosition 6: 9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carry 1.\n\nPosition 7: 5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nPosition 8: 1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5. Write 5, carry 0.\n\nPosition 9: 2 + 9 = 11. Write 1, carry 1. Add 1 at the beginning.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition 9: 1\n\nPosition 8: 5\n\nPosition 7: 1\n\nPosition 6: 8\n\nPosition 5: 2\n\nPosition 4: 6\n\nPosition 3: 7\n\nPosition 2: 0\n\nPosition 1: 1\n\nSo the number is 1,518,626,701.\n\nWait, that's different again. Now I'm really confused. What's going on here?\n\nWait, let's list the digits in order from highest to lowest:\n\nPosition 9: 1\n\nPosition 8: 5\n\nPosition 7: 1\n\nPosition 6: 8\n\nPosition 5: 2\n\nPosition 4: 6\n\nPosition 3: 7\n\nPosition 2: 0\n\nPosition 1: 1\n\nSo the number is 1 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (ones). So 1,518,626,701.\n\nBut according to the calculator, the correct sum is 1,151,736,701. So this is conflicting.\n\nWait, now I realize the mistake. When adding the millions place, the first number has 5 (millions) and the second number has 5 (millions). So 5 + 5 = 10. Then we write 0 and carry 1. But in my previous step-by-step, I thought the first number's millions place was 8 and the second was 5. That's incorrect. The first number's millions place is 5, not 8. Wait, no. Wait, the first number is 215,890,309. Breaking down:\n\n- 2 (hundred millions)\n\n- 1 (ten millions)\n\n- 5 (millions)\n\n- 8 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 9 (ten thousands)\n\n- 0 (thousands)\n\n- 3 (hundreds)\n\n- 0 (tens)\n\n- 9 (ones)\n\nSo the millions place is 5. The second number is 935,846,392:\n\n- 9 (hundred millions)\n\n- 3 (ten millions)\n\n- 5 (millions)\n\n- 8 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 4 (ten thousands)\n\n- 6 (thousands)\n\n- 3 (hundreds)\n\n- 9 (tens)\n\n- 2 (ones)\n\nSo the millions place in the second number is 5. Therefore, when adding the millions place: 5 (from first number) + 5 (from second number) = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nBut in my previous calculation, I had 8 + 5 = 13 + 1. That was incorrect. The first number's millions place is 5, not 8. The first number's 8 is in the hundred thousands place. So that was the mistake.\n\nSo correcting that:\n\nPosition 7 (millions place): 5 + 5 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands place): 9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carry 1.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands place): 9 + 4 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands place): 0 + 6 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds place): 3 + 3 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nPosition 2 (tens place): 0 + 9 = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\nPosition 1 (ones place): 9 + 2 = 11. Write 1, carry 1. Add 1 at the beginning.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition 9: 1\n\nPosition 8: 5\n\nPosition 7: 0\n\nPosition 6: 8\n\nPosition 5: 3\n\nPosition 4: 6\n\nPosition 3: 6\n\nPosition 2: 9\n\nPosition 1: 1\n\nSo the number is 1,508,636,901.\n\nWait, but according to the calculator, the correct sum is 1,151,736,701. So this is still conflicting. What's happening here?\n\nWait, l