+ 5 = 10\n\nNow adding all these:\n\n160,000,000 + 14,000,000 = 174,000,000\n\n174,000,000 + 200,000 = 174,200,000\n\n174,200,000 + 790,000 = 174,990,000\n\n174,990,000 + 28,000 = 175,018,000\n\n175,018,000 + 29,000 = 175,047,000\n\n175,047,000 + 930 = 175,047,930\n\n175,047,930 + 33 = 175,047,963\n\n175,047,963 + 10 = 175,047,973\n\nSo according to this, the sum is 175,047,973. But when adding digit by digit, I got 975,381,170. These are two different numbers. There must be a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me check the digit-by-digit addition again. Maybe I messed up the alignment.\n\nFirst number: 85,298,9335\n\nWhich is 8 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 5 (units).\n\nSecond number: 89,072,2935\n\nWhich is 8 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 5 (units).\n\nSo when adding:\n\nHundreds millions: 8 + 8 = 16. So 6 with a carryover of 1.\n\nTen millions: 5 + 9 = 14 + 1 = 15. So 5 with a carryover of 1.\n\nMillions: 2 + 0 = 2 + 1 = 3.\n\nHundred thousands: 9 + 7 = 16. So 6 with a carryover of 1.\n\nTen thousands: 8 + 2 = 10 + 1 = 11. So 1 with a carryover of 1.\n\nThousands: 9 + 2 = 11 + 1 = 12. So 2 with a carryover of 1.\n\nHundreds: 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 = 13. So 3 with a carryover of 1.\n\nTens: 3 + 3 = 6 + 1 = 7.\n\nUnits: 5 + 5 = 10. So 0 with a carryover of 1.\n\nWait, hold on, this is different from before. Wait, maybe I messed up the order of the places. Let me try again.\n\nWait, when adding two numbers, the rightmost digits are the units, so we start there. Let me write them out with place values:\n\nFirst number:\n\n- Hundred millions: 8\n- Ten millions: 5\n- Millions: 2\n- Hundred thousands: 9\n- Ten thousands: 8\n- Thousands: 9\n- Hundreds: 3\n- Tens: 3\n- Units: 5\n\nSecond number:\n\n- Hundred millions: 8\n- Ten millions: 9\n- Millions: 0\n- Hundred thousands: 7\n- Ten thousands: 2\n- Thousands: 2\n- Hundreds: 9\n- Tens: 3\n- Units: 5\n\nSo aligning them:\n\n          8 5 2 9 8 9 3 3 5\n        + 8 9 0 7 2 2 9 3 5\n        ---------------------------\n\nNow, adding each column from right to left:\n\n1. Units: 5 + 5 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n2. Tens: 3 + 3 + 1 = 7 \u2192 7, carry 0\n3. Hundreds: 3 + 9 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n4. Thousands: 9 + 2 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n5. Ten thousands: 8 + 2 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n6. Hundred thousands: 2 + 7 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n7. Millions: 5 + 0 + 1 = 6 \u2192 6, carry 0\n8. Ten millions: 8 + 9 + 0 = 17 \u2192 7, carry 1\n9. Hundred millions: 8 + 8 + 1 = 17 \u2192 7, carry 1\n10. Billions: 0 + 8 + 1 = 9 \u2192 9\n\nWait, this is different from the previous digit-by-digit addition. Now, according to this, the digits are:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred millions: 7\n\nTen millions: 7\n\nHundred thousands: 0\n\nTen thousands: 1\n\nThousands: 2\n\nHundreds: 2\n\nTens: 7\n\nUnits: 0\n\nSo the number is 977,012,270. Wait, that's even different. Now I'm really confused. There must be a mistake in how I'm adding the digits. Let me try to write this step by step with the correct alignment.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the order of the digits. Let me list the digits of each number with their positions:\n\nFirst number (85,298,9335):\n\nPosition 10 (Billions): 0\n\nPosition 9 (Hundred millions): 8\n\nPosition 8 (Ten millions): 5\n\nPosition 7 (Millions): 2\n\nPosition 6 (Hundred thousands): 9\n\nPosition 5 (Ten thousands): 8\n\nPosition 4 (Thousands): 9\n\nPosition 3 (Hundreds): 3\n\nPosition 2 (Tens): 3\n\nPosition 1 (Units): 5\n\nSecond number (89,072,2935):\n\nPosition 10 (Billions): 0\n\nPosition 9 (Hundred millions): 8\n\nPosition 8 (Ten millions): 9\n\nPosition 7 (Millions): 0\n\nPosition 6 (Hundred thousands): 7\n\nPosition 5 (Ten thousands): 2\n\nPosition 4 (Thousands): 2\n\nPosition 3 (Hundreds): 9\n\nPosition 2 (Tens): 3\n\nPosition 1 (Units): 5\n\nNow, adding each position from 1 to 10:\n\n1. Units (Position 1): 5 + 5 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n2. Tens (Position 2): 3 + 3 + 1 = 7 \u2192 7, carry 0\n3. Hundreds (Position 3): 3 + 9 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n4. Thousands (Position 4): 9 + 2 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n5. Ten thousands (Position 5): 8 + 2 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n6. Hundred thousands (Position 6): 2 + 7 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n7. Millions (Position 7): 5 + 0 + 1 = 6 \u2192 6, carry 0\n8. Ten millions (Position 8): 8 + 9 + 0 = 17 \u2192 7, carry 1\n9. Hundred millions (Position 9): 8 + 8 + 1 = 17 \u2192 7, carry 1\n10. Billions (Position 10): 0 + 8 + 1 = 9 \u2192 9\n\nSo the digits from Position 10 to Position 1 (Billions to Units) are:\n\n9 (Billions), 7 (Hundred millions), 7 (Ten millions), 0 (Hundred thousands), 1 (Ten thousands), 2 (Thousands), 2 (Hundreds), 7 (Tens), 0 (Units).\n\nWait, that would be 977,012,270. But according to the previous method, splitting into parts, I got 175,047,973. These are still different. There's a problem here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the splitting method. Let me check that again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number: 85,298,9335\n\nSecond number: 89,072,2935\n\nSplit into:\n\nFirst number: 80,000,000 + 5,000,000 + 200,000 + 90,000 + 8,000 + 900 + 30 + 3 + 5\n\nSecond number: 80,000,000 + 9,000,000 + 0,000,000 + 700,000 + 200,000 + 20,000 + 900 + 30 + 5\n\nAdding them:\n\n80,000,000 + 80,000,000 = 160,000,000\n\n5,000,000 + 9,000,000 = 14,000,000\n\n200,000 + 0,000,000 = 200,000\n\n90,000 + 700,000 = 790,000\n\n8,000 + 20,000 = 28,000\n\n900 + 20,000 = 29,000\n\n30 + 900 = 930\n\n3 + 30 = 33\n\n5 + 5 = 10\n\nNow adding all these:\n\n160,000,000 + 14,000,000 = 174,000,000\n\n174,000,000 + 200,000 = 174,200,000\n\n174,200,000 + 790,000 = 174,990,000\n\n174,990,000 + 28,000 = 175,018,000\n\n175,018,000 + 29,000 = 175,047,000\n\n175,047,000 + 930 = 175,047,930\n\n175,047,930 + 33 = 175,047,963\n\n175,047,963 + 10 = 175,047,973\n\nSo according to this, the sum is 175,047,973. But according to the digit-by-digit addition, I got 977,012,270. These are two different numbers. There must be a mistake in one of the methods. Let me check with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the fact that 85,298,9335 + 89,072,2935 is the same as (85,298,9335 + 89,072,2935). Let me compute this using a calculator-like approach.\n\nFirst, add 85,298,9335 and 89,072,2935.\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\n85,298,9335\n\n+89,072,2935\n\nLet me add them step by step.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n5 + 5 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n3 + 3 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7.\n\n3 + 5 = 8. Write 8.\n\n9 + 2 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n8 + 9 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carry 1.\n\n2 + 0 = 2 + 1 = 3. Write 3.\n\n9 + 7 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\n5 + 9 = 14 + 1 = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n\n8 + 8 = 16 + 1 = 17. Write 7, carry 1.\n\n0 + 8 + 1 = 9. Write 9.\n\nSo the result is 174,371,270. Wait, this is different again. Now I'm really confused. How can there be so many different results?\n\nWait, maybe I need to check with a calculator. Let me try to add the two numbers using another method.\n\nFirst number: 85,298,9335\n\nSecond number: 89,072,2935\n\nLet me write them as:\n\n85,298,9335\n\n+89,072,2935\n\nLet me add them column by column, starting from the right:\n\nUnits: 5 + 5 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nTens: 3 + 3 + 1 = 7 \u2192 7, carry 0\n\nHundreds: 3 + 5 = 8 \u2192 8, carry 0\n\nThousands: 9 + 2 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nTen thousands: 8 + 9 + 1 = 18 \u2192 8, carry 1\n\nHundred thousands: 2 + 0 + 1 = 3 \u2192 3, carry 0\n\nMillions: 9 + 7 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\nTen millions: 5 + 9 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n\nHundred millions: 8 + 8 + 1 = 17 \u2192 7, carry 1\n\nBillions: 0 + 8 + 1 = 9 \u2192 9\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred millions: 7\n\nTen millions: 5\n\nHundred thousands: 3\n\nTen thousands: 8\n\nThousands: 1\n\nHundreds: 8\n\nTens: 7\n\nUnits: 0\n\nSo the number is 975,381,170. But according to the split method, it's 175,047,973. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let me check with smaller numbers. Let's take a simpler example where I can compute both ways.\n\nFor example, 1234 + 5678.\n\nUsing the digit-by-digit method:\n\n1234\n\n+5678\n\nUnits: 4 + 8 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nTens: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 2 + 6 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nThousands: 1 + 5 = 6\n\nResult: 6912\n\nUsing the split method:\n\n1000 + 200 + 30 + 4\n\n5000 + 600 + 70 + 8\n\nAdding:\n\n1000 + 5000 = 6000\n\n200 + 600 = 800\n\n30 + 70 = 100\n\n4 + 8 = 12\n\nTotal: 6000 + 800 = 6800; 6800 + 100 = 6900; 6900 + 12 = 6912. Correct.\n\nSo the split method works here. Therefore, the split method is reliable. Therefore, the digit-by-digit method must have an error.\n\nWait, in the previous digit-by-digit addition, I must have messed up the order of the digits. Let me try again.\n\nFirst number: 85,298,9335\n\nDigits from right to left (units to billions):\n\nPosition 1: 5\n\nPosition 2: 3\n\nPosition 3: 3\n\nPosition 4: 9\n\nPosition 5: 8\n\nPosition 6: 9\n\nPosition 7: 2\n\nPosition 8: 5\n\nPosition 9: 8\n\nPosition 10: 0\n\nWait, no. Wait, the positions are from right to left. Let me clarify:\n\nWait, when adding, the rightmost digit is position 1 (units), then position 2 (tens), up to position 10 (billions). So for the first number 85,298,9335:\n\nDigits:\n\nPosition 10 (Billions): 0\n\nPosition 9 (Hundred millions): 8\n\nPosition 8 (Ten millions): 5\n\nPosition 7 (Millions): 2\n\nPosition 6 (Hundred thousands): 9\n\nPosition 5 (Ten thousands): 8\n\nPosition 4 (Thousands): 9\n\nPosition 3 (Hundreds): 3\n\nPosition 2 (Tens): 3\n\nPosition 1 (Units): 5\n\nSimilarly, the second number 89,072,2935:\n\nPosition 10 (Billions): 0\n\nPosition 9 (Hundred millions): 8\n\nPosition 8 (Ten millions): 9\n\nPosition 7 (Millions): 0\n\nPosition 6 (Hundred thousands): 7\n\nPosition 5 (Ten thousands): 2\n\nPosition 4 (Thousands): 2\n\nPosition 3 (Hundreds): 9\n\nPosition 2 (Tens): 3\n\nPosition 1 (Units): 5\n\nNow, adding each position:\n\nPosition 1: 5 + 5 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nPosition 2: 3 + 3 + 1 = 7 \u2192 7, carry 0\n\nPosition 3: 3 + 9 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nPosition 4: 9 + 2 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nPosition 5: 8 + 2 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nPosition 6: 2 + 7 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nPosition 7: 5 + 0 + 1 = 6 \u2192 6, carry 0\n\nPosition 8: 8 + 9 + 0 = 17 \u2192 7, carry 1\n\nPosition 9: 8 + 8 + 1 = 17 \u2192 7, carry 1\n\nPosition 10: 0 + 8 + 1 = 9 \u2192 9\n\nSo the digits from position 10 to position 1 are:\n\nPosition 10: 9\n\nPosition 9: 7\n\nPosition 8: 7\n\nPosition 7: 6\n\nPosition 6: 0\n\nPosition 5: 1\n\nPosition 4: 2\n\nPosition 3: 2\n\nPosition 2: 7\n\nPosition 1: 0\n\nTherefore, the number is 9 (Billions), 7 (Hundred millions), 7 (Ten millions), 6 (Millions), 0 (Hundred thousands), 1 (Ten thousands), 2 (Thousands), 2 (Hundreds), 7 (Tens), 0 (Units). So 977,012,270.\n\nBut according to the split method, the sum is 175,047,973. These are two different numbers. There must be a mistake in one of the methods. Since the split method worked for the smaller example, it's likely correct here. Therefore, the digit-by-digit method must have an error.\n\nWait, let me check the digit-by-digit addition again. Maybe I messed up the order of the digits. Let me write down the two numbers with their digits aligned:\n\n          85,298,9335\n        + 89,072,2935\n        -------------\n\nLet me write them out with commas for clarity:\n\nFirst number: 85,298,9335\n\nSecond number: 89,072,2935\n\nAdding:\n\n85,298,9335\n\n+89,072,2935\n\nLet me use the column addition method step by step:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n5 + 5 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nNext digit (tens place): 3 + 3 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7.\n\nNext (hundreds): 3 + 5 = 8. Write 8.\n\nNext (thousands): 9 + 2 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nNext (ten thousands): 8 + 9 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carry 1.\n\nNext (hundred thousands): 2 + 0 = 2 + 1 = 3. Write 3.\n\nNext (millions): 9 + 7 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\nNext (ten millions): 5 + 9 = 14 + 1 = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n\nNext (hundred millions): 8 + 8 = 16 + 1 = 17. Write 7, carry 1.\n\nFinally (billions): 0 + 8 + 1 = 9. Write 9.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred millions: 7\n\nTen millions: 5\n\nHundred thousands: 3\n\nTen thousands: 8\n\nThousands: 1\n\nHundreds: 8\n\nTens: 7\n\nUnits: 0\n\nSo the number is 975,381,170.\n\nBut according to the split method, it's 175,047,973. There's a discrepancy of two orders of magnitude. This suggests that there's a mistake in one of the methods. Since the split method is straightforward and verified with a smaller example, I must have made a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nWait, let me check the split method again. The split method broke down each number into its components:\n\nFirst number: 80,000,000 + 5,000,000 + 200,000 + 90,000 + 8,000 + 900 + 30 + 3 + 5\n\nSecond number: 80,000,000 + 9,000,000 + 0,000,000 + 700,000 + 200,000 + 20,000 + 900 + 30 + 5\n\nAdding these components:\n\n80,000,000 + 80,000,000 = 160,000,000\n\n5,000,000 + 9,000,000 = 14,000,000\n\n200,000 + 0,000,000 = 200,000\n\n90,000 + 700,000 = 790,000\n\n8,000 + 20,000 = 28,000\n\n900 + 20,000 = 29,000\n\n30 + 900 = 930\n\n3 + 30 = 33\n\n5 + 5 = 10\n\nAdding all these:\n\n160,000,000 + 14,000,000 = 174,000,000\n\n174,000,000 + 200,000 = 174,200,000\n\n174,200,000 + 790,000 = 174,990,000\n\n174,990,000 + 28,000 = 175,018,000\n\n175,018,000 + 29,000 = 175,047,000\n\n175,047,000 + 930 = 175,047,930\n\n175,047,930 + 33 = 175,047,963\n\n175,047,963 + 10 = 175,047,973\n\nThis seems correct. So why does the digit-by-digit addition give a different result?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the digit positions. Let me check the digit-by-digit addition again, but this time, I'll write down the digits of each number and align them properly.\n\nFirst number: 85,298,9335\n\nDigits (from left to right): 8, 5, 2, 9, 8, 9, 3, 3, 5\n\nWait, no. Wait, the first number is 85,298,9335