right to left (ones to hundred millions):\n\n1. 4\n2. 5\n3. 1\n4. 1\n5. 7\n6. 7\n7. 7\n8. 8\n9. 1\n\nNow, let's set them up for addition:\n\nLet me write down the digits vertically, aligning the ones place:\n\n          7 0 7 , 7 0 1 , 9 2 0\n        + 1 8 7 , 7 7 1 , 1 5 4\n        ---------------------------\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\nDigit 9 (ones): 0 + 4 = 4\nDigit 8 (tens): 2 + 5 = 7\nDigit 7 (hundreds): 9 + 1 = 10. Hmm, so we write down 0 and carry over 1.\nDigit 6 (thousands): 1 + 1 = 2, plus the carried over 1 makes 3.\nDigit 5 (ten thousands): 0 + 7 = 7. No carry.\nDigit 4 (hundred thousands): 7 + 7 = 14. Write down 4, carry over 1.\nDigit 3 (millions): 7 + 7 = 14, plus the carried 1 makes 15. Write down 5, carry over 1.\nDigit 2 (ten millions): 0 + 8 = 8, plus the carried 1 makes 9.\nDigit 1 (hundred millions): 7 + 1 = 8.\n\nWait, let me check each step carefully.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones place: 0 (from first number) + 4 (from second number) = 4. No carry.\n2. Tens place: 2 + 5 = 7. No carry.\n3. Hundreds place: 9 + 1 = 10. So we write 0 and carry over 1.\n4. Thousands place: 1 + 1 = 2. Then add the carried 1: 2 + 1 = 3. No carry.\n5. Ten thousands place: 0 + 7 = 7. No carry.\n6. Hundred thousands place: 7 + 7 = 14. Write down 4, carry over 1.\n7. Millions place: 7 + 7 = 14. Add carried 1: 14 + 1 = 15. Write down 5, carry over 1.\n8. Ten millions place: 0 + 8 = 8. Add carried 1: 8 + 1 = 9. No carry.\n9. Hundred millions place: 7 + 1 = 8. No carry.\n\nSo putting all those together, starting from the rightmost digit:\n\n4 (ones), 7 (tens), 0 (hundreds), 3 (thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 4 (hundred thousands), 5 (millions), 9 (ten millions), 8 (hundred millions).\n\nSo the sum is 855,474,537.\n\nWait, let me verify that. Let me write the digits in order:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 4\n\nTens: 7\n\nHundreds: 0\n\nThousands: 3\n\nTen thousands: 7\n\nHundred thousands: 4\n\nMillions: 5\n\nTen millions: 9\n\nHundred millions: 8\n\nSo the number is 855,474,537.\n\nBut let me check this result by another method to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe by adding the numbers step by step.\n\nFirst, let's take 707,701,920 + 187,771,154.\n\nAlternatively, maybe break down the numbers into smaller parts.\n\n707,701,920 can be thought of as 700,000,000 + 7,000,000 + 700,000 + 19,20.\n\nSimilarly, 187,771,154 is 100,000,000 + 80,000,000 + 7,000,000 + 700,000 + 15,4.\n\nAdding these parts:\n\n700,000,000 + 100,000,000 = 800,000,000\n\n7,000,000 + 80,000,000 = 87,000,000\n\n700,000 + 700,000 = 1,400,000\n\n19,20 + 15,4 = 34,640\n\nNow add all those together:\n\n800,000,000 + 87,000,000 = 887,000,000\n\n887,000,000 + 1,400,000 = 888,400,000\n\n888,400,000 + 34,640 = 888,434,640\n\nWait, that's 888,434,640. But that's different from the previous result of 855,474,537. Hmm, so there's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in breaking down the numbers. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n707,701,920\n\n187,771,154\n\nBreaking down 707,701,920:\n\n700,000,000 + 7,000,000 + 700,000 + 19,200.\n\nWait, 19,200 is 19,200, not 19,20. Because the last three digits are 920, so 920 is 9 hundreds, 2 tens, 0 ones. So 19,200.\n\nSimilarly, 187,771,154:\n\n100,000,000 + 8,000,000 + 700,000 + 70,000 + 154.\n\nWait, 187,771,154:\n\nBreaking into parts:\n\n100,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n80,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n7,000,000 (millions)\n\n700,000 (ten thousands)\n\n15,400 (thousands + tens + ones). Wait, 154,000? Wait, 154,000 is 154 thousand. But in the original number, it's 154. Wait, confusion between thousands and ten thousands.\n\nWait, 187,771,154:\n\nDigits: 1 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 4 (ones). Wait, no. Wait, 187,771,154 is:\n\n1 (hundred millions)\n\n8 (ten millions)\n\n7 (millions)\n\n7 (hundred thousands)\n\n7 (ten thousands)\n\n1 (thousand)\n\n5 (hundred)\n\n4 (ten)\n\n4 (one)\n\nSo when breaking down:\n\n100,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n80,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n7,000,000 (millions)\n\n700,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n70,000 (ten thousands)\n\n1,000 (thousands)\n\n500 (hundreds)\n\n40 (tens)\n\n4 (ones)\n\nTherefore, when adding the parts:\n\nFirst number parts:\n\n700,000,000 (millions)\n\n7,000,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n700,000 (ten thousands)\n\n19,200 (ones, tens, hundreds, thousands)\n\nSecond number parts:\n\n100,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n80,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n7,000,000 (millions)\n\n700,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n70,000 (ten thousands)\n\n1,000 (thousands)\n\n500 (hundreds)\n\n40 (tens)\n\n4 (ones)\n\nSo adding all parts:\n\nStarting with the largest:\n\n700,000,000 + 100,000,000 = 800,000,000\n\n7,000,000 + 80,000,000 = 87,000,000\n\n700,000 + 700,000 = 1,400,000\n\n19,200 + 1,000 = 20,200\n\n20,200 + 500 = 20,700\n\n20,700 + 40 = 20,740\n\n20,740 + 4 = 20,744\n\nNow, adding all these:\n\n800,000,000 + 87,000,000 = 887,000,000\n\n887,000,000 + 1,400,000 = 888,400,000\n\n888,400,000 + 20,744 = 888,420,744\n\nWait, that's 888,420,744. But this contradicts the previous result. Hmm, so clearly, my initial breakdown was wrong. I need to figure out where I messed up.\n\nWait, let's start over. Let's take the original numbers:\n\n707,701,920\n\n187,771,154\n\nLet me add them step by step using the standard addition method, carefully.\n\nFirst, write them aligned:\n\n        707,701,920\n\n      + 187,771,154\n\n      -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nDigit 1 (ones): 0 + 4 = 4\n\nDigit 2 (tens): 2 + 5 = 7\n\nDigit 3 (hundreds): 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nDigit 4 (thousands): 1 + 1 = 2 + carry 1 = 3.\n\nDigit 5 (ten thousands): 0 + 7 = 7.\n\nDigit 6 (hundred thousands): 7 + 7 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nDigit 7 (millions): 7 + 7 = 14 + carry 1 = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n\nDigit 8 (ten millions): 0 + 8 = 8 + carry 1 = 9.\n\nDigit 9 (hundred millions): 7 + 1 = 8.\n\nSo the sum is 855,474,537.\n\nBut when I broke down the numbers into parts, I got 888,420,744. That's a big difference. So there must be an error in my breakdown method.\n\nWait, let's check the breakdown again. Let's take the first number: 707,701,920.\n\nBreaking into parts:\n\n700,000,000 + 7,000,000 + 700,000 + 19,200.\n\nWait, 700,000,000 + 7,000,000 is 707,000,000. Then + 700,000 is 707,700,000. Then + 19,200 is 707,719,200. But the original number is 707,701,920. So that's incorrect. Wait, so my breakdown was wrong.\n\nWait, 707,701,920 is 700,000,000 + 7,000,000 + 700,000 + 19,200. But 700,000,000 + 7,000,000 is 707,000,000. Then + 700,000 is 707,700,000. Then + 19,200 is 707,719,200. But the original number is 707,701,920. So that's 707,701,920. So the breakdown is wrong. The 707,701,920 is 700,000,000 + 7,000,000 + 700,000 + 19,200. But 700,000,000 + 7,000,000 + 700,000 = 707,700,000. Then adding 19,200 gives 707,719,200. But the original number is 707,701,920. So there's a discrepancy of 19,200 vs 19,20. So the breakdown is wrong. Therefore, my initial breakdown was incorrect. That's why when I added the parts, I got a different result. So the error was in how I broke down the numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct way is to use the column addition method, which gave me 855,474,537. Let me verify this result by another method.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the fact that 707,701,920 + 187,771,154.\n\nLet me add 707,701,920 + 187,771,154.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the method of adding each place value:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 0 + 4 = 4\n\nTens: 2 + 5 = 7\n\nHundreds: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nThousands: 1 + 1 = 2 + 1 (carry) = 3\n\nTen thousands: 0 + 7 = 7\n\nHundred thousands: 7 + 7 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\nMillions: 7 + 7 = 14 + 1 (carry) = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n\nTen millions: 0 + 8 = 8 + 1 (carry) = 9\n\nHundred millions: 7 + 1 = 8\n\nSo the sum is 855,474,537.\n\nTo verify, let's subtract 187,771,154 from 855,474,537 and see if we get 707,701,920.\n\n855,474,537 - 187,771,154.\n\nLet's compute:\n\n855,474,537\n\n- 187,771,154\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n7 - 4 = 3\n\n3 - 5. Hmm, can't do that. Need to borrow.\n\nSo, the ones place: 7 - 4 = 3.\n\nTens place: 3 - 5. Can't do, borrow from hundreds place.\n\nHundreds place: 5 becomes 4, tens place becomes 13. 13 - 5 = 8.\n\nThousands place: 4 - 1 = 3.\n\nTen thousands place: 7 - 7 = 0.\n\nHundred thousands place: 7 - 7 = 0.\n\nMillions place: 4 - 8. Can't do, need to borrow.\n\nWait, maybe this is getting too complicated. Alternatively, let's do the subtraction step by step.\n\n855,474,537\n\n- 187,771,154\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nOnes: 7 - 4 = 3\n\nTens: 3 - 5. Can't do, borrow 1 from hundreds place. So hundreds digit becomes 4, tens becomes 13. 13 - 5 = 8.\n\nHundreds: 4 - 1 = 3.\n\nThousands: 4 - 1 = 3.\n\nTen thousands: 7 - 7 = 0.\n\nHundred thousands: 7 - 7 = 0.\n\nMillions: 4 - 8. Can't do, need to borrow. So millions digit becomes 14, but since it's the millions place, we have to borrow from the hundred millions place.\n\nHundred millions: 8 becomes 7, millions digit becomes 14. 14 - 8 = 6.\n\nTen millions: 7 (after borrowing) - 7 = 0.\n\nHundred millions: 7 - 1 = 6.\n\nWait, let me check again:\n\nWait, original number: 8 5 5 , 4 7 4 , 5 3 7\n\nSubtracting 1 8 7 , 7 7 1 , 1 5 4\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nDigit 9 (ones): 7 - 4 = 3\n\nDigit 8 (tens): 3 - 5. Can't do, borrow 1 from hundreds. So hundreds digit becomes 4, tens becomes 13. 13 - 5 = 8.\n\nDigit 7 (hundreds): 4 - 1 = 3.\n\nDigit 6 (thousands): 4 - 1 = 3.\n\nDigit 5 (ten thousands): 7 - 7 = 0.\n\nDigit 4 (hundred thousands): 7 - 7 = 0.\n\nDigit 3 (millions): 4 - 8. Can't do, borrow 1 from hundred millions. So hundred millions digit becomes 7, millions digit becomes 14. 14 - 8 = 6.\n\nDigit 2 (ten millions): 7 (after borrowing) - 7 = 0.\n\nDigit 1 (hundred millions): 7 - 1 = 6.\n\nSo the result is 677,700,383. Wait, that's not matching. Wait, this can't be right. Wait, maybe I messed up the borrowing steps.\n\nWait, let's try again. Maybe writing it out:\n\n          855,474,537\n\n        - 187,771,154\n\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1. Ones: 7 - 4 = 3\n\n2. Tens: 3 - 5. Can't do, borrow 1 from hundreds. So hundreds digit becomes 4, tens becomes 13. 13 - 5 = 8.\n\n3. Hundreds: 4 - 1 = 3.\n\n4. Thousands: 4 - 1 = 3.\n\n5. Ten thousands: 7 - 7 = 0.\n\n6. Hundred thousands: 7 - 7 = 0.\n\n7. Millions: 4 - 8. Can't do, borrow 1 from hundred millions. So hundred millions digit becomes 7, millions digit becomes 14. 14 - 8 = 6.\n\n8. Ten millions: 7 (hundred millions became 7, so ten millions is 7) - 7 = 0.\n\n9. Hundred millions: 7 - 1 = 6.\n\nSo the result is 677,700,383. Wait, but 855,474,537 - 187,771,154 should be 707,701,920. But according to this subtraction, it's 677,700,383. That's a problem. So there must be an error in my subtraction steps.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the subtraction. Let me check with another method. Let's use the calculator approach.\n\nWait, since I don't have a calculator, maybe I can use another way. Let's compute 855,474,537 - 187,771,154.\n\nFirst, subtract 100,000,000 from 855,474,537: 855,474,537 - 100,000,000 = 755,474,537\n\nThen subtract 87,771,154 from 755,474,537.\n\n755,474,537 - 80,000,000 = 675,474,537\n\n675,474,537 - 7,771,154.\n\n675,474,537 - 7,000,000 = 668,474,537\n\n668,474,537 - 700,000 = 667,774,537\n\n667,774,537 - 7,000 = 667,767,537\n\n667,767,537 - 100 = 667,767,437\n\n667,767,437 - 40 = 667,767,437 - 40 = 667,767,397\n\n667,767,397 - 3 = 667,767,394\n\nWait, this is getting too convoluted. Maybe this subtraction method isn't working. Alternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in the column addition. Let me check the column addition again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n707,701,920\n\n+ 187,771,154\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n        707,701,920\n\n      + 187,771,154\n\n      -------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nDigit 1: 0 + 4 = 4\n\nDigit 2: 2 + 5 = 7\n\nDigit 3: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nDigit 4: 1 + 1 = 2 + 1 = 3\n\nDigit 5: 0 + 7 = 7\n\nDigit 6: 7 + 7 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\nDigit 7: 7 + 7 = 14 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n\nDigit 8: 0 + 8 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nDigit 9: 7 + 1 = 8\n\nSo the sum is 855,474,537.\n\nBut when I subtract 187,771,154 from 855,474,537, I get 677,700,383, which is not 707,701,920. Therefore, there must be a mistake in the addition. But where?\n\nWait, let's check the addition step by step again.\n\nFirst number: 707,701,920\n\nSecond number: 187,771,154\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n0 + 4 = 4 (ones)\n\n2 + 5 = 7 (tens)\n\n9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1 (hundreds)\n\n1 + 1 = 2 + 1 = 3 (thousands)\n\n0 + 7 = 7 (ten thousands)\n\n7 + 7 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1 (hundred thousands)\n\n7 + 7 = 14 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1 (millions)\n\n0 + 8 = 8 + 1 = 9 (ten millions)\n\n7 + 1 = 8 (hundred millions)\n\nSo the sum is 855,474,537.\n\nBut when subtracting, it's not matching. Therefore, the error must be in the subtraction steps. Let me try subtracting 187,771,154 from 855,474,537 again.\n\n855,474,537\n\n- 187,771,154\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n7 - 4 = 3 (ones)\n\n3 - 5: Can't do, borrow 1 from hundreds. So hundreds digit becomes 4, tens becomes 13. 13 - 5 = 8 (tens place)\n\n4 (hundreds) - 1 = 3 (hundreds)\n\n4 (thousands) - 1 = 3 (thousands)\n\n7 (ten thousands) - 7 = 0 (ten thousands)\n\n7 (hundred thousands) - 7 = 0 (hundred thousands)\n\n4 (millions) - 8: Can't do, borrow 1 from hundred millions. So hundred millions becomes 7, millions becomes 14. 14 - 8 = 6 (millions)\n\n7 (ten millions) - 7 = 0 (ten millions)\n\n7 (hundred millions) - 1 = 6 (hundred millions)\n\nSo the result is 677,700,383. But 855,474,537 - 187,771,154 should be 707,701,920. Therefore, there's a mistake in the column addition. So where is the error?\n\nWait, let's check the column addition again. Let's use another method. Let's write the numbers as they are:\n\nFirst number: 707,