000,000 + 109,774,760 = 1,009,774,760\n\nWait, that's different from 101,090,560. Wait, something's wrong here. Wait, 137,931,054 + 871,843,706.\n\nWait, 137,931,054 + 871,843,706.\n\nLet me compute 137,931,054 + 871,843,706:\n\nStart with 137,931,054 + 871,843,706.\n\nLet me add the numbers:\n\n137,931,054\n\n+871,843,706\n\n=?\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, add 137,931,054 + 800,000,000 = 937,931,054\n\nThen add 71,843,706 to 937,931,054:\n\n937,931,054 + 70,000,000 = 1,007,931,054\n\n1,007,931,054 + 1,843,706 = 1,009,774,760\n\nWait, so according to this, the sum is 1,009,774,760. But according to the digit-by-digit addition, I got 101,090,560. These two results are different. There must be a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let's check the digit-by-digit addition again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n137,931,054\n\n+871,843,706\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n```\n 137,931,054\n+871,843,706\n------------\n```\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n4 + 6 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nTens place: 5 + 0 = 5 + 1 = 6\n\nHundreds: 0 + 7 = 7\n\nThousands: 3 + 3 = 6\n\nTen thousands: 1 + 4 = 5\n\nHundred thousands: 9 + 8 = 17 \u2192 7, carry 1\n\nMillions: 7 + 1 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nTen millions: 3 + 7 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nHundred millions: 1 + 8 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nFinally, the carryover 1.\n\nSo the digits from left to right are:\n\n1 (hundred millions)\n\n0 (ten millions)\n\n1 (millions)\n\n0 (hundred thousands)\n\n9 (ten thousands)\n\n0 (thousands)\n\n5 (hundreds)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n0 (units)\n\nSo the number is 101,090,560.\n\nBut when I added the numbers by breaking them down, I got 1,009,774,760. These are conflicting results. There must be an error in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let's check the breakdown method again.\n\n137,931,054 + 871,843,706.\n\nBreaking into 137,931,054 + 800,000,000 = 937,931,054\n\nThen 937,931,054 + 71,843,706.\n\n937,931,054 + 70,000,000 = 1,007,931,054\n\n1,007,931,054 + 1,843,706 = 1,009,774,760.\n\nBut according