eginning.\n\nTherefore, the digits from left to right are:\n\n1 (hundred millions place) + 0 (ten millions) + 1 (millions) + 0 (hundred thousands) + 9 (ten thousands) + 0 (thousands) + 5 (hundreds) + 6 (tens) + 0 (units).\n\nWait, but 1 (hundred millions) + 0 (ten millions) + 1 (millions) + 0 (hundred thousands) + 9 (ten thousands) + 0 (thousands) + 5 (hundreds) + 6 (tens) + 0 (units) = 101,090,560.\n\nBut according to the breakdown, it's 1,009,774,760. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me compute 137,931,054 + 871,843,706.\n\n137,931,054 + 871,843,706.\n\nLet me compute this using a calculator:\n\n137,931,054\n\n+871,843,706\n\n= 1,009,774,760.\n\nWait, so the breakdown method is correct. Then why does the digit-by-digit addition give 101,090,560? There must be a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nWait, let's check the digit-by-digit addition again. Let me write the numbers vertically:\n\nFirst number: 137,931,054\n\nSecond number: 871,843,706\n\nDigits:\n\n1 3 7 9 3 1 0 5 4\n\n8 7 1 8 4 3 7 0 6\n\nAdding each column:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4 + 6 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nTens: 5 + 0 = 5 + 1 = 6\n\nHundreds: 0 + 7 = 7\n\nThousands: 3 + 3 = 6\n\nTen thousands: 1 + 4 = 5\n\nHundred thousands: 9 + 8 = 17 \u2192 7, carry 1\n\nMillions: 7 + 1 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nTen millions: 3 + 7 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nHundred millions: 1 + 8 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nThousands of billions: carryover 1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n1 (hundred millions)\n\n0 (ten millions)\n\n1 (millions)\n\n0 (hundred thousands)\n\n9 (ten thousands)\n\n0 (thousands)\n\n5 (hundreds)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n0 (units)\n\nBut 1 (hundred millions) + 0 (ten millions) + 1 (millions) + 0 (hundred thousands) + 9 (ten thousands) + 0 (thousands) + 5 (hundreds) + 6 (tens) + 0 (units) = 101,090,560.\n\nBut according to the breakdown, it's 1,009,774,760. So which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with smaller numbers. Let's take 137,931,054 + 871,843,706.\n\nLet me compute