over 1\n\nHundred-millions: 0 + 4 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, 873,892 is 8 7 3 8 9 2. Wait, no. Wait, 873,892 is 8 hundred thousands, 7 ten thousands, 3 thousands, 8 hundreds, 9 tens, 2 units. So when adding to 46,861,563:\n\n46,861,563 is 4 ten millions, 6 millions, 8 hundred thousands, 6 ten thousands, 1 thousand, 5 hundreds, 6 tens, 3 units.\n\nWait, no. Wait, 46,861,563 is 46,861,563. Breaking down:\n\n4 (ten millions)\n\n6 (millions)\n\n8 (hundred thousands)\n\n6 (ten thousands)\n\n1 (thousands)\n\n5 (hundreds)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n3 (units)\n\nSo when adding 873,892 (which is 8 7 3 8 9 2) to 46,861,563 (which is 0 4 6 8 6 1 5 6 3), we need to align the digits properly:\n\nLet's write both numbers with the same number of digits:\n\n873,892 \u2192 000873,892\n\n46,861,563 \u2192 046,861,563\n\nAdding:\n\n000873,892\n\n+046,861,563\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 2 + 3 = 5\n\nTens: 9 + 6 = 15 \u2192 5, carryover 1\n\nHundreds: 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1\n\nThousands: 3 + 1 = 4 + 0 = 4\n\nTen-thousands: 7 + 6 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nHundred-thousands: 0 + 8 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nMillions: 0 + 6 = 6\n\nTen-millions: 0 + 4 = 4\n\nSo the sum is 459,534,455.\n\nTherefore, adding 769,000,000 + 459,534,455 = 1,228,534,455. Wait, that's not possible. Wait, 769,000,000 + 459,534,455. Let's compute that:\n\n769,000,000\n\n+459,534,455\n\n= 1,228,534,455.\n\nBut that's not matching the previous result. So clearly, there's a mistake in the breakdown method. Therefore, the error must be in that approach.\n\nWait, so the problem is that when I broke down 269,873,892 into 269,000,000 + 873,892 and 546,861,563 into 500,000,000 + 46,861,563, and then added them, I must have made a mistake in the addition of 873,892 + 46,861,563. Because when I added those two numbers using the column method, I got 459,534,455, but when I broke them down into parts, I must have miscalculated.\n\nWait, let's check 873,892 + 46,861,563 again.\n\nWait, 873,892 + 46,861,563. Let'