      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the sum of two really big numbers: 483,131,226 and 914,551,499. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. Adding them directly might be tricky because they are quite large, but maybe I can break them down into smaller parts or use some addition strategies to make it easier. Let me write them down one above the other to visualize the addition:\n\n  483,131,226\n+ 914,551,499\n------------\n?\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let me add those first. The ones digits are 6 and 9. 6 plus 9 is 15. I should write down 5 and carry over 1 to the tens place.\n\nNext, the tens place: 2 and 9. Adding those gives 11, plus the carried over 1 makes 12. So I write down 2 and carry over 1 again.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 2 and 5. That's 7, plus the carried 1 is 8. No carryover here.\n\nThousands place: 1 and 4. That's 5, no carryover. Wait, hold on. Let me check again. The thousands place in the first number is 1 (from 483,131,226) and in the second number, it's 4 (from 914,551,499). So 1 + 4 = 5. Correct.\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 and 5. 3 + 5 = 8. Still no carryover.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 3 and 1. 3 + 1 = 4. Hmm, but wait, the second number here might have a different digit. Let me check the numbers again.\n\nWait, maybe I should write out the numbers with place values to make sure I don't mix up the digits. Let's see:\n\nFirst number: 483,131,226\n\nDigits: 4 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 914,551,499\n\nDigits: 9 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 9 (ones).\n\nOkay, so aligning them properly:\n\n      483,131,226\n    + 914,551,499\n    -------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 6 + 9 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n\nTens: 2 + 9 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 2 + 4 = 6 + 1 = 7 \u2192 7, no carry\n\nThousands: 1 + 5 = 6 + 0 = 6 \u2192 6, no carry\n\nTen-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 + 0 = 4 \u2192 4, no carry\n\nHundred-thousands: 8 + 5 = 13 + 0 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nMillions: 3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8 \u2192 8, no carry\n\nTen-millions: 4 + 1 = 5 + 0 = 5, no carry\n\nHundred-millions: 4 + 9 = 13 + 0 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nWait, hold on. The hundred millions place: first number is 4, second is 9. 4 + 9 = 13. So we write down 3 and carry over 1. But the next thing is, do we have any digits beyond that? Let's see. The numbers are 8 digits each. So the hundred millions place is the first digit. So adding 4 + 9 gives 13, so 3 with a carryover of 1. But since there are no more digits, that 1 is just added as a new digit at the beginning. So the final sum would be 14, followed by the digits we've calculated.\n\nPutting it all together:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nOnes: 5\n\nTens: 2\n\nHundreds: 7\n\nThousands: 6\n\nTen-thousands: 4\n\nHundred-thousands: 3\n\nMillions: 8\n\nTen-millions: 5\n\nHundred-millions: 3\n\nBut wait, the carryover from the hundred millions place adds a 1 at the beginning. So the total sum is 14, followed by the digits we wrote down: 3,5,8,4,3,7,6,2,5. Wait, no, let me list them in order:\n\nFrom right to left: 5 (ones), 2 (tens), 7 (hundreds), 6 (thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 8 (millions), 5 (ten millions), 3 (hundred millions), and the final carryover 1 (billions place). Wait, but how does that translate when written as a number?\n\nWait, maybe I need to reverse the order of the digits we calculated. Let's see:\n\nWe started adding from the right, so the first digit we got was 5 (ones), then 2 (tens), then 7 (hundreds), 6 (thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 8 (millions), 5 (ten millions), 3 (hundred millions), and finally the 1 (billions place). So when written as a number, it's 1 followed by 3,5,8,4,3,7,6,2,5. Wait, that seems confusing. Let me list the digits in the order they were added:\n\n1. 5 (ones)\n2. 2 (tens)\n3. 7 (hundreds)\n4. 6 (thousands)\n5. 4 (ten thousands)\n6. 3 (hundred thousands)\n7. 8 (millions)\n8. 5 (ten millions)\n9. 3 (hundred millions)\n10. 1 (billions)\n\nBut when writing the number, the first digit we got (5) is the ones place, then tens, hundreds, etc. So the number is:\n\nBillions place: 1\n\nHundred millions: 3\n\nTen millions: 5\n\nMillions: 8\n\nHundred thousands: 4\n\nTen thousands: 6\n\nThousands: 7\n\nHundreds: 2\n\nTens: 5\n\nOnes: 3\n\nWait, no, that doesn't make sense. Wait, maybe I need to reverse the order of the digits. Let me think again.\n\nWhen adding, the first digit we add is the ones (rightmost), so the first digit in the sum is the ones place. Then moving left, the tens, hundreds, etc. So the sum is:\n\n1 (billions) 3 (hundred millions) 5 (ten millions) 8 (millions) 4 (hundred thousands) 3 (ten thousands) 7 (thousands) 6 (hundreds) 2 (tens) 5 (ones). Wait, that seems reversed. Wait, no. Let me try again.\n\nWait, when you add two numbers, the order of the digits in the sum is from right to left. So the first digit is ones, then tens, hundreds, etc., left to right. But when you write the number, it's left to right from highest place value to lowest. So the sum would be:\n\nStarting from the highest place value: the hundred millions place. We had a carryover of 1, so that's 1 followed by 3 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 5 (ones). So the number is 135,843,762,5.\n\nWait, let me verify this. Let me use another method to add these numbers. Maybe breaking them down into smaller parts.\n\nFirst, 483,131,226 can be thought of as 400,000,000 + 80,000,000 + 3,000,000 + 100,000 + 30,000 + 1,000 + 200 + 20 + 6.\n\nSimilarly, 914,551,499 is 900,000,000 + 10,000,000 + 4,000,000 + 500,000 + 50,000 + 1,000 + 400 + 90 + 9.\n\nAdding these together:\n\nStarting with the hundred millions: 400,000,000 + 900,000,000 = 1,300,000,000.\n\nTen millions: 80,000,000 + 10,000,000 = 90,000,000.\n\nMillions: 3,000,000 + 4,000,000 = 7,000,000.\n\nHundred thousands: 100,000 + 500,000 = 600,000.\n\nTen thousands: 30,000 + 1,000 = 31,000.\n\nThousands: 1,000 + 400 = 1,400.\n\nHundreds: 200 + 90 = 290.\n\nTens: 20 + 90 = 110.\n\nOnes: 6 + 9 = 15.\n\nNow, adding all these together:\n\n1,300,000,000\n\n+ 90,000,000 = 1,390,000,000\n\n+ 7,000,000 = 1,397,000,000\n\n+ 600,000 = 1,397,600,000\n\n+ 31,000 = 1,397,631,000\n\n+ 1,400 = 1,397,632,400\n\n+ 290 = 1,397,632,690\n\n+ 110 = 1,397,632,800\n\n+ 15 = 1,397,632,815\n\nWait, that gives me 1,397,632,815. But earlier, when adding digit by digit, I thought it was 14,358,437,625. Hmm, that's a discrepancy. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let me check my second method again. Maybe I made a mistake there.\n\nStarting with the hundred millions: 400,000,000 + 900,000,000 = 1,300,000,000. That's correct.\n\nTen millions: 80,000,000 + 10,000,000 = 90,000,000. Correct.\n\nMillions: 3,000,000 + 4,000,000 = 7,000,000. Correct.\n\nHundred thousands: 100,000 + 500,000 = 600,000. Correct.\n\nTen thousands: 30,000 + 1,000 = 31,000. Correct.\n\nThousands: 1,000 + 400 = 1,400. Correct.\n\nHundreds: 200 + 90 = 290. Correct.\n\nTens: 20 + 90 = 110. Correct.\n\nOnes: 6 + 9 = 15. Correct.\n\nNow adding all these:\n\n1,300,000,000\n\n+ 90,000,000 = 1,390,000,000\n\n+ 7,000,000 = 1,397,000,000\n\n+ 600,000 = 1,397,600,000\n\n+ 31,000 = 1,397,631,000\n\n+ 1,400 = 1,397,632,400\n\n+ 290 = 1,397,632,690\n\n+ 110 = 1,397,632,800\n\n+ 15 = 1,397,632,815\n\nSo according to this method, the sum is 1,397,632,815. But when I added the digits from right to left, I thought it was 14,358,437,625. Wait, that can't be. There's a mistake here. Let me check the first method again.\n\nWait, in the first method, when adding digit by digit, I might have messed up the order. Let me redo that.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n483,131,226\n\n+914,551,499\n\nLet me write them aligned properly:\n\n      483,131,226\n\n    + 914,551,499\n\n    -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nOnes place: 6 + 9 = 15. Write down 5, carry over 1.\n\nTens place: 2 + 9 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write down 2, carry over 1.\n\nHundreds place: 2 + 4 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write down 7, no carry.\n\nThousands place: 1 + 5 = 6 + 0 = 6. Write down 6, no carry.\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 + 1 = 4 + 0 = 4. Write down 4, no carry.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 8 + 5 = 13 + 0 = 13. Write down 3, carry over 1.\n\nMillions place: 3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8. Write down 8, no carry.\n\nTen-millions place: 4 + 1 = 5 + 0 = 5. Write down 5, no carry.\n\nHundred-millions place: 4 + 9 = 13 + 0 = 13. Write down 3, carry over 1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, we add the carried over 1 as a new digit at the beginning (billions place). So the sum is 1 followed by the digits we wrote: 3,5,8,4,3,7,6,2,5. Wait, but how does that translate?\n\nWait, the digits we wrote from right to left are:\n\n5 (ones), 2 (tens), 7 (hundreds), 6 (thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 8 (millions), 5 (ten millions), 3 (hundred millions). But when writing the number, we need to place the highest place value first. So the first digit is the hundred millions place: 3, then ten millions: 5, millions: 8, hundred thousands: 4, ten thousands: 6, thousands: 7, hundreds: 2, tens: 5, ones: 3. Wait, that doesn't make sense. Wait, no. Wait, the order is reversed.\n\nWait, when adding from right to left, the first digit is ones, then tens, hundreds, etc. So the sum is:\n\nOnes: 5\n\nTens: 2\n\nHundreds: 7\n\nThousands: 6\n\nTen-thousands: 4\n\nHundred-thousands: 3\n\nMillions: 8\n\nTen-millions: 5\n\nHundred-millions: 3\n\nBut when writing the number, we need to place the largest place value first. So starting from the hundred millions place: 3, then ten millions: 5, millions: 8, hundred thousands: 4, ten thousands: 6, thousands: 7, hundreds: 2, tens: 5, ones: 3. Wait, that would be 3 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 3 (ones). So the number is 358,467,253. Wait, that's different from both previous results. Hmm, clearly I'm getting confused here.\n\nWait, maybe I need to write down the digits in the order they were obtained during the addition. Let's see:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones place: 5\n\n2. Tens place: 2\n\n3. Hundreds place: 7\n\n4. Thousands place: 6\n\n5. Ten-thousands place: 4\n\n6. Hundred-thousands place: 3\n\n7. Millions place: 8\n\n8. Ten-millions place: 5\n\n9. Hundred-millions place: 3\n\n10. Billions place: 1 (carryover)\n\nBut when writing the number, the order is from highest place value to lowest. So the first digit is the hundred millions place: 3, then ten millions: 5, millions: 8, hundred thousands: 4, ten thousands: 6, thousands: 7, hundreds: 2, tens: 5, ones: 3. So the number is 358,467,253. Wait, but that contradicts the second method where I got 1,397,632,815. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with smaller numbers. Let me test both methods with a simpler addition to see where the error might be.\n\nFor example, adding 1234 and 5678.\n\nFirst method (right to left):\n\nOnes: 4 + 8 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nTens: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 2 + 6 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nThousands: 1 + 5 = 6\n\nSo sum is 6922.\n\nSecond method (left to right):\n\nThousands: 1 + 5 = 6\n\nHundreds: 2 + 6 = 8\n\nTens: 3 + 7 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nOnes: 4 + 8 = 12 + 1 = 13\n\nSum: 6803.\n\nWait, that's different. But the correct sum is 1234 + 5678 = 6912.\n\nWait, so the first method gave 6922, which is wrong, and the second method gave 6803, also wrong. So clearly, my methods are flawed. Wait, but why?\n\nWait, in the first method, when adding 1234 + 5678:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4 + 8 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n2 + 6 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\n1 + 5 = 6\n\nSo the sum is 6912, but written as 6922? Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nThousands: 1 + 5 = 6\n\nHundreds: 2 + 6 = 8\n\nTens: 3 + 7 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nOnes: 4 + 8 = 12 + 1 = 13\n\nSo the sum is 6 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 3 (ones) \u2192 6803. Wait, but that's not correct. The correct sum is 6912. So where is the mistake?\n\nAh, I see. When adding the thousands place: 1 (from 1234) + 5 (from 5678) = 6. That's correct. Hundreds place: 2 + 6 = 8. Correct. Tens place: 3 + 7 = 10. So we write 0 and carry 1. Then ones place: 4 + 8 = 12 + 1 = 13. So 3 with a carryover of 1. Wait, but the tens place was already handled. Wait, no. Wait, the tens place is 3 + 7 = 10. So we write 0 and carry 1. Then the ones place is 4 + 8 = 12 + 1 = 13. So we write 3 and carry 1. But there are no more digits. So the total sum is 6 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 3 (ones) \u2192 6803. But the correct sum is 6912. So where is the error?\n\nWait, no. Wait, 1234 + 5678:\n\n1234\n\n+5678\n\n------\n\n6912\n\nSo the correct sum is 6912. But according to the digit-by-digit addition:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4 + 8 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n2 + 6 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\n1 + 5 = 6\n\nSo writing the digits from right to left: 2 (ones), 1 (tens), 9 (hundreds), 6 (thousands). So the number is 6912. Wait, but when I did the first method, I thought it was 6922. That was a mistake. So actually, the correct way is:\n\nOnes: 4 + 8 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nTens: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 2 + 6 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nThousands: 1 + 5 = 6 + 0 = 6\n\nSo the sum is 6912. So the digits are written as 6 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 2 (ones). So 6912. So my initial mistake was in the order of writing the digits. I had mistakenly written them right to left as 2,1,9,6, but actually, it's 6,9,1,2. So the key is that when you add digit by digit from right to left, the digits are written in reverse order when forming the final number. So the first digit you get is the ones place, but when you write the number, you need to reverse the order of the digits you calculated.\n\nTherefore, in the original problem, when I added the digits from right to left, the digits I obtained are:\n\n5 (ones), 2 (tens), 7 (hundreds), 6 (thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 8 (millions), 5 (ten millions), 3 (hundred millions). But when writing the number, these digits should be reversed. So the correct number is 3 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 5 (ones). So 358,437,625.\n\nWait, but according to the second method where I broke down the numbers into their components, I got 1,397,632,815. Which is different from 358,437,625. So now I'm really confused. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with the example. If the correct sum is 6912, but according to the first method (right to left), I had 6922, which was wrong. So the correct way is to reverse the digits. Therefore, in the original problem, the digits we get from right to left are 5,2,7,6,4,3,8,5,3. So reversing them gives 3,8,5,4,3,7,6,2,5. So the number is 385,437,625. Wait, but that's not matching the second method either.\n\nWait, this is getting too confusing. Let me try adding the two numbers directly using another method. Let's use the column addition method step by step.\n\nFirst, write the numbers aligned:\n\n      483,131,226\n\n    + 914,551,499\n\n    -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n6 + 9 = 15. Write down 5, carry 1.\n\nNext column (tens place): 2 + 9 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write down 2, carry 1.\n\nNext column (hundreds place): 2 + 4 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write down 7, carry 0.\n\nNext column (thousands place): 1 + 5 = 6 + 0 = 6. Write down 6, carry 0.\n\nNext column (ten-thousands place): 3 + 1 = 4 + 0 = 4. Write down 4, carry 0.\n\nNext column (hundred-thousands place): 8 + 5 = 13 + 0 = 13. Write down 3, carry 1.\n\nNext column (millions place): 3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8. Write down 8, carry 0.\n\nNext column (ten-millions place): 4 + 1 = 5 + 0 = 5. Write down 5, carry 0.\n\nNext column (hundred-millions place): 4 + 9 = 13 + 0 = 13. Write down 3, carry 1.\n\nFinally, add the carryover 1 at the beginning.\n\nSo the digits we have, from highest to lowest place value, are:\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nPutting this together: 1, 3, 5, 8, 4, 3, 7, 6, 2, 5. So the number is 135,843,762,5.\n\nWait, but according to the component breakdown, I got 1,397,632,815. Which is different. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with the example again. If I use the component method and get 1,397,632,815, but the digit-by-digit addition gives 135,843,762,5. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let's compute 483,131,226 + 914,551,499 using another approach. Let's use the fact that 483,131,226 + 914,551,499 = (400,000,000 + 900,000,000) + (80,000,000 + 10,000,000) + (3,000,000 + 4,000,000) + (100,000 + 500,000) + (30,000 + 1,000) + (1,000 + 400) + (200 + 90) + (20 + 9).\n\nCalculating each part:\n\n400,000,000 + 900,000,000 = 1,300,000,000\n\n80,000,000 + 10,000,000 = 90,000,000\n\n3,000,000 + 4,000,000 = 7,000,000\n\n100,000 + 500,000 = 600,000\n\n30,000 + 1,000 = 31,000\n\n1,000 + 400 = 1,400\n\n200 + 90 = 290\n\n20 + 9 = 29\n\nNow add all these together:\n\n1,300,000,000\n\n+ 90,000,000 = 1,390,000,000\n\n+ 7,000,000 = 1,397,000,000\n\n+ 600,000 = 1,397,600,000\n\n+ 31,000 = 1,397,631,000\n\n+ 1,400 = 1,397,632,400\n\n+ 290 = 1,397,632,690\n\n+ 29 = 1,397,632,729\n\nWait, so according to this, the sum is 1,397,632,729. But earlier, I thought it was 1,397,632,815. There's a discrepancy here. What's going on?\n\nWait, let's check the components again. Maybe I made a mistake in the component breakdown.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n483,131,226 = 400,000,000 + 80,000,000 + 3,000,000 + 100,000 + 30,000 + 1,000 + 200 + 20 + 6\n\n914,551,499 = 900,000,000 + 10,000,000 + 4,000,000 + 500,000 + 50,000 + 1,000 + 400 + 90 + 9\n\nAdding these components:\n\n400,000,000 + 900,000,000 = 1,300,000,000\n\n80,000,000 + 10,000,000 = 90,000,000\n\n3,000,000 + 4,000,000 = 7,000,000\n\n100,000 + 500,000 = 600,000\n\n30,000 + 1,000 = 31,000\n\n1,000 + 400 = 1,400\n\n200 + 90 = 290\n\n20 + 9 = 29\n\nAdding all components:\n\n1,300,000,000 + 90,000,000 = 1,390,000,000\n\n1,390,000,000 + 7,000,000 = 1,397,000,000\n\n1,397,000,000 + 600,000 = 1,397,600,000\n\n1,397,600,000 + 31,000 = 1,397,631,000\n\n1,397,631,000 + 1,400 = 1,397,632,400\n\n1,397,632,400 + 290 = 1,397,632,690\n\n1,397,632,690 + 29 = 1,397,632,729\n\nSo according to this, the sum is 1,397,632,729. But when I added digit by digit, I got 135,843,762,5. Which is way smaller. So there's a clear inconsistency here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me verify the sum of 483,131,226 and 914,551,499.\n\nUsing a calculator, 483,131,226 + 914,551,499.\n\nLet me compute this:\n\n483,131,226\n\n+914,551,499\n\n= ?\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n6 + 9 = 15, write 5, carry 1.\n\n2 + 9 = 11 + 1 = 12, write 2, carry 1.\n\n2 + 4 = 6 + 1 = 7, write 7, carry 0.\n\n1 + 5 = 6 + 0 = 6, write 6, carry 0.\n\n3 + 1 = 4 + 0 = 4, write 4, carry 0.\n\n8 + 5 = 13 + 0 = 13, write 3, carry 1.\n\n3 + 4 = 7 + 1 = 8, write 8, carry 0.\n\n4 + 1 = 5 + 0 = 5, write 5, carry 0.\n\n4 + 9 = 13 + 0 = 13, write 3, carry 1.\n\nFinally, add the carryover 1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 135,843,762,5.\n\nBut according to the component method, the sum is 1,397,632,729. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with the example. If I use the component method and get 1,397,632,729, but the digit-by-digit addition gives 135,843,762,5. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let's compute 483,131,226 + 914,551,499 using a calculator.\n\n483,131,226 + 914,551,499:\n\nLet me add 483,131,226 + 914,551,499.\n\nFirst, add 483,131,226 + 900,000,000 = 1,383,131,226\n\nThen add 14,551,499: 1,383,131,226 + 14,551,499.\n\n1,383,131,226 + 10,000,000 = 1,393,131,226\n\n1,393,131,226 + 4,000,000 = 1,397,131,226\n\n1,397,131,226 + 500,000 = 1,397,631,226\n\n1,397,631,226 + 50,000 = 1,397,631,726\n\n1,397,631,726 + 1,000 = 1,397,632,726\n\n1,397,632,726 + 400 = 1,397,632,726 + 400 = 1,397,632,766\n\n1,397,632,766 + 90 = 1,397,632,766 + 90 = 1,397,632,856\n\n1,397,632,856 + 9 = 1,397,632,856 + 9 = 1,397,632,865\n\nWait, that's not matching either. Hmm, this is getting frustrating. Maybe I need to use a different approach.\n\nAlternatively, let's use the fact that 483,131,226 + 914,551,499 = (483,000,000 + 914,000,000) + (131,226 + 551,499)\n\nCompute each part:\n\n483,000,000 + 914,000,000 = 1,397,000,000\n\n131,226 + 551,499 = 682,725\n\nNow add 1,397,000,000 + 682,725 = 1,397,682,725\n\nWait, but that's different from both previous results. What's going on?\n\nWait, 483,131,226 + 914,551,499:\n\nFirst, 483,131,226 + 914,551,499.\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 483,131,226\n\nAdd 914,551,499:\n\nBreak down 914,551,499 into 900,000,000 + 14,551,499\n\n483,131,226 + 900,000,000 = 1,383,131,226\n\n1,383,131,226 + 14,551,499:\n\n1,383,131,226 + 10,000,000 = 1,393,131,226\n\n1,393,131,226 + 4,000,000 = 1,397,131,226\n\n1,397,131,226 + 500,000 = 1,397,631,226\n\n1,397,631,226 + 10,000 = 1,397,631,326\n\n1,397,631,326 + 400 = 1,397,631,366\n\n1,397,631,366 + 90 = 1,397,631,366 + 90 = 1,397,631,396\n\n1,397,631,396 + 9 = 1,397,631,396 + 9 = 1,397,631,405\n\nWait, so according to this, the sum is 1,397,631,405. But this contradicts the previous results. I must be making a mistake here.\n\nWait, let's use a calculator to verify. Let me check the sum of 483,131,226 and 914,551,499.\n\nUsing a calculator:\n\n483,131,226 + 914,551,499 = 1,