ands)\n\n7 + 7 + 1 = 15 (millions) \u2192 5, carryover 1\n\n6 + 4 + 1 = 11 (ten-millions) \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\n6 + 4 + 1 = 11 (hundred-millions) \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\n4 + 4 + 1 = 9 (billions)\n\nFinally, the carryover 1 is added to the leftmost digit (ten-billions place).\n\nSo, the result is 11,116,630,7794? Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nBillions: 1\n\nHundred millions: 1\n\nTen millions: 1\n\nMillions: 1\n\nHundred thousands: 6\n\nTen thousands: 3\n\nThousands: 0\n\nHundreds: 7\n\nTens: 7\n\nUnits: 4\n\nSo, written as 11,116,630,774.\n\nWait, but when I first thought, I had 11,116,630,777. Hmm, so which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with smaller numbers. Let me verify the intermediate steps.\n\nStarting with the units:\n\n4 + 3 = 7. Correct.\n\nTens: 0 + 7 = 7. Correct.\n\nHundreds: 8 + 1 = 9. Correct.\n\nThousands: 7 + 7 = 14. So, 4 in thousands place, carryover 1. Correct.\n\nTen-thousands: 5 + 5 = 10, plus 1 = 11. So, 1 in ten-thousands, carryover 1. Correct.\n\nHundred-thousands: 3 + 5 = 8, plus 1 = 9. Correct.\n\nMillions: 7 + 7 = 14, plus 1 = 15. So, 5 in millions, carryover 1. Correct.\n\nTen-millions: 6 + 4 = 10, plus 1 = 11. So, 1 in ten-millions, carryover 1. Correct.\n\nHundred-millions: 6 + 4 = 10, plus 1 = 11. So, 1 in hundred-millions, carryover 1. Correct.\n\nBillions: 4 + 4 = 8, plus 1 = 9. Wait, hold on! Wait, in the original numbers, the billions place in 667,358,704 is 6 (hundred millions), 4 in the other number is 4 (hundred millions). Wait, no, wait. Wait, the numbers are:\n\nFirst number: 667,358,704\n\nWhich is 6 hundred millions, 6 ten millions, 7 millions, 3 hundred thousands, 5 ten thousands, 8 thousands, 7 hundreds, 0 tens, 4 units.\n\nSecond number: 447,557,173\n\nWhich is 4 hundred millions, 4 ten millions, 7 millions, 5 hundred thousands, 5 ten thousands, 7 thousands, 1 hundred, 7 tens, 3 units.\n\nSo, when adding the billions place (the leftmost digits), which are both 6 and 4. Wait, but in the previous step-by-step addition, I thought the hundreds millions were 6 and 4, but actually, the hundreds millions place is the fifth digit from the right. Wait, no, let's clarify.\n\nWait, in the number 667,358,704:\n\nDigits from right to left (units to ten-billions):\n\nPosition 1: 4 (units)\n\nPosition 2: 0 (tens)\n\nPosition 3: 8 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 4: 7 (thousands)\n\nPosition 5: 5 (ten-thousands)\n\nPosition 6: 3 (hundred-thousands)\n\nPosition 7: 7 (millions)\n\nPosition 8: 6 (ten-millions)\n\nPosition 9: 6 (hundred-millions)\n\nPosition 10: 4 (hundred-millions)\n\nWait, no, hold on. Wait, the number 667,358,704 is:\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n6 (ten millions)\n\n7 (millions)\n\n3 (hundred thousands)\n\n5 (ten thousands)\n\n8 (thousands)\n\n7 (hundreds)\n\n0 (tens)\n\n4 (units)\n\nSimilarly, 447,557,173 is:\n\n4 (hundred millions)\n\n4 (ten millions)\n\n7 (millions)\n\n5 (hundred thousands)\n\n5 (ten thousands)\n\n7 (thousands)\n\n1 (hundred)\n\n7 (tens)\n\n3 (units)\n\nSo, when adding the numbers, we have to align them by place value. So, the hundred millions place is the 9th digit from the right. Let's list all digits with their positions:\n\nFor 667,358,704:\n\nPosition 1: 4 (units)\n\nPosition 2: 0 (tens)\n\nPosition 3: 8 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 4: 7 (thousands)\n\nPosition 5: 5 (ten-thousands)\n\nPosition 6: 3 (hundred-thousands)\n\nPosition 7: 7 (millions)\n\nPosition 8: 6 (ten-millions)\n\nPosition 9: 6 (hundred-millions)\n\nPosition 10: 4 (hundred-millions)\n\nWait, no, hold on. Wait, the hundred millions place is the 9th digit from the right. So, in 667,358,704:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition 1: units (4)\n\nPosition 2: tens (0)\n\nPosition 3: hundreds (8)\n\nPosition 4: thousands (7)\n\nPosition 5: ten-thousands (5)\n\nPosition 6: hundred-thousands (3)\n\nPosition 7: millions (7)\n\nPosition 8: ten-millions (6)\n\nPosition 9: hundred-millions (6)\n\nPosition 10: billions (4)\n\nWait, no, that can't be. Wait, the number 667,358,704 is:\n\n6 (hundred millions)\