sition 3 is hundreds, position 2 is tens, position 1 is units.\n\nSo, the digits in order from left to right (ten-billions to units):\n\n1 (ten-billions), 1 (hundred-millions), 1 (ten-millions), 1 (millions), 5 (hundred-thousands), 9 (ten-thousands), 1 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 7 (units).\n\nTherefore, the number is 11,115,910,797.\n\nWait, let's check that again. Wait, the digits are:\n\n1 (ten-billions)\n\n1 (hundred-millions)\n\n1 (ten-millions)\n\n1 (millions)\n\n5 (hundred-thousands)\n\n9 (ten-thousands)\n\n1 (thousands)\n\n9 (hundreds)\n\n7 (tens)\n\n7 (units)\n\nSo, writing them out: 11,115,910,797.\n\nWait, but this contradicts my previous result. Hmm, so where is the mistake here?\n\nWait, let's re-examine the positions. When adding, the leftmost digit in the sum is the result of adding the leftmost digits of the two numbers. In this case, both numbers have 6 in the hundred millions place. Wait, no, hold on. Wait, in the original numbers:\n\nFirst number: 667,358,704\n\nHundred millions: 6\n\nTen millions: 6\n\nMillions: 7\n\nHundred thousands: 3\n\nTen thousands: 5\n\nThousands: 8\n\nHundreds: 7\n\nTens: 0\n\nUnits: 4\n\nSecond number: 447,557,173\n\nHundred millions: 4\n\nTen millions: 4\n\nMillions: 7\n\nHundred thousands: 5\n\nTen thousands: 5\n\nThousands: 7\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nTens: 7\n\nUnits: 3\n\nSo, when adding the hundred millions place: 6 + 4 = 10. So, the hundred millions digit is 0 with a carryover of 1. Wait, hold on! Wait, I think I made a mistake earlier. The hundred millions place is the 9th digit from the right. So, in the first number, the hundred millions digit is 6, and in the second number, it's 4. So, adding them: 6 + 4 = 10. Therefore, the hundred millions digit is 0 and carryover 1. But in my previous addition, I added the hundred millions digits as 6 + 4 = 10, which would give 0 with a carryover 1. But in my initial step-by-step, I had written:\n\n\"Billions place: 4 + 4 = 8 + 1 = 9\"\n\nWait, no. Wait, confusion between hundred millions and billions. Let me clarify:\n\nThe first number: 667,358,704\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 6 (hundred millions)\n\n- 6 (ten millions)\n\n- 7 (millions)\n\n- 3 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 5 (ten thousands)\n\n- 8 (thousands)\n\n- 7 (hundreds)\n\n- 0 (tens)\n\n- 4 (units)\n\nThe second number: 447,557,173\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 4 (hundred millions)\n\n- 4 (ten millions)\n\n- 7 (millions)\n\n- 5 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 5 (ten thousands)\n\n- 7 (thousands)\n\n- 1 (hundred)\n\n- 7 (tens)\n\n- 3 (units)\n\nSo, when adding the hundred millions place (the 9th digit from the right), it's 6 + 4 = 10. So, the hundred millions digit is 0, carryover 1. Then, moving to the ten millions place (8th digit from the right): 6 + 4 = 10 + 1 (carryover) = 11. So, ten millions digit is 1, carryover 1. Then millions place (7th digit from the right): 7 + 7 = 14 + 1 = 15. Millions digit is 5, carryover 1. Ten thousands place (5th digit from the right): 5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11. Ten thousands digit is 1, carryover 1. Thousands place (4th digit from the right): 8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16. Thousands digit is 6, carryover 1. Hundreds place (3rd digit from the right): 7 + 1 = 8 + 1 = 9. Hundreds digit is 9. Tens place (2nd digit from the right): 0 + 7 = 7. Tens digit is 7. Units place (1st digit from the right): 4 + 3 = 7. Units digit is 7.\n\nWait, this is different from my previous breakdown. So, where was the mistake?\n\nAh! I see. Earlier, I incorrectly associated the hundred millions place with the 9th digit from the right, but in reality, the hundred millions place is the 9th digit from the right, but when adding, the carryover from the millions place affects the ten millions place, not the hundred millions place. Wait, no. Wait, let's do this step by step again, carefully.\n\nLet me list all digits with their positions (from right to left, units to ten-billions):\n\nPosition 1: 4 (units) - 4 + 3 = 7\n\nPosition 2: 0 (tens) - 0 + 7 = 7\n\nPosition 3: 8 (hundreds) - 8 + 1 = 9\n\nPosition 4: 7 (thousands) - 7 + 7 = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1\n\nPosition 5: 5 (ten-thousands) - 5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nPosition 6: 3 (hundred-thousands) - 3 + 5 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nPosition 7: 7 (millions) - 7 + 7 = 14 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carryover 1\n\nPosition 8: 6 (ten-millions) - 6 + 4 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nPosition 9: 6 (hundred-millions) - 6 + 4 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nPosition 10: 4 (billions) - 4 + 4 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nPosition 11: 1 (ten-billions) - carryover 1\n\nSo, the digits from position 11 to 1 (left to right):\n\n1 (ten-billions), 1 (hundred-millions), 1 (ten-millions), 1 (millions), 5 (hundred-thousands), 9 (ten-thousands), 1 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 7 (units)\n\nTherefore, the number is 11,115,910,797.\n\nWait, let's verify this result by another method. Let's compute the sum step by step:\n\nFirst, let's add the two numbers:\n\n667,358,704\n\n+447,557,173\n\nLet me add them digit by digit with carries:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nDigit 1: 4 + 3 = 7\n\nDigit 2: 0 + 7 = 7\n\nDigit 3: 8 + 1 = 9\n\nDigit 4: 7 + 7 = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1\n\nDigit 5: 5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nDigit 6: 3 + 5 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nDigit 7: 7 + 7 = 14 + 1 = 15 \u2192 5, carryover 1\n\nDigit 8: 6 + 4 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nDigit 9: 6 + 4 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nDigit 10: 4 + 4 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nDigit 11: 1 (carryover)\n\nSo, the digits from left to right (ten-billions to units):\n\n1, 1, 1, 1, 5, 9, 1, 9, 7, 7\n\nWhich is 11,115,910,797.\n\nTo confirm, let's perform the addition using another approach, perhaps breaking down the numbers:\n\n667,358,704 = 600,000,000 + 60,000,000 + 7,000,000 + 300,000 + 50,000 + 8,000 + 700 + 0 + 4\n\n447,557,173 = 400,000,000 + 40,000,000 + 7,000,000 + 500,000 + 50,000 + 7,000 + 100 + 70 + 3\n\nAdding these together:\n\n600,000,000 + 400,000,000 = 1,000,000,000\n\n60,000,000 + 40,000,000 = 100,000,000\n\n7,000,000 + 7,000,000 = 14,000,000\n\n300,000 + 500