      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the sum of two really big numbers: 547,410,910 and 712,680,990. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. Adding them directly might be tricky because of all those zeros and the different lengths of the numbers. Maybe I can break them down into smaller parts or use some place value understanding?\n\nFirst, let me write down both numbers aligned properly to add them vertically. That usually helps with larger numbers. So:\n\n547,410,910  \n+712,680,990  \n---------------\n\nNow, let's add each column starting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place.\n\nStarting with the ones place: 0 + 0 = 0. That's straightforward.\n\nMoving to the tens place: 1 + 9 = 10. Hmm, I need to carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nNext, the hundreds place: 9 + 0 = 9, plus the carried over 1 makes 10. Another carryover of 1.\n\nThousands place: 0 + 0 = 0, plus the carried 1 is still 1.\n\nTen thousands place: 1 + 8 = 9.\n\nHundred thousands place: 4 + 6 = 10. Another carryover of 1.\n\nMillions place: 7 + 2 = 9, plus the carried 1 makes 10. So another carryover of 1.\n\nTen millions place: 5 + 1 = 6, plus the carried 1 is 7.\n\nHundred millions place: 4 + 7 = 11. Wait, hold on. Let me check that again. The first number has 4 in the hundred millions place, and the second number has 7. So 4 + 7 = 11. Then we have to add any carried over 1 from the previous step. Wait, actually, in the previous step, the millions place was 7 + 2 = 9 with a carryover of 1. Then moving to the hundred millions place, it's 4 + 7. But wait, the second number is 712,680,990. Let me make sure I have the places right.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the places when writing them down. Let me check again.\n\nFirst number: 547,410,910. Breaking it down:\n\n- 5 (hundred millions)\n- 4 (ten millions)\n- 7 (millions)\n- 4 (hundred thousands)\n- 1 (ten thousands)\n- 0 (thousands)\n- 9 (hundreds)\n- 1 (tens)\n- 0 (ones)\n\nSecond number: 712,680,990.\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n- 7 (hundred millions)\n- 1 (ten millions)\n- 2 (millions)\n- 6 (hundred thousands)\n- 8 (ten thousands)\n- 0 (thousands)\n- 9 (hundreds)\n- 9 (tens)\n- 0 (ones)\n\nSo when adding vertically:\n\nHundred millions: 5 + 7 = 12. Then carryover 1.\n\nTen millions: 4 + 1 = 5, plus the carried 1 is 6.\n\nMillions: 7 + 2 = 9.\n\nHundred thousands: 4 + 6 = 10. Carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands: 1 + 8 = 9, plus 1 is 10. Carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 0 + 0 = 0, plus 1 is 1.\n\nHundreds: 9 + 9 = 18. Carryover 1.\n\nTens: 1 + 9 = 10, plus 1 is 11. Carryover 1.\n\nOnes: 0 + 0 = 0, plus 1 is 1.\n\nWait, this seems conflicting with my initial breakdown. Let me try again step by step, column by column, writing down each place.\n\nLet me list the numbers with all the digits aligned:\n\n          547,410,910  \n        + 712,680,990  \n        -------------\n           125,999,800\n\nWait, maybe I can check by adding the two numbers step by step.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see. 547,410,910 is 547 million plus 410 thousand plus 910. Similarly, 712,680,990 is 712 million plus 680 thousand plus 990. Adding the millions: 547 + 712 = 1,259 million. Then the thousands: 410,000 + 680,000 = 1,090,000. Then the remaining numbers: 910 + 990 = 1,900. Then adding all together: 1,259,000,000 + 1,090,000 + 1,900. Let's compute that.\n\n1,259,000,000 + 1,090,000 = 1,260,090,000. Then adding 1,900 gives 1,260,091,900. Wait, but that contradicts the previous thought. Hmm, maybe I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, 547,410,910 is 547 million 410 thousand 910. So 547 million + 410,000 + 910. Similarly, 712,680,990 is 712 million + 680,000 + 990. So adding the millions: 547 + 712 = 1,259 million. Then thousands: 410,000 + 680,000 = 1,090,000. Then the remaining parts: 910 + 990 = 1,900. So total is 1,259,000,000 + 1,090,000 + 1,900. Let's compute:\n\n1,259,000,000 + 1,090,000 = 1,260,090,000. Then 1,260,090,000 + 1,900 = 1,260,091,900. But when I did the vertical addition earlier, I go