sands), 9 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 0 (ones)  \n\nAdding each column:\n\n1. Hundred millions: 5 + 7 = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 1  \n2. Ten millions: 4 + 1 = 5 + 1 = 6  \n3. Millions: 7 + 2 = 9  \n4. Hundred thousands: 4 + 6 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1  \n5. Ten thousands: 1 + 8 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1  \n6. Thousands: 0 + 0 = 0 + 1 = 1  \n7. Hundreds: 9 + 9 = 18 \u2192 8, carryover 1  \n8. Tens: 1 + 9 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1  \n9. Ones: 0 + 0 = 0 + 1 = 1  \n\nSo the sum is 2 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (ones). So the number is 269,000,180,01. But this is incorrect. So why the discrepancy?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the alignment when writing the numbers. Let me check the original numbers again:\n\nFirst number: 547,410,910  \nSecond number: 712,680,990  \n\nWait, 547,410,910 is 547 million 410 thousand 910.  \n712,680,990 is 712 million 680 thousand 990.  \n\nAdding these: 547 million + 712 million = 1,259 million.  \n410 thousand + 680 thousand = 1,090 thousand.  \n910 + 990 = 1,900.  \nTotal: 1,259,000,000 + 1,090,000 + 1,900 = 1,260,091,900.  \n\nYes, that's correct. So why does the vertical addition give a different result? Because I must have misaligned the digits. Let me check the vertical addition again.\n\nWait, perhaps I added the hundred millions place incorrectly. Let me write the numbers with all digits:\n\nFirst number: 5 4 7 4 1 0 9 1 0  \nSecond number: 7 1 2 6 8 0 9 9 0  \n\nAdding each column:\n\n1. Ones: 0 + 0 = 0  \n2. Tens: 1 + 9 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1  \n3. Hundreds: 9 + 9 = 18 \u2192 8, carryover 1  \n4. Thousands: 0 + 0 = 0 \u2192 0, carryover 0  \n5. Ten thousands: 1 + 8 = 9 \u2192 9, carryover 0  \n6. Hundred thousands: 4 + 6 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1  \n7. Millions: 7 + 2 = 9 \u2192 9, carryover 0  \n8. Ten millions: 4 + 1 = 5 \u2192 5, carryover 0  \n9. Hundred millions: 5 + 7 = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 1  \n\nWait, but the 