ake a mistake with so many digits.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes place: 7 + 3 = 10. Write 0, carry 1. Correct.\n\nTens: 9 + 6 = 15 + 1 = 16. Write 6, carry 1. Correct.\n\nHundreds: 5 + 4 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1. Correct.\n\nThousands: 9 + 3 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1. Correct.\n\nTen-thousands: 8 + 9 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carry 1. Correct.\n\nHundred-thousands: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1. Correct.\n\nMillions: 0 + 2 = 2 + 1 = 3. Write 3, carry 0. Correct.\n\nTen-millions: 3 + 4 = 7 + 0 = 7. Write 7. Correct.\n\nSo putting all those together: 7 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (ones). Wait, but hold on, the hundreds place was 0? Let me check.\n\nWait, when adding the hundreds place: 5 + 4 = 9, plus the carried 1 is 10. So we write 0 and carry 1. So the hundreds digit is 0. Then thousands place: 9 + 3 = 12 + 1 = 13. So thousands digit is 3. Ten-thousands: 8 + 9 = 17 + 1 = 18. So ten-thousands digit is 8. Hundred-thousands: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 = 11. So hundred-thousands digit is 1. Then millions: 0 + 2 = 2 + 1 = 3. Ten-millions: 3 + 4 = 7. So the total is 73,718,0630? Wait, but that seems like 73,718,0630. Wait, but let me check again.\n\nWait, the digits from left to right after adding:\n\nTen millions: 7\n\nMillions: 3\n\nHundred thousands: 1\n\nTen thousands: 8\n\nThousands: 3\n\nHundreds: 0\n\nTens: 6\n\nOnes: 0\n\nSo the number is 73,718,0630. But let me verify this with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can add the numbers digit by digit without commas:\n\n30,384,9597 is 3 0 3 8 4 9 5 9 7\n\nWait, no. Wait, 30,384,9597: breaking it down:\n\n3 (ten millions)\n\n0 (millions)\n\n3 (hundred thousands)\n\n8 (ten thousands)\n\n4 (thousands)\n\n9 (hundreds)\n\n5 (tens)\n\n9 (ones)\n\nWait, no. Wait, commas separate every three digits starting from the right. So 30,384,9597 is:\n\n30 million, 384 thousand, 9597. So the digits are:\n\n3 0 3 8 4 9 5 9 7\n\nSimilarly, 42,573,9463 is:\n\n4 2 5 7 3 9 4 6 3\n\nSo aligning them:\n\n3 0 3 8 4 9 5 9 7\n\n+4 2 5 7 3 9 4 6 3\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n      3 0 3 8 4 9 5 9 7\n\n    + 4 2 5 7 3 9 4 6 3\n\n    ---------------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n7 + 3 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nNext digit (tens place): 9 + 6 = 15 + 1 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\nHundreds place: 5 + 4 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nThousands place: 8 + 3 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1. Wait, hold on, here's a discrepancy. Wait, in my initial calculation, I thought thousands place was 9 + 3 = 12 + 1 = 13. But here, the thousands place in the first number is 8, and in the second number, it's 3. So 8 + 3 = 11, plus the carried 1 is 12. So we write 2 and carry 1. Wait, but in my first calculation, I had 13. So here's a mistake. Let me correct that.\n\nWait, so in the thousands place:\n\nFirst number: 8 (ten thousands place is 8, right? Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nFirst number: 3 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 9 (ones). So the thousands place is 4.\n\nSecond number: 4 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 6 (ones). So the thousands place is 3.\n\nTherefore, adding thousands place: 4 + 3 = 7. Wait, but in my first calculation, I had 9 + 3 = 12. Wait, no. Wait, no, the first number's thousands place is 4, not 9. Wait, I think I messed up the places.\n\nWait, let's clarify:\n\nFirst number: 30,384,9597\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 3 (ten millions)\n\n- 0 (millions)\n\n- 3 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 8 (ten thousands)\n\n- 4 (thousands)\n\n- 9 (hundreds)\n\n- 5 (tens)\n\n- 9 (ones)\n\nSecond number: 42,573,9463\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 4 (ten millions)\n\n- 2 (millions)\n\n- 5 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 7 (ten thousands)\n\n- 3 (thousands)\n\n- 9 (hundreds)\n\n- 4 (tens)\n\n- 6 (ones)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 7 + 3 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nTens: 5 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nHundreds: 4 (from first number) + 4 (from second number) = 8 + 1 = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\nThousands: 8 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nTen-thousands: 3 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nHundred-thousands: 0 (from first number) + 5 (from second number) = 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nMillions: 0 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) = 2 + 0 = 2. Write 2, carry 0.\n\nTen-millions: 3 (from first number) + 4 (from second number) = 7 + 0 = 7. Write 7.\n\nWait, so this is different from my initial calculation. So where did I go wrong earlier?\n\nAh, I see. Initially, I thought the thousands place was 9 + 3, but actually, in the first number, the thousands place is 4, not 9. I must have miscounted the places when converting to digits. So this is the correct step-by-step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 7 + 3 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\n2. Tens: 5 (from first number's thousands place? Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nWait, let me list the digits properly.\n\nFirst number: 3 0 3 8 4 9 5 9 7\n\nPositions (from right):\n\n1: 7 (ones)\n\n2: 5 (tens)\n\n3: 9 (hundreds)\n\n4: 4 (thousands)\n\n5: 8 (ten thousands)\n\n6: 3 (hundred thousands)\n\n7: 0 (millions)\n\n8: 3 (ten millions)\n\nSecond number: 4 2 5 7 3 9 4 6 3\n\nDigits (from right):\n\n1: 3 (ones)\n\n2: 4 (tens)\n\n3: 9 (hundreds)\n\n4: 3 (thousands)\n\n5: 7 (ten thousands)\n\n6: 5 (hundred thousands)\n\n7: 2 (millions)\n\n8: 4 (ten millions)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nPosition 1: 7 + 3 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nPosition 2: 5 (from first number) + 4 (from second number) = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, position 2 is tens place. The first number's tens digit is 5, and the second number's tens digit is 4. So 5 + 4 = 9, plus carry 1 is 10. So write 0, carry 1.\n\nPosition 3: Hundreds place. First number's hundreds digit is 9, second number's hundreds digit is 9. 9 + 9 = 18 + 1 (carry) = 19. Write 9, carry 1.\n\nWait, no. Wait, position 3 is hundreds place. First number's hundreds digit is 9, second number's hundreds digit is 9. So 9 + 9 = 18 + 1 (carry from tens place) = 19. So write 9, carry 1.\n\nPosition 4: Thousands place. First number's thousands digit is 4, second number's thousands digit is 3. 4 + 3 = 7 + 1 (carry from hundreds place) = 8. Write 8, carry 0.\n\nPosition 5: Ten-thousands place. First number's ten-thousands digit is 8, second number's ten-thousands digit is 7. 8 + 7 = 15 + 0 = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n\nWait, no. Wait, position 5 is ten-thousands place. First number's ten-thousands digit is 8, second number's ten-thousands digit is 7. 8 + 7 = 15. So write 5, carry 1.\n\nPosition 6: Hundred-thousands place. First number's hundred-thousands digit is 3, second number's hundred-thousands digit is 5. 3 + 5 = 8 + 1 (carry from ten-thousands place) = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\nPosition 7: Millions place. First number's millions digit is 0, second number's millions digit is 2. 0 + 2 = 2 + 0 = 2. Write 2, carry 0.\n\nPosition 8: Ten-millions place. First number's ten-millions digit is 3, second number's ten-millions digit is 4. 3 + 4 = 7 + 0 = 7. Write 7.\n\nSo putting all those together:\n\nTen-millions: 7\n\nMillions: 2\n\nHundred-thousands: 9\n\nTen-thousands: 5\n\nThousands: 8\n\nHundreds: 9\n\nTens: 0\n\nOnes: 0\n\nWait, that gives 72,958,090. Wait, but let me check again.\n\nWait, positions:\n\n1. Ones: 0\n\n2. Tens: 0\n\n3. Hundreds: 9\n\n4. Thousands: 8\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 5\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 9\n\n7. Millions: 2\n\n8. Ten-millions: 7\n\nSo the number is 7 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 0 (ones). So 72,958,090.\n\nBut wait, this contradicts my previous result. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's verify with another method. Let's