n4 + 7 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n6 + 5 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n0 + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2. Write 2, carry 0.\n\n6 + 3 = 9 + 0 = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\n3 + 5 = 8 + 0 = 8. Write 8, carry 0.\n\n4 + 6 = 10 + 0 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nFinally, the carryover 1 is written as the next digit.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to hundred millions) are:\n\n4, 3, 9, 2, 2, 2, 6, 9, 8, 0, 1.\n\nWait, but that would be 1,067,982,014? Wait, no. Wait, let's list them:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nPosition 1: 4 (units)\n\nPosition 2: 3 (tens)\n\nPosition 3: 9 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 4: 2 (thousands)\n\nPosition 5: 2 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition 6: 2 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition 7: 6 (millions)\n\nPosition 8: 9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition 9: 8 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition 10: 0 (billions)\n\nPosition 11: 1 (ten billions)\n\nWait, but that would be 11 digits. However, the numerical sum is 1,067,645,711, which is 10 digits. So there's a problem here.\n\nWait, let's check the numerical sum again:\n\n436,064,957 + 631,579,777.\n\nLet me add them using another method.\n\n436,064,957\n\n+631,579,777\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\nStart with the first number: 436,064,957\n\nAdd 631,579,777:\n\n436,064,957 + 631,579,777\n\n= (400,000,000 + 36,064,957) + (600,000,000 + 31,579,777)\n\n= 400,000,000 + 600,000,000 + 36,064,957 + 31,579,777\n\n= 1,000,000,000 + (36,064,957 + 31,579,777)\n\n= 1,000,000,000 + 68,644,734\n\n= 1,068,644,734\n\nWait, but this contradicts the previous calculation. Wait, what's going on?\n\nWait, 436,064,957 + 631,579,777.\n\nLet me compute 436,064,957 + 631,579,777.\n\nStart from the right:\n\n7 + 7 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\n5 + 7 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\n9 + 9 = 18 + 1 = 19. Write 9, carry 1.\n\n4 + 7 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n6 + 5 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n0 + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2. Write 2, carry 0.\n\n6 + 3 = 9 + 0 = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\n3 + 5 = 8 + 0 = 8. Write 8, carry 0.\n\n4 + 6 = 10 + 0 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nFinally, the carryover 1 is written as the next digit.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n1 (ten billions place)\n\n0 (billions place)\n\n6 (hundred millions place)\n\n7 (ten millions place)\n\n4 (millions place)\n\n4 (hundred thousands place)\n\n4 (ten thousands place)\n\n7 (thousands place)\n\n3 (hundreds place)\n\n2 (tens place)\n\n4 (units place)\n\nWait, that would be 1,067,447,342. But this is different from the previous results. I'm getting confused here.\n\nWait, let's use a calculator to verify.\n\nFirst number: 436,064,957\n\nSecond number: 631,579,777\n\nSum: 436,064,957 + 631,579,777.\n\nLet me compute this:\n\n436,064,957\n\n+631,579,777\n\n= ?\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n7 + 7 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\n5 + 7 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\n9 + 9 = 18 + 1 = 19. Write 9, carry 1.\n\n4 + 7 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n6 + 5 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n0 + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2. Write 2, carry 0.\n\n6 + 3 = 9 + 0 = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\n3 + 5 = 8 + 0 = 8. Write 8, carry 0.\n\n4 + 6 = 10 + 0 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nFinally, the carryover 1 is written as the next digit.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n1 (ten billions place)\n\n0 (billions place)\n\n6 (hundred millions place)\n\n7 (ten millions place)\n\n4 (millions place)\n\n4 (hundred thousands place)\n\n4 (ten thousands place)\n\n7 (thousands place)\n\n2 (hundreds place)\n\n3 (tens place)\n\n4 (units place)\n\nWait, that would be 1,067,447,342. But when I added them step by step before, I got 1,067,645,711. There's a discrepancy here. What's wrong?\n\nWait, let's check with actual numbers:\n\n436,064,957 + 631,579,777.\n\nLet me compute 436,064,957 + 631,579,777.\n\nFirst, 436,064,957 + 600,000,000 = 1,036,064,957\n\nThen, 1,036,064,957 + 31,579,777 = ?\n\n1,036,064,957 + 30,000,000 = 1,066,064,957\n\n1,066,064,957 + 1,579,777 = 1,067,644,734\n\n1,067,644,734 + 977 = 1,067,645,711.\n\nSo the correct sum is 1,067,645,711.\n\nBut according to the digit