      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the sum of two really big numbers: 50,584,0948 and 31,518,5871. Hmm, let me think. Adding them directly might be tricky because they're so large. Maybe I can use the standard addition method, aligning them by place value and adding each column step by step. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, I'll write down the numbers one on top of the other, making sure the commas are in the right places. Wait, in some countries, commas are used as thousand separators, right? So 50,584,0948 would be 50 million, 584 thousand, and 948. Similarly, 31,518,5871 is 31 million, 518 thousand, and 5871. Let me confirm that. Yeah, commas separate thousands, millions, etc. So breaking them down:\n\n50,584,0948:\n- 50 million\n- 584 thousand\n- 948\n\n31,518,5871:\n- 31 million\n- 518 thousand\n- 5871\n\nSo when adding, I need to add each corresponding place value. Let me write them out vertically, aligning the digits:\n\n```\n 50,584,0948\n+31,518,5871\n------------\n```\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (the units place):\n\n8 (from 50,584,0948) + 1 (from 31,518,5871) = 9\n\nNext column (tens place):\n\n4 + 7 = 11. Write down 1, carry over 1.\n\nNext (hundreds place):\n\n9 + 8 = 17, plus the carried 1 makes 18. Write down 8, carry over 1.\n\nThousands place:\n\n0 + 5 = 5, plus the carried 1 = 6. Write down 6, no carry.\n\nTen-thousands place:\n\n4 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 12. Write down 2, carry over 1.\n\nHundred-thousands place:\n\n8 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) = 9, plus the carried 1 = 10. Write down 0, carry over 1.\n\nMillions place:\n\n5 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) = 8, plus the carried 1 = 9. Write down 9.\n\nTen-millions place:\n\n0 (from first number) + 5 (from second number) = 5.\n\nHundred-millions place:\n\n5 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) = 6.\n\nSo putting all those together, starting from the right:\n\nUnits: 9\n\nTens: 1 (with carryover 1)\n\nHundreds: 8 (with carryover 1)\n\nThousands: 6\n\nTen-thousands: 2 (with carryover 1)\n\nHundred-thousands: 0 (with carryover 1)\n\nMillions: 9\n\nTen-millions: 5\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nWait, let me check that again. Maybe I messed up the order. Let me write the digits step by step from right to left as we add each column:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n8 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) = 9. So units place is 9.\n\nNext (tens place):\n\n4 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) = 11. So write 1, carry over 1.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n9 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 17, plus the carried 1 = 18. Write 8, carry over 1.\n\nThousands place:\n\n0 (from first number) + 5 (from second number) = 5, plus carried 1 = 6. Write 6, no carry.\n\nTen-thousands place:\n\n4 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 12, plus any carryover? Wait, the thousands place already handled the carryover. Wait, no. Wait, the thousands place was the fourth digit from the right. Let me list all the digits with their positions:\n\nFirst number: 5 0 5 8 4 0 9 4 8\n\nPositions (from right): 8 (units), 7 (tens), 6 (hundreds), 5 (thousands), 4 (ten-thousands), 3 (hundred-thousands), 2 (millions), 1 (ten-millions), 0 (hundred-millions)\n\nWait, maybe I should index them from right to left. Let me try that.\n\nFirst number: 50,584,0948\n\nDigits from right (units to hundred-millions):\n\nPosition 1: 8 (units)\n\nPosition 2: 4 (tens)\n\nPosition 3: 9 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 4: 0 (thousands)\n\nPosition 5: 4 (ten-thousands)\n\nPosition 6: 8 (hundred-thousands)\n\nPosition 7: 5 (millions)\n\nPosition 8: 0 (ten-millions)\n\nPosition 9: 5 (hundred-millions)\n\nPosition 10: 5 (billions place? Wait, no. Wait, 50,584,0948 is 5*10^9 + 0*10^8 + 5*10^7 + 8*10^6 + 4*10^5 + 0*10^4 + 9*10^3 + 4*10^2 + 8*10^1 + 8*10^0.\n\nSo the digits from right (units) to left (hundred-millions):\n\nPosition 1: 8\n\nPosition 2: 4\n\nPosition 3: 9\n\nPosition 4: 0\n\nPosition 5: 4\n\nPosition 6: 8\n\nPosition 7: 5\n\nPosition 8: 0\n\nPosition 9: 5\n\nPosition 10: 5\n\nWait, maybe I need to reverse the digits. Let me write the number as 50,584,0948:\n\nDigits: 5, 0, 5, 8, 4, 0, 9, 4, 8\n\nPositions from right (units) to left (hundred-millions):\n\n1: 8\n\n2: 4\n\n3: 9\n\n4: 0\n\n5: 4\n\n6: 8\n\n7: 5\n\n8: 0\n\n9: 5\n\n10: 5\n\nWait, no. Wait, the first digit from the left is the hundred millions place. So 50,584,0948 is:\n\n5 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 8 (units). So positions from right:\n\nUnits (position 1): 8\n\nTens (position 2): 4\n\nHundreds (position 3): 9\n\nThousands (position 4): 0\n\nTen thousands (position 5): 4\n\nHundred thousands (position 6): 8\n\nMillions (position 7): 5\n\nTen millions (position 8): 0\n\nHundred millions (position 9): 5\n\nSo when adding, starting from position 1 (units) to position 9 (hundred millions):\n\nLet me list the digits with their positions:\n\nPosition 1: 8\n\nPosition 2: 4\n\nPosition 3: 9\n\nPosition 4: 0\n\nPosition 5: 4\n\nPosition 6: 8\n\nPosition 7: 5\n\nPosition 8: 0\n\nPosition 9: 5\n\nNow, adding them step by step:\n\nStart with position 1: 8\n\nPosition 2: 4 + 8 (carryover?) Wait, no. Wait, when adding, we start from the rightmost digit (position 1). Let me do it step by step:\n\nPosition 1: 8\n\nPosition 2: 4 + 0 (since the second number's position 2 is 0) = 4. No carryover.\n\nPosition 3: 9 + 9 = 18. Write down 8, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 4: 0 + 5 + 1 (carryover) = 6. Write down 6, no carryover.\n\nPosition 5: 4 + 4 = 8. Write down 8, no carryover.\n\nPosition 6: 8 + 8 = 16. Write down 6, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 7: 5 + 5 + 1 (carryover) = 11. Write down 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 8: 0 + 0 + 1 (carryover) = 1. Write down 1, no carryover.\n\nPosition 9: 5 + 3 = 8. Write down 8, no carryover.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, the second number is 31,518,5871. Let me check its digits:\n\n31,518,5871:\n\nDigits from left: 3,1,5,1,8,5,8,7,1\n\nSo positions from right:\n\nPosition 1: 1\n\nPosition 2: 7\n\nPosition 3: 8\n\nPosition 4: 5\n\nPosition 5: 8\n\nPosition 6: 5\n\nPosition 7: 1\n\nPosition 8: 5\n\nPosition 9: 3\n\nPosition 10: 1\n\nWait, maybe I confused the positions. Let me clarify.\n\nWait, the first number is 50,584,0948. Its digits from left to right (billions to units):\n\n5 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 8 (units).\n\nThe second number is 31,518,5871. Its digits from left to right:\n\n3 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 1 (units).\n\nSo when adding, aligning the numbers:\n\n```\n    50,584,0948\n  +31,518,5871\n------------\n```\n\nSo the digits are aligned as:\n\nHundred millions: 5 + 3\n\nTen millions: 0 + 1\n\nMillions: 5 + 5\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 1\n\nTen thousands: 4 + 8\n\nThousands: 0 + 5\n\nHundreds: 9 + 8\n\nTens: 4 + 7\n\nUnits: 8 + 1\n\nWait, that's a different way. Maybe this is clearer. Let me list each place value:\n\nHundred millions: 5 + 3 = 8\n\nTen millions: 0 + 1 = 1\n\nMillions: 5 + 5 = 10\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 1 = 9\n\nTen thousands: 4 + 8 = 12\n\nThousands: 0 + 5 = 5\n\nHundreds: 9 + 8 = 17\n\nTens: 4 + 7 = 11\n\nUnits: 8 + 1 = 9\n\nNow, adding these together:\n\nStart from the rightmost (units): 9\n\nTens: 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands: 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nMillions: 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen millions: 1 + 1 = 2. Write 2, no carryover.\n\nHundred millions: 8. Write 8.\n\nSo putting all together from right to left (units to hundred millions):\n\n9 (units), 1 (tens), 8 (hundreds), 6 (thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 0 (hundred thousands), 1 (millions), 2 (ten millions), 8 (hundred millions).\n\nSo the total is 82,102,1689? Wait, wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, when we added the hundred millions place: 5 + 3 = 8. Ten millions: 0 + 1 = 1. Millions: 5 + 5 = 10. Hundred thousands: 8 + 1 = 9. Ten thousands: 4 + 8 = 12. Thousands: 0 + 5 = 5. Hundreds: 9 + 8 = 17. Tens: 4 + 7 = 11. Units: 8 + 1 = 9.\n\nSo writing the digits in order from hundred millions to units:\n\n8 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 9 (units).\n\nWait, that would be 82,102,1689. But let me verify this with another method.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can add the numbers step by step:\n\n50,584,0948 + 31,518,5871.\n\nLet me add 50,584,0948 + 30,000,0000 = 80,584,0948\n\nThen add 1,518,5871: 80,584,0948 + 1,518,5871.\n\nAdding 80,584,0948 + 1,000,000 = 81,584,0948\n\nThen 81,584,0948 + 518,5871.\n\nLet's break down 518,5871:\n\n500,000 + 18,5871 = 518,5871\n\nSo 81,584,0948 + 500,000 = 81,634,0948\n\nThen 81,634,0948 + 18,5871.\n\n81,634,0948 + 10,000 = 81,644,0948\n\n81,644,0948 + 8,5871.\n\n81,644,0948 + 8,000 = 81,652,0948\n\n81,652,0948 + 5,871 = 81,652,0948 + 5,000 = 81,657,0948\n\n81,657,0948 + 371 = 81,657,0948 + 300 = 81,657,3948\n\n81,657,3948 + 71 = 81,657,3948 + 70 = 81,657,4018\n\n81,657,4018 + 8 = 81,657,4026\n\nWait, that seems different. Hmm, maybe I made a mistake here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, 50,584,0948 + 31,518,5871.\n\nFirst, adding 50,584,0948 + 30,000,0000 = 80,584,0948.\n\nThen add 1,518,5871:\n\n80,584,0948 + 1,518,5871.\n\nLet's add 80,584,0948 + 1,000,000 = 81,584,0948\n\nThen 81,584,0948 + 518,5871.\n\nBreak down 518,5871:\n\n500,000 + 18,5871.\n\nSo 81,584,0948 + 500,000 = 82,084,0948\n\nThen 82,084,0948 + 18,5871.\n\n82,084,0948 + 10,000 = 82,094,0948\n\n82,094,0948 + 8,5871.\n\n82,094,0948 + 8,000 = 82,094,1848\n\n82,094,1848 + 5,871.\n\n82,094,1848 + 5,000 = 82,094,2348\n\n82,094,2348 + 871.\n\n82,094,2348 + 800 = 82,094,2448\n\n82,094,2448 + 71 = 82,094,2519\n\nWait, so according to this method, the sum is 82,094,2519. But earlier, when adding digit by digit, I got 82,102,1689. These two results are different. That means I must have made a mistake somewhere.\n\nHmm, this is concerning. Let me check both methods again.\n\nFirst method: Adding each place value.\n\nHundred millions: 5 + 3 = 8\n\nTen millions: 0 + 1 = 1\n\nMillions: 5 + 5 = 10\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 1 = 9\n\nTen thousands: 4 + 8 = 12\n\nThousands: 0 + 5 = 5\n\nHundreds: 9 + 8 = 17\n\nTens: 4 + 7 = 11\n\nUnits: 8 + 1 = 9\n\nSo writing the digits from hundred millions to units:\n\n8 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 9 (units). So 82,102,1689.\n\nSecond method: Adding step by step.\n\nFirst, 50,584,0948 + 30,000,0000 = 80,584,0948\n\nThen 80,584,0948 + 1,518,5871.\n\nLet me do this addition again:\n\n80,584,0948\n\n+1,518,5871\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n8 + 1 = 9\n\n4 + 7 = 11 (carryover 1)\n\n9 + 8 + 1 = 18 (carryover 1)\n\n0 + 5 + 1 = 6\n\n4 + 8 = 12 (carryover 1)\n\n8 + 1 = 9\n\n5 + 5 = 10 (carryover 1)\n\n0 + 1 + 1 = 2\n\n8 + 3 = 11 (carryover 1)\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, in the second method, when adding 80,584,0948 + 1,518,5871, we have:\n\nDigits:\n\n80,584,0948\n\n+1,518,5871\n\nAligning:\n\n 80,584,0948\n\n+ 1,518,5871\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 8 + 1 = 9\n\nTens: 4 + 7 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6.\n\nTen thousands: 4 + 8 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 1 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nMillions: 5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen millions: 0 + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2.\n\nHundred millions: 8 + 3 = 11. Wait, but in the first method, the hundred millions place was 5 + 3 = 8. Wait, but here, 80,584,0948 has 0 in the ten millions place. Wait, no:\n\nWait, 80,584,0948 is 80 million, 584 thousand, 948. So in terms of place values:\n\nHundred millions: 8\n\nTen millions: 0\n\nMillions: 5\n\nHundred thousands: 8\n\nTen thousands: 4\n\nThousands: 0\n\nHundreds: 9\n\nTens: 4\n\nUnits: 8\n\nWait, no. Wait, 80,584,0948:\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n80,000,000 (80 million) +\n\n584,000 (584 thousand) +\n\n948.\n\nSo in terms of digits:\n\nHundred millions: 8\n\nTen millions: 0\n\nMillions: 5\n\nHundred thousands: 8\n\nTen thousands: 4\n\nThousands: 0\n\nHundreds: 9\n\nTens: 4\n\nUnits: 8\n\nSo when adding 80,584,0948 + 1,518,5871:\n\nAligning:\n\n  80,584,0948\n\n+  1,518,5871\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 8 + 1 = 9\n\nTens: 4 + 7 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6.\n\nTen thousands: 4 + 8 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 1 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nMillions: 5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen millions: 0 + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2.\n\nHundred millions: 8 + 3 = 11. Wait, but in the first method, we had 5 + 3 = 8. Wait, this is conflicting.\n\nWait, hold on. The first number is 50,584,0948. Its hundred millions digit is 5. The second number is 31,518,5871. Its hundred millions digit is 3. So when adding the hundred millions place, it's 5 + 3 = 8. But in the second method, when we added 80,584,0948 (which is 80 million) to 1,518,5871, the hundred millions place would be 0 + 3 = 3. Wait, but 80,584,0948 is 80 million, which is 8*10^8. The second number is 1,518,5871, which is 1.5 million. So when adding 80,584,0948 + 1,518,5871, the hundred millions place is 8 + 0 = 8. Wait, but 80,584,0948 is 80,584,0948, which is 80 million, and 1,518,5871 is 1.5 million. So adding them gives 82,102,1619? Wait, but according to the digit-by-digit addition, we have 82,102,1689. But according to this step-by-step addition, we get 82,094,2519. There's a discrepancy here. Clearly, I made a mistake somewhere.\n\nWait, let's do the addition using the second method again, carefully.\n\nFirst, 50,584,0948 + 31,518,5871.\n\nFirst, split 31,518,5871 into 30,000,0000 + 1,518,5871.\n\nSo 50,584,0948 + 30,000,0000 = 80,584,0948.\n\nNow, add 1,518,5871 to 80,584,0948.\n\nLet's write this out:\n\n80,584,0948\n\n+ 1,518,5871\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nUnits: 8 + 1 = 9\n\nTens: 4 + 7 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6.\n\nTen thousands: 4 + 8 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 1 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nMillions: 5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen millions: 0 + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2.\n\nHundred millions: 8 + 3 = 11. Wait, but 80,584,0948 has 0 in the ten millions place. Wait, no:\n\nWait, 80,584,0948 is 80 million, 584 thousand, 948. So in terms of place values:\n\n- Hundred millions: 8\n\n- Ten millions: 0\n\n- Millions: 5\n\n- Hundred thousands: 8\n\n- Ten thousands: 4\n\n- Thousands: 0\n\n- Hundreds: 9\n\n- Tens: 4\n\n- Units: 8\n\nSo when adding 80,584,0948 + 1,518,5871, the hundred millions place is 8 (from 80,584,0948) + 0 (from 1,518,5871) = 8. The ten millions place is 0 + 1 = 1. The millions place is 5 + 5 = 10. The hundred thousands place is 8 + 1 = 9. The ten thousands place is 4 + 8 = 12. The thousands place is 0 + 5 = 5. The hundreds place is 9 + 8 = 17. The tens place is 4 + 7 = 11. The units place is 8 + 1 = 9.\n\nWait, so this is different from the previous step-by-step where I thought the hundred millions place was 8 from 50,584,0948 and 3 from 31,518,5871. But in reality, when splitting 31,518,5871 into 30,000,0000 + 1,518,5871, the 30,000,0000 has 3 in the ten millions place, but when adding to 80,584,0948, which has 0 in the ten millions place, the ten millions place becomes 0 + 3 = 3. Wait, no. Wait, 30,000,0000 is 30,000,0000, which is 3*10^9. So when adding to 80,584,0948 (8*10^8 + 5*10^7 + ...), adding 30,000,0000 would add 3*10^9, making the hundred millions place 8 + 3 = 11. But 80,584,0948 is 80,584,0948, which is 8*10^8 + 5*10^7 + ... So adding 30,000,0000 (3*10^9) would result in 11*10^8 + 5*10^7 + ... So the hundred millions place becomes 11, which is 1*10^9 + 1*10^8. But in reality, numbers don't have digits beyond the hundred millions place unless specified. Wait, this is getting confusing.\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion arises from how the numbers are split. Let me represent both numbers in their full digit places.\n\nFirst number: 50,584,0948\n\nDigits: 5 0 5 8 4 0 9 4 8\n\nPositions (from right): 8 (units), 4 (tens), 9 (hundreds), 0 (thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 8 (hundred thousands), 5 (millions), 0 (ten millions), 5 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number: 31,518,5871\n\nDigits: 3 1 5 1 8 5 8 7 1\n\nPositions (from right): 1 (units), 7 (tens), 8 (hundreds), 5 (thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 5 (hundred thousands), 1 (millions), 1 (ten millions), 3 (hundred millions)\n\nNow, adding them digit by digit from right to left:\n\nPosition 1 (units): 8 + 1 = 9\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 4 + 7 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 4 + 8 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 5 + 1 = 6 + 1 = 7.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 0 + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 5 + 3 = 8.\n\nSo the result is 8 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 9 (units). Wait, that gives 82,742,1819. But this contradicts previous results. I'm getting more confused now.\n\nWait, let's do this step by step with proper alignment:\n\nFirst number: 50,584,0948\n\nSecond number: 31,518,5871\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n```\n    50,584,0948\n  + 31,518,5871\n------------\n```\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n8 + 1 = 9 (units)\n\n4 + 7 = 11 (tens, write 1, carryover 1)\n\n9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18 (hundreds, write 8, carryover 1)\n\n0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6 (thousands, write 6, no carryover)\n\n4 + 8 = 12 (ten thousands, write 2, carryover 1)\n\n8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14 (hundred thousands, write 4, carryover 1)\n\n5 + 1 = 6 + 1 = 7 (millions, write 7, no carryover)\n\n0 + 5 = 5 (ten millions, write 5, no carryover)\n\n5 + 3 = 8 (hundred millions, write 8, no carryover)\n\nSo the sum is 82,742,1819. Wait, but this is different from the first method's result of 82,102,1689. Clearly, there's a mistake here. Let me check each step again.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Units: 8 + 1 = 9 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n2. Tens: 4 + 7 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n3. Hundreds: 9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n4. Thousands: 0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n5. Ten thousands: 4 + 8 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n6. Hundred thousands: 8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n7. Millions: 5 + 1 = 6 + 1 = 7 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n8. Ten millions: 0 + 5 = 5 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n9. Hundred millions: 5 + 3 = 8 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nSo the digits from hundred millions to units are:\n\n8 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 9 (units). So the number is 82,742,1819.\n\nBut earlier, when I added using the first method, I got 82,102,1689. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with another approach. Let's compute the sum using another digit-wise addition.\n\nFirst number: 50,584,0948\n\nSecond number: 31,518,5871\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n8 + 1 = 9\n\n4 + 7 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\n9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18 \u2192 8, carryover 1\n\n0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6\n\n4 + 8 = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 1\n\n8 + 5 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1\n\n5 + 1 = 6 + 1 = 7\n\n0 + 5 = 5\n\n5 + 3 = 8\n\nSo the result is 82,742,1819.\n\nBut when I added using the first me