hem together.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the numbers are written with the commas in the wrong places. For example, 667325620 could be 667,325,620 (667 million, 325 thousand, 620) and 571128998 could be 571,128,998 (571 million, 128 thousand, 998). That would make more sense because those numbers are in the billions. Wait, 667 million is a billion? No, 1 billion is 1,000,000,000. So 667,325,620 is 667 million, 325 thousand, 620. Similarly, 571,128,998 is 571 million, 128 thousand, 998. Then adding those two numbers. That seems more reasonable. Maybe the original problem had commas as thousand separators but the numbers were written with an extra comma. Let me check the original problem again.\n\nThe problem says: \"What is the sum of 667325620 and 571128998?\" So written as numbers: 667325620 and 571128998. If we consider standard international notation, which uses commas as thousand separators and spaces for millions, then 66,732,5620 would be 66 million, 732 thousand, 5620. But 66 million is 66,000,000, so 66,732,5620 would be 66,732,5620. Wait, but 66,732,5620 is 66 million, 732 thousand, 5620. But 66 million is 66,000,000, so adding 732,000 gives 66,732,000, then adding 5,620 gives 66,737,620. But the original number is 66,732,5620. Wait, maybe the last part is 5620, so 5,620. So 66,732,5620 is 66 million, 732 thousand, 5,620. So 66,732,5620. Similarly, 57,112,8998 would be 57 million, 112 thousand, 8,998. So 57,112,8998. Then adding 66,732,5620 and 57,112,8998.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the numbers are written with the commas in the wrong places. For example, 667325620 is 667,325,620 (667 million, 325 thousand, 620) and 571128998 is 571,128,998 (571 million, 128 thousand, 998). That would make sense. So perhaps the original numbers were written with an extra comma by mistake. Let me check the numbers:\n\n667325620: Let's count the digits. 667325620 has 9 digits. 667,325,620 is 9 digits: 6-6-7-3-2-5-6-2-0. Similarly, 571128998 is 9 digits: 5-7-1-1-2-8-9-9-8. So if they are 667 million and 571 million, then adding 667 million and 571 million gives 1,238 million. Then the thousands and lower digits. So maybe the correct numbers are 667,325,620 and 571,128,998. That would make the addition more manageable. Let me confirm with the original problem.\n\nThe problem is written as: \"667325620\" and \"571128998\". If we consider that in some countries, like India, they use commas as thousand separators but sometimes use spaces for millions. So 66,732,5620 could be 66 million, 732 thousand, 5620. But 66 million is 66,000,000, so 66,732,5620 would be 66,732,5620. Wait, but 66,732,5620 is 66 million, 732 thousand, 5620. But 66 million is 66,000,000, so adding 732,000 gives 66,732,000, then adding 5,620 gives 66,737,620. But the original number is 66,732,5620. Wait, maybe the last part is 5620, so 5,620. So 66,732,5620 is 66 million, 732 thousand, 5,620. So 66,732,5620. Similarly, 57,112,8998 is 57 million, 112 thousand, 8,998. So adding 66,732,5620 and 57,112,8998.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the numbers are written with the commas in the wrong places. For example, 667325620 is 667,325,620 (667 million, 325 thousand, 620) and 571128998 is 571,128,998 (571 million, 128 thousand, 998). That would make sense. Let me check the digits:\n\n667,325,620: 6-6-7-3-2-5-6-2-0. Yes, 9 digits. 571,128,998: 5-7-1-1-2-8-9-9-8. Also 9 digits. So adding these two numbers:\n\n667,325,620\n\n+571,128,998\n\nLet me add them step by step.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digits:\n\n0 + 8 = 8\n\n2 + 9 = 11. Write down 1, carry over 1.\n\n6 + 9 + 1 (carry) = 16. Write down 6, carry over 1.\n\n5 + 8 + 1 = 14. Write down 4, carry over 1.\n\n2 + 2 + 1 = 5. Write down 5.\n\n7 + 1 = 8. Write down 8.\n\n1 + 1 = 2. Write down 2.\n\n6 + 5 = 11. Write down 1, carry over 1.\n\n6 + 7 + 1 = 14. Write down 4, carry over 1.\n\nFinally, the leftmost digit: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1.\n\nWait, let me check again. Wait, the numbers are:\n\nFirst number: 667,325,620\n\nSecond number: 571,128,998\n\nLet me write them aligned properly:\n\n        667,325,620\n\n      + 571,128,998\n\n--------------------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nDigits: 0 (units) + 8 (units) = 8\n\n0 (tens) + 9 (tens) = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n2 (hundreds) + 9 (hundreds) + 1 (carry) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n6 (thousands) + 8 (thousands) + 1 (carry) = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n\n5 (ten thousands) + 2 (ten thousands) + 1 (carry) = 8. Write 8, carry 0.\n\n3 (hundred thousands) + 1 (hundred thousands) = 4. Write 4, carry 0.\n\n7 (millions) + 5 (millions) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n6 (ten millions) + 7 (ten millions) + 1 (carry) = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nFinally, the remaining digit: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1.\n\nSo putting it all together: 1,438,455,618.\n\nWait, let me verify:\n\nFirst number: 667,325,620\n\nSecond number: 571,128,998\n\nAdding them:\n\n667,325,620\n\n+571,128,998\n\n= (600,000,000 + 70,000,000) + (60,000,000 + 10,000,000) + (2,000,000 + 100,000) + (50,000 + 2,000) + (600 + 80) + (20 + 9) + (0 + 8)\n\nWait, maybe breaking it down:\n\n667,325,620 = 600,000,000 + 60,000,000 + 7,000,000 + 300,000 + 20,000 + 500 + 20\n\n571,128,998 = 500,000,000 + 70,000,000 + 10,000,000 + 1,000,000 + 200,000 + 800 + 98\n\nAdding them:\n\n600,000,000 + 500,000,000 = 1,100,000,000\n\n60,000,000 + 70,000,000 = 130,000,000\n\n7,000,000 + 10,000,000 = 17,000,000\n\n300,000 + 200,000 = 500,000\n\n20,000 + 800 = 20,800\n\n500 + 98 = 598\n\nThen adding the remaining parts:\n\n1,100,000,000 + 130,000,000 = 1,230,000,000\n\n1,230,000,000 + 17,000,000 = 1,247,000,000\n\n1,247,000,000 + 500,000 = 1,247,500,000\n\n1,247,500,000 + 20,800 = 1,247,520,800\n\n1,247,520,800 + 598 = 1,247,521,398\n\nWait, that's different from the previous result. Hmm, so which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe my initial step-by-step addition was wrong. Let me try again.\n\nFirst number: 667,325,620\n\nSecond number: 571,128,998\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units place):\n\n0 + 8 = 8\n\nNext digit (tens place):\n\n2 + 9 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nNext digit (hundreds place):\n\n6 + 9 + 1 (carry) = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\nNext digit (thousands place):\n\n5 + 8 + 1 (carry) = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nNext digit (ten thousands place):\n\n2 + 2 + 1 (carry) = 5. Write 5, carry 0.\n\nNext digit (hundred thousands place):\n\n3 + 1 + 0 (carry) = 4. Write 4, carry 0.\n\nNext digit (millions place):\n\n7 + 5 + 0 (carry) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nNext digit (ten millions place):\n\n6 + 7 + 1 (carry) = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nFinally, the next digit (hundred millions place):\n\n0 + 0 + 1 (carry) = 1.\n\nSo putting all the digits together from left to right:\n\n1 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 8 (units). So 1,424,554,68.\n\nWait, but that contradicts the previous total of 1,247,521,398. Hmm, so which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in breaking down the numbers. Let me check the original numbers again.\n\nFirst number: 667,325,620\n\nSecond number: 571,128,998\n\nAdding them:\n\n667,325,620\n\n+571,128,998\n\nLet me add them as:\n\n667,325,620\n\n+571,128,998\n\n= (600,000,000 + 70,000,000 + 6,000,000 + 300,000 + 20,000 + 500 + 20) + (500,000,000 + 70,000,000 + 1,000,000 + 100,000 + 200,000 + 800 + 98)\n\n= 1,100,000,000 + 130,000,000 + 17,000,000 + 500,000 + 20,000 + 800 + 598\n\nWait, 600,000,000 + 500,000,000 = 1,100,000,000\n\n60,000,000 + 70,000,000 = 130,000,000\n\n6,000,000 + 1,000,000 = 7,000,000\n\n300,000 + 200,000 = 500,000\n\n20,000 + 800 = 20,800\n\n500 + 98 = 598\n\nNow adding these together:\n\n1,100,000,000 + 130,000,000 = 1,230,000,000\n\n1,230,000,000 + 7,000,000 = 1,237,000,000\n\n1,237,000,000 + 500,000 = 1,237,500,000\n\n1,237,500,000 + 20,800 = 1,237,520,800\n\n1,237,520,800 + 598 = 1,237,521,398\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, we got 1,424,554,68. These two results are different. There must be a mistake here.\n\nWait, let's check the digit-by-digit addition again. Maybe I messed up the places.\n\nFirst number: 667,325,620\n\nDigits (from right to left):\n\nPosition 1: 0 (units)\n\nPosition 2: 2 (tens)\n\nPosition 3: 6 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 4: 5 (thousands)\n\nPosition 5: 2 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition 6: 3 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition 7: 7 (millions)\n\nPosition 8: 6 (ten millions)\n\nPosition 9: 5 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, no. Wait, in standard notation, the rightmost digit is the units (10^0), then tens (10^1), hundreds (10^2), etc. So for the number 667,325,620:\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n6 (ten millions)\n\n7 (millions)\n\n3 (hundred thousands)\n\n2 (ten thousands)\n\n5 (thousands)\n\n6 (hundreds)\n\n2 (tens)\n\n0 (units)\n\nWait, no. Wait, 667,325,620:\n\n- The first digit from the left is 6 (hundred millions)\n\n- Second digit: 6 (ten millions)\n\n- Third digit: 7 (millions)\n\n- Fourth digit: 3 (hundred thousands)\n\n- Fifth digit: 2 (ten thousands)\n\n- Sixth digit: 5 (thousands)\n\n- Seventh digit: 6 (hundreds)\n\n- Eighth digit: 2 (tens)\n\n- Ninth digit: 0 (units)\n\nSimilarly, 571,128,998:\n\n- 5 (hundred millions)\n\n- 7 (ten millions)\n\n- 1 (millions)\n\n- 1 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 2 (ten thousands)\n\n- 8 (thousands)\n\n- 9 (hundreds)\n\n- 9 (tens)\n\n- 8 (units)\n\nSo when adding:\n\n        667,325,620\n\n      + 571,128,998\n\n--------------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n0 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 8\n\nTens place:\n\n2 (from first number) + 9 (from second number) = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n6 (from first number) + 9 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\nThousands place:\n\n5 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nTen thousands place:\n\n2 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 5. Write 5, carry 0.\n\nHundred thousands place:\n\n3 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) + 0 (carry) = 4. Write 4, carry 0.\n\nMillions place:\n\n7 (from first number) + 5 (from second number) + 0 (carry) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nTen millions place:\n\n6 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nHundred millions place:\n\n6 (from first number) + 5 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nFinally, the remaining digit (billions place):\n\n0 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 1.\n\nPutting it all together:\n\nBillions place: 1\n\nHundred millions place: 2\n\nTen millions place: 4\n\nMillions place: 2\n\nHundred thousands place: 4\n\nTen thousands place: 5\n\nThousands place: 4\n\nHundreds place: 6\n\nTens place: 1\n\nUnits place: 8\n\nSo the total is 1,247,524,456,118? Wait, no. Wait, let's list the digits in order from left to right:\n\n1 (billions)\n\n2 (hundred millions)\n\n4 (ten millions)\n\n7 (millions)\n\n5 (hundred thousands)\n\n2 (ten thousands)\n\n4 (thousands)\n\n5 (hundreds)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n8 (units)\n\nWait, that would be 1,247,524,456,118? Wait, no. Wait, the digits after the first digit (billions place) are:\n\n2 (hundred millions)\n\n4 (ten millions)\n\n7 (millions)\n\n5 (hundred thousands)\n\n2 (ten thousands)\n\n4 (thousands)\n\n5 (hundreds)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n8 (units)\n\nSo writing them all together: 1,247,524,456,118? Wait, no. Wait, the places are:\n\nBillions: 1\n\nHundred millions: 2\n\nTen millions: 4\n\nMillions: 7\n\nHundred thousands: 5\n\nTen thousands: 2\n\nThousands: 4\n\nHundreds: 5\n\nTens: 6\n\nUnits: 8\n\nSo the number is 1,247,524,456,118? Wait, that seems too long. Wait, 1 (billion), 2 (hundred million), 4 (ten million), 7 (million), 5 (hundred thousand), 2 (ten thousand), 4 (thousand), 5 (hundred), 6 (ten), 8 (unit). So in digits: 1,247,524,456,118? Wait, no. Wait, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So:\n\n1 (billion)\n\n247 (million)\n\n524 (thousand)\n\n456 (unit)\n\n118? Wait, no. Wait, the digits after the billion are:\n\n2 (hundred million), 4 (ten million), 7 (million), 5 (hundred thousand), 2 (ten thousand), 4 (thousand), 5 (hundred), 6 (ten), 8 (unit). So the number is 1,247,524,456,118? Wait, no. Wait, the breakdown is:\n\n1 (billions)\n\n2 (hundred millions)\n\n4 (ten millions)\n\n7 (millions)\n\n5 (hundred thousands)\n\n2 (ten thousands)\n\n4 (thousands)\n\n5 (hundreds)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n8 (units)\n\nSo written as 1,247,524,456,118? Wait, no. Wait, the commas are placed as follows:\n\nBillions: 1\n\nHundred millions: 2\n\nTen millions: 4\n\nMillions: 7\n\nHundred thousands: 5\n\nTen thousands: 2\n\nThousands: 4\n\nHundreds: 5\n\nTens: 6\n\nUnits: 8\n\nSo the number is 1,247,524,456,118? Wait, no. Wait, the commas are only placed every three digits. So starting from the right:\n\nUnits: 8\n\nTens: 6\n\nHundreds: 5\n\nThousands: 4\n\nTen thousands: 2\n\nHundred thousands: 5\n\nMillions: 7\n\nTen millions: 4\n\nHundred millions: 2\n\nBillions: 1\n\nSo the number is 1 (billions), 2 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 8 (units). So written as 1,247,524,456,118? Wait, no. Wait, the commas are only placed every three digits. So:\n\n1 (billions)\n\n247 (millions)\n\n524 (thousands)\n\n456 (units)\n\n118? Wait, no. Wait, the breakdown is:\n\n1 (billions)\n\n2 (hundred millions)\n\n4 (ten millions)\n\n7 (millions)\n\n5 (hundred thousands)\n\n2 (ten thousands)\n\n4 (thousands)\n\n5 (hundreds)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n8 (units)\n\nSo the number is 1,247,524,456,118? Wait, no. Wait, the commas are only placed every three digits. So:\n\n1 (billions)\n\n247 (millions)\n\n524 (thousands)\n\n456 (units)\n\nBut wait, 1,247,524,456,118 would be 1 billion, 247 million, 524 thousand, 456, and 118. But that doesn't make sense. Wait, no. Wait, the commas are only placed every three digits starting from the right. So:\n\nOriginal digits after the first digit (billions place):\n\n2 (hundred millions)\n\n4 (ten millions)\n\n7 (millions)\n\n5 (hundred thousands)\n\n2 (ten thousands)\n\n4 (thousands)\n\n5 (hundreds)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n8 (units)\n\nSo when written with commas, it's 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, but that's 1 followed by 247 million, 524 thousand, 456, and 118. But 1,247,524,456,118 is 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, but 1,247,524,456,118 is 1 billion, 247 million, 524 thousand, 456, and 118. But that's not correct because 1,247,524,456,118 is 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, but 1,247,524,456,118 is 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, no. Wait, the commas are only placed every three digits. So:\n\n1 (billions)\n\n247 (millions)\n\n524 (thousands)\n\n456 (units)\n\nBut 1,247,524,456,118 would be 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, but that's 1 billion, 247 million, 524 thousand, 456, and 118. But 1,247,524,456,118 is 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, but 1,247,524,456,118 is 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, no. Wait, the commas are only placed every three digits. So:\n\n1 (billions)\n\n247 (millions)\n\n524 (thousands)\n\n456 (units)\n\nBut 1,247,524,456,118 is 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, but that's 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, no. Wait, the commas are only placed every three digits. So:\n\n1 (billions)\n\n247 (millions)\n\n524 (thousands)\n\n456 (units)\n\nBut 1,247,524,456,118 is 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, but that's 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, no. Wait, the commas are only placed every three digits. So:\n\n1 (billions)\n\n247 (millions)\n\n524 (thousands)\n\n456 (units)\n\nBut 1,247,524,456,118 is 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, but that's 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, no. Wait, the commas are only placed every three digits. So:\n\n1 (billions)\n\n247 (millions)\n\n524 (thousands)\n\n456 (units)\n\nBut 1,247,524,456,118 is 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, but that's 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, no. Wait, the commas are only placed every three digits. So:\n\n1 (billions)\n\n247 (millions)\n\n524 (thousands)\n\n456 (units)\n\nBut 1,247,524,456,118 is 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, but that's 1,247,524,456,118. Wait, no. Wait, the commas are only placed every three digits