ns place):\n\n3 + 0 = 3. No carry. Write down 3.\n\nNext digit (ten millions place):\n\n3 + 6 = 9. No carry. Write down 9.\n\nNext digit (hundred millions place):\n\n6 + 6 = 12. Write down 2, carry over 1.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, the first number is 633,106,007. Let me break down each digit properly.\n\nWait, maybe I should write both numbers with all digits aligned:\n\nFirst number: 6 3 3 1 0 6 0 0 7\n\nSecond number: 6 0 8 7 0 7 7 5 4\n\nWait, but how are the digits aligned? Let me check:\n\nFirst number: 633,106,007 is 6 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 7 (units).\n\nSecond number: 608,707,754 is 6 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 4 (units).\n\nSo, aligning them:\n\n        6 3 3 1 0 6 0 0 7\n\n      + 6 0 8 7 0 7 7 5 4\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n          6 3 3 1 0 6 0 0 7\n\n        + 6 0 8 7 0 7 7 5 4\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n7 + 4 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nTens place: 0 + 5 = 5 + 1 (carry) = 6. Write 6.\n\nHundreds place: 0 + 7 = 7. Write 7.\n\nThousands place: 0 + 7 = 7. Write 7.\n\nTen thousands place: 6 + 0 = 6. Write 6.\n\nHundred thousands place: 1 + 8 = 9. Write 9.\n\nMillions place: 3 + 7 = 10. Wait, hold on. Wait, the first number's millions place is 3, and the second number's millions place is 0? Wait, no. Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, first number: 633,106,007.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- Hundred millions: 6\n\n- Ten millions: 3\n\n- Millions: 3\n\n- Hundred thousands: 1\n\n- Ten thousands: 0\n\n- Thousands: 6\n\n- Hundreds: 0\n\n- Tens: 0\n\n- Units: 7\n\nSecond number: 608,707,754.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- Hundred millions: 6\n\n- Ten millions: 0\n\n- Millions: 8\n\n- Hundred thousands: 7\n\n- Ten thousands: 0\n\n- Thousands: 7\n\n- Hundreds: 7\n\n- Tens: 5\n\n- Units: 4\n\nSo, when adding:\n\nHundred millions: 6 + 6 = 12. Write down 2, carry 1.\n\nTen millions: 3 + 0 = 3 + 1 (carry) = 4. Write 4, carry 0.\n\nMillions: 3 + 7 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 1 + 0 = 1 + 1 (carry) = 2. Write 2, carry 0.\n\nTen thousands: 0 + 7 = 7. Write 7.\n\nThousands: 6 + 7 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nHundreds: 0 + 7 = 7 + 1 (carry) = 8. Write 8.\n\nTens: 0 + 5 = 5. Write 5.\n\nUnits: 7 + 4 = 11. Write 1, carry 1. But since there are no more digits, the carry 1 becomes a new digit.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, let's go step by step:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n7 (from first number) + 4 (from second number) = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nTens place: 0 (from first number) + 5 (from second number) = 5 + 1 (carry) = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nHundreds place: 0 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\nThousands place: 0 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\nTen thousands place: 6 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nHundred thousands place: 1 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\nMillions place: 3 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nTen millions place: 3 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) = 3 + 1 (carry) = 4. Write 4, carry 0.\n\nHundred millions place: 6 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, the carry 1 becomes a new digit in the next place. But since these are 9-digit numbers, adding two 9-digit numbers can result in a 10-digit number. Let's check:\n\nThe carry 1 is added as the next digit. So the final sum would be 12,456,038,11? Wait, let me reconstruct the digits.\n\nWait, let's list the digits as we wrote them:\n\nHundred millions: 2\n\nTen millions: 4\n\nMillions: 0\n\nHundred thousands: 2\n\nTen thousands: 6\n\nThousands: 3\n\nHundreds: 8\n\nTens: 5\n\nUnits: 1\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait, when we added the hundred millions place: 6 + 6 = 12, so we write 2 and carry 1.\n\nThen ten millions place: 3 + 0 = 3 + 1 (carry) = 4. So 4, no carry.\n\nMillions place: 3 + 7 = 10. So 0, carry 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 1 + 0 = 1 + 1 (carry) = 2. So 2, no carry.\n\nTen thousands place: 0 + 7 = 7. So 7, no carry.\n\nThousands place: 6 + 7 = 13. So 3, carry 1.\n\nHundreds place: 0 + 7 = 7 + 1 = 8. So 8, no carry.\n\nTens place: 0 + 5 = 5. So 5, no carry.\n\nUnits place: 7 + 4 = 11. So 1, carry 1. But since there are no more digits, we add the carry 1 as a new digit.\n\nWait, but in the order we wrote the digits, starting from the rightmost (units) to the leftmost (hundred millions):\n\n1 (units), 5 (tens), 8 (hundreds), 3 (thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 2 (hundred thousands), 0 (millions), 4 (ten millions), 2 (hundred millions). Then the carry 1 is added as the next digit. Wait, no. Wait, when adding, the carry propagates to the next higher place. Let me try again.\n\nWait, when adding the hundred millions place: 6 + 6 = 12. So we write 2 in the hundred millions place and carry 1 to the ten millions place.\n\nThen, ten millions place: 3 + 0 + 1 (carry) = 4. So 4 in ten millions, no carry.\n\nMillions place: 3 + 7 = 10. So 0 in millions, carry 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 1 + 0 + 1 (carry) = 2. So 2 in hundred thousands, no carry.\n\nTen thousands place: 0 + 7 = 7. So 7 in ten thousands, no carry.\n\nThousands place: 6 + 7 = 13. So 3 in thousands, carry 1.\n\nHundreds place: 0 + 7 + 1 (carry) = 8. So 8 in hundreds, no carry.\n\nTens place: 0 + 5 = 5. So 5 in tens, no carry.\n\nUnits place: 7 + 4 = 11. So 1 in units, carry 1. But since there are no more digits, we add the carry 1 as a new digit in the next place. But since we started from the right, the carry 1 would be added after the units place. Wait, but in our previous step-by-step, we already added the carry 1 to the tens place. Wait, maybe I messed up the order.\n\nWait, let's try writing the digits in the order they were added:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units: 7 + 4 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n2. Tens: 0 + 5 + 1 (carry) = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\n3. Hundreds: 0 + 7 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\n4. Thousands: 0 + 7 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\n5. Ten thousands: 6 + 0 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\n6. Hundred thousands: 1 + 8 = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\n7. Millions: 3 + 7 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n8. Ten millions: 3 + 0 + 1 (carry) = 4. Write 4, carry 0.\n\n9. Hundred millions: 6 + 6 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nNow, after adding all digits, we still have a carry 1. Since there are no more digits to add, we need to write this carry 1 as the next digit. So the final sum is 12,456,038,111?\n\nWait, let's reconstruct the digits:\n\nStarting from the leftmost (hundred millions) to the rightmost (units):\n\n1. Hundred millions: 2\n\n2. Ten millions: 4\n\n3. Millions: 0\n\n4. Hundred thousands: 2\n\n5. Ten thousands: 6\n\n6. Thousands: 3\n\n7. Hundreds: 8\n\n8. Tens: 5\n\n9. Units: 1\n\nBut wait, that would be 2,4,0,2,6,3,8,5,1. So 24,026,385,1. But that seems too low. Wait, but let's check with another method.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the carry-over. Let me verify by adding the numbers directly.\n\nFirst number: 633,106,007\n\nSecond number: 608,707,754\n\nAdding them:\n\n633,106,007\n\n+608,707,754\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n7 + 4 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\n0 + 7 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\n0 + 7 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\n6 + 0 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\n1 + 8 = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\n3 + 7 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n3 + 0 = 3 + 1 = 4. Write 4, carry 0.\n\n6 + 6 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nNow, since there's a carry 1, we need to add that as a new digit. So the total is 12,456,038,111.\n\nWait, but let's check with another approach. Let's convert both numbers to their actual values and add them.\n\nFirst number: 633,106,007\n\nSecond number: 608,707,754\n\nAdding them:\n\n633,106,007 + 608,707,754\n\nLet me add the millions:\n\n633 million + 608 million = 1,241 million\n\nThen the remaining parts:\n\n106,007 + 707,754\n\n106,007 + 707,754 = 813,761\n\nSo total is 1,241,000,000 + 813,761 = 1,241,813,761\n\nWait, that's different from the previous result. Hmm, so which one is correct?\n\nWait, 633,106,007 + 608,707,754.\n\nLet me compute this step by step.\n\nFirst, 633,106,007 is 633 million 106 thousand 7.\n\n608,707,754 is 608 million 707 thousand 754.\n\nAdding them:\n\n633,106,007\n\n+608,707,754\n\nStart from the right:\n\n7 + 4 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n0 (from 106,007) + 5 (from 707,754) + 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\n0 (from 106,007) + 7 (from 707,754) = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\n0 (from 106,007) + 7 (from 707,754) = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\n6 (from 106,007) + 0 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\n1 (from 106,007) + 8 (from 707,754) = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\n3 (from 633,106,007) + 0 (from 608,707,754) = 3. Write 3, carry 0.\n\n3 (from 633,106,007) + 6 (from 608,707,754) = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\n6 (from 633,106,007) + 6 (from 608,707,754) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nNow, the carry 1 is added as a new digit. So total is 12,456,038,111.\n\nBut when I converted to millions and added, I got 1,241,813,761. Wait, that's conflicting. Which is correct?\n\nWait, 633,106,007 + 608,707,754.\n\nLet me compute 633,106,007 + 608,707,754.\n\nFirst, 633,106,007 + 600,000,000 = 1,233,106,007\n\nThen, 106,007 + 8,707,754 = ?\n\n106,007 + 8,707,754 = 8,813,761\n\nThen, 1,233,106,007 + 8,813,761 = 1,241,919,768\n\nWait, that's different again. Wait, now I'm confused. What's going on here?\n\nWait, let me check with actual number addition.\n\nFirst number: 633,106,007\n\nSecond number: 608,707,754\n\nLet me write them as:\n\n633,106,007\n\n+608,707,754\n\nStart from the rightmost digit:\n\n7 + 4 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\n0 + 7 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\n0 + 7 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\n6 + 0 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\n1 + 8 = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\n3 + 0 = 3 + 0 = 3. Wait, hold on. Wait, the first number's millions place is 3, and the second number's millions place is 0. So 3 + 0 = 3. No carry.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought the first number's millions place is 3, and the second number's millions place is 0. So 3 + 0 = 3. Then, moving to the next place (ten millions):\n\n3 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) = 3. No carry.\n\nWait, but in the previous step-by-step, I had:\n\nWait, let's redo the addition step by step carefully:\n\nNumber1: 6 3 3 1 0 6 0 0 7\n\nNumber2: 6 0 8 7 0 7 7 5 4\n\nAligning:\n\n        6 3 3 1 0 6 0 0 7\n\n      + 6 0 8 7 0 7 7 5 4\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n7 (from Number1) + 4 (from Number2) = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nTens place:\n\n0 (from Number1) + 5 (from Number2) + 1 (carry) = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n0 (from Number1) + 7 (from Number2) = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\nThousands place:\n\n0 (from Number1) + 7 (from Number2) = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\nTen thousands place:\n\n6 (from Number1) + 0 (from Number2) = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nHundred thousands place:\n\n1 (from Number1) + 8 (from Number2) = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\nMillions place:\n\n3 (from Number1) + 7 (from Number2) = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nTen millions place:\n\n3 (from Number1) + 0 (from Number2) + 1 (carry) = 4. Write 4, carry 0.\n\nHundred millions place:\n\n6 (from Number1) + 6 (from Number2) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, the carry 1 is added as a new digit. So the total is 12,456,038,111.\n\nBut when I converted to millions, I got 1,241,919,768. Wait, that's conflicting. Let me check the conversion again.\n\nWait, 633,106,007 is 633 million 106 thousand 7.\n\n608,707,754 is 608 million 707 thousand 754.\n\nAdding them:\n\n633,106,007 + 608,707,754.\n\nLet me add the millions:\n\n633,000,000 + 608,000,000 = 1,241,000,000\n\nThen the remaining parts:\n\n106,007 + 707,754 = 813,761\n\nSo total is 1,241,000,000 + 813,761 = 1,241,813,761.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, we have 12,456,038,111. Which is 12.456 billion vs 1.241 billion. These are conflicting results. There must be a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let's check the digit-by-digit addition again. Maybe I messed up the places.\n\nWait, the first number is 633,106,007. Breaking down:\n\n- 6 (hundred millions)\n\n- 3 (ten millions)\n\n- 3 (millions)\n\n- 1 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 0 (ten thousands)\n\n- 6 (thousands)\n\n- 0 (hundreds)\n\n- 0 (tens)\n\n- 7 (units)\n\nSecond number: 608,707,754.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 6 (hundred millions)\n\n- 0 (ten millions)\n\n- 8 (millions)\n\n- 7 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 0 (ten thousands)\n\n- 7 (thousands)\n\n- 7 (hundreds)\n\n- 5 (tens)\n\n- 4 (units)\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n7 + 4 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nTens place:\n\n0 (from first number) + 5 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n0 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\nThousands place:\n\n0 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\nTen thousands place:\n\n6 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nHundred thousands place:\n\n1 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\nMillions place:\n\n3 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nTen millions place:\n\n3 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) + 1 (carry) = 4. Write 4, carry 0.\n\nHundred millions place:\n\n6 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nNow, the carry 1 is added as a new digit. So the total is 12,456,038,111.\n\nBut according to the conversion to millions, it's 1,241,813,761. Which is 12.418 billion vs 1.241 billion. These are different by a factor of 10. So there's a mistake here.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the conversion. Let me check:\n\n633,106,007 is 633 million 106 thousand 7. So 633,000,000 + 106,000 + 7 = 633,106,007.\n\n608,707,754 is 608 million 707 thousand 754. So 608,000,000 + 707,000 + 754 = 608,707,754.\n\nAdding them:\n\n633,106,007 + 608,707,754.\n\nLet me add 633,106,007 + 600,000,000 = 1,233,106,007.\n\nThen add the remaining 8,707,754:\n\n1,233,106,007 + 8,707,754.\n\n1,233,106,007 + 8,000,000 = 1,241,106,007.\n\n1,241,106,007 + 707,754 = 1,241,813,761.\n\nSo according to this, the total is 1,241,813,761.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, it's 12,456,038,111. Which is 12.456 billion vs 1.241 billion. So there's a discrepancy.\n\nWait, this is a problem. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me verify the sum of 633,106,007 and 608,707,754.\n\nFirst, 633,106,007 + 608,707,754.\n\nLet me compute this using a calculator.\n\n633,106,007\n\n+608,707,754\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n7 + 4 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n0 + 5 = 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\n0 + 7 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\n0 + 7 = 7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\n6 + 0 = 6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\n1 + 8 = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\n3 + 0 = 3. Write 3, carry 0.\n\n3 + 6 = 9. Write 9, carry 0.\n\n6 + 6 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nNow, the carry 1 is added as a new digit. So the total is 12,456,038,111.\n\nBut according to the conversion to millions, it's 1,241,813,761. Which is 12.418 billion vs 1.241 billion. So there's a factor of 10 difference. Wait, why?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the conversion. Let me check:\n\n633,106,007 is 633 million 106 thousand 7. So 633,000,000 + 106,000 + 7 = 633,106,007.\n\n608,707,754 is 608 million 707 thousand 754. So 608,000,000 + 707,000 + 754 = 608,707,754.\n\nAdding them:\n\n633,106,007 + 608,707,754.\n\nLet me add 633,106,007 + 600,000,000 = 1,233,106,007.\n\nThen add 8,707,754:\n\n1,233,106,007 + 8,707,754.\n\n1,233,106,007 + 8,000,000 = 1,241,106,007.\n\n1,241,106,007 + 707,754 = 1,241,813,761.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, it's 12,456,038,111. Which is 12.456 b