      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to add two really big numbers: 363,171,983 and 336,390,092. Hmm, how do I do this? Well, I remember from school that you add the numbers digit by digit, starting from the rightmost digit, carrying over if needed. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, let me write down the numbers aligned properly, making sure the digits are in the same place. So:\n\n  363,171,983\n+ 336,390,092\n------------\n?\n\nAlright, let's start adding from the rightmost digit. Let's break it down digit by digit.\n\nStarting with the units place (the last digit):\n\n3 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) = 5. So, the units digit is 5. No carryover needed here.\n\nNext, the tens place:\n\n8 + 9 = 17. So, 7 in the tens place, and carryover 1. Okay, that's straightforward.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n1 + 0 = 1. But wait, there was a carryover of 1 from the previous step. So, 1 + 1 = 2. So, 2 in the hundreds place. No more carryover.\n\nThousands place:\n\n9 + 9 = 18. So, 8 in the thousands place, carryover 1. Hmm, same as before.\n\nTen-thousands place:\n\n7 + 0 = 7. But we have a carryover of 1. So, 7 + 1 = 8. No carryover.\n\nHundred-thousands place:\n\n1 + 3 = 4. No carryover here.\n\nMillions place:\n\n7 + 6 = 13. So, 3 in the millions place, carryover 1. Wait, hold on. Let me check again. The first number has 7 in the millions place, and the second number has 6. So 7 + 6 = 13. So, 3 with a carryover of 1. Right.\n\nTen-millions place:\n\n1 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) + 1 (carryover) = 5. So, 5 in the ten-millions place. No carryover.\n\nHundred-millions place:\n\n3 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) = 6. No carryover here.\n\nBillions place:\n\n6 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) = 9. No carryover. So, 9 in the billions place.\n\nTen-billions place:\n\n3 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) = 6. No carryover. So, 6 in the ten-billions place.\n\nSo, putting all those digits together:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 5\n\nTens: 7\n\nHundreds: 2\n\nThousands: 8\n\nTen-thousands: 7\n\nHundred-thousands: 4\n\nMillions: 3 (with carryover)\n\nTen-millions: 5\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nBillions: 9\n\nTen-billions: 6\n\nSo, combining all these digits from right to left:\n\n5, 7, 2, 8, 7, 4, 3, 5, 6, 9, 6\n\nWait, let me check again. Let me list each place:\n\n1. Units: 5\n\n2. Tens: 7\n\n3. Hundreds: 2\n\n4. Thousands: 8\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 7\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 4\n\n7. Millions: 3\n\n8. Ten-millions: 5\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 6\n\n10. Billions: 9\n\n11. Ten-billions: 6\n\nSo, writing them out as a number: 6 (ten-billions), 9 (billions), 6 (hundred-millions), 5 (ten-millions), 4 (hundred-thousands), 3 (millions), 7 (ten-thousands), 8 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 5 (units). Wait, that seems reversed. Wait, no. Wait, when we add, the rightmost digit is units, then tens, etc. So the digits we calculated are:\n\nUnits: 5\n\nTens: 7\n\nHundreds: 2\n\nThousands: 8\n\nTen-thousands: 7\n\nHundred-thousands: 4\n\nMillions: 3\n\nTen-millions: 5\n\nHundred-millions: 6\n\nBillions: 9\n\nTen-billions: 6\n\nSo, starting from the right, the digits are:\n\n5 (units), 7 (tens), 2 (hundreds), 8 (thousands), 7 (ten-thousands), 4 (hundred-thousands), 3 (millions), 5 (ten-millions), 6 (hundred-millions), 9 (billions), 6 (ten-billions).\n\nSo, putting them all together: 6,9,6,5,4,3,7,8,2,7,5. Wait, no. Wait, the first digit we got was 5 (units), then 7 (tens), 2 (hundreds), 8 (thousands), 7 (ten-thousands), 4 (hundred-thousands), 3 (millions), 5 (ten-millions), 6 (hundred-millions), 9 (billions), 6 (ten-billions). So the number is 6 (ten-billions), 9 (billions), 6 (hundred-millions), 5 (ten-millions), 4 (hundred-thousands), 3 (millions), 7 (ten-thousands), 8 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 5 (units). So written out: 6,969,543,782,57. Wait, let me check.\n\nWait, let's list the digits in order from left to right as per the places:\n\n1. Ten-billions: 6\n\n2. Billions: 9\n\n3. Hundred-millions: 6\n\n4. Ten-millions: 5\n\n5. Hundred-thousands: 4\n\n6. Millions: 3\n\n7. Ten-thousands: 7\n\n8. Thousands: 8\n\n9. Hundreds: 2\n\n10. Tens: 7\n\n11. Units: 5\n\nSo, combining these digits: 6,9,6,5,4,3,7,8,2,7,5. So the number is 6,969,543,782,57. Wait, but let me check again. Wait, 6 (ten-billions), 9 (billions), 6 (hundred-millions), 5 (ten-millions), 4 (hundred-thousands), 3 (millions), 7 (ten-thousands), 8 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 5 (units). So that's 6,969,543,782,57. Let me verify this.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the order. Let me write the digits step by step:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units: 5\n\n2. Tens: 7\n\n3. Hundreds: 2\n\n4. Thousands: 8\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 7\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 4\n\n7. Millions: 3\n\n8. Ten-millions: 5\n\n9. Hundred-millions: 6\n\n10. Billions: 9\n\n11. Ten-billions: 6\n\nSo, when constructing the number, the first digit (leftmost) is the ten-billions place: 6\n\nThen hundred-millions: 6\n\nThen ten-millions: 5\n\nThen millions: 3\n\nThen hundred-thousands: 4\n\nThen ten-thousands: 7\n\nThen thousands: 8\n\nThen hundreds: 2\n\nThen tens: 7\n\nThen units: 5\n\nSo putting them all together: 6,6,5,3,4,7,8,2,7,5. Wait, that's 665,347,827,5? Wait, no. Wait, 6 (ten-billions), 6 (hundred-millions), 5 (ten-millions), 3 (millions), 4 (hundred-thousands), 7 (ten-thousands), 8 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 5 (units). So that's 665,347,827,5. Wait, but that seems different from before. Wait, maybe I messed up the order.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to write the number as:\n\nStarting from the left:\n\nFirst digit: 6 (ten-billions place)\n\nSecond digit: 9 (billions place)\n\nThird digit: 6 (hundred-millions place)\n\nFourth digit: 5 (ten-millions place)\n\nFifth digit: 4 (hundred-thousands place)\n\nSixth digit: 3 (millions place)\n\nSeventh digit: 7 (ten-thousands place)\n\nEighth digit: 8 (thousands place)\n\nNinth digit: 2 (hundreds place)\n\nTenth digit: 7 (tens place)\n\nEleventh digit: 5 (units place)\n\nSo, the number is 6,965,437,827,5. Wait, let me check that. Let's break it down:\n\n- 6 (ten-billions)\n\n- 9 (billions)\n\n- 6 (hundred-millions)\n\n- 5 (ten-millions)\n\n- 4 (hundred-thousands)\n\n- 3 (millions)\n\n- 7 (ten-thousands)\n\n- 8 (thousands)\n\n- 2 (hundreds)\n\n- 7 (tens)\n\n- 5 (units)\n\nSo, writing them together: 6,965,437,827,5. Let me verify this.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can add the numbers using another method to check.\n\nAnother way to add 363,171,983 and 336,390,092 is to break them down:\n\n363,171,983 = 300,000,000 + 60,000,000 + 3,000,000 + 100,000 + 70,000 + 1,000 + 900 + 80 + 3\n\n336,390,092 = 300,000,000 + 30,000,000 + 6,000,000 + 3,000,000 + 900,000 + 0 + 90 + 2\n\nAdding these components:\n\n300,000,000 + 300,000,000 = 600,000,000\n\n60,000,000 + 30,000,000 = 90,000,000\n\n3,000,000 + 6,000,000 = 9,000,000\n\n100,000 + 900,000 = 1,000,000\n\n70,000 + 0 = 70,000\n\n1,000 + 3 = 1,003\n\n900 + 90 = 990\n\n80 + 2 = 82\n\n3 + 2 = 5\n\nNow, adding all these together:\n\n600,000,000 + 90,000,000 = 690,000,000\n\n690,000,000 + 9,000,000 = 699,000,000\n\n699,000,000 + 1,000,000 = 700,000,000\n\n700,000,000 + 70,000 = 700,070,000\n\n700,070,000 + 1,003 = 700,071,003\n\n700,071,003 + 990 = 700,071,993\n\n700,071,993 + 82 = 700,071,993 + 80 + 2 = 700,071,993 + 82 = 700,071,993 + 80 = 700,071,973 + 2 = 700,071,975\n\n700,071,975 + 5 = 700,071,980\n\nWait, hold on. That can't be right. Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, breaking down:\n\nFirst component: 300,000,000 + 300,000,000 = 600,000,000\n\nSecond component: 60,000,000 + 30,000,000 = 90,000,000\n\nThird component: 3,000,000 + 6,000,000 = 9,000,000\n\nFourth component: 100,000 + 900,000 = 1,000,000\n\nFifth component: 70,000 + 0 = 70,000\n\nSixth component: 1,000 + 3 = 1,003\n\nSeventh component: 900 + 90 = 990\n\nEighth component: 80 + 2 = 82\n\nNinth component: 3 + 2 = 5\n\nNow, adding all these:\n\n600,000,000 + 90,000,000 = 690,000,000\n\n690,000,000 + 9,000,000 = 699,000,000\n\n699,000,000 + 1,000,000 = 700,000,000\n\n700,000,000 + 70,000 = 700,070,000\n\n700,070,000 + 1,003 = 700,071,003\n\n700,071,003 + 990 = 700,071,993\n\n700,071,993 + 82 = 700,071,993 + 80 = 700,071,973 + 2 = 700,071,975\n\n700,071,975 + 5 = 700,071,980\n\nWait, so according to this method, the sum is 700,071,980. But when I did the digit-by-digit addition earlier, I got 6,969,543,782,57. These two results are different. That means I must have made a mistake somewhere. Let's figure out which one is correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's use the standard addition algorithm step by step.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically:\n\n      363,171,983\n\n    + 336,390,092\n\n    -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n3 + 2 = 5. Write down 5.\n\n8 + 9 = 17. Write down 7, carryover 1.\n\n1 + 0 = 1 + 1 (carryover) = 2. Write down 2.\n\n9 + 9 = 18. Write down 8, carryover 1.\n\n7 + 0 = 7 + 1 = 8. Write down 8.\n\n1 + 3 = 4. Write down 4.\n\n7 + 6 = 13. Write down 3, carryover 1.\n\n1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5. Write down 5.\n\n3 + 3 = 6. Write down 6.\n\n6 + 3 = 9. Write down 9.\n\n3 + 3 = 6. Write down 6.\n\nSo, putting all the digits together from right to left: 5,7,2,8,8,4,3,5,6,9,6. Wait, that's 5,7,2,8,8,4,3,5,6,9,6. So the number is 6,969,548,832,57? Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Units: 5\n\n2. Tens: 7\n\n3. Hundreds: 2\n\n4. Thousands: 8\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 8\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 4\n\n7. Millions: 3\n\n8. Ten-millions: 5\n\n9. Billions: 6\n\n10. Ten-billions: 9\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, when adding, the first digit we get is the units, then tens, hundreds, etc. So the digits are:\n\nUnits: 5\n\nTens: 7\n\nHundreds: 2\n\nThousands: 8\n\nTen-thousands: 8\n\nHundred-thousands: 4\n\nMillions: 3\n\nTen-millions: 5\n\nBillions: 6\n\nTen-billions: 9\n\nSo, writing them in order from left to right (highest place to lowest):\n\n9 (ten-billions), 6 (billions), 5 (hundred-millions), 4 (ten-thousands), 3 (thousands), 8 (hundred-thousands), 8 (ten-thousands?), Wait, no. Wait, let's list them properly.\n\nWait, the first digit we calculated was 5 (units), then 7 (tens), 2 (hundreds), 8 (thousands), 8 (ten-thousands), 4 (hundred-thousands), 3 (millions), 5 (ten-millions), 6 (billions), 9 (ten-billions). So the number is 9,6,5,4,3,8,8,3,5,6,5. Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nFrom right to left: 5,7,2,8,8,4,3,5,6,9,6\n\nBut when writing the number, we start from the leftmost digit, which is the highest place value. So the digits are:\n\n9 (ten-billions), 6 (billions), 5 (hundred-millions), 4 (ten-thousands), 3 (thousands), 8 (hundred-thousands), 8 (ten-thousands?), Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nFirst digit (leftmost): 9 (ten-billions)\n\nSecond digit: 6 (billions)\n\nThird digit: 5 (hundred-millions)\n\nFourth digit: 4 (ten-thousands)\n\nFifth digit: 3 (thousands)\n\nSixth digit: 8 (hundred-thousands)\n\nSeventh digit: 8 (ten-thousands) \u2013 Wait, no. Wait, the digits from right to left are 5,7,2,8,8,4,3,5,6,9,6. So when written left to right, it's 9,6,5,4,3,8,8,3,5,6,5. Wait, that can't be. Wait, no. Wait, the rightmost digit is 5, so when we write the number, the first digit is the rightmost. Wait, no. Wait, confusion here.\n\nWait, in standard notation, the rightmost digit is the units (1s place), then tens (10s), hundreds (100s), etc. So when we add, the first digit we get is the units, then tens, hundreds, etc., from right to left. But when writing the sum, we need to place the digits in the correct positions from left to right, starting with the highest place value.\n\nSo, the digits we calculated are:\n\nUnits: 5\n\nTens: 7\n\nHundreds: 2\n\nThousands: 8\n\nTen-thousands: 8\n\nHundred-thousands: 4\n\nMillions: 3\n\nTen-millions: 5\n\nBillions: 6\n\nTen-billions: 9\n\nSo, writing them in order from left to right (highest place to lowest):\n\n9 (ten-billions)\n\n6 (billions)\n\n5 (hundred-millions)\n\n4 (ten-thousands)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n8 (hundred-thousands)\n\n8 (ten-thousands) \u2013 Wait, no. Wait, ten-thousands is the fourth place. Wait, let's list all the digits with their place values:\n\n1. Ten-billions: 9\n\n2. Billions: 6\n\n3. Hundred-millions: 5\n\n4. Ten-thousands: 4\n\n5. Thousands: 3\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 8\n\n7. Ten-thousands: 8\n\nWait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nFrom right to left: 5 (units), 7 (tens), 2 (hundreds), 8 (thousands), 8 (ten-thousands), 4 (hundred-thousands), 3 (millions), 5 (ten-millions), 6 (billions), 9 (ten-billions)\n\nBut when writing the number, we need to reverse the order of these digits. So the leftmost digit is the highest place value, which is ten-billions: 9\n\nNext is billions: 6\n\nThen hundred-millions: 5\n\nThen ten-thousands: 4\n\nThen thousands: 3\n\nThen hundred-thousands: 8\n\nThen ten-thousands: 8\n\nWait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nFirst digit (leftmost): 9 (ten-billions)\n\nSecond digit: 6 (billions)\n\nThird digit: 5 (hundred-millions)\n\nFourth digit: 4 (ten-thousands)\n\nFifth digit: 3 (thousands)\n\nSixth digit: 8 (hundred-thousands)\n\nSeventh digit: 8 (ten-thousands) \u2013 Wait, no. Wait, the digits from right to left are 5,7,2,8,8,4,3,5,6,9. So when written left to right, they are 9,6,5,4,3,8,8,3,5,6,5. Wait, that can't be. Wait, no. Wait, the rightmost digit is 5, so when we write the number, the first digit is 5 (units), then 7 (tens), 2 (hundreds), 8 (thousands), 8 (ten-thousands), 4 (hundred-thousands), 3 (millions), 5 (ten-millions), 6 (billions), 9 (ten-billions). So the number is 5,7,2,8,8,4,3,5,6,9. Wait, but that's only 10 digits. Wait, 363,171,983 is 9 digits, and 336,390,092 is also 9 digits. So their sum should be 11 digits? Wait, 363,171,983 is approximately 3.6 billion, and 336,390,092 is approximately 3.36 billion. So adding them gives roughly 7 billion, which is 11 digits. So the sum should be 11 digits. Let's check:\n\nFrom the digit-by-digit addition, we had:\n\n9 (ten-billions)\n\n6 (billions)\n\n5 (hundred-millions)\n\n4 (ten-thousands)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n8 (hundred-thousands)\n\n8 (ten-thousands)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n5 (ten-millions)\n\n6 (billions)\n\n9 (ten-billions)\n\nWait, but that seems conflicting. Wait, perhaps I need to list all 11 digits:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units: 5\n\n2. Tens: 7\n\n3. Hundreds: 2\n\n4. Thousands: 8\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 8\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 4\n\n7. Millions: 3\n\n8. Ten-millions: 5\n\n9. Billions: 6\n\n10. Ten-billions: 9\n\n11. Hundred-billions: 0 (since we didn't have a carryover beyond the ten-billions place)\n\nWait, but in the previous step-by-step addition, we had:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n3 + 2 = 5 (units)\n\n8 + 9 = 17 (tens: 7, carryover 1)\n\n1 + 0 = 1 + 1 = 2 (hundreds)\n\n9 + 9 = 18 (thousands: 8, carryover 1)\n\n7 + 0 = 7 + 1 = 8 (ten-thousands)\n\n1 + 3 = 4 + 0 = 4 (hundred-thousands)\n\n7 + 6 = 13 (millions: 3, carryover 1)\n\n1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5 (ten-millions)\n\n3 + 3 = 6 (billions)\n\n6 + 3 = 9 (ten-billions)\n\n3 + 3 = 6 (hundred-billions)\n\nSo, the digits are:\n\nUnits: 5\n\nTens: 7\n\nHundreds: 2\n\nThousands: 8\n\nTen-thousands: 8\n\nHundred-thousands: 4\n\nMillions: 3\n\nTen-millions: 5\n\nBillions: 6\n\nTen-billions: 9\n\nHundred-billions: 0 (since we didn't have a carryover beyond the ten-billions place)\n\nWait, but 363,171,983 and 336,390,092 are both 9-digit numbers, so their sum can be at most 11 digits (since 9+9=18, which is two digits). So the sum should be 11 digits. So the hundred-billions place would be 0. So the digits are:\n\nFrom hundred-billions to units:\n\n0,9,6,5,4,3,8,8,3,2,7,5\n\nWait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nStarting from the rightmost (units):\n\n1. 5\n\n2. 7\n\n3. 2\n\n4. 8\n\n5. 8\n\n6. 4\n\n7. 3\n\n8. 5\n\n9. 6\n\n10. 9\n\n11. 0\n\nSo the number is 0,9,6,5,4,3,8,8,3,2,7,5. But leading zeros are not written, so it's 9,654,388,327,5. Wait, but that's 11 digits. Let's check:\n\n0 (hundred-billions)\n\n9 (billions)\n\n6 (hundred-millions)\n\n5 (ten-millions)\n\n4 (millions)\n\n3 (hundred-thousands)\n\n8 (ten-thousands)\n\n8 (thousands)\n\n3 (hundreds)\n\n2 (tens)\n\n7 (units)\n\nWait, but that's 11 digits. So the number is 965,438,832,75. Wait, but that conflicts with the previous results. This is getting confusing. Maybe I need to use another method.\n\nAlternatively, let's use the standard addition algorithm step by step, carefully.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically:\n\n      363,171,983\n\n    + 336,390,092\n\n    -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units place):\n\n3 + 2 = 5. Write down 5.\n\nNext digit (tens place):\n\n8 + 9 = 17. Write down 7, carryover 1.\n\nNext digit (hundreds place):\n\n1 + 0 = 1 + 1 (carryover) = 2. Write down 2.\n\nNext digit (thousands place):\n\n9 + 9 = 18. Write down 8, carryover 1.\n\nNext digit (ten-thousands place):\n\n7 + 0 = 7 + 1 = 8. Write down 8.\n\nNext digit (hundred-thousands place):\n\n1 + 3 = 4. Write down 4.\n\nNext digit (millions place):\n\n7 + 6 = 13. Write down 3, carryover 1.\n\nNext digit (ten-millions place):\n\n1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5. Write down 5.\n\nNext digit (billions place):\n\n3 + 3 = 6. Write down 6.\n\nNext digit (ten-billions place):\n\n6 + 3 = 9. Write down 9.\n\nNext digit (hundred-billions place):\n\n0 + 0 = 0. Write down 0.\n\nSo, putting all the digits together from right to left (units to hundred-billions):\n\n5, 7, 2, 8, 8, 4, 3, 5, 6, 9, 0\n\nBut when writing the number, we start from the highest place value. So the digits are:\n\n0 (hundred-billions)\n\n9 (billions)\n\n6 (hundred-millions)\n\n5 (ten-millions)\n\n4 (millions)\n\n3 (hundred-thousands)\n\n8 (ten-thousands)\n\n8 (thousands)\n\n3 (hundreds)\n\n2 (tens)\n\n7 (units)\n\nSo the number is 096,543,883,275. But leading zeros are not written, so it's 965,438,832,75. Wait, but that's 11 digits. Let's check:\n\n- Hundred-billions: 0\n\n- Billions: 9\n\n- Hundred-millions: 6\n\n- Ten-millions: 5\n\n- Millions: 4\n\n- Hundred-thousands: 3\n\n- Ten-thousands: 8\n\n- Thousands: 8\n\n- Hundreds: 3\n\n- Tens: 2\n\n- Units: 7\n\nSo, combining these: 096,543,883,275. Removing the leading zero: 965,438,832,75. Wait, but that's 11 digits. Let's check the count:\n\nDigits:\n\n1. 0\n\n2. 9\n\n3. 6\n\n4. 5\n\n5. 4\n\n6. 3\n\n7. 8\n\n8. 8\n\n9. 3\n\n10. 2\n\n11. 7\n\nYes, 11 digits. So the sum is 965,438,832,75. But wait, this contradicts the previous results. What's going on here?\n\nWait, let's check with another method. Let's use the fact that 363,171,983 + 336,390,092.\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\n363,171,983\n\n+336,390,092\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, add 363,171,983 + 300,000,000 = 663,171,983\n\nThen add 30,000,000: 663,171,983 + 30,000,000 = 693,171,983\n\nThen add 6,000,000: 693,171,983 + 6,000,000 = 700,171,983\n\nThen add 3,000,000: 700,171,983 + 3,000,000 = 703,171,983\n\nThen add 900,000: 703,171,983 + 900,000 = 703,172,883\n\nThen add 0 (since 336,390,092 has no hundreds left): 703,172,883\n\nThen add 90,000: 703,172,883 + 90,000 = 703,173,783\n\nThen add 0: 703,173,783\n\nThen add 0: 703,173,783\n\nThen add 2: 703,173,783 + 2 = 703,173,785\n\nWait, that can't be right. Wait, this method is getting messy. Let me try again.\n\nWait, perhaps breaking down each number:\n\n363,171,983 = 300,000,000 + 60,000,000 + 3,000,000 + 100,000 + 70,000 + 1,000 + 900 + 80 + 3\n\n336,390,092 = 300,000,000 + 30,000,000 + 6,000,000 + 3,000,000 + 900,000 + 0 + 90 + 2\n\nAdding these:\n\n300,000,000 + 300,000,000 = 600,000,000\n\n60,000,000 + 30,000,000 = 90,000,000\n\n3,000,000 + 6,000,000 = 9,000,000\n\n100,000 + 900,000 = 1,000,000\n\n70,000 + 0 = 70,000\n\n1,000 + 3 = 1,003\n\n900 + 90 = 990\n\n80 + 2 = 82\n\n3 + 2 = 5\n\nNow, adding all these components:\n\n600,000,000 + 90,000,000 = 690,000,000\n\n690,000,000 + 9,000,000 = 699,000,000\n\n699,000,000 + 1,000,000 = 700,000,000\n\n700,000,000 + 70,000 = 700,070,000\n\n700,070,000 + 1,003 = 700,071,003\n\n700,071,003 + 990 = 700,071,993\n\n700,071,993 + 82 = 700,071,993 